const QUESTIONS = [
{"id": "o1-001", "t": "docs", "q": "In the SPARROW memory aid for documents required on board, what does the 'O' stand for?", "a": "Operating limitations: AFM/POH, supplements, placards, markings", "d": ["Owner's maintenance records: airframe, engine and propeller logbooks", "Overhaul records: engine and propeller time since overhaul", "Operating checklist: normal and emergency checklists"], "why": "SPARROW = Supplements, Placards, Airworthiness certificate, Registration, Radio station license (if required), Operating limitations, Weight and balance. The operating limitations come from the AFM/POH, its supplements, placards and markings (14 CFR 91.9). Logbooks are maintenance records and are not on the required-on-board list.", "src": "OEG 1A-1 · 14 CFR 91.9"},
{"id": "o1-002", "t": "docs", "q": "When does a U.S.-registered airplane need an FCC radio station license on board?", "a": "Only when it is operated outside the United States", "d": ["Whenever any transmitting radio is installed", "Only when it is operated under IFR", "Only when it is used for hire or flight instruction"], "why": "The OEG lists the radio station license as required only for operations outside the U.S., an FCC rule (47 CFR 87.18). Domestic flights don't need one even with radios installed.", "src": "OEG 1A-1 · 47 CFR 87.18"},
{"id": "o1-003", "t": "docs", "q": "On preflight you find the airworthiness certificate displayed but no registration certificate anywhere in the aircraft. May you fly it?", "a": "No; 14 CFR 91.203 requires the registration certificate on board", "d": ["Yes; the registration is already on file with the FAA registry", "Yes, but only for day VFR flights that stay in the local area", "Yes; 14 CFR 91.203 requires only the airworthiness certificate"], "why": "14 CFR 91.203 requires both the airworthiness certificate and the registration certificate to be in the aircraft. A record at the FAA registry does not substitute for the certificate on board.", "src": "OEG 1A-1 · 14 CFR 91.203"},
{"id": "o1-004", "t": "docs", "q": "Besides the SPARROW documents, which item does the OEG list as required in the aircraft?", "a": "A magnetic compass deviation card", "d": ["The airframe and engine logbooks", "A copy of the Type Certificate Data Sheet", "The pilot's logbook with flight review endorsement"], "why": "The OEG adds the compass deviation card (14 CFR 23.1547) and the external data plate/serial number (14 CFR 45.11) to the SPARROW documents. The logbooks and the TCDS are not on that list.", "src": "OEG 1A-1 · 14 CFR 23.1547"},
{"id": "o1-005", "t": "docs", "q": "How recently must the VOR equipment have been checked for it to be used under IFR?", "a": "Within the preceding 30 days", "d": ["Within the preceding 10 days", "Within the preceding 90 days", "Within the preceding 24 calendar months"], "why": "14 CFR 91.171 requires a VOR accuracy check within the preceding 30 days for IFR use. 24 calendar months is the interval for the altimeter/static system and transponder checks.", "src": "OEG 1A-2 · 14 CFR 91.171"},
{"id": "o1-006", "t": "docs", "q": "For IFR flight, how often must the altimeter, altitude reporting equipment and static system be tested and inspected?", "a": "Every 24 calendar months", "d": ["Every 12 calendar months", "Every 100 hours of time in service", "Every 30 days, by a pilot ground check"], "why": "14 CFR 91.411 sets a 24-calendar-month test of the altimeter, encoder and static system for IFR. The transponder (14 CFR 91.413) is also on a 24-calendar-month cycle.", "src": "OEG 1A-2 · 14 CFR 91.411"},
{"id": "o1-007", "t": "docs", "q": "How often must an ELT's operation and battery condition be inspected under 14 CFR 91.207?", "a": "Every 12 calendar months", "d": ["Every 24 calendar months", "Every 100 hours of time in service", "Every 6 calendar months"], "why": "The OEG's inspection list includes an ELT check of operation and battery condition every 12 calendar months (14 CFR 91.207). 24 calendar months is the altimeter/static and transponder interval.", "src": "OEG 1A-2 · 14 CFR 91.207"},
{"id": "o1-008", "t": "docs", "q": "An annual inspection was signed off on March 10, 2025. With no new annual, through what date may the airplane be flown?", "a": "March 31, 2026", "d": ["March 10, 2026", "February 28, 2026", "March 31, 2027"], "why": "The annual must be within the preceding 12 calendar months (14 CFR 91.409), so it runs to the end of the same month one year later.", "src": "OEG 1A-2 · 14 CFR 91.409"},
{"id": "o1-009", "t": "docs", "q": "A flight school's twin is used for its multi-engine training, flown with the school's own instructors. What inspection does this use add to the annual?", "a": "A 100-hour inspection", "d": ["A 50-hour inspection", "A 200-hour inspection", "A second annual every 6 months"], "why": "An aircraft used for hire, or for flight instruction for hire in an aircraft the instructor provides, needs a 100-hour inspection in addition to the annual (14 CFR 91.409).", "src": "OEG 1A-2 · 14 CFR 91.409"},
{"id": "o1-010", "t": "docs", "q": "You own a twin and hire a freelance CFI, who brings no aircraft, to train you in it. Does your twin need a 100-hour inspection?", "a": "No; the 100-hour applies when the instructor provides the aircraft", "d": ["Yes; any flight instruction given for hire requires a 100-hour inspection", "Yes; every multi-engine airplane needs a 100-hour inspection", "No, but only if its annual was done within the last 6 months"], "why": "The OEG ties the 100-hour to use for hire or to flight instruction in an aircraft the CFI provides (14 CFR 91.409). When the student supplies the airplane, the annual and other applicable checks are what's required.", "src": "OEG 1A-2 · 14 CFR 91.409"},
{"id": "o1-011", "t": "docs", "q": "During the oral, where would you show the examiner that ADs and life-limited parts have been complied with?", "a": "In the airframe and engine logbooks", "d": ["In the Limitations section of the POH", "On the back of the airworthiness certificate", "On the equipment list in the AFM"], "why": "AD and life-limited part compliance is recorded in the maintenance records (14 CFR 91.403, 91.417). The OEG suggests tabbing those logbook pages and making a status sheet showing when each item comes due.", "src": "OEG 1A-2 · 14 CFR 91.403, 91.417"},
{"id": "o1-012", "t": "docs", "q": "Part 91 allows operation with certain inoperative equipment by which two methods?", "a": "An approved MEL (91.213(a)), or the no-MEL procedure of 91.213(d)", "d": ["A special flight permit, or an FAA Form 337 field approval", "The KOEL alone, or an INOP placard approved by the pilot in command", "The POH emergency checklist, or a waiver obtained from ATC"], "why": "14 CFR 91.213 lets inoperative items that aren't essential for safe flight be deferred either with an FAA-approved MEL or, with no MEL, by the 91.213(d) procedure. The OEG notes many applicants are weak on this rule.", "src": "OEG 1A-3 · 14 CFR 91.213"},
{"id": "o1-013", "t": "docs", "q": "You discover inoperative equipment during preflight. Which three choices does the OEG give you?", "a": "Cancel the flight, get maintenance before flight, or defer the item", "d": ["Defer the item, notify ATC, or limit the flight to day VFR", "Cancel the flight, fly with a placard, or file an ASRS report", "Get maintenance, obtain a special flight permit, or fly the airplane as is"], "why": "For items found during preflight or before departure, the decision is to cancel, obtain maintenance before flight, or defer the item using the MEL or 91.213(d).", "src": "OEG 1A-4 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o1-014", "t": "docs", "q": "In cruise, the landing light circuit breaker pops and won't reset. How does the 91.213(d) deferral process apply?", "a": "It doesn't apply in flight; follow the POH/AFM procedures", "d": ["Placard the switch INOP in flight and continue normally", "Write a deferral entry in flight, then continue the flight", "Declare an emergency; the airplane is now unairworthy"], "why": "Deferral under an MEL or 91.213(d) is a preflight tool for items found before departure. A failure that happens in flight is handled with the POH/AFM procedures.", "src": "OEG 1A-4 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o1-015", "t": "docs", "q": "No MEL. Preflight reveals an inoperative item that an airworthiness directive requires to be operational. What is the airplane's status?", "a": "Not airworthy; maintenance is required before flight", "d": ["Airworthy once the item is placarded INOPERATIVE", "Airworthy for day VFR, but not for night or IFR", "Airworthy if the pilot writes it up in the squawk log"], "why": "If the item is required by regulation for the flight, by the type certification or equipment list/KOEL, or by an AD, the airplane is unairworthy and needs maintenance. Placarding only works for items nothing requires.", "src": "OEG 1A-5 · 14 CFR 91.213(d)"},
{"id": "o1-016", "t": "docs", "q": "An inoperative item turns out to be something you may repair as preventive maintenance. Per the OEG, how is the airplane returned to service?", "a": "The pilot may fix it and record the work in the maintenance records", "d": ["The pilot may fix it, but an A&P mechanic must sign the return to service", "The pilot fixes it; preventive maintenance needs no record", "Only a mechanic holding an IA may approve the repair"], "why": "Preventive maintenance (14 CFR Part 43 Appendix A) may be done by a certificated pilot on an aircraft they own or operate, but the work must be logged. The OEG says the pilot may then return the airplane to airworthy condition.", "src": "OEG 1A-5 · 14 CFR 91.213(d)"},
{"id": "o1-017", "t": "docs", "q": "An FAA-approved MEL for a Part 91 airplane applies to which aircraft?", "a": "One specific aircraft, by serial and registration number", "d": ["Every aircraft of that make and model, in any operator's fleet", "Any aircraft that is listed in the operator's fleet", "Any aircraft that the MEL holder happens to be flying"], "why": "An approved MEL is tied to one airframe, identified by its serial and registration numbers. The master MEL (MMEL) for the make and model is only its basis, not authority to operate.", "src": "OEG 1A-6 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o1-018", "t": "docs", "q": "An inoperative item does not appear anywhere in the aircraft's approved MEL. How should you treat it?", "a": "As required equipment that must be working", "d": ["As optional equipment, deferrable at pilot discretion", "As deferrable once a mechanic signs a logbook entry", "As allowed to be inoperative for day VFR flight"], "why": "An MEL works as a list of permissions: only the items listed, under their stated conditions, may be inoperative. Anything outside the list or its conditions must be operational.", "src": "OEG 1A-6 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o1-019", "t": "docs", "q": "What is the difference between an MEL and the aircraft's equipment list?", "a": "The MEL says what may be inop; the equipment list shows what's installed", "d": ["They are one document, filed under two different names in the AFM", "The equipment list says what may be inop; the MEL shows what's installed", "The MEL covers only the avionics; the equipment list covers the rest"], "why": "The OEG warns not to confuse them. The equipment list (in the AFM) records installed equipment and marks some items required; the MEL is an FAA-approved authorization to fly with specific items inoperative.", "src": "OEG 1A-6 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o1-020", "t": "docs", "q": "With an approved MEL, you find the position lights inoperative before a day flight. Which sequence is correct?", "a": "Record it, repair or defer per MEL, switch OFF, open CB, placard INOP", "d": ["Placard INOP, fly the day flight, then record it after landing", "Open the CB, fly day VFR, then have maintenance check the MEL", "Defer per MEL, fly the day flight, then make the maintenance record entry"], "why": "The OEG sequence: enter the discrepancy in the maintenance record, repair or defer under the MEL, then (once day flight is confirmed allowed) leave the switch OFF, open the circuit breaker or follow the procedures document, and placard the switch INOPERATIVE.", "src": "OEG 1A-7 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o1-021", "t": "docs", "q": "What FAA document authorizes a Part 91 operator to use an MEL for a specific aircraft?", "a": "A Letter of Authorization (LOA)", "d": ["A special flight permit", "An FAA Form 337 field approval", "An amended Type Certificate Data Sheet"], "why": "Once the operator requests an MEL and the FAA issues an LOA, the MEL applies to that aircraft and becomes mandatory.", "src": "OEG 1A-8 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o1-022", "t": "docs", "q": "After the FAA issues an LOA for an aircraft's MEL, may the owner defer an item under 91.213(d) instead?", "a": "No; the MEL becomes mandatory for that aircraft", "d": ["Yes; the pilot may pick either method on any flight", "Yes, but only for flights conducted in day VFR", "Yes, if a mechanic agrees and signs the logbook"], "why": "Once the LOA is issued the MEL is mandatory, and every deferral must follow its terms and the operator's procedures document.", "src": "OEG 1A-8 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o1-023", "t": "docs", "q": "How does the FAA regard an approved MEL issued to a small Part 91 airplane?", "a": "As an STC for that aircraft, by serial and registration number", "d": ["As an advisory guide that the pilot in command may set aside", "As an amendment to the type certificate for that whole model", "As a temporary airworthiness certificate for that aircraft"], "why": "The OEG says the FAA treats an approved MEL as a supplemental type certificate tied to one aircraft's serial and registration numbers, not to the whole model.", "src": "OEG 1A-8 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o1-024", "t": "docs", "q": "Which inoperative equipment may a pilot deactivate without help from a maintenance provider?", "a": "Items an MEL or KOEL allows inop, or preventive maintenance items", "d": ["Any item at all, as long as it is then placarded INOPERATIVE", "Any item, provided the pilot holds a commercial certificate", "Only items that are specifically listed on the Type Certificate Data Sheet"], "why": "Per the OEG, other deactivations need a maintenance provider to decide whether the aircraft remains airworthy, disable or remove the item, placard it and make the logbook entry.", "src": "OEG 1A-9 · FAA-H-8083-25, 14 CFR Part 43 App A"},
{"id": "o1-025", "t": "docs", "q": "Per the OEG, 'deactivating' an inoperative item consists of what?", "a": "Disabling it, placarding it, and making a maintenance log entry", "d": ["Removing it, re-weighing the airplane, and filing a Form 337", "Pulling its breaker and briefing the next pilot verbally", "Placarding it and noting it in the flight plan remarks"], "why": "Deactivation means disabling the item, placarding it inoperative, and recording it in the maintenance logs. If it involves maintenance, it must be done and recorded under Part 43.", "src": "OEG 1A-9 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o1-026", "t": "pilot", "q": "A pilot with ASEL and AMEL ratings did a flight review in a Cessna 172 fourteen months ago. Does it satisfy 61.56 for flying a twin?", "a": "Yes; it may be in any aircraft the pilot is rated in", "d": ["No; a separate flight review is needed in each class", "No; multi-engine pilots need a review every 12 months", "Yes, but only if the review included engine-out tasks"], "why": "14 CFR 61.56 requires a flight review every 24 calendar months in an aircraft for which the pilot is rated; one review in either class covers both. Alternating classes is good practice, not a rule.", "src": "OEG 1B-1 · 14 CFR 61.56"},
{"id": "o1-027", "t": "pilot", "q": "A pilot's only recent landings are three in a Piper Seminole last month. May they carry passengers in a Cessna 172?", "a": "No; single- and multi-engine land are different classes", "d": ["Yes; the twin is more complex, so its landings count", "Yes, provided all three landings were to a full stop", "Yes, because both airplanes are in the same category"], "why": "14 CFR 61.57 requires three takeoffs and landings within 90 days in the same category, class and type (if a type rating is required). Twin landings don't count toward single-engine currency.", "src": "OEG 1B-2 · 14 CFR 61.57"},
{"id": "o1-028", "t": "pilot", "q": "A type-rated pilot made three takeoffs and landings in a Boeing 777 last week. Do they count for carrying passengers in a Baron?", "a": "Yes; same category and class, and the Baron needs no type rating", "d": ["No; the landings must be in the same make and model", "No; turbine-airplane landings don't count for pistons", "No; type-rated aircraft count only for other type-rated aircraft"], "why": "Both are airplane multi-engine land. The type requirement applies only when the airplane being flown requires a type rating, which the Baron doesn't.", "src": "OEG 1B-2 · 14 CFR 61.57"},
{"id": "o1-029", "t": "pilot", "q": "A pilot holding a 777 type rating made three landings in a Baron last week. May they now carry passengers in a 777?", "a": "No; the 777 needs the landings in the same type", "d": ["Yes; both are multi-engine land airplanes", "Yes, because the pilot holds a current 777 type rating", "Yes, provided the Baron landings were made at night"], "why": "For an aircraft that requires a type rating, passenger-carrying currency must be in that type (14 CFR 61.57). Currency flows from the 777 to the Baron, not the other way.", "src": "OEG 1B-2 · 14 CFR 61.57"},
{"id": "o1-030", "t": "pilot", "q": "To carry passengers at night in a light twin, what recent experience does 14 CFR 61.57 require?", "a": "Three takeoffs and landings at night in a multi-engine airplane within 90 days", "d": ["Three takeoffs and landings at night in any airplane within 90 days", "Three takeoffs and landings at night in a twin within 6 calendar months", "One takeoff and landing at night in a multi-engine airplane within 90 days"], "why": "Passenger currency is three takeoffs and landings in the preceding 90 days in the same category and class, done at night if the flight is at night (61.57(b) also requires the night landings to be to a full stop). Single-engine landings don't count for the twin.", "src": "OEG 1B-2 · 14 CFR 61.57"},
{"id": "o1-031", "t": "pilot", "q": "A multi-engine, instrument-rated pilot met all instrument currency in a single-engine airplane. May they fly IFR in a twin?", "a": "Yes; instrument currency is by category (airplane), not class", "d": ["No; the approaches must be flown in a multi-engine airplane", "No; an instrument proficiency check in a twin is required", "Yes, but only after flying three IFR approaches in the twin"], "why": "14 CFR 61.57 instrument experience must be in an airplane, powered-lift, helicopter or airship as appropriate, or an appropriately certificated simulator. None of it needs to be done in a twin.", "src": "OEG 1B-3 · 14 CFR 61.57"},
{"id": "o1-032", "t": "pilot", "q": "Which FAA program lets a pilot select multi-engine proficiency tasks and document them, going beyond basic currency?", "a": "The FAA WINGS Pilot Proficiency Program", "d": ["The Aviation Safety Reporting System (ASRS)", "The BasicMed online recurrent medical course", "The Part 141 school stage-check program"], "why": "The OEG suggests practicing ACS tasks, flying with a proficient instructor more often than the 24-month flight review, and completing WINGS tasks documented through the FAA Safety Program.", "src": "OEG 1B-4 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o1-033", "t": "pilot", "q": "Below what altitude does the OEG say simulated engine failures should be reserved for simulators and training devices?", "a": "200 feet AGL", "d": ["400 feet AGL", "500 feet AGL", "1,000 feet AGL"], "why": "Simulated failures below 400 ft AGL call for extreme caution, and those below 200 ft AGL belong in simulators; a low-altitude engine failure isn't worth the risk in the airplane.", "src": "OEG 1B-5 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o1-034", "t": "pilot", "q": "According to the OEG, how should simulated engine failures below 3,000 feet AGL be introduced?", "a": "By smoothly retarding the throttle", "d": ["By moving the mixture to idle cutoff", "By turning the fuel selector OFF", "By switching off the engine's magnetos"], "why": "Using the throttle keeps the engine running so power can be restored at once. Cutting fuel or ignition close to the ground risks a real failure with no time to recover.", "src": "OEG 1B-5 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o1-035", "t": "pilot", "q": "When are unannounced simulated engine failures appropriate in multi-engine flying, per the OEG?", "a": "Only for testing or checking, agreed on before the flight", "d": ["On any training flight, to build realistic reactions", "Whenever the airplane is above 3,000 feet AGL", "Only in the traffic pattern, where real failures occur"], "why": "Simulated failures should be briefed in advance, including how they'll be introduced and the expected response. Surprise failures are only for testing and checking, agreed on before the flight.", "src": "OEG 1B-5 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o1-036", "t": "pilot", "q": "Besides engine failures, what other practice does the OEG recommend to reduce multi-engine training risk?", "a": "Systems failures, including partial-panel instrument work", "d": ["Engine failures close to the ground, below 200 feet AGL", "Unannounced engine cuts on short final approach", "Vmc demonstrations at low altitude near the airport"], "why": "The OEG stresses that systems knowledge is critically important and recommends practicing systems failures and partial-panel flying. It specifically warns against low-altitude and unbriefed engine failures.", "src": "OEG 1B-5 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o1-037", "t": "pilot", "q": "Per the OEG's risk-mitigation guidance, what should you avoid doing on the runway after landing?", "a": "Changing the airplane's configuration without a clear need", "d": ["Using differential braking for directional control", "Holding aileron into the wind during the rollout", "Leaving the flaps extended until the airplane is clear of the runway"], "why": "Reaching for flaps or other controls during the rollout invites grabbing the wrong one, such as the gear selector. Leave the configuration alone until clear unless there's a clear operational need.", "src": "OEG 1B-5 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o1-038", "t": "pilot", "q": "During normal taxi in a light twin, what is the primary means of steering?", "a": "The steerable nosewheel", "d": ["Differential engine power", "Differential braking", "Rudder deflection in the propwash"], "why": "Steering is done mainly with the steerable nosewheel. Differential power can help if needed, and brakes and throttles control momentum.", "src": "OEG 1C-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o1-039", "t": "pilot", "q": "Per the OEG, how should taxi speed be controlled in a light twin?", "a": "With throttles and brakes, keeping power low to avoid riding brakes", "d": ["With steady light braking held against a moderate power setting", "With differential power as the main means of speed control", "With nosewheel steering and rudder alone, without the brakes"], "why": "Keep power low so you aren't dragging the brakes, and use throttles and brakes together to manage speed. A heavier twin carries more momentum and takes longer to stop, so plan ahead.", "src": "OEG 1C-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o1-040", "t": "pilot", "q": "Using differential power to tighten a right turn while taxiing, what do you do?", "a": "Reduce right engine power, add left engine power, use right rudder/brake", "d": ["Add right engine power, reduce left engine power, use right rudder/brake", "Reduce both engines to idle and use the right brake alone", "Add left engine power, reduce right, and hold left rudder/brake"], "why": "More thrust on the left and less on the right yaws the nose right, helping the turn along with right rudder and brake.", "src": "OEG 1C-2 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o1-041", "t": "pilot", "q": "Taxiing with a strong crosswind from the left, which engine gets more power to help hold direction?", "a": "The left (upwind) engine, turning the nose away from the wind", "d": ["The right (downwind) engine, turning the nose into the wind", "Both engines equally, using brakes alone for direction", "The right engine, to counter the left-turning tendency"], "why": "A crosswind makes the airplane weathervane into the wind. Extra power on the upwind engine creates a turning moment away from the wind to counter it.", "src": "OEG 1C-2 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o1-042", "t": "pilot", "q": "Why does the OEG warn against very sharp taxi turns using brakes and differential power?", "a": "It can pivot on a stationary inboard wheel, overstressing the gear", "d": ["Differential power can overheat the inboard engine's cylinders", "Sharp turns can let the propeller tips contact the ground", "It can unlatch the nosewheel steering and jam it off-center"], "why": "Pivoting about a stationary inboard wheel puts loads on the landing gear it wasn't designed for.", "src": "OEG 1C-2 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o1-043", "t": "pilot", "q": "An engine fails during a critical phase of flight. How does the OEG say the checklist should be used?", "a": "Do the memorized action items first, then verify with the checklist", "d": ["Read each item from the printed checklist before taking any action", "Skip the checklist entirely until the airplane is on the ground", "Use the checklist only if the failed engine can't be restarted"], "why": "Time-critical items are flown from memory; once things settle, the written checklist is used to confirm each action. A checklist can be done-then-verify rather than read-and-do, but one is always used.", "src": "OEG 1C-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o1-044", "t": "pilot", "q": "Which is one reason the OEG gives for multi-engine training carrying greater risk?", "a": "Much more training time goes to emergency and engine-out procedures", "d": ["Light twins are required to be flown by a two-pilot crew", "Multi-engine airplanes are inherently less stable in pitch", "Twins have fewer redundant systems than single-engine airplanes"], "why": "The OEG also cites heavier, faster airplanes with complex systems, more planning and skill required, higher workload (worse if not current), and emergencies that can deteriorate much faster than in a single.", "src": "OEG 1C-4 · FAA-H-8083-2, FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o1-045", "t": "pilot", "q": "Fixation is the situational-awareness trap the OEG links to which factor?", "a": "Troubleshooting an equipment malfunction", "d": ["Depending on advanced avionics", "Handling a high workload", "Lacking aircraft knowledge and experience"], "why": "The OEG pairs high workload with task saturation, malfunctions with fixation while troubleshooting, lack of knowledge with confusion, and avionics dependency with complacency and inattention. Distractions and interruptions are the fifth factor.", "src": "OEG 1C-5 · FAA-H-8083-2"},
{"id": "o1-046", "t": "pilot", "q": "What are the three most critical phases of flight in a multi-engine airplane, per the OEG?", "a": "Takeoff, initial climb, and landing", "d": ["Taxi, takeoff, and landing", "Takeoff, cruise, and approach", "En route climb, descent, and go-around"], "why": "Takeoff and initial climb put the airplane low, slow and at its heaviest weight; landing is also slow and close to the ground, so there is little margin.", "src": "OEG 1C-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o1-047", "t": "pilot", "q": "Why does the OEG recommend starting the before-landing checklist early in a twin?", "a": "Patterns and approaches are flown faster than in most singles", "d": ["The landing gear takes several minutes to extend", "ATC requires it before you enter the traffic pattern", "The checklist must be read twice in a multi-engine airplane"], "why": "Higher pattern and approach speeds leave less time, so an early start on the checklist gives room to plan, set up spacing and stay ahead of the airplane.", "src": "OEG 1C-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o1-048", "t": "perf", "q": "In a typical light twin, what is the pilot's first priority during the takeoff roll, per the OEG?", "a": "Reaching at least Vmc + 5 knots before liftoff", "d": ["Reaching Vyse before the nosewheel lifts off", "Lifting off as early as possible to save runway", "Reaching Vx before liftoff to clear obstacles"], "why": "Below Vmc, directional control after an engine failure may be impossible unless power on the good engine is cut right away; the rudder can't counter the asymmetric yaw. The second priority is reaching Vy quickly.", "src": "OEG 1C-8 · FAA Safety ALC-30"},
{"id": "o1-049", "t": "perf", "q": "After liftoff with both engines running, what is the multi-engine pilot's second priority, per the OEG?", "a": "Reaching Vy in the least amount of time", "d": ["Holding Vx until reaching pattern altitude", "Reaching cruise-climb airspeed immediately", "Holding Vmc + 5 until reaching 400 feet AGL"], "why": "Vy gives the greatest rate of climb with both engines operating, gaining altitude quickly. If an engine then fails, Vyse is held.", "src": "OEG 1C-8 · FAA Safety ALC-30"},
{"id": "o1-050", "t": "perf", "q": "If an engine fails after takeoff, why does the OEG say to hold Vyse?", "a": "It gives the best OEI climb rate, or the least sink", "d": ["It is the lowest speed with full rudder authority", "It gives the most altitude per distance with OEI", "It is the minimum speed for intentional engine cuts"], "why": "With the dead engine feathered and a slight bank into the good engine, Vyse yields the most climb per minute, or the least sink if the airplane can't climb. Most gain per distance is Vxse; intentional cuts are at or above Vsse.", "src": "OEG 1C-8 · FAA Safety ALC-30"},
{"id": "o1-051", "t": "perf", "q": "If the manufacturer publishes no rotation speed, what minimum Vr does the OEG suggest for a light twin?", "a": "Vmc + 5 knots", "d": ["Vmc (red line)", "Vyse (blue line)", "1.3 Vso"], "why": "Use the published Vr or Vlof when given; otherwise use at least Vmc + 5 knots. As a rule, light twins shouldn't be airborne before Vmc.", "src": "OEG 1C-9 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o1-052", "t": "perf", "q": "If climbing at Vy after takeoff would need a very steep nose-up attitude, what does the OEG recommend?", "a": "Limit the initial pitch attitude to 15 degrees", "d": ["Limit the initial pitch attitude to 10 degrees", "Limit the initial pitch attitude to 20 degrees", "Climb at Vx instead until reaching 400 feet AGL"], "why": "A pitch attitude above about 15 degrees would make control difficult if an engine quits, so the OEG suggests capping initial pitch at 15 degrees.", "src": "OEG 1C-9 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o1-053", "t": "perf", "q": "On a normal multi-engine departure, Vy is held until reaching what, per the OEG?", "a": "A safe single-engine maneuvering altitude, usually 400+ ft AGL", "d": ["Traffic pattern altitude, typically 1,000 feet AGL", "The single-engine service ceiling for the day's conditions", "A safe gear-retraction height, typically 200 feet AGL"], "why": "Right after takeoff, altitude is the priority over an en route climb speed. Vy is held until reaching a height, typically at least 400 ft AGL, from which single-engine maneuvering is safe.", "src": "OEG 1C-9 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o1-054", "t": "perf", "q": "Normally, when should the landing gear be retracted after a multi-engine takeoff?", "a": "Positive rate of climb and too little runway left to land", "d": ["As soon as the main wheels have left the runway surface", "Upon reaching Vy and an altitude of 400 feet AGL", "Upon reaching Vyse, regardless of how much runway remains"], "why": "Keep the gear down while a landing straight ahead on the remaining runway or overrun is still possible, and raise it once a positive rate of climb shows on the altimeter.", "src": "OEG 1C-9, 1C-10 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o1-055", "t": "perf", "q": "Departing a 9,000-ft runway at an airport 5,000 ft MSL, why not keep the gear down until the runway is used up?", "a": "It would give up climb performance and acceleration for too long", "d": ["The gear extension speed limit is lower at high-altitude airports", "The gear takes much longer to retract in thin, high-altitude air", "Regulations require retraction before passing the departure end"], "why": "On a very long runway, waiting until the end to raise the gear costs climb and acceleration for little benefit. The extra drag hurts even more in thin, high-density-altitude air.", "src": "OEG 1C-10 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o1-056", "t": "perf", "q": "Why might waiting for a positive rate of climb before raising the gear be impractical at a high-density-altitude airport?", "a": "Some twins can't climb at all there with the gear down", "d": ["The altimeter lags too much at high density altitude", "Gear retraction is prohibited above a set density altitude", "The gear may fail to lock up in thin air"], "why": "At high density altitude some twins have so little excess power that gear drag alone prevents a climb, so waiting for a positive rate on the altimeter could mean waiting indefinitely.", "src": "OEG 1C-10 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o1-058", "t": "docs", "q": "No MEL. What is the first question in the OEG's 91.213(d) decision sequence for inoperative equipment?", "a": "Is the item part of the VFR-day type certification requirements?", "d": ["Is the item required to be working by an airworthiness directive?", "Is the item required by 14 CFR 91.205 for the planned flight?", "Can the item be deactivated and placarded inoperative?"], "why": "The order is: (1) VFR-day type certification, (2) 'Required' on the equipment list or KOEL, (3) 91.205 or other Part 91 rules, (4) an AD. Any yes means unairworthy. Then remove or deactivate and placard, and a pilot or mechanic decides it's not a hazard.", "src": "OEG App 3 · 14 CFR 91.213(d)(2)(i)"},
{"id": "o1-059", "t": "docs", "q": "Where can you generally find the equipment required by an airplane's certification regulations?", "a": "The aircraft's equipment list and Type Certificate Data Sheet", "d": ["The airworthiness certificate and the registration certificate", "14 CFR 91.205 and the Aeronautical Information Manual", "The pilot's checklist and the placards in the cockpit"], "why": "Checking the certification rules (Part 23, or CAR 3 for much older aircraft) directly is complex; the OEG points to the equipment list and TCDS instead. 91.205 lists equipment for kinds of operation, which is a separate step.", "src": "OEG App 3 · 14 CFR 91.213(d)(2)(i)"},
{"id": "o1-060", "t": "docs", "q": "Which kinds of operation does a typical kinds of operations equipment list (KOEL) address?", "a": "VFR day, VFR night, IFR day, IFR night, and icing", "d": ["Normal, utility, acrobatic, and commuter categories", "Part 91, Part 135, and Part 121 operations", "Day, night, mountain, and overwater operations"], "why": "The KOEL shows required equipment for each kind of operation the airplane is approved for. The equipment list and KOEL are in the AFM; an item marked 'Required' for the planned operation makes the airplane unairworthy if it's inoperative.", "src": "OEG App 3 · 14 CFR 91.213(d)(2)(ii)"},
{"id": "o1-061", "t": "docs", "q": "No MEL. You plan a night VFR flight and find the position lights inoperative. What applies?", "a": "It isn't airworthy for that flight until they're repaired", "d": ["Placard them INOP and fly; the strobes satisfy the rule", "Deactivate and placard them; you may then fly at night", "They aren't required under VFR, so no action is needed"], "why": "Step 3 asks whether 91.205 or another Part 91 rule (such as 91.209, aircraft lights) requires the item for the kind of operation. Position lights are required at night, so they can't be deferred for a night flight.", "src": "OEG App 3 · 14 CFR 91.213(d)(2)(iii)"},
{"id": "o1-062", "t": "docs", "q": "In the no-MEL decision sequence, an inoperative ATC transponder would be checked against which Part 91 equipment rule?", "a": "14 CFR 91.215", "d": ["14 CFR 91.207", "14 CFR 91.209", "14 CFR 91.211"], "why": "The OEG lists 91.205 (VFR day, VFR night, IFR equipment), 91.207 (ELTs), 91.209 (aircraft lights) and 91.215 (ATC transponders). 91.211 covers supplemental oxygen.", "src": "OEG App 3 · 14 CFR 91.213(d)(2)(iii)"},
{"id": "o1-063", "t": "docs", "q": "No MEL. If no type certification rule, equipment list/KOEL, Part 91 rule or AD requires the inoperative item, what must happen before flight?", "a": "Remove or deactivate it, and placard it or its control", "d": ["Nothing, as long as it isn't needed for the planned flight", "Note it on the squawk sheet for the next annual", "Obtain a special flight permit from the FSDO"], "why": "14 CFR 91.213(d)(3) requires the item to be removed (flight deck control placarded, logbook updated per 14 CFR 43.9) or deactivated and placarded 'Inoperative'. Deactivation that involves maintenance must be done and recorded under Part 43.", "src": "OEG App 3 · 14 CFR 91.213(d)(3)"},
{"id": "o1-064", "t": "docs", "q": "Under 91.213(d), who makes the final determination that an inoperative item is not a hazard for the planned flight?", "a": "A certificated, appropriately rated pilot or mechanic", "d": ["An FAA aviation safety inspector at the local FSDO", "The registered owner shown on the aircraft's registration", "Only a mechanic holding an Inspection Authorization"], "why": "The final step: a certificated, appropriately rated pilot or mechanic decides the inoperative item creates no hazard for the planned flight conditions (e.g., day VFR, night VFR).", "src": "OEG App 3 · 14 CFR 91.213(d)"},
{"id": "o1-065", "t": "docs", "q": "Your airplane has an MEL. An inoperative item isn't in the MEL but is required by the type certification. What is the status?", "a": "Not airworthy; maintenance is required before flight", "d": ["Airworthy if deferred under 91.213(d) instead", "Airworthy once an 'O' procedure is done and it is placarded", "Airworthy for day VFR flights, once placarded"], "why": "The first MEL step asks whether the item is missing from the MEL but required by type certification, an AD or other special conditions. If so, it must be repaired; 91.213(d) is not an option once an MEL is in use.", "src": "OEG App 3 · 14 CFR 91.213"},
{"id": "o1-066", "t": "docs", "q": "In an MEL, what does an 'M' procedure mean?", "a": "Maintenance personnel must do it before operating with the item inop", "d": ["The pilot may do it before or during operation with the item inop", "It is a monitoring item the pilot checks before each flight", "Maintenance must repair the item within the next 10 days"], "why": "'M' (maintenance) procedures must be done by maintenance personnel before operation begins. 'O' (operations) procedures can be done by the pilot, before or during operation with the item inoperative.", "src": "OEG App 3 · 14 CFR 91.213"},
{"id": "o1-067", "t": "docs", "q": "Under an MEL, after the O or M procedure is done, what are the remaining steps?", "a": "Placard it, update the logbook, and confirm it poses no hazard", "d": ["Obtain a new LOA from the FAA, then placard the item", "Notify the local FSDO by phone, then fly only the next scheduled flight", "Update the logbook; placards are not used with an MEL"], "why": "The OEG's MEL sequence: (1) check it isn't required outside the MEL, (2) do the O or M procedure, (3) placard and update the maintenance record, (4) the pilot confirms it doesn't create a hazard for the flight conditions.", "src": "OEG App 3 · 14 CFR 91.213"},
{"id": "o1-068", "t": "eo", "q": "In the OEG light-twin takeoff briefing, what is the plan if an engine fails before reaching Vmc or Vr?", "a": "Retard both throttles and abort the takeoff", "d": ["Continue the takeoff and feather the failed engine", "Rotate immediately and climb out at Vxse", "Keep full power on the good engine and steer with rudder"], "why": "Below Vmc the airplane can't be controlled with asymmetric thrust, and below Vr it isn't ready to fly, so the plan is to close both throttles and stop.", "src": "OEG 1C-7 · OEG App 4 · FAA Safety ALC-30"},
{"id": "o1-071", "t": "eo", "q": "What does the 'Configure' step of the OEG engine-out takeoff briefing include?", "a": "Flaps, gear, and propeller", "d": ["Cowl flaps, trim, and fuel pumps", "Mixtures, magnetos, and alternators", "Lights, transponder, and radios"], "why": "Configure means cleaning up drag: flaps and gear up, and the failed engine's prop feathered.", "src": "OEG 1C-7 · OEG App 4 · FAA Safety ALC-30"},
{"id": "o1-072", "t": "eo", "q": "In the 'Climb' step after an engine failure on takeoff, what do you maintain?", "a": "Vyse with zero sideslip", "d": ["Vy with the wings level", "Vxse with the ball centered", "Vmc with full rudder applied"], "why": "Hold Vyse and fly zero sideslip (a small bank toward the operating engine) to minimize drag and get the best one-engine climb.", "src": "OEG 1C-7 · OEG App 4 · FAA Safety ALC-30"},
{"id": "o1-073", "t": "eo", "q": "Per the OEG takeoff briefing, when is the checklist run after an engine failure with the gear up?", "a": "Upon reaching 400 feet AGL", "d": ["Immediately, before any other action", "Upon reaching 1,000 feet AGL", "Only after returning to land"], "why": "The memorized control, configure and climb actions come first; the written checklist is used once the airplane reaches 400 ft AGL.", "src": "OEG 1C-7 · OEG App 4 · FAA Safety ALC-30"},
{"id": "o1-074", "t": "perf", "q": "Which distance, besides takeoff distance, is stated in the OEG light-twin takeoff briefing?", "a": "Accelerate-stop distance", "d": ["Accelerate-go distance", "Landing distance over a 50-ft obstacle", "Balanced field length"], "why": "The template covers density altitude, runway length, takeoff weight, takeoff distance, accelerate-stop distance, single-engine climb rate and service ceiling, and Vmc, Vr, Vyse and Vy.", "src": "OEG 1C-7 · OEG App 4 · FAA Safety ALC-30"},
{"id": "o1-075", "t": "eo", "q": "In the OEG takeoff briefing, the gear-up branch (control, configure, climb, checklist) applies once airborne at or above what speed?", "a": "Vxse", "d": ["Vmc", "Vsse", "Vr"], "why": "The briefing splits failures by phase: below Vmc or Vr, abort; airborne with the gear down, close both throttles and land ahead; airborne at or above Vxse with the gear up, fly the POH/AFM engine-out procedure.", "src": "OEG 1C-7 · OEG App 4 · FAA Safety ALC-30"},
{"id": "o1-076", "t": "docs", "q": "You plan a VFR flight through Class C airspace. How recently must the ATC transponder have been tested and inspected?", "a": "Within the preceding 24 calendar months", "d": ["Within the preceding 12 calendar months", "Within the last 100 hours of time in service", "Within the preceding 30 days"], "why": "14 CFR 91.413 requires a transponder test and inspection within the preceding 24 calendar months for any use, VFR or IFR. 30 days is the IFR VOR check; 12 calendar months is the annual and ELT interval.", "src": "OEG 1A-2 · 14 CFR 91.413"},
{"id": "o1-077", "t": "docs", "q": "In the SPARROW list of documents required on board, what does the 'W' stand for?", "a": "Current weight and balance data", "d": ["Current weather briefing record", "Current pilot logbook and medical", "Current airframe and engine logbooks"], "why": "The OEG lists current weight and balance data (citing 14 CFR 23.2620) as the last SPARROW item. Maintenance logbooks, the pilot's logbook and a weather briefing record are not on the required-on-board list.", "src": "OEG 1A-1 · 14 CFR 23.2620"},
{"id": "o1-078", "t": "docs", "q": "No MEL. The inoperative item is required by the aircraft's TCDS or was installed under an STC. Per OEG 1A-5, who must address it?", "a": "A maintenance provider, who decides if it can be made airworthy", "d": ["The pilot, by placarding it INOPERATIVE and logging it", "The pilot, as preventive maintenance under 14 CFR Part 43 Appendix A", "The registered owner, by signing a logbook entry"], "why": "Per OEG 1A-5, items tied to the type certificate data sheet, the factory equipment list or an STC go to a maintenance provider, who decides whether the airplane can be made airworthy and makes a logbook entry. Equipment nothing requires that won't be repaired is disabled, placarded inoperative and logged; an item the type certification requires can't be deferred this way (App 3).", "src": "OEG 1A-5 · 14 CFR 91.213(d)"},
{"id": "o2-002", "t": "perf", "q": "What is VR?", "a": "Rotation speed, where back pressure is applied to pitch up to takeoff attitude", "d": ["Lift-off speed, the speed at which the airplane actually leaves the runway surface", "Refusal speed, the highest speed from which a stop is still possible", "Reference landing speed, the final approach speed corrected for wind"], "why": "VR is when you start the rotation; VLOF is when the wheels actually leave the surface. Depending on the manufacturer, takeoff performance data may be referenced to either one.", "src": "OEG 1D-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-003", "t": "perf", "q": "Which describes Vx?", "a": "Best angle of climb: most altitude per distance traveled forward", "d": ["Best rate of climb: most altitude per unit of time elapsed", "Best glide: least altitude lost per distance traveled forward", "Cruise climb: best balance of climb rate, cooling, and groundspeed"], "why": "Vx maximizes altitude over a given horizontal distance (obstacle clearance); Vy maximizes altitude over a given time.", "src": "OEG 1D-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-004", "t": "perf", "q": "With both engines running, you want to gain the most altitude in the least time after takeoff. Which speed do you fly?", "a": "Vy", "d": ["Vx", "Vxse", "Vsse"], "why": "Vy is the best rate-of-climb speed: greatest altitude per unit of time. Vx gives the most altitude per distance, which is for obstacles.", "src": "OEG 1D-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-005", "t": "perf", "q": "What is Vxse?", "a": "Best angle-of-climb speed with one engine inoperative", "d": ["Best rate-of-climb speed with one engine inoperative", "Minimum speed for intentionally failing an engine in training", "Minimum control speed with the critical engine inoperative"], "why": "The 'se' suffix means single engine: Vxse is the one-engine best angle speed. Vyse is one-engine best rate, Vsse is the training speed, Vmc is control speed.", "src": "OEG 1D-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-006", "t": "perf", "q": "On most light-twin airspeed indicators, what do the low-speed red radial line and the blue radial line mark?", "a": "Red: Vmc. Blue: Vyse", "d": ["Red: Vyse. Blue: Vmc", "Red: Vsse. Blue: Vxse", "Red: Vmc. Blue: Vxse"], "why": "The red radial at the low end is the critical-engine-inoperative minimum control speed; the blue radial ('blue line') is the one-engine best rate-of-climb speed.", "src": "OEG 1D-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-007", "t": "perf", "q": "What is Vsse?", "a": "Minimum speed at which to intentionally render the critical engine inoperative", "d": ["Minimum speed at which directional control can be held with the critical engine out", "Speed giving the best rate of climb with one engine inoperative", "Speed giving the best angle of climb with one engine inoperative"], "why": "Vsse (once called safe single-engine speed) is the floor for simulated engine failures. The directional-control definition describes Vmc.", "src": "OEG 1D-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-009", "t": "perf", "q": "What does VA represent?", "a": "Design maneuvering speed", "d": ["Approach speed with flaps extended", "Maximum gear-extended speed", "Accelerate-stop decision speed"], "why": "VA is design maneuvering speed. The gear-extended limits are VLE/VLO, and VA is not an approach or decision speed.", "src": "OEG 1D-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-010", "t": "perf", "q": "How does the OEG define VREF?", "a": "1.3 times the stall speed in the landing configuration", "d": ["1.2 times the stall speed in the takeoff configuration", "1.3 times Vmc in the landing configuration", "1.3 times the clean (flaps-up) stall speed"], "why": "The OEG defines VREF as 1.3 Vso (stall speed in the landing configuration), the final approach reference that is then adjusted for wind and gusts.", "src": "OEG 1D-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-011", "t": "perf", "q": "Stall speed in the landing configuration is 62 knots. What is VREF before any wind or gust adjustment?", "a": "About 81 knots", "d": ["About 74 knots", "About 93 knots", "About 67 knots"], "why": "VREF = 1.3 x 62 = 80.6 knots. 74 knots would be 1.2 times, and 67 knots just adds 5 knots.", "src": "OEG 1D-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-012", "t": "perf", "q": "Why do manufacturers publish Vsse?", "a": "To reduce loss-of-control risk when engine failures are simulated near Vmc", "d": ["To set the minimum safe speed for retracting the landing gear after takeoff", "To define the speed for the best one-engine climb gradient over obstacles", "To set the maximum speed for flight in severe turbulence with one engine"], "why": "Vsse builds in a margin above Vmc for intentional engine cuts, lowering the chance of losing control when a failure is simulated at or near minimum control speed.", "src": "OEG 1D-2 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o2-013", "t": "perf", "q": "Your instructor will reduce power on one engine to simulate a failure in flight. Per the OEG, what conditions should be met first?", "a": "A safe altitude above terrain and an airspeed at or above Vsse", "d": ["An airspeed at or above Vmc and below maneuvering speed", "An airspeed at or above Vxse with flaps fully extended", "Gear and flaps extended and an airspeed just above Vmc"], "why": "Engine failure demonstrations belong at a safe altitude, with the power reduction made at or above Vsse, not merely above Vmc.", "src": "OEG 1D-2 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o2-014", "t": "perf", "q": "What is accelerate-stop distance?", "a": "Runway needed to reach VR or VLOF, lose an engine, and come to a complete stop", "d": ["Distance to reach VR or VLOF, lose an engine, and climb on one engine to 50 feet", "Distance to lift off and clear a 50-foot obstacle with both engines operating", "Runway needed to accelerate to Vyse on the ground and then stop with full braking"], "why": "Accelerate-stop is the runway to accelerate to the manufacturer's specified speed (VR or VLOF), have an engine fail, and stop. Climbing to 50 feet on one engine is accelerate-go.", "src": "OEG 1D-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-015", "t": "perf", "q": "Part 91 private flight. The POH lists accelerate-stop as advisory data: 3,400 feet. The runway is 3,000 feet. Which is correct?", "a": "Legal, but unwise: a stop after an engine failure isn't assured", "d": ["Prohibited under Part 91 for all multi-engine airplanes", "Acceptable, because only accelerate-go distance matters for a twin", "Acceptable if you rotate 5 knots below the published VR"], "why": "It isn't prohibited for private carriage, but if an engine fails on the roll you may not be able to stop on the runway. If you go anyway, do so knowing the risk.", "src": "OEG 1D-4 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-016", "t": "perf", "q": "When does accelerate-stop distance become a binding limitation rather than advisory data?", "a": "When it's in the POH/AFM Limitations section or required for commercial carriage", "d": ["Whenever the POH publishes it anywhere, including in the performance section", "Only when the density altitude at the field exceeds the SE service ceiling", "Only when the runway is wet, contaminated, or shorter than 3,000 feet"], "why": "In most POHs the figure is only advisory. It is binding when the manufacturer puts it in the Limitations section, or when commercial operating rules require it.", "src": "OEG 1D-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-017", "t": "perf", "q": "Density altitude is above your SE service ceiling and accelerate-stop exceeds the runway. If you still take off, what should your plan include?", "a": "Review nearby landing areas and OEI procedures; cut fuel, bags or passengers", "d": ["Plan to continue after any engine failure, since stopping is impossible", "Use full flaps and a lower rotation speed to shorten the ground roll", "Leave the gear down until 1,000 feet AGL to keep a landing option open"], "why": "The OEG's alternate plan: know emergency landing areas and other runways near the field, review engine-out speeds and procedures, and reduce weight to improve one-engine performance.", "src": "OEG 1D-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-018", "t": "perf", "q": "What is accelerate-go distance?", "a": "Distance to reach VR or VLOF, lose an engine, and climb on one engine to 50 feet", "d": ["Runway needed to reach VR or VLOF, lose an engine, and come to a complete stop", "Distance to lift off and clear a 50-foot obstacle with both engines operating", "Distance to reach VR or VLOF, lose an engine, and climb on one engine to 400 ft AGL"], "why": "Accelerate-go assumes the failure at VR or VLOF and ends at 50 feet above the runway on the remaining engine, not at a safe maneuvering altitude.", "src": "OEG 1D-7 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o2-019", "t": "perf", "q": "A twin's accelerate-go chart says you can lose the left engine at lift-off and still clear a 50-foot obstacle. What does that really promise?", "a": "About one wingspan of height, under ideal conditions and flawless flying", "d": ["A safe climb to 400 feet AGL at Vyse, as long as you feather promptly", "Obstacle clearance with a 50 percent safety margin built into the data", "Obstacle clearance even with the gear down and the propeller windmilling"], "why": "The chart only gets you to 50 feet, and only if you instantly recognize the failure, raise the gear, identify and feather the failed engine, and hold precise control while easing to Vyse. Density altitude and weight cut into it further.", "src": "OEG 1D-8 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-020", "t": "perf", "q": "Are accelerate-go distances published for every light twin?", "a": "No; not all POHs include them, and published data reflect ideal tests", "d": ["Yes; Part 23 requires them for all multi-engine airplanes since 1991", "Yes; they appear in the Limitations section of every twin's AFM", "No; they are published only for twins over 6,000 lb max weight"], "why": "Some manufacturers don't publish them, and published figures come from ideal test flying that an everyday airplane and pilot are unlikely to match.", "src": "OEG 1D-9 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-021", "t": "perf", "q": "If your checkride airplane's POH has accelerate-stop/go charts (the Seneca has accelerate-stop, chart 8-6), how should you use them on exam day?", "a": "Work them for today's conditions, brief them, and add a safety margin", "d": ["Use the figures as published, since they already include a safety margin", "Skip them, since accelerate-stop/go data are advisory only under Part 91", "Use the sea level standard-day values as a conservative estimate"], "why": "The examiner expects you to work the charts for the actual conditions and apply them in the pre-takeoff briefing, while treating chart numbers skeptically.", "src": "OEG 1D-9 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-022", "t": "perf", "q": "If a twin's POH gives no accelerate-stop data, how does the OEG suggest getting a practical figure for that airplane?", "a": "Do several accelerate-and-stop runs at max gross weight on a long runway", "d": ["Multiply the published takeoff ground roll by 1.3 for a safety margin", "Add 1,000 feet to the published takeoff distance over a 50-foot obstacle", "Use the published accelerate-go distance, since it is always longer"], "why": "Accelerate to takeoff speed and stop, several times, measuring distance by counting runway lights (the airport can tell you their spacing).", "src": "OEG 1D-10 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o2-023", "t": "perf", "q": "Normal takeoff distance is 1,400 ft; landing distance over a 50-ft obstacle is 1,600 ft. Using the OEG rule of thumb with a 50% margin, rough accelerate-stop?", "a": "About 4,500 feet", "d": ["About 3,000 feet", "About 3,900 feet", "About 2,400 feet"], "why": "Add normal takeoff distance to landing distance over 50 feet (3,000 ft), then add a 50 percent real-world margin: 3,000 x 1.5 = 4,500 ft.", "src": "OEG 1D-10 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o2-024", "t": "perf", "q": "After a short-field takeoff, why does the OEG recommend moving to Vyse or faster as soon as obstacles allow?", "a": "Vx and Vy may sit close to Vmc, inviting loss of control if an engine fails", "d": ["Vyse gives the steepest all-engine climb angle for clearing the obstacles", "Part 23 requires a climb at Vyse as soon as the landing gear is retracted", "The stall warning system is unreliable at the low airspeeds of Vx and Vy"], "why": "In some light twins Vx (and even Vy) sits close to Vmc. Accelerating to blue line or faster restores control margin if an engine quits.", "src": "OEG 1D-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-025", "t": "perf", "q": "Which set matches the performance factors the OEG says to review when planning a multi-engine takeoff?", "a": "W&B, takeoff and 50-ft distances, accel-stop/go, DA vs. SE ceiling, OEI climb", "d": ["Cruise TAS, enroute fuel burn, top-of-descent point, and landing ground roll", "Glide range, Vne, maneuvering speed, and the maximum demonstrated crosswind", "Range, endurance, cruise power setting, and the all-engine absolute ceiling"], "why": "Takeoff planning centers on weight and balance, takeoff distances, accelerate-stop/go, density altitude versus single-engine service ceiling, and expected OEI climb, with margins added.", "src": "OEG 1D-12 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-026", "t": "perf", "q": "Which sources does the OEG suggest for spotting obstructions that could matter after an engine failure on departure?", "a": "Sectional/TAC charts plus SIAPs and departure procedures", "d": ["The airport diagram and the ATIS or AWOS broadcast for the field", "The POH performance section and the W&B worksheet", "Pilot reports and the winds and temperatures aloft"], "why": "Trees, towers, power lines and rising terrain become hazards in an emergency. VFR charts and instrument procedure charts both depict them.", "src": "OEG 1D-13 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-027", "t": "perf", "q": "Per the OEG, what should your multi-engine takeoff briefing review?", "a": "Emergency procedures, V-speeds, and your plan of action", "d": ["Cruise altitude, planned fuel stops, and destination weather", "Passenger items only, such as seat belts and exits", "ATC frequencies and the expected taxi route"], "why": "The briefing prepares you to act immediately if an engine fails. The OEG also notes that night or low ceilings leave fewer options, so factor in time and weather.", "src": "OEG 1D-13 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-028", "t": "perf", "q": "Why does a higher density altitude increase a normally aspirated twin's takeoff distance?", "a": "Lift-off needs a higher TAS, while engines and props produce less thrust", "d": ["The indicated airspeed needed for lift-off rises as the air gets thinner", "Tire rolling friction and brake drag both increase in the thinner air", "Vmc increases with altitude, so the rotation speed must be raised to match"], "why": "You lift off at the same IAS, but that is a higher TAS in thin air, and a normally aspirated engine and prop deliver less thrust to get there. Accelerate-stop/go distances grow for the same reasons.", "src": "OEG 1D-14 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-029", "t": "perf", "q": "You land at a high density altitude airport using your normal approach IAS. What should you expect?", "a": "Higher true airspeed, so a longer landing roll", "d": ["Lower true airspeed, so a shorter landing roll", "A need for a higher IAS to avoid stalling", "The same true airspeed and landing roll as at sea level"], "why": "Fly the same indicated speeds; the airplane stalls at the same IAS. But the TAS is higher, so the ground roll is longer.", "src": "OEG 1D-14 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-031", "t": "perf", "q": "What effect does increasing density altitude have on climb performance and ceilings?", "a": "Lower all-engine and OEI climb rates, and lower service ceilings", "d": ["Lower climb rates, but the service ceilings remain unchanged", "Lower all-engine climb only; OEI climb is unaffected", "Lower climb rates, but higher ceilings due to the higher true airspeed"], "why": "Both all-engine and one-engine-inoperative rates of climb drop, and so do the all-engine and single-engine service ceilings.", "src": "OEG 1D-14 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-032", "t": "perf", "q": "You plan a short-field takeoff in a twin whose Vx is only 3 knots above Vmc. What does the OEG advise?", "a": "Reduce useful load or pick another runway to avoid short-field technique", "d": ["Use extra takeoff flaps to widen the margin between Vx and Vmc", "Rotate right at Vmc and hold Vx until all obstacles are cleared, then Vy", "Plan to climb at Vxse instead, since Vxse is always well above Vmc"], "why": "With Vx less than 5 knots above Vmc there is almost no margin if an engine fails. Flaps add drag and make it worse; Vxse is also often close to Vmc.", "src": "OEG 1D-15 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-033", "t": "perf", "q": "What four main factors determine climb performance?", "a": "Airspeed, drag, power, and weight", "d": ["Airspeed, altitude, temperature, and humidity", "Power, thrust, lift, and pitch attitude", "Weight, CG, wing area, and flap setting"], "why": "Airspeed (too slow or too fast both hurt), drag (gear, flaps, cowl flaps, prop), power in excess of that needed for level flight, and weight (people, bags, fuel).", "src": "OEG 1D-16 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o2-034", "t": "perf", "q": "Late in the takeoff roll, acceleration seems poor and you aren't sure the takeoff can be continued safely. Little runway remains. OEG guidance?", "a": "Abort; a low-energy overrun beats a high-energy accident", "d": ["Continue; once past midfield, an abort is always the riskier choice", "Continue, lift off at Vmc, and then accelerate in ground effect", "Abort only if you're sure you can stop on the remaining pavement"], "why": "Abort whenever it isn't clear the takeoff can continue safely, even if it means running off the end. Low-energy accidents are far more survivable.", "src": "OEG 1D-17 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-036", "t": "perf", "q": "Why does losing half the power cost far more than half of the climb performance?", "a": "Climb uses only power above that needed for level flight, and drag rises", "d": ["The operating engine loses power from disturbed inlet airflow in a sideslip", "The operating engine's propeller is much less efficient at blue line speed", "Total thrust drops by well over 50 percent when one engine is lost"], "why": "Climb comes only from surplus power. The remaining engine must supply nearly all the power for level flight, and a windmilling prop, gear or flaps add drag on top.", "src": "OEG 1D-18 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o2-037", "t": "perf", "q": "As airspeed increases, how do drag and the power required to maintain it change?", "a": "Drag rises with the square of airspeed; power required with the cube", "d": ["Both rise in direct proportion to airspeed", "Drag rises with the cube of airspeed; power required with the square", "Drag rises with the square of airspeed; power required stays constant"], "why": "Power is drag times speed, so if drag grows with V squared, power required grows with V cubed. Excess speed quickly eats the little surplus power left on one engine.", "src": "OEG 1D-18 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o2-038", "t": "perf", "q": "On an extended climb, what is an advantage of a cruise-climb airspeed over Vy?", "a": "Better engine cooling and visibility, with only a small loss of climb rate", "d": ["A higher rate of climb, at the cost of reduced engine cooling and visibility", "A steeper climb angle for terrain clearance, with better forward visibility", "Lower groundspeed and climb rate, but less total fuel burned en route"], "why": "Cruise climb also gives a higher groundspeed and shorter time en route, while costing only a little rate of climb.", "src": "OEG 1D-19 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o2-039", "t": "perf", "q": "Which airplanes does the FAA require to demonstrate continued takeoff capability with one engine inoperative?", "a": "Turboprop, turbojet, large (10+ passenger), and air taxi airplanes", "d": ["All multi-engine airplanes type-certificated after February 1991", "Any piston twin flown under IFR in Part 91 operations", "Only transport-category airplanes operated under Part 121"], "why": "Light piston twins in private use generally have no such requirement; turbine, larger passenger and air taxi airplanes must show they can continue a takeoff on one engine.", "src": "OEG 1D-20 · 14 CFR Part 23"},
{"id": "o2-040", "t": "perf", "q": "A twin type-certificated before Feb 4, 1991 has a 6,500-lb max takeoff weight and a Vso of 70 knots. Required one-engine climb at 5,000 ft MSL?", "a": "About 132 fpm", "d": ["About 50 fpm", "About 100 fpm", "About 189 fpm"], "why": "Twins over 6,000 lb and/or with Vso above 61 knots must climb at least 0.027 Vso² fpm at 5,000 ft MSL: 0.027 x 70² = 132 fpm.", "src": "OEG 1D-21 · 14 CFR Part 23"},
{"id": "o2-041", "t": "perf", "q": "For twins over 6,000 lb or with a Vso above 61 knots, type-certificated on or after Feb 4, 1991, how is the one-engine climb requirement expressed?", "a": "As a 1.5 percent climb gradient", "d": ["As 0.027 Vso² feet per minute at 5,000 feet", "As 50 feet per minute at 5,000 feet", "As 100 feet per minute at sea level"], "why": "The newer rule uses a 1.5 percent gradient. It is not a direct equivalent of the older 0.027 Vso² rate-of-climb formula.", "src": "OEG 1D-21 · 14 CFR Part 23"},
{"id": "o2-042", "t": "perf", "q": "A light twin has a max takeoff weight of 4,500 lb and a Vso of 58 knots. What one-engine climb at 5,000 ft MSL must it demonstrate?", "a": "None; the rate must only be determined, and it may be negative", "d": ["At least 50 fpm at 5,000 feet MSL, with the propeller feathered", "At least 0.027 Vso² fpm, the same as heavier twins", "At least a 1.5 percent gradient, regardless of certification date"], "why": "For light twins under 6,000 lb with Vso under 61 knots, the one-engine climb (or, for newer types, gradient) at 5,000 ft is simply determined. No positive climb is required at any altitude.", "src": "OEG 1D-21 · 14 CFR Part 23"},
{"id": "o2-043", "t": "perf", "q": "For a 2005-model light twin, which date decides which Part 23 one-engine climb standard applies?", "a": "Its type certification date, which may trace back to CAR 3", "d": ["Its model year, as shown on the registration", "The date of manufacture shown on its data plate", "The date its standard airworthiness certificate was issued"], "why": "Don't confuse model year with the type certification basis. Many current twins are certificated under the old Civil Air Regulations (CAR 3).", "src": "OEG 1D-21 · 14 CFR Part 23"},
{"id": "o2-044", "t": "perf", "q": "What is the (all-engine) service ceiling?", "a": "Highest DA where Vy yields 100 fpm: max weight, clean, max continuous power", "d": ["Highest DA where Vy yields 50 fpm with both engines at max weight, clean", "Highest DA where no further climb is possible at any airspeed", "Highest DA where Vy yields 100 fpm with the gear and flaps extended"], "why": "Service ceiling uses a 100 fpm climb in the clean configuration. The altitude where no climb is possible is the absolute ceiling.", "src": "OEG 1D-22 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o2-045", "t": "perf", "q": "Newer POHs present service ceiling as a function of which variables?", "a": "Weight, pressure altitude, and temperature", "d": ["Weight, CG location, and headwind component", "Pressure altitude, humidity, and runway length", "Power setting, CG, and fuel load"], "why": "Weight, pressure altitude and temperature all affect service ceiling. Older POHs often combine the last two and use density altitude.", "src": "OEG 1D-23 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o2-046", "t": "perf", "q": "At the absolute ceiling, which is true?", "a": "Vx equals Vy, and no further climb is possible", "d": ["Vx is about 5 knots faster than Vy at that altitude", "Vy equals Vmc, and climb is limited to 50 fpm", "Vx equals the stall speed, and Vy equals Vyse"], "why": "As altitude increases, Vx rises and Vy falls until they meet at the absolute ceiling, where the airplane can no longer climb.", "src": "OEG 1D-24 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-047", "t": "perf", "q": "What defines the single-engine service ceiling?", "a": "Altitude where the twin can no longer climb faster than 50 fpm with one engine out", "d": ["Altitude where the twin can no longer climb faster than 100 fpm with one engine out", "Altitude where the twin can no longer climb at all with one engine out", "Altitude where the twin can no longer climb faster than 50 fpm on both engines"], "why": "Single-engine service ceiling uses 50 fpm with one engine inoperative. Zero climb on one engine is the single-engine absolute ceiling; 100 fpm is the all-engine service ceiling.", "src": "OEG 1D-25 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-048", "t": "perf", "q": "What does a climb gradient of 1.5 percent mean?", "a": "1.5 feet of altitude gained per 100 feet of horizontal travel", "d": ["1.5 feet of altitude gained per 1,000 feet of horizontal travel", "150 feet of altitude gained per nautical mile", "15 feet of altitude gained per 100 feet of horizontal travel"], "why": "As a percentage, climb gradient is altitude gain per 100 feet of horizontal distance. It can also be given in feet per nautical mile or as a ratio such as 50:1 (2 percent).", "src": "OEG 1D-26 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-049", "t": "perf", "q": "Field density altitude is 6,500 ft. Your single-engine service ceiling at today's weight is 5,000 ft. Is a takeoff advisable?", "a": "No; after an engine failure you likely couldn't climb or even hold altitude", "d": ["Yes, as long as the runway is longer than the accelerate-go distance", "Yes, because the all-engine service ceiling is still well above the field", "Yes, if you plan to hold Vxse rather than Vyse after an engine failure"], "why": "Above the single-engine service ceiling there is little or no one-engine climb. Before taking the runway, know whether you could keep control and climb if an engine quit with the gear still down.", "src": "OEG 1D-27 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-050", "t": "perf", "q": "Under Part 91, may you take off when the field density altitude exceeds the single-engine service ceiling?", "a": "Yes; no Part 91 rule prohibits it, though it is unwise", "d": ["No; Part 91 prohibits it for all multi-engine airplanes", "Only with a waiver from the local Flight Standards office", "Only in day VFR conditions with no passengers aboard"], "why": "It is legal under Part 91, but if an engine fails the airplane probably couldn't climb, and might not even get off the ground.", "src": "OEG 1D-28 · 14 CFR Part 91"},
{"id": "o2-051", "t": "perf", "q": "Departing a high density altitude field, the POH takeoff charts show performance is inadequate for the runway. Which options does the OEG suggest?", "a": "Reduce weight, use a longer runway, or delay until conditions improve", "d": ["Use extra flaps and rotate a few knots below the published VR", "Wait for midday, when the winds are usually stronger down the runway", "Plan to climb out at Vx regardless of its margin above Vmc"], "why": "Compare the density altitude against the published takeoff distance values. If performance falls short, change the weight, the runway, or the time (cooler conditions).", "src": "OEG 1D-29 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-052", "t": "perf", "q": "Per the OEG, an engine fails on takeoff at a field whose density altitude is above the single-engine service ceiling. What one-engine performance can you expect?", "a": "Essentially none; you may not climb or even get airborne", "d": ["At least 50 fpm of climb at Vyse once the propeller is feathered", "About 100 fpm of climb with the gear up and propeller feathered", "Normal OEI climb, since the SE ceiling matters only en route"], "why": "Above the single-engine service ceiling, one-engine climb is under 50 fpm at best and often nil. The OEG says to expect no usable performance: you may not climb or even get off the ground.", "src": "OEG 1D-30 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-053", "t": "perf", "q": "Your IFR route has a 9,000-ft MEA. The SE service ceiling chart shows 6,000 ft at your weight and temperature. What is the concern?", "a": "After an engine failure you could not maintain the MEA", "d": ["None; the MEA applies only with all engines operating", "Only that one-engine cruise TAS will be lower than planned", "Only that you must file at least 1,000 feet above the MEA"], "why": "Use the SE service ceiling chart in planning to see whether, as loaded, you can hold the MEA (IFR) or clear terrain (VFR) after an engine failure.", "src": "OEG 1D-31 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o2-055", "t": "perf", "q": "During a drift-down after an engine failure, when is the rate of descent greatest?", "a": "Immediately after the failure", "d": ["Just before reaching the SE absolute ceiling", "Constant throughout the drift-down", "Halfway down to the ceiling"], "why": "The sink rate is highest right after the failure and tapers off as the airplane nears its single-engine absolute ceiling.", "src": "OEG 1D-32 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-056", "t": "perf", "q": "Why take extra care departing an airport whose elevation is above your drift-down altitude?", "a": "On one engine you couldn't hold an altitude as high as the field", "d": ["Vmc would be higher than the rotation speed at that elevation", "The engines would exceed their maximum continuous power there", "The landing gear may not retract fully in the thinner air"], "why": "If the field is above the single-engine absolute ceiling, the airplane will sink on one engine, so plan how you'd handle a failure during the climb-out.", "src": "OEG 1D-32 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-057", "t": "perf", "q": "Your chart predicts a 200 fpm OEI climb at Vyse. The engines are high-time and it's gusty. What is a realistic expectation?", "a": "Gusts, wear and imperfect technique could erase that climb entirely", "d": ["About 200 fpm, since chart data already includes service margins", "More than 200 fpm, since gusts add lift on average in a climb", "At least 150 fpm, since such losses are limited to 25 percent"], "why": "Turbulence, engine and prop wear, or sloppy airspeed, bank and rudder control can easily negate even 200 fpm. Read the fine print and add margin.", "src": "OEG 1D-33 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o2-058", "t": "perf", "q": "Besides takeoff, climb and landing data, which items does the OEG expect you to determine for your airplane?", "a": "Fuel burn, range and endurance, and the time and point of descent", "d": ["Propeller blade angles and governor settings at each cruise RPM setting", "Oil consumption per hour and the cylinder head temperatures at cruise", "Brake energy limits and tire speed ratings for each runway"], "why": "The list also includes accelerate-stop (failure at takeoff decision speed), accelerate-go (failure at VR or VLOF), SE service ceiling and OEI enroute performance.", "src": "OEG 1D-33 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o2-059", "t": "wb", "q": "Which effects will an overloaded airplane show?", "a": "Higher stall speed and a longer landing roll", "d": ["Lower stall speed and a shorter takeoff run", "Higher cruise speed and longer range", "Better climb angle but reduced climb rate"], "why": "Extra weight raises stall, approach and landing speeds and lengthens the landing roll. It also shortens range, lowers cruise speed and maximum altitude, and reduces maneuverability.", "src": "OEG 1E-1 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o2-060", "t": "wb", "q": "You depart 150 lb over max takeoff weight. What effect on the takeoff and climb should you expect?", "a": "Higher takeoff speed, longer run, and reduced climb rate and angle", "d": ["The same takeoff speed and run, but a reduced rate of climb", "Higher takeoff speed, shorter takeoff run, and reduced climb angle only", "Normal takeoff and climb until above 5,000 ft density altitude"], "why": "Overloading raises takeoff speed, lengthens the run, and reduces both rate and angle of climb, with extra load on the nose wheel as well.", "src": "OEG 1E-1 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o2-061", "t": "wb", "q": "Why does a forward CG raise the stall speed?", "a": "Greater wing loading means the stalling AOA is reached at a higher speed", "d": ["The wing's critical angle of attack becomes smaller with a forward CG", "Less tail-down force reduces the wing's effective loading", "The horizontal tail stalls before the wing reaches its critical angle of attack"], "why": "A forward CG needs more tail-down force, which the wing must also lift, so the effective wing loading rises and the stall comes at a higher speed.", "src": "OEG 1E-2 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-062", "t": "wb", "q": "Compared with an aft CG, a forward CG gives which combination?", "a": "Slower cruise, greater stability, and lower Vmc", "d": ["Faster cruise, less stability, and higher Vmc", "Slower cruise, less stability, and higher Vmc", "Faster cruise, greater stability, and higher Vmc"], "why": "Forward CG adds drag (higher AOA to hold altitude), increases longitudinal stability, and lowers Vmc because the rudder is more effective.", "src": "OEG 1E-2 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-064", "t": "wb", "q": "Loaded near the forward CG limit, what should you expect on takeoff and landing?", "a": "More back pressure: longer takeoff roll, harder flare, longer landing", "d": ["Less back pressure needed: earlier rotation and a shorter landing roll", "A tendency to over-rotate on takeoff and to float during the flare", "No change, since CG location affects only cruise speed and stability"], "why": "A nose-heavy airplane needs more elevator to rotate and flare, so takeoff rolls are longer, approach speeds higher, and landings longer.", "src": "OEG 1E-2 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-065", "t": "wb", "q": "How does an aft CG affect Vmc, and why?", "a": "Vmc rises because the rudder is less effective", "d": ["Vmc falls because the rudder is more effective", "Vmc rises because wing loading increases", "No effect; Vmc depends only on engine power"], "why": "With the CG aft, the rudder's arm is shorter, so it yields less yawing moment and more speed is needed to keep directional control.", "src": "OEG 1E-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-066", "t": "wb", "q": "Loaded near the aft CG limit, which concern is greatest during stall training?", "a": "Reduced stability, making stall and spin recovery more difficult", "d": ["A higher stall speed caused by the increased effective wing loading", "Being unable to raise the nose high enough to reach a stall", "An excessive nose-down pitch tendency during the recovery"], "why": "With an aft CG, raising the AOA tends to increase it further, so the airplane is less stable and harder to recover from a stall or spin.", "src": "OEG 1E-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-067", "t": "wb", "q": "How does an aft CG affect stall speed and cruise speed?", "a": "Lower stall speed and higher cruise speed", "d": ["Higher stall speed and higher cruise speed", "Lower stall speed and lower cruise speed", "Higher stall speed and lower cruise speed"], "why": "Less tail-down force means less wing loading (lower stall speed) and a smaller AOA to hold altitude (less drag, faster cruise).", "src": "OEG 1E-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-068", "t": "wb", "q": "What does standard empty weight (GAMA) include?", "a": "Standard airplane, unusable fuel, full operating fluids, and full oil", "d": ["Standard airplane, optional equipment, unusable fuel, and no oil", "Standard airplane, full usable fuel, full operating fluids, and full oil", "Standard airplane, unusable fuel, fluids, and undrainable oil only"], "why": "Standard empty weight is the standard airplane with unusable fuel, full operating fluids and full oil. Basic empty weight adds the optional equipment installed.", "src": "OEG 1E-4 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-069", "t": "wb", "q": "What is the major difference between basic empty weight and licensed empty weight?", "a": "Basic includes full oil; licensed includes only undrainable oil", "d": ["Basic includes usable fuel; licensed does not", "Licensed includes optional equipment; basic does not", "Licensed includes full oil; basic includes only undrainable oil"], "why": "Licensed empty weight (the older method) includes the standard airplane, optional equipment, full hydraulic fluid, unusable fuel and undrainable oil, but not full oil.", "src": "OEG 1E-4 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-070", "t": "wb", "q": "An older airplane's records use licensed empty weight. What must your loading computation add that a GAMA basic empty weight already includes?", "a": "Engine oil beyond the undrainable portion", "d": ["Unusable fuel in the tanks and lines", "Hydraulic fluid for the landing gear and brakes", "Optional equipment such as avionics"], "why": "Licensed empty weight already counts unusable fuel, full hydraulic fluid and optional equipment, but only undrainable oil, so the usable oil must be added.", "src": "OEG 1E-4 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-071", "t": "wb", "q": "A twin's max ramp weight is 50 lb above its max takeoff weight. What is that allowance for?", "a": "Taxi and runup fuel burn, so takeoff can start at max takeoff weight", "d": ["Baggage or passengers boarded after the engines have been started", "Extra fuel carried as a reserve for holding or diverting at the destination", "Ice or frost that may accumulate on the airframe during taxi"], "why": "Ramp weight lets you start above max takeoff weight, since taxi and runup fuel bring you down to it by the start of the takeoff run, which is where max takeoff weight applies.", "src": "OEG 1E-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-072", "t": "wb", "q": "Shortly after takeoff at max weight, an emergency requires an immediate landing above max landing weight. What should you recognize?", "a": "Land as needed, but the full structural margins are not available", "d": ["You must hold until below max landing weight", "Max landing weight applies only to normal landings, so no added risk", "Landing overweight is prohibited under Part 91 in all cases"], "why": "In an emergency, land; just recognize that landing above max landing weight eats into the airplane's designed structural margins, so touch down gently.", "src": "OEG 1E-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-073", "t": "wb", "q": "Max takeoff weight is 5,500 lb, max landing weight 5,300 lb. You take off at 5,500 lb burning 120 lb of fuel per hour. Minimum time before a normal landing?", "a": "About 1 hour 40 minutes", "d": ["About 1 hour", "About 2 hours 20 minutes", "About 45 minutes"], "why": "You must burn 200 lb to get down to max landing weight: 200 / 120 lb per hour = 1.67 hours, about 1 hour 40 minutes. Plan fuel burn so arrival weight is legal.", "src": "OEG 1E-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-074", "t": "wb", "q": "What is a zero fuel weight limit?", "a": "Max weight of airplane plus payload with no usable fuel aboard", "d": ["Weight of the airplane with all fuel drained, including unusable", "Max weight at which you may land with the fuel tanks nearly empty", "Minimum weight the airplane must have before fuel is loaded"], "why": "It's a computed figure; the airplane is never actually fuel-free when loaded. When a ZFW limit is published, any weight above it must be usable fuel.", "src": "OEG 1E-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-075", "t": "wb", "q": "Why do some airplanes have a zero fuel weight limit?", "a": "To limit loads on the cabin floor, wing spars and fuselage structure", "d": ["To ensure enough fuel is always carried for a 45-minute IFR reserve", "To keep the CG within its forward and aft limits as fuel burns off", "To limit loads on the landing gear and tires at touchdown"], "why": "Payload carried in the fuselage loads the floor and wing spars in a way fuel weight doesn't, so the airplane-plus-payload weight is capped.", "src": "OEG 1E-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-076", "t": "wb", "q": "Zero fuel weight limit is 4,700 lb. With crew, passengers and bags but no usable fuel, you weigh 4,780 lb. Fuel keeps you under max takeoff. Can you go?", "a": "No; offload 80 lb of payload, since fuel can't fix a ZFW excess", "d": ["Yes, because the total weight is still under max takeoff weight", "Yes, as long as you burn at least 80 lb of fuel before landing", "Yes, as long as the loaded CG stays within the envelope"], "why": "Any weight above the zero fuel weight must be usable fuel. Being under max takeoff weight doesn't fix too much payload.", "src": "OEG 1E-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-077", "t": "wb", "q": "How does the OEG define payload?", "a": "The weight of occupants, cargo, and baggage", "d": ["The weight of occupants, cargo, baggage, and usable fuel", "Maximum takeoff weight minus basic empty weight", "The weight of usable fuel and oil only"], "why": "Payload is people and things. Adding usable fuel (or subtracting empty weight from max weight) gives useful load, a larger figure.", "src": "OEG 1E-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-078", "t": "wb", "q": "Basic empty weight is 3,350 lb, full usable fuel weighs 600 lb, and max takeoff weight is 5,000 lb. What payload can you carry with full fuel?", "a": "1,050 lb", "d": ["1,650 lb", "950 lb", "1,250 lb"], "why": "With full fuel, payload = max takeoff weight minus fueled airplane weight: 5,000 - (3,350 + 600) = 1,050 lb. 1,650 lb forgets the fuel.", "src": "OEG 1E-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o2-079", "t": "wb", "q": "Airplane with occupants: 3,600 lb at 88.0 in. Fuel: 480 lb at 95.0 in. Baggage: 120 lb at 140.0 in. What is the loaded CG?", "a": "About 90.3 in", "d": ["About 88.8 in", "About 89.7 in", "About 107.7 in"], "why": "Moments: 316,800 + 45,600 + 16,800 = 379,200 lb-in; divided by 4,200 lb = 90.3 in. 88.8 in leaves out the bags, 89.7 in leaves out the fuel, and 107.7 in just averages the arms.", "src": "OEG 1E-7 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o2-080", "t": "wb", "q": "Takeoff: 4,200 lb, moment 379,200 lb-in (CG 90.3 in). After 2 hours burning 120 lb/hr of fuel at arm 95.0 in, what are the weight and CG?", "a": "3,960 lb at about 90.0 in", "d": ["3,960 lb at about 90.6 in", "3,960 lb at about 90.3 in", "4,080 lb at about 90.1 in"], "why": "Burn 240 lb x 95.0 = 22,800 lb-in. New moment 356,400 / 3,960 lb = 90.0 in. Fuel aft of the CG was burned, so the CG moves forward.", "src": "OEG 1E-7 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o2-081", "t": "wb", "q": "Fuel is burned first from tanks located forward of the airplane's CG. What happens to the CG?", "a": "It moves aft", "d": ["It moves forward", "It doesn't change", "It shifts laterally"], "why": "Removing weight ahead of the CG shifts the CG aft; burning from tanks aft of the CG shifts it forward. In most GA twins the tanks sit near the CG, so the shift is small.", "src": "OEG 1E-8 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o2-082", "t": "wb", "q": "The right engine has been shut down for an hour, and you have fed the left engine from the left tank without crossfeed. What develops?", "a": "A lateral imbalance, right wing heavy, causing a roll tendency", "d": ["A lateral imbalance, left wing heavy, causing a roll tendency", "No imbalance, as long as the right propeller is feathered", "No imbalance, since fuel burn doesn't affect lateral CG"], "why": "The right tank stays full while the left empties, shifting the CG laterally toward the right wing. Crossfeeding over time prevents this.", "src": "OEG 1E-8 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o2-083", "t": "wb", "q": "Can a typical light twin be flown with every seat filled, full baggage, and full fuel?", "a": "Usually not; you often must trade fuel against payload", "d": ["Yes; certification requires full seats and bags with full fuel", "Yes, as long as the CG remains within the forward and aft limits", "Only if the airplane has no published zero fuel weight limit"], "why": "Most airplanes can't fill everything and stay within gross, takeoff and zero fuel weight limits. Check CG limits for each loading too.", "src": "OEG 1E-9 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o2-084", "t": "wb", "q": "Your passengers and bags put you at max takeoff weight with only partial fuel, not enough to go nonstop. What does the OEG suggest?", "a": "Plan a fuel stop with shorter legs, or carry fewer people or bags", "d": ["Take full fuel anyway, since burn-off will fix it before landing", "Shift baggage aft so the CG limits allow the extra fuel", "Take off slightly over gross weight and climb at Vx to compensate"], "why": "As payload grows you give up fuel and fly shorter legs, or add fuel and cut passengers or baggage. Moving the CG doesn't change a weight limit.", "src": "OEG 1E-9 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o2-085", "t": "perf", "q": "Why does the OEG list the time of departure and the weather among takeoff risk factors in a twin?", "a": "Night or low ceilings leave fewer options if an engine fails", "d": ["Engine power is lower at night, which lengthens the takeoff roll", "Part 91 requires a takeoff alternate for night departures in twins", "Accelerate-go data are valid only for daytime VFR departures"], "why": "If an engine quits after takeoff, darkness or a low ceiling limits where you can see to land or maneuver, so the plan must account for it. Cool night air actually helps engine power.", "src": "OEG 1D-13 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-001", "t": "controls", "q": "Which surfaces make up the primary flight controls of a typical multi-engine airplane?", "a": "Ailerons, elevator (or stabilator), and rudder", "d": ["Ailerons, wing flaps, and rudder", "Elevator (or stabilator), rudder, and trim tabs", "Ailerons, spoilers, and elevator (or stabilator)"], "why": "Primary controls are the ones required to control the airplane safely in flight: ailerons, elevator/stabilator and rudder. Flaps, spoilers and trim are secondary controls.", "src": "OEG 2A-1 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-002", "t": "controls", "q": "Which pairing of flight control and axis is correct?", "a": "Elevator: pitch about the lateral axis", "d": ["Elevator: pitch about the longitudinal axis", "Rudder: yaw about the lateral axis", "Ailerons: roll about the vertical axis"], "why": "Ailerons roll the airplane about the longitudinal axis, the elevator pitches it about the lateral axis, and the rudder yaws it about the vertical axis.", "src": "OEG 2A-2 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-003", "t": "controls", "q": "What is a stabilator?", "a": "A one-piece horizontal tail that pivots as a unit", "d": ["A fixed horizontal stabilizer with a hinged elevator", "A small tab on the elevator that relieves pressure", "A V-tail surface combining rudder and elevator"], "why": "A stabilator replaces the stabilizer-plus-elevator with a single all-moving slab. Because it pivots as a whole it is very sensitive to inputs and air loads.", "src": "OEG 2A-3 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-004", "t": "controls", "q": "Why is an anti-servo tab fitted to the trailing edge of a stabilator?", "a": "It makes the all-moving surface less sensitive", "d": ["It boosts surface movement so the pilot needs less force", "It replaces the balance weight ahead of the spar", "It locks the stabilator at high angles of attack"], "why": "An all-moving stabilator reacts strongly to small inputs and air loads, so the anti-servo tab adds resistance (feel) that makes it less sensitive. A balance weight ahead of the main spar is usually fitted as well.", "src": "OEG 2A-3 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-005", "t": "controls", "q": "In flight the elevator jams and the yoke will no longer move it. What can still give you a limited amount of pitch control?", "a": "Changes in power and flap setting, aided by trim", "d": ["Coordinated rudder and aileron inputs, aided by trim", "Differential power on the two engines to raise or lower the nose", "Differential braking and nosewheel steering after touchdown"], "why": "With a jammed elevator, changing power and flap extension changes the pitching moments enough for limited pitch control, and trim may also help. The book warns that landing this way is still difficult.", "src": "OEG 2A-4 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-006", "t": "controls", "q": "Which list contains only secondary flight controls?", "a": "Wing flaps, leading edge devices, spoilers, and trim systems", "d": ["Ailerons, wing flaps, leading edge devices, and trim systems", "Rudder, wing flaps, leading edge devices, and trim systems", "Wing flaps, cowl flaps, leading edge devices, and spoilers"], "why": "Secondary controls improve performance or relieve the pilot of heavy control forces: flaps, leading edge devices, spoilers and trim. Ailerons and rudder are primary controls; cowl flaps are engine cooling devices.", "src": "OEG 2B-1 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-007", "t": "controls", "q": "At any given angle of attack, what does extending trailing-edge flaps do?", "a": "Increases both lift and induced drag", "d": ["Increases lift and reduces induced drag", "Increases drag but leaves lift unchanged", "Reduces lift and increases parasite drag only"], "why": "Flaps raise the lift and induced drag produced at a given angle of attack. That lets the airplane cruise fast with flaps retracted yet land slowly with them extended.", "src": "OEG 2B-2 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-008", "t": "controls", "q": "What are the four most common types of trailing-edge wing flaps?", "a": "Plain, split, slotted, and Fowler", "d": ["Plain, split, slotted, and Krueger", "Split, slotted, Fowler, and slat", "Plain, split, Fowler, and spoiler"], "why": "The four common trailing-edge flaps are plain, split, slotted and Fowler. Krueger flaps and slats are leading edge devices; spoilers reduce lift.", "src": "OEG 2B-2 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-009", "t": "controls", "q": "Which description fits a plain flap?", "a": "Adds camber, raising lift and drag and pitching the nose down", "d": ["Increases wing area by sliding aft, adding lift with little drag", "Ducts high-energy air over its upper surface to delay separation", "Deflects only from the wing's lower surface, leaving a turbulent wake"], "why": "A plain flap simply hinges down, adding camber; lift and drag both rise and the center of pressure moves aft, pitching the nose down. The other choices describe Fowler, slotted and split flaps.", "src": "OEG 2B-3 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-010", "t": "controls", "q": "How does a slotted flap get a higher lift coefficient than a plain or split flap?", "a": "A slot feeds lower-surface air over the flap, delaying separation", "d": ["It slides aft on tracks so that the total wing area is increased", "It deflects from the lower surface, increasing camber only", "It extends from the leading edge to raise the critical AOA"], "why": "When lowered, a slotted flap opens a duct between the wing and the flap; lower-surface air flows through it and energizes the upper-surface boundary layer, delaying separation. It is the most popular flap today, and the Seneca uses slotted flaps.", "src": "OEG 2B-3 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-011", "t": "controls", "q": "What distinguishes a Fowler flap from the other common flap types?", "a": "It increases wing area as well as camber by moving aft on tracks", "d": ["It increases camber only, by hinging down from the trailing edge", "It is deflected from the lower surface of the wing only", "It is mounted on the leading edge to delay airflow separation"], "why": "A Fowler flap is a slotted flap that first slides backward on tracks, enlarging the wing area, and then deflects downward, adding camber.", "src": "OEG 2B-3 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-012", "t": "controls", "q": "Generally, how are the flaps driven in the most common type of flap system? (The Seneca I's flaps are manual.)", "a": "One electric motor drives both flaps via rods, bellcranks and cables", "d": ["A separate electric motor in each wing, synchronized electronically", "An engine-driven hydraulic pump that powers an actuator in each wing", "A cockpit lever linked by cables, with springs to help extend them"], "why": "Flaps can be electric, hydraulic or manual, but the most common system is a flap switch and a single electric motor linked to both flaps, protected by a push-to-reset breaker or a fuse. The Seneca I is different: its slotted flaps are moved by a manual lever between the front seats and spring-loaded to retract.", "src": "OEG 2B-4 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o3-013", "t": "controls", "q": "On approach you lower the flaps. The left flap extends but the right flap stays up. Which way will the airplane tend to roll?", "a": "Right, toward the wing with less flap", "d": ["Left, toward the wing with the extended flap", "It will pitch nose-down without any tendency to roll", "Neither way; it will only yaw to the right"], "why": "The extended flap makes more lift, so the airplane rolls toward the wing with less flap. Its extra drag yaws the nose toward the extended flap, so you end up cross-controlled with heavy opposite rudder and nearly full aileron, worst at slow approach speeds.", "src": "OEG 2B-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-014", "t": "controls", "q": "With a conventional elevator trim tab, where does the tab sit when the trim is set to full nose-down?", "a": "Full up", "d": ["Full down", "Neutral, streamlined with the elevator", "Up or down, depending on airspeed"], "why": "A trim tab moves opposite to the way it pushes the surface. Full nose-down trim puts the tab full up, and the air load on it pushes the elevator trailing edge down.", "src": "OEG 2B-6 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-015", "t": "controls", "q": "How does a balance tab move when its control surface is deflected?", "a": "Automatically, in the opposite direction to the surface", "d": ["Automatically, in the same direction as the surface", "Only when the pilot turns the cockpit trim wheel or switch", "It stays fixed and can only be bent on the ground"], "why": "A balance tab is linked to the control surface rod so it moves opposite to the surface, helping the pilot move it. An anti-servo tab moves the same way as the surface, which adds resistance instead.", "src": "OEG 2B-6 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-016", "t": "controls", "q": "When the stabilator trailing edge moves up, what does its anti-servo tab do?", "a": "It also moves up, making the stabilator less sensitive", "d": ["Its trailing edge moves down, opposite the stabilator, to aid the pilot", "It stays in its trimmed position unless the trim wheel is moved", "Its trailing edge moves up only when the autopilot is engaged"], "why": "The anti-servo linkage forces the tab to move in the same direction as the stabilator trailing edge, which resists the movement and makes the surface less sensitive. The tab also works as the pitch trim device.", "src": "OEG 2B-6 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-017", "t": "controls", "q": "What is a ground-adjustable tab?", "a": "A fixed tab, usually on the rudder, bent on the ground for trim", "d": ["A cockpit-adjustable tab that is used only to set takeoff trim", "A tab that moves opposite the rudder whenever it deflects", "A tab that is locked in place on the ground by a gust lock"], "why": "A ground-adjustable tab cannot be moved in flight. It is bent one way or the other on the ground, and the right amount is found by trial and error.", "src": "OEG 2B-6 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-018", "t": "controls", "q": "The autopilot suddenly starts flying erratically. What is the first and closest way to disconnect it?", "a": "The disconnect switch on the control yoke", "d": ["The autopilot circuit breaker on the panel", "The mode buttons on the autopilot panel", "The avionics or battery master switch"], "why": "The yoke-mounted disconnect switch is the quickest first step. If it fails, try the mode buttons, then pull the trim/autopilot circuit breakers, and you can overpower the servo clutch with the yoke.", "src": "OEG 2B-7 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o3-019", "t": "controls", "q": "How can a pilot physically overpower a malfunctioning autopilot or trim servo, and when is this normally checked?", "a": "Force the yoke against the servo clutch; check it before takeoff", "d": ["Trim the opposite way to cancel it; check it during shutdown", "Pull the yoke full aft to disengage it; check it after each landing", "Hold the yoke still until it stops; check it during cruise"], "why": "Most autopilot and trim servos drive through a simple clutch that the pilot can overpower by forcing the yoke. Check this before takeoff, for example during the after-start or run-up checks.", "src": "OEG 2B-7 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o3-020", "t": "controls", "q": "What does a typical stall warning system sense to trigger its warning?", "a": "Angle of attack, through a sensor or vane on the wing", "d": ["Indicated airspeed sensed through the pitot-static system", "Load factor from an accelerometer in the panel", "Pitch attitude from the attitude indicator"], "why": "Stall warning is based on angle of attack: a vane or sensor on the wing measures the angle between the chord line and the relative wind and triggers a horn and/or light as the critical angle is approached.", "src": "OEG 2B-8 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o3-021", "t": "controls", "q": "When is the stall warning designed to activate?", "a": "Before the stall, leaving a margin to recover", "d": ["At the exact critical angle of attack, as the wing stalls", "Just after the wing stalls, when buffet begins", "Only when indicated airspeed falls below Vs1"], "why": "The warning is set to come on before the critical angle of attack is reached, so the pilot has time to take corrective action.", "src": "OEG 2B-8 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o3-022", "t": "engine", "q": "Where does the term 'reciprocating engine' come from?", "a": "The back-and-forth motion of the pistons", "d": ["The rotary motion of the crankshaft", "The alternating firing of the two magnetos", "The exchange of intake and exhaust gases"], "why": "The pistons move back and forth (reciprocate), and that motion produces the mechanical energy that does the work.", "src": "OEG 2C-1 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-023", "t": "engine", "q": "What is the key difference between a carburetor and a fuel-injection induction system?", "a": "Carburetor mixes before the manifold; injection mixes at each cylinder", "d": ["Carburetor mixes at each cylinder; injection mixes before the manifold", "Carburetor needs a fuel pump; fuel injection is fed by gravity", "Carburetor meters by engine RPM; injection meters by manifold pressure"], "why": "A carburetor mixes fuel and air before the mixture enters the intake manifold. Fuel injection mixes just before each cylinder intake or injects directly into each cylinder.", "src": "OEG 2C-2 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-024", "t": "engine", "q": "In a carbureted airplane with a constant-speed propeller, what will be the first indication of carburetor ice?", "a": "A drop in manifold pressure", "d": ["A gradual drop in RPM", "A rise in cylinder head temperature", "A rise in oil pressure"], "why": "The governor holds RPM constant, so RPM won't drop at first. As ice restricts the induction airflow, manifold pressure falls.", "src": "OEG 2C-2 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-025", "t": "engine", "q": "What does 'horizontally opposed' mean when describing an engine?", "a": "Cylinders face each other across the crankcase, always an even number", "d": ["Cylinders are arranged radially, in a star shape around the crankshaft", "Two banks of cylinders form a V shape above the crankshaft", "Cylinders sit in a single inline row laid flat in the nacelle"], "why": "Each cylinder on one side faces a cylinder on the other side, so there is always an even number. Most are air cooled and mounted horizontally in airplanes.", "src": "OEG 2C-3 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-026", "t": "engine", "q": "What does 'direct-drive' mean for an aircraft engine?", "a": "The prop is bolted to the crankshaft and turns at the same RPM", "d": ["A reduction gearbox turns the propeller slower than the crankshaft", "A clutch lets the propeller stop while the engine keeps running", "The propeller is driven by a turbine spun by exhaust gases"], "why": "In a direct-drive engine there is no reduction gearing; the propeller turns at crankshaft RPM.", "src": "OEG 2C-4 · FAA-P-8740-13"},
{"id": "o3-027", "t": "engine", "q": "What does 'normally aspirated' mean, and how does it affect performance?", "a": "No supercharger or turbocharger, so maximum power falls off with altitude", "d": ["Turbocharged to sea level pressure, so power holds to a critical altitude", "Fuel-injected, so available power is unaffected by altitude", "Fitted with alternate air, so it resists induction icing"], "why": "A normally aspirated engine has nothing to restore sea level pressure in the induction system, so its maximum available power decreases as altitude increases.", "src": "OEG 2C-4 · FAA-P-8740-13"},
{"id": "o3-028", "t": "engine", "q": "How is ignition provided in a typical piston twin's engines?", "a": "Two engine-driven magnetos and two plugs per cylinder", "d": ["One magneto plus one battery-powered coil for each engine", "Two magnetos that are powered from the aircraft's electrical bus", "One magneto firing both of the spark plugs in each cylinder"], "why": "Each engine has two engine-driven magnetos and two plugs per cylinder. The magnetos generate their own current, so the ignition is independent of the airplane's electrical system.", "src": "OEG 2C-5 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o3-029", "t": "engine", "q": "Both alternators fail and the battery is eventually depleted. What happens to engine ignition?", "a": "Nothing; the magnetos need no aircraft electrical power", "d": ["The engines quit once the battery is completely depleted", "One magneto keeps firing while the other one stops working", "Spark weakens as bus voltage drops, so both engines start to run rough"], "why": "Magnetos are engine-driven and make their own electrical current, so the ignition system is completely independent of the airplane's electrical system.", "src": "OEG 2C-5 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o3-030", "t": "engine", "q": "Brake horsepower is the power...", "a": "Delivered at the engine's propeller shaft", "d": ["Developed inside the cylinders by combustion", "Converted into thrust by the propeller", "Lost to friction inside the engine"], "why": "Brake horsepower is the usable power at the propeller (main drive) shaft. Power developed in the cylinders is indicated horsepower, and the propeller's useful output is thrust horsepower.", "src": "OEG 2C-6 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-031", "t": "engine", "q": "During the run-up magneto check, why does RPM drop when one magneto is switched off?", "a": "One plug per cylinder fires, so combustion is less complete", "d": ["The other magneto carries twice the load and runs weaker", "Fuel injection is cut to the cylinders that magneto serves", "The alternator load shifts onto the remaining magneto"], "why": "With one magneto off, its plugs stop firing and each cylinder burns its charge less completely from one plug. Power falls, so RPM drops.", "src": "OEG 2C-7 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o3-032", "t": "engine", "q": "In a normally aspirated twin, RPM drops during the mag check (or prop exercise) and manifold pressure rises slightly. What does this mean?", "a": "Normal: the slower engine draws less air past the throttle", "d": ["Induction ice is beginning to form, so alternate air is needed", "There's an intake leak downstream of the throttle butterfly valve", "A magneto is failing and backfiring into the induction"], "why": "At a lower RPM the engine pumps less air, so there is less suction in the manifold behind the fixed throttle and manifold pressure creeps up. This is a normal response. Induction ice would lower manifold pressure.", "src": "OEG 2C-8 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-033", "t": "engine", "q": "What is manifold pressure?", "a": "Absolute pressure in the intake manifold, in inches of mercury", "d": ["Pressure difference between the manifold and the cabin, in PSI", "Pressure of oil delivered to the prop governor, in inches of mercury", "Absolute pressure of exhaust gas in the exhaust manifold, in PSI"], "why": "Manifold pressure is an absolute pressure, measured in the intake manifold and normally shown in inches of mercury.", "src": "OEG 2C-9 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-034", "t": "engine", "q": "A normally aspirated twin shows about 29 inHg at full throttle near sea level. Climbing at full throttle to 6,000 ft, about what manifold pressure should you expect?", "a": "About 23 inHg", "d": ["About 29 inHg", "About 26 inHg", "About 17 inHg"], "why": "Air density falls with altitude, so a non-turbocharged engine loses about 1 inch of manifold pressure per 1,000 ft: 29 - 6 = about 23 inHg.", "src": "OEG 2C-10 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-035", "t": "engine", "q": "Before start the manifold pressure gauge reads 29.9 inHg. What should it do once the engine is running at a steady idle?", "a": "Drop well below ambient, to around 12 inHg", "d": ["Rise above ambient as the propeller pushes air in", "Stay at ambient until the throttle is advanced", "Drop to zero, then recover as RPM builds"], "why": "Stopped, the gauge shows ambient pressure. Running, the engine sucks air past the nearly closed throttle, so manifold pressure sits well below ambient, about 12 inHg at idle. (The OEG's 2C-12 discussion of the start transient is muddled; at a steady idle the 2C-11 values are the ones to know.)", "src": "OEG 2C-11 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-036", "t": "engine", "q": "In cruise one engine fails. What will that engine's manifold pressure gauge show?", "a": "A rise toward ambient pressure at the current altitude", "d": ["A drop to zero, since the engine is no longer running", "A drop to about the idle value, roughly 12 inHg or less", "No change, because the throttle hasn't moved"], "why": "Once the engine stops pumping air, nothing creates suction in the manifold, so the gauge rises to the ambient pressure at that altitude. Manifold pressure alone is therefore a poor way to spot the failed engine.", "src": "OEG 2C-11 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-037", "t": "engine", "q": "In a twin with constant-speed propellers, which controls set manifold pressure and RPM for cruise?", "a": "Throttle sets manifold pressure; propeller control sets RPM", "d": ["Propeller control sets manifold pressure; throttle sets RPM", "Throttle sets both; the propeller control is only for feathering", "Mixture sets manifold pressure; throttle sets RPM"], "why": "Manifold pressure is set with the throttle and RPM separately with the propeller control. Pick the combination for the power you want from the POH power setting table.", "src": "OEG 2C-13 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-038", "t": "engine", "q": "Cylinder head temperatures are running high in a climb. Which actions help bring them down?", "a": "Increase airspeed, enrich, open cowl flaps, or reduce power", "d": ["Reduce airspeed, lean the mixture, or close the cowl flaps", "Increase RPM, lean the mixture, or close the cowl flaps", "Reduce airspeed, enrich the mixture, or close the cowl flaps"], "why": "More cooling airflow (higher airspeed, open cowl flaps), a richer mixture, and less power all lower engine temperature.", "src": "OEG 2C-14 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-039", "t": "engine", "q": "What do cowl flaps do when they are opened?", "a": "Let more hot air escape, increasing cooling airflow", "d": ["Admit warm air into the induction system to prevent icing", "Reduce cooling drag, which improves single-engine climb rate", "Direct extra cooling air over the oil cooler only"], "why": "Cowl flaps are hinged covers over an opening through which hot air leaves the cowling. Open them to cool a hot engine; close them to restrict airflow and warm a cold one.", "src": "OEG 2C-15 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-040", "t": "engine", "q": "What does an EGT probe measure, and what is it used for?", "a": "Exhaust gas temperature at the manifold, used to set the mixture", "d": ["Cylinder head temperature at the hottest cylinder, used to set cowl flaps", "Exhaust pressure at the turbine inlet, used to set turbo boost", "Oil temperature leaving the oil cooler, used to set cruise power"], "why": "EGT changes with the fuel/air ratio entering the cylinders, so the pilot can use it as a guide for leaning.", "src": "OEG 2C-16 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-041", "t": "engine", "q": "Using an EGT gauge for the common 'rich of peak' leaning method, what is the technique?", "a": "Lean slowly to peak EGT, then enrich slightly for cooling", "d": ["Lean slowly to peak EGT, then keep leaning past the peak", "Lean until the engine runs rough, then lean a little further", "Go full rich, then lean until EGT starts to fall"], "why": "Lean gradually until EGT peaks, then enrich a little to keep the engine cool and avoid damage. Leaning past peak ('lean of peak') should be done only if the engine manufacturer allows it and exactly as it recommends.", "src": "OEG 2C-17 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o3-042", "t": "engine", "q": "What is the purpose of a turbocharger?", "a": "Compress induction air for sea-level (or more) power at altitude", "d": ["Cool the induction air to make detonation less likely", "Recover exhaust heat to warm the cabin and defroster air", "Increase propeller efficiency in the thin air at high altitude"], "why": "A turbocharger compresses induction air so the engine can develop sea-level or greater horsepower at higher altitudes. Cooling the compressed air is the intercooler's job.", "src": "OEG 2C-18 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-043", "t": "engine", "q": "What drives a turbocharger's compressor impeller?", "a": "An exhaust-driven turbine wheel on the same shaft", "d": ["A gear train driven directly off the engine crankshaft", "A belt driven from a pulley on the accessory case", "An electric motor powered by the engine's alternator"], "why": "Exhaust gas spins a turbine on one end of a shaft, and the impeller on the other end compresses induction air. A gear- or belt-driven compressor would be a supercharger.", "src": "OEG 2C-18 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-044", "t": "engine", "q": "The waste gate in a turbocharged engine is fully closed. Where does the exhaust go, and what happens to boost?", "a": "Most of it is forced through the turbine, giving maximum boost", "d": ["It bypasses the turbine and exits the tailpipe, giving minimum boost", "It is split evenly between turbine and tailpipe, holding boost constant", "It is routed through the intercooler, cooling the induction charge"], "why": "The waste gate is a butterfly valve in the exhaust that controls how much exhaust reaches the turbine. Closed, most exhaust drives the turbine; open, exhaust bypasses it out the exhaust pipe.", "src": "OEG 2C-19 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-045", "t": "engine", "q": "Taking off in a turbocharged twin, manifold pressure rises excessively as you advance the throttle. Without specific AFM guidance, what is likely and what do you do?", "a": "Overboost (possibly waste gate); smoothly retard the throttle", "d": ["An exhaust leak; shut the engine down immediately", "Normal turbo lag; continue the takeoff and let the MP settle down", "Intercooler failure; enrich the mixture and continue"], "why": "An excessive manifold pressure rise points to an overboost, often from a waste gate fault. Smoothly retard the throttle to keep manifold pressure below the limit for the RPM and mixture, and avoid further overboost. Always follow the AFM first.", "src": "OEG 2C-20 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-046", "t": "engine", "q": "In flight a turbocharged engine shows unexpectedly low manifold pressure. Why take this seriously?", "a": "It may be a serious exhaust leak; shut down unless it's needed", "d": ["It means the waste gate is stuck closed; reduce RPM", "It is almost always a gauge fault; continue the flight normally", "It means intercooler icing; select alternate air"], "why": "Low manifold pressure may come from a minor fault, but it can also mean a dangerous exhaust leak. Shut the engine down per the engine failure procedure unless a greater emergency needs it; if you must keep it running, use the lowest power needed and land as soon as practicable.", "src": "OEG 2C-20 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-047", "t": "engine", "q": "What does an intercooler do in a turbocharged engine?", "a": "Cools the hot compressed air, reducing detonation risk", "d": ["Cools the exhaust gas before it reaches the turbine wheel", "Cools the engine oil returning from the turbocharger bearings", "Heats the compressed induction air to prevent induction system icing"], "why": "Compressing air heats it. An intercooler is a small heat exchanger that uses outside air to cool the compressed air before it reaches the fuel metering device, reducing the chance of detonation.", "src": "OEG 2C-21 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-048", "t": "engine", "q": "What is a constant-speed propeller?", "a": "A governor varies its blade angle to hold the selected RPM", "d": ["Its blade angle is fixed, so RPM varies with throttle and airspeed", "Its blade angle is reset by the pilot by hand for each airspeed", "It holds whatever blade angle the pilot last selected"], "why": "A governor automatically changes blade pitch to keep RPM constant despite changing air loads. The pilot selects RPM with the propeller control (or a FADEC does it automatically).", "src": "OEG 2C-22 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-049", "t": "engine", "q": "With power and RPM set, you pitch up from level flight into a climb. What does the propeller governor do?", "a": "Decreases blade angle to keep RPM from falling", "d": ["Increases blade angle to keep RPM from falling", "Decreases blade angle to keep RPM from rising", "Leaves blade angle alone and lets RPM fall"], "why": "In the climb the engine tends to slow, so the governor flattens (decreases) the blade angle just enough to hold the selected RPM.", "src": "OEG 2C-23 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-050", "t": "engine", "q": "You lower the nose into a descent without touching the power or propeller controls. What does the governor do?", "a": "Increases blade angle to prevent an overspeed", "d": ["Decreases blade angle to prevent an overspeed", "Moves the blades to the low-pitch stop", "Leaves blade angle alone and lets RPM rise"], "why": "As airspeed builds the prop tends to speed up, so the governor coarsens (increases) the blade angle to hold the set RPM and keep the engine from overspeeding.", "src": "OEG 2C-23 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-051", "t": "engine", "q": "In most single-engine airplanes, where does a constant-speed propeller go if it loses oil pressure in the hub?", "a": "To low pitch, high RPM", "d": ["To high pitch, low RPM", "To the feathered position", "It holds its last blade angle"], "why": "In most singles, losing hub oil pressure lets the blades go to flat pitch/high RPM. Twin props are built the other way: they head toward high pitch and feather, which cuts drag from a dead engine and helps avoid a Vmc problem.", "src": "OEG 2C-24 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-052", "t": "engine", "q": "Why do you 'exercise' the propellers before takeoff?", "a": "To check them and get warm oil into the governor and hub", "d": ["To adjust the governor's high-RPM stop for takeoff power", "To recharge the nitrogen in the propeller dome before each flight", "To check each magneto while the prop is under load"], "why": "Cycling the prop control checks that the system works, shows any leaks, swaps fresh warm oil into the governor and hub, and confirms the prop returns to high RPM when the control is pushed forward.", "src": "OEG 2C-25 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-053", "t": "engine", "q": "On a cold morning you skip the propeller exercise during run-up. What problem could show up on takeoff?", "a": "An overspeed, from congealed oil in the prop cylinder", "d": ["An excessive magneto drop, from fouled plugs on the roll", "An overboost, from a waste gate stuck in the closed position", "Rough running, from fuel vapor forming in the hot injector lines"], "why": "Oil left in the prop cylinder since the last shutdown leaks away and congeals, especially when it's cold. Without exercising the prop, the engine may overspeed on takeoff.", "src": "OEG 2C-26 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-054", "t": "engine", "q": "What is a propeller overspeed, and what usually causes it?", "a": "RPM above the set value; usually a failed governor driving low pitch", "d": ["RPM above redline; usually from leaning the mixture too far", "RPM below the set value; usually from congealed oil in the hub", "RPM above the set value; usually from a waste gate stuck closed"], "why": "An overspeed is propeller speed higher than the propeller control setting. A malfunctioning governor that puts the blades into full low pitch/high RPM is the usual cause.", "src": "OEG 2C-27 · AFM"},
{"id": "o3-055", "t": "engine", "q": "A propeller overspeeds in flight. What is the general response (always subject to your AFM)?", "a": "Reduce power, prop to full decrease RPM, slow down if needed", "d": ["Push the prop control to full increase RPM, then add more power", "Feather immediately, then restart once the RPM has settled down", "Lower the nose to build airspeed, then reduce power on it"], "why": "Reduce power on that engine and move its propeller control fully to decrease RPM. If needed, raise the pitch attitude to lower the airspeed, which lowers the windmilling RPM.", "src": "OEG 2C-28 · AFM"},
{"id": "o3-056", "t": "engine", "q": "What does feathering a propeller accomplish?", "a": "It stops rotation and streamlines the blades, minimizing drag", "d": ["The blades go flat so the engine windmills for an air restart", "The blades reverse pitch to give braking thrust on landing", "The engine keeps idling while the blades streamline in the wind"], "why": "A feathered prop stops turning and its blades line up with the relative wind, which minimizes drag from the dead engine.", "src": "OEG 2C-29 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-057", "t": "engine", "q": "In the typical full-feathering prop on a light twin, what does increasing governor oil pressure in the hub do?", "a": "Drives the blades toward low pitch, high RPM", "d": ["Drives the blades toward high pitch, low RPM", "Drives the blades toward the feathered position", "Locks the blades at their current angle"], "why": "Most twins use counterweighted props in which oil pressure decreases pitch. Governor oil pressure pushes the blades toward low pitch/high RPM, away from feather.", "src": "OEG 2C-30 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-058", "t": "engine", "q": "An engine in a typical light twin loses all oil pressure in flight. What happens to its propeller, and why?", "a": "It heads toward feather, since only oil pressure kept it out", "d": ["It goes to low pitch/high RPM, since oil is what drives it to high pitch", "It stays at its last blade angle, since the governor locks it there", "It goes to low pitch, since the counterweights need oil to work"], "why": "In an oil-pressure-to-decrease-pitch design, a steady supply of high-pressure oil is all that keeps the blades out of feather. Lose oil pressure or the governor and the prop heads toward feather.", "src": "OEG 2C-30 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-059", "t": "engine", "q": "On a typical windmilling counterweighted twin propeller, which way do the main forces act on the blades?", "a": "Air loads push toward low pitch; counterweights push toward high pitch", "d": ["Air loads push toward high pitch; counterweights push toward low pitch", "Both push toward low pitch; only oil can move the blades to feather", "Both push toward high pitch; only oil holds the blades out of feather"], "why": "Aerodynamic forces on a windmilling prop drive the blades to low pitch. Centrifugal force on counterweights at the blade shanks drives them toward high pitch and is usually slightly stronger. (In the Seneca, POH 2-4 credits the dome's nitrogen charge with driving the prop toward feather; POH 9-6 gives that charge for counterweight-type props.)", "src": "OEG 2C-31 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-060", "t": "engine", "q": "Why does a typical feathering prop need a spring or stored air charge as well as counterweights?", "a": "The counterweights' centrifugal force fades as RPM decays", "d": ["Counterweights only work while oil pressure is high", "Counterweights move the blades toward low pitch, not feather", "The spring is what blocks feathering below about 800 RPM"], "why": "As the engine slows, centrifugal force on the counterweights weakens, so a spring or high-pressure air in the prop dome finishes driving the blades fully into feather.", "src": "OEG 2C-32 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-061", "t": "engine", "q": "In a typical twin, what is the sequence when you pull the propeller control fully aft to feather?", "a": "Oil dumps, counterweights start, a spring or air charge finishes", "d": ["Governor oil pressure drives the blades to feather, then a pin locks them", "The starter stops the engine, then oil pressure drives the blades to feather", "A spring starts the blades moving, then governor oil finishes the job"], "why": "With the control fully aft all oil pressure is dumped, the counterweights begin moving the blades toward feather, and a spring or dome air charge completes it. The whole process can take up to about 10 seconds.", "src": "OEG 2C-32 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-062", "t": "engine", "q": "Without an unfeathering accumulator, how is a feathered propeller brought out of feather in flight?", "a": "Crank it with the starter so oil pressure moves the blades", "d": ["Push the prop control forward and let the airflow windmill it", "Dive to build airspeed until air loads unfeather the blades", "Pump the throttle several times to build governor oil pressure"], "why": "A feathered engine isn't turning, so it makes no oil pressure. The starter turns it over, oil pressure builds and moves the blades out of feather, and the engine windmills, starts and runs.", "src": "OEG 2C-33 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-063", "t": "engine", "q": "In the general light-twin unfeathering procedure (not the Seneca I POH's), how are the controls set before engaging the starter?", "a": "Ignition on, throttle low idle, mixture rich, prop control high RPM", "d": ["Ignition off, throttle full, mixture cutoff, prop control in feather", "Ignition on, throttle full, mixture lean, prop control low RPM", "Ignition off, throttle idle, mixture rich, prop control in feather"], "why": "In the general procedure, ignition goes on before the engine is rotated, with the throttle at low idle, mixture rich and prop control forward, then the starter is engaged. Your school's Seneca air-start checklist differs: throttle open 1/4 inch and prop control full forward before engaging the starter.", "src": "OEG 2C-33 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-064", "t": "engine", "q": "On twins that have one, what is an unfeathering accumulator?", "a": "An optional reservoir of oil under gas pressure, used to unfeather", "d": ["The nitrogen charge in the prop dome that drives the blades to feather", "A hydraulic accumulator that holds pressure to extend the landing gear", "A spring device that stops the blades feathering at engine shutdown"], "why": "It holds a small reserve of engine oil pressurized by compressed air or nitrogen. Releasing it to the hub drives the blades toward low pitch so the prop windmills, allowing a restart with little or no starter use. The Seneca I POH lists no accumulator; it unfeathers with the starter.", "src": "OEG 2C-34 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-065", "t": "engine", "q": "In a twin with unfeathering accumulators, how do you release the stored oil to unfeather a prop?", "a": "Move the prop control forward out of the feather position", "d": ["Engage the starter, which opens the accumulator valve", "Pull the prop control aft past the feather detent", "Turn on that engine's electric fuel pump to prime it"], "why": "Moving the prop control out of feather releases the accumulator pressure. Oil flows to the hub, drives the blades toward low pitch/high RPM, and the prop usually begins to windmill. With fuel and ignition, the engine starts.", "src": "OEG 2C-34 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-066", "t": "engine", "q": "How is a typical electrically actuated propeller synchronizer used?", "a": "Roughly match RPMs, then engage; it trims slave to master", "d": ["Engage it before takeoff; it sets both props to takeoff RPM", "Engage it with RPMs far apart; it averages the two settings", "Engage it; it then adjusts the master engine to match the slave"], "why": "The pilot coarsely matches the RPMs and engages the system. It adjusts the 'slave' engine's RPM to precisely match the 'master' engine and holds that relationship.", "src": "OEG 2C-35 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-067", "t": "engine", "q": "Prop sync is engaged in cruise and you want a new RPM. What should you do?", "a": "Disengage sync, set the new RPM, then re-engage it", "d": ["Leave sync on and move only the slave prop lever", "Leave sync on and move both levers together", "Turn sync off and leave it off for the rest of the flight"], "why": "Turn the synchronizer off when selecting a new RPM and turn it back on after the new RPM is set.", "src": "OEG 2C-35 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-068", "t": "engine", "q": "How does a propeller synchrophaser differ from a synchronizer?", "a": "It also matches blade positions in their arcs, cutting noise more", "d": ["It matches manifold pressure between both engines as well as RPM", "It works only during takeoff and climb, then disengages itself", "It matches RPM mechanically, with no electrical power needed"], "why": "A synchronizer matches RPM. A synchrophaser also compares and adjusts the relative position (phase) of the blades, which reduces propeller noise and vibration significantly.", "src": "OEG 2C-36 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-069", "t": "engine", "q": "With no synchronizer installed, you hear a slow 'drumming' beat in cruise. How do you sync the props manually?", "a": "Set one prop (usually left), adjust the other until the beat stops", "d": ["Adjust the throttles until both manifold pressure needles are matched", "Move both prop levers together until the beat gets faster", "Set one prop, then adjust the other until the beat gets faster"], "why": "Put one prop (usually the left) on the RPM you want, then fine-tune the other until the audible beat slows and disappears. Digital RPM readouts, if fitted, help.", "src": "OEG 2C-37 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o3-070", "t": "engine", "q": "Why don't the propellers feather every time the engines are shut down on the ground?", "a": "A lockout pin blocks feathering below about 800 RPM", "d": ["Oil pressure stays trapped in the propeller hub after shutdown", "The governor keeps holding the blades at low pitch until restart", "The dome's nitrogen charge is vented when the engine stops"], "why": "A small pin in the pitch-change mechanism drops into place when centrifugal force is lost below about 800 RPM, so the blades stay out of feather as oil pressure falls to zero at shutdown.", "src": "OEG 2C-38 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-071", "t": "engine", "q": "An engine fails and you react slowly; RPM decays below about 800 before you pull the prop control to feather. What happens?", "a": "It likely won't feather; the lockout pin has engaged", "d": ["It feathers normally, only taking a little longer", "It feathers instantly from the stored dome air charge", "It moves to low pitch and the engine overspeeds"], "why": "The lockout pin engages as RPM falls below about 800, which stops the blades from feathering. That's why you must feather before RPM decays that far. The Seneca POH gives the same 800 RPM limit.", "src": "OEG 2C-38 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-072", "t": "engine", "q": "In a twin with an alternator on each engine, what is an electrical effect of shutting one engine down?", "a": "Only one alternator remains, so charging capacity is reduced", "d": ["The battery is automatically taken off line to protect it", "The operating engine's magnetos lose their power source", "All avionics automatically shed to the standby bus"], "why": "Securing an engine takes its alternator off line, leaving less power for loads and battery charging, so manage the electrical load.", "src": "OEG 2C-39 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o3-073", "t": "gear", "q": "A twin's hydraulic gear relies on one engine-driven pump, on the left engine (unlike the Seneca I's electric pump). The left engine fails and is secured. What may you need to do before landing?", "a": "Extend the gear with the emergency extension system", "d": ["Lower the gear normally; the right engine supplies pressure", "Plan a gear-up landing, since the gear can't be lowered", "Cycle the gear switch repeatedly to build pressure"], "why": "If the only hydraulic pump is on the failed engine, normal hydraulic extension is lost and you may need the emergency extension. Hydraulically powered flaps would be affected too. Twins with a pump on each engine have redundancy.", "src": "OEG 2C-39 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o3-074", "t": "gear", "q": "During the exterior preflight, which landing gear items should be inspected?", "a": "Struts, squat switches, tires, actuators, wheel wells, and seals", "d": ["Tires and brakes only; switches and actuators are maintenance items", "Gear doors and position lights only, checked from the cockpit", "Struts and tires only; seals and switches can't be seen on preflight"], "why": "A thorough gear preflight covers shocks and struts, squat switches and warning micro-switches, gear alignment, tire pressure, clear actuator arms and wheel wells, and seals for leaks. Use your POH to see what a pilot can inspect.", "src": "OEG 2D-1 · POH/AFM, FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-075", "t": "gear", "q": "How does an electric landing gear system raise and lower the gear?", "a": "A reversible electric motor drives the gear via shafts and gears", "d": ["An electric pump sends hydraulic fluid to actuating cylinders", "An engine-driven pump sends hydraulic fluid to actuating cylinders", "A cockpit hand crank drives each gear leg through cables and chains"], "why": "An electric gear system is essentially an electrically driven jack: the motor drives the drag strut linkages and reverses for extension. An electric pump pushing hydraulic fluid is an electro-hydraulic system, a different design.", "src": "OEG 2D-2 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-076", "t": "gear", "q": "In an electric landing gear system, what stops the gear motor at the end of travel?", "a": "An up or down limit switch on the gearbox", "d": ["A pressure switch in the hydraulic line", "A timer that cuts motor power after a set time", "The squat switch on the main gear strut"], "why": "Once started, the gear motor keeps running until it trips an up or down limit switch on its gearbox.", "src": "OEG 2D-2 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-077", "t": "gear", "q": "What is an electro-hydraulic landing gear system?", "a": "Hydraulic gear whose pump is electrically powered", "d": ["Electric-motor gear with a hydraulic emergency backup", "Hydraulic gear whose pump is driven by an engine", "Electric-motor gear with hydraulically operated doors"], "why": "In hydraulic gear the pump can be engine-driven or electric. When an electric pump pressurizes the fluid, the system is called electro-hydraulic. The Seneca's gear uses an electrically powered reversible pump.", "src": "OEG 2D-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-078", "t": "gear", "q": "What do hydraulic limit switches do in a landing gear system?", "a": "Shut off the pump once the gear has completed its cycle", "d": ["Limit how fast the gear can move during the extension cycle", "Prevent gear retraction while weight is on the wheels", "Sound the warning horn when the gear isn't down and locked"], "why": "Limit switches de-energize the pump after the gear finishes its cycle. If one fails, a backup pressure relief valve relieves excess system pressure.", "src": "OEG 2D-4 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-079", "t": "gear", "q": "What holds retractable landing gear in the up position?", "a": "Uplocks on some airplanes, hydraulic pressure on others", "d": ["Air loads on the closed gear doors, on all airplanes", "The closed gear doors, latched shut by over-center springs", "Spring tension in the drag struts and the side braces"], "why": "Depending on the design, the retracted gear is held by uplocks or by hydraulic pressure. Know which your airplane uses, because it decides how emergency extension works.", "src": "OEG 2D-5 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o3-080", "t": "gear", "q": "When will the landing gear warning horn typically sound?", "a": "Set up to land (power, flaps, speed) with gear not down and locked", "d": ["Whenever the gear is in transit, regardless of power or flaps", "When airspeed exceeds VLO while the gear is down and locked", "Whenever the gear is down and locked and either throttle is advanced"], "why": "The horn is usually tied to throttle position, flap position and/or airspeed, so it sounds when you're configured to land without the gear down and locked. It is a backup if a gear light fails.", "src": "OEG 2D-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-081", "t": "gear", "q": "Which devices prevent inadvertent landing gear retraction on the ground?", "a": "Mechanical downlocks, safety (squat) switches, and ground locks", "d": ["Uplocks, hydraulic limit switches, and pressure relief valves", "Gear doors, sequence valves, and the warning horn", "The parking brake, the nosewheel steering lock, and wheel chocks"], "why": "Downlocks are built in and work automatically; electrically operated safety switches stop the downlocks releasing on the ground; and ground locks (pins or clips) physically hold the gear.", "src": "OEG 2D-7 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-082", "t": "gear", "q": "How does a landing gear squat (safety) switch prevent retraction on the ground?", "a": "A compressed main strut opens the circuit to the retraction motor", "d": ["A compressed main strut closes the circuit to the downlock release", "An extended main strut opens the circuit to the retraction motor", "It senses wheel rotation and blocks retraction until the wheels stop"], "why": "The switch sits on a main gear strut. With weight on the wheels the strut compresses and the switch opens the circuit to the retraction motor or mechanism.", "src": "OEG 2D-8 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-083", "t": "gear", "q": "On the ramp, someone moves the gear selector to UP. With a working squat switch, what should happen?", "a": "The gear stays down, and the warning horn may sound", "d": ["The gear retracts, and the warning horn sounds afterward", "The nose gear retracts while the mains stay down", "The gear motor runs until a limit switch stops it"], "why": "With weight on the wheels the squat switch keeps the retraction circuit open, so the gear stays extended and the horn may warn of the unsafe selection. Never rely on it; check the selector is DOWN before start.", "src": "OEG 2D-8 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-084", "t": "gear", "q": "What types of ground locks are used to prevent gear collapse on the ground?", "a": "Pins in aligned holes or spring clips, with red streamers", "d": ["Wheel chocks and tie-down ropes, flagged with red streamers", "Control column gust locks that also lock out the gear selector", "Cockpit valve locks that trap fluid in the gear cylinders"], "why": "Gear pins go through aligned holes in the gear support structure; spring-loaded clips hold parts of the structure together. Red streamers show at a glance whether they're still installed.", "src": "OEG 2D-9 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-085", "t": "gear", "q": "In a free-fall emergency gear extension, how does the gear get down?", "a": "Uplocks (or holding pressure) are released and it falls by gravity", "d": ["A hand pump pressurizes the gear-down line to drive the gear down", "A compressed gas bottle drives the gear actuators to the down position", "A hand crank turns the gear motor's gearbox to drive the gear down"], "why": "Free-fall systems release whatever holds the gear up: mechanical uplocks (by a handle, or gas-powered uplock release cylinders) or, as in the Seneca I, the hydraulic pressure. The gear then drops under its own weight. The other choices are forced-extension methods used where free fall isn't practical.", "src": "OEG 2D-10 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-086", "t": "gear", "q": "In designs that force the gear down hydraulically in an emergency, where might the pressure come from?", "a": "A hand pump, an accumulator, or an electric hydraulic pump", "d": ["Only the engine-driven pump on the remaining operating engine", "The brake master cylinders at the rudder pedals", "Oil pressure tapped from the propeller governor"], "why": "When gravity and air loads aren't enough, emergency hydraulic pressure may come from an auxiliary hand pump, an accumulator or an electric hydraulic pump, depending on the design. Other designs use compressed gas or a hand crank.", "src": "OEG 2D-10 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-087", "t": "gear", "q": "Which is a common landing gear position indication scheme?", "a": "Three greens for down and locked; red or amber for in transit", "d": ["Three ambers for down and locked; three greens for gear up", "One red light for down and locked; no light at all for gear up", "Three greens for up and locked; one amber for gear down"], "why": "Common schemes are three greens for down and locked, or one green (down) plus one amber (up), with a red or amber light for gear in transit or unsafe. Some airplanes add mechanical indicators or show gear status on a PFD/MFD.", "src": "OEG 2D-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-088", "t": "gear", "q": "Gear selected down: two greens, one light dark, but you're fairly sure the gear is down. What is the first check?", "a": "Press-to-test the bulb or swap in a known good one", "d": ["Recycle the gear up and down right away to reset it", "Use the emergency gear extension system right away", "Request a low fly-by so the tower can look first"], "why": "A burned-out bulb is the likely cause, so first press-to-test it or swap in a bulb you know works. Then use other cues, such as whether the gear horn sounds. (If all the lights look dim or out, check the nav lights: on many airplanes, the Seneca included, they dim the gear lights.)", "src": "OEG 2D-12 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o3-089", "t": "gear", "q": "A gear light is out. You retard a throttle and extend flaps, and no gear warning horn sounds. What does this suggest?", "a": "Gear is probably down and locked; likely an indicator fault", "d": ["The gear is likely still up, and the horn has also failed", "The gear is still in transit and will lock shortly", "The squat switch has failed and silenced the horn"], "why": "The horn sounds when you're configured to land with the gear not down and locked. No horn when you trigger those conditions usually means the gear is down and the problem is in the indicator.", "src": "OEG 2D-12 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o3-090", "t": "gear", "q": "Besides the indicator lights, what cues help confirm the gear has extended or retracted?", "a": "Airspeed and pitch changes, and the cycle's sound and feel", "d": ["Changes in manifold pressure and RPM on both engines", "Changes in oil pressure and oil temperature readings", "Changes in fuel flow and exhaust gas temperature on each engine"], "why": "Airspeed and pitch attitude change as the gear moves, and each airplane has a typical sound, feel and timing. Learn what normal is, because an abnormal retraction often means the extension will be abnormal too.", "src": "OEG 2D-13 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o3-092", "t": "gear", "q": "What is VLE?", "a": "The maximum speed with the landing gear extended", "d": ["The maximum speed for extending or retracting the gear", "The minimum speed for retracting the gear after takeoff", "The maximum speed with the wing flaps extended"], "why": "VLE is the maximum speed at which the airplane can be flown with the gear extended. VLO is the maximum speed for operating (extending or retracting) the gear.", "src": "OEG 2D-15 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-093", "t": "gear", "q": "What is the main concern behind the VLO limit?", "a": "Air loads on the gear and doors while in transit", "d": ["Stability and controllability while the gear is extended", "Tire speed ratings and the wheel spin-up loads at each touchdown", "Wing structural loads with the flaps extended"], "why": "VLO protects the operating mechanism, such as the gear doors, from air loads while the gear is in transit. The OEG ties VLE to stability and controllability with the gear extended.", "src": "OEG 2D-15 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o3-094", "t": "gear", "q": "In daylight you select gear down and the gear lights are so dim you can hardly see them. On many twins, what harmless cause should you check first?", "a": "The nav lights are on, which automatically dims the gear lights", "d": ["The gear is still in transit, so the lights stay dim until locked", "The emergency gear knob is partly out, dimming the indicators", "The squat switches dim the lights until weight is on the wheels"], "why": "On many airplanes, the Seneca I included, turning on the navigation lights dims the gear indicator lights for night use, which makes them hard to see in daylight. Check the nav light switch before assuming a gear problem.", "src": "OEG 2D-12 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-001", "t": "fuel", "q": "Where is the approved fuel grade for an airplane stated?", "a": "In the AFM/POH, on flight deck placards, and next to the filler caps", "d": ["On the airworthiness certificate and in the engine maintenance logbook", "On the registration certificate and the weight and balance report", "In the Chart Supplement entry for your departure airport"], "why": "The POH/AFM specifies the fuel grade, and it is repeated on placards in the flight deck and at each filler cap. The Chart Supplement only tells you what fuel an airport sells.", "src": "OEG 2E-1 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-002", "t": "fuel", "q": "Your piston twin's POH calls for 100LL avgas. Line service arrives with a truck marked Jet A. What should you do?", "a": "Stop the fueling; use only a fuel grade the POH/AFM approves", "d": ["Let it continue; Jet A has more energy per gallon than avgas", "Accept a half load of Jet A mixed with the 100LL already aboard", "Accept it, but plan to enrich the mixture for takeoff"], "why": "Most light training twins burn 100LL. A few modern piston engines are approved for Jet A, but an engine certified for avgas is not, and jet fuel in it can cause detonation and engine failure. Know what fuels (including any SAF) your engines may use.", "src": "OEG 2E-1 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-003", "t": "fuel", "q": "Besides tank locations and capacities (total and usable), what fuel tank details should a multi-engine pilot know from the POH?", "a": "Which tanks are required for takeoff/landing, cruise-only, or transfer fuel", "d": ["Which tanks have the newest sealant, vent lines, and drain valve seals installed", "Which tanks hold the warmest fuel, and their vapor pressures", "Which tanks were filled last, and the octane of each load"], "why": "Tank layouts vary: inboard and outboard wing tanks, and fuselage tanks in larger twins. Know whether certain tanks must be selected for takeoff and landing, whether some are limited to straight-and-level cruise, and whether a tank feeds an engine directly or pumps into another tank.", "src": "OEG 2E-2 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-004", "t": "fuel", "q": "How are light-aircraft fuel tanks typically vented to the outside atmosphere?", "a": "Through the filler cap, a vent tube in the wing, or both", "d": ["Through the fuel sump drains located at the bottom of each tank", "Through the engine crankcase breather lines", "Through the fuel vapor return line from the engine"], "why": "Vents let air replace the fuel as it is burned. Depending on the design, venting is through the filler cap, a tube that protrudes through the wing skin, or both.", "src": "OEG 2E-3 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-005", "t": "fuel", "q": "You top off the tanks on a hot afternoon and later see fuel dripping from a tank overflow drain. What does this most likely mean?", "a": "The fuel is expanding with heat and escaping through the overflow", "d": ["A fuel cell has ruptured and fuel is leaking into the wing structure", "A sump drain valve is stuck open after the preflight", "The fuel selector was left in the crossfeed position"], "why": "The overflow drain, standalone or combined with the vent, lets fuel expand as it warms without damaging the tank. After filling on a hot day, some fuel from the overflow is normal.", "src": "OEG 2E-4 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-006", "t": "fuel", "q": "In many aircraft with engine-driven fuel pumps, what role does the electric fuel pump play?", "a": "It supplements the engine-driven pump for priming or as a backup", "d": ["It is the primary pump; the engine-driven pump serves only as backup", "It transfers fuel between tanks to keep the wings balanced", "It pressurizes the tanks so fuel is pushed to the engine"], "why": "Fuel reaches the engines either by gravity or by mechanical and/or electric pumps. Typically an engine-driven pump does the work and an electric pump supplements it for priming or backup.", "src": "OEG 2E-5 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-007", "t": "fuel", "q": "In a typical light twin with a pump-fed fuel system, how many fuel pumps does each engine have?", "a": "Two: an engine-driven main pump and an electric auxiliary pump", "d": ["One: an engine-driven pump, with crossfeed serving as the backup", "One: an electric pump, with gravity feed as the backup", "Two: both engine-driven, mounted on the engine's accessory case"], "why": "Each engine has an engine-driven main pump plus an electric auxiliary (boost) pump, switched from the flight deck, used for engine starting and if the engine-driven pump fails.", "src": "OEG 2E-6 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-008", "t": "fuel", "q": "What limitation can apply to an electric auxiliary (boost) fuel pump in some airplanes?", "a": "It may not supply enough fuel pressure for full-power operation", "d": ["It may be used only on the ground for engine starting and priming", "It must never run at the same time as the engine pump", "It can be operated only when crossfeed is selected"], "why": "The boost pump adds reliability for starting and for an engine-driven pump failure, but in some airplanes it cannot deliver enough pressure to run the engine at full power. Check your POH.", "src": "OEG 2E-6 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-009", "t": "fuel", "q": "In cruise, one engine's fuel pressure drops to zero because its engine-driven pump has failed. In a typical twin, what supplies fuel pressure to that engine?", "a": "That engine's electric auxiliary (boost) pump", "d": ["The fuel primer, held on continuously until landing", "Gravity flow through the tank vent system", "The opposite engine's engine-driven pump"], "why": "The electric auxiliary pump exists to back up the engine-driven pump, so turning it on for the affected engine is the normal response. Remember it may not support full power in some airplanes.", "src": "OEG 2E-6 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-010", "t": "fuel", "q": "Why do water and sediment collect in fuel strainer bowls and tank sumps?", "a": "They are heavier than avgas, so they settle at the low points", "d": ["They are lighter than avgas, so they float up to the drains", "The strainer chemically separates them from the fuel", "Vent air carries them to the sumps as fuel is burned"], "why": "Strainers trap moisture and sediment, which settle to the bottom of the strainer assembly because they are heavier than fuel. A sump is a low point in a fuel system or tank, which is why you drain there.", "src": "OEG 2E-7 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-011", "t": "fuel", "q": "How does a fuel primer help an engine start?", "a": "It delivers fuel directly into the cylinders before start", "d": ["It adds extra fuel to the carburetor float bowl before start", "It pressurizes the fuel tanks to push fuel to the pump", "It preheats the fuel so it vaporizes in the carburetor"], "why": "The primer draws fuel from the tanks and sprays it straight into the cylinders. It helps most in cold weather, when there isn't enough heat to vaporize fuel in the carburetor.", "src": "OEG 2E-8 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-012", "t": "fuel", "q": "On a fuel-injected engine, what causes vapor lock?", "a": "Fuel gets hot enough to vaporize in the lines, blocking flow", "d": ["Ice crystals form in the fuel and plug the injector nozzles", "Water in the fuel collects in the fuel distribution manifold", "Too rich a mixture floods the cylinders with liquid fuel"], "why": "Vapor lock is vapor or air in the fuel system that keeps liquid fuel from reaching the cylinders and can make a restart hard or impossible. Running a tank dry can also let air into the system.", "src": "OEG 2E-9 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-013", "t": "fuel", "q": "After a short stop on a hot day, your fuel-injected engine will not restart. What is the likely cause, and what should you use?", "a": "Vapor lock; use the POH hot-start procedure", "d": ["Induction icing; use alternate air during the start", "Water in the fuel; drain the sumps and crank again", "A weak battery; use the engine's cold-start procedure"], "why": "Heat soak after shutdown can vaporize the fuel in a fuel-injected engine's lines. Know your airplane's hot-start procedure and use it when vapor lock is suspected.", "src": "OEG 2E-9 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-014", "t": "fuel", "q": "What positions are commonly found on each engine's fuel selector valve in a light twin?", "a": "ON, OFF, and X-FEED", "d": ["LEFT, RIGHT, BOTH, and OFF", "ON, RESERVE, and OFF", "MAIN, BOOST, and OFF"], "why": "A three-position selector for each engine (ON, OFF, X-FEED) is the common arrangement. LEFT/RIGHT/BOTH is typical of many single-engine airplanes, not twins.", "src": "OEG 2E-10 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-015", "t": "fuel", "q": "Per the OEG, what is the primary purpose of the fuel crossfeed system on most light twins?", "a": "To extend range and endurance in one-engine-inoperative flight", "d": ["To balance fuel between the wings during normal two-engine cruise", "To let both engines feed from one tank for takeoff", "To supply fuel pressure when an engine-driven pump fails"], "why": "In most twins crossfeed is an emergency procedure that lets the operating engine use the dead engine's fuel. Some models also permit it for routine fuel balancing; the Seneca I POH (2-10) lists both single-engine range and fuel balance. Follow your POH limits.", "src": "OEG 2E-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-016", "t": "fuel", "q": "One engine begins running rough and you suspect contaminated fuel in that side's tank. Besides OEI range, how might crossfeed help?", "a": "It lets that engine draw fuel from the opposite side's tank", "d": ["It drains the contaminated tank overboard through the vent", "It returns the bad fuel to the tank through the vapor return line", "It routes the fuel through the boost pump filter a second time"], "why": "Crossfeed may also be used when an engine is running poorly and fuel contamination is suspected. Know your POH crossfeed procedure thoroughly.", "src": "OEG 2E-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-017", "t": "fuel", "q": "During preflight, a student flips both fuel selectors to crossfeed and back, then says the crossfeed is checked. What did that actually confirm?", "a": "Only that the handles move freely, not that crossfeed works", "d": ["That fuel will flow from the opposite tank to each engine", "That water and debris were purged from the crossfeed lines", "That the crossfeed check required before every flight is done"], "why": "Moving the selectors on the ground proves nothing beyond freedom of handle motion. Verifying crossfeed takes a complete functional check with each engine running on crossfeed.", "src": "OEG 2E-12 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-018", "t": "fuel", "q": "During a complete functional crossfeed check at run-up, how should each engine be run on crossfeed?", "a": "Individually, at 1,500 RPM minimum, for at least 1 minute", "d": ["Individually, at full takeoff power, for at least 1 minute", "Both together, at idle power, for at least 10 seconds", "Both together, at 1,000 RPM, for at least 5 minutes"], "why": "Running each engine on its crossfeed source at moderate power for at least a minute ensures fuel flow is truly established from that source, not just from fuel already in the lines.", "src": "OEG 2E-13 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-019", "t": "fuel", "q": "You have just completed a functional crossfeed check during the run-up. What should you do before takeoff?", "a": "Run each engine 1 minute or more at moderate power on its main tank", "d": ["Leave the selectors on crossfeed, since fuel flow was just proven", "Shut down and drain the crossfeed lines before departing", "Take off promptly while the engines are still warmed up"], "why": "Once the crossfeed check is done, select the main (takeoff) tanks again and give each engine a minute or more at moderate power, proving it is feeding normally from the tanks you will take off on.", "src": "OEG 2E-13 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-020", "t": "fuel", "q": "Is a complete crossfeed check needed before every flight, and why do it at all?", "a": "Not every flight, but unused crossfeed lines collect water and debris", "d": ["Yes, every flight, because the regulations require it", "No, it is done only at the annual inspection by a mechanic", "No, because crossfeed lines are sealed and can't collect contamination"], "why": "A full check usually isn't needed before each flight. But crossfeed lines that sit unused tend to collect water and debris, so use crossfeed occasionally and drain its external drains on preflight.", "src": "OEG 2E-14 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-021", "t": "fuel", "q": "Your fuel gauges show both tanks full, but you have not looked in the tanks. What is the best practice?", "a": "Visually check the fuel quantity and compare it with the gauges", "d": ["Rely on the gauges, since both of them read the same quantity", "Skip the visual check if the airplane has digital fuel sensors", "Use the last fuel receipt to confirm the tanks are topped off"], "why": "Gauges display what a sensing unit in each tank measures (float types in older aircraft, digital sensors in newer ones), in gallons or pounds. Whatever the type, always verify fuel visually and compare it with the gauges.", "src": "OEG 2E-15 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-022", "t": "fuel", "q": "What is an aircraft fuel vapor return system primarily designed to do?", "a": "Prevent tank over-pressurization and limit fuel vapor buildup", "d": ["Meet emissions rules in the same way as a car's vapor system", "Feed fuel vapor to the primer for easier cold-weather starts", "Vent fuel vapor overboard through the engine exhaust"], "why": "The OEG contrasts it with car systems: in aircraft its main jobs are preventing tank over-pressurization and reducing the risk of a flammable vapor buildup. A return line connects to each tank, often with check valves against backflow.", "src": "OEG 2E-16 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-023", "t": "fuel", "q": "Why must aircraft fuel tanks be vented?", "a": "To prevent a vacuum or excess pressure inside the tanks", "d": ["To let water vapor escape so condensation cannot form", "To cool the fuel by circulating outside air through the tank", "To let vapor reach the engine for smoother idling"], "why": "Air must enter and leave as fuel is consumed and as temperature changes. Without venting, a vacuum could impede fuel flow or pressure could deform the tank.", "src": "OEG 2E-16 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-024", "t": "fuel", "q": "Which list correctly states functions of the engine oil system?", "a": "Lubricate, cool, seal pistons to cylinders, and remove contaminants", "d": ["Lubricate, cool, prime the cylinders, and filter the induction air", "Lubricate, seal, pressurize the fuel, and heat the carburetor", "Lubricate, cool, drive the vacuum pump, and heat the cabin"], "why": "Oil lubricates moving parts, cools by reducing friction and removing heat from the cylinders, seals between the cylinder walls and pistons, and carries contaminants away.", "src": "OEG 2E-17 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-025", "t": "fuel", "q": "What is the difference between a dry-sump and a wet-sump oil system?", "a": "Dry sump: oil in a separate tank; wet sump: oil in the engine's sump", "d": ["Dry sump needs no oil pump; wet sump uses a pressure pump", "Dry sump uses synthetic oil; wet sump uses mineral oil", "Dry sump is for carbureted engines; wet sump is for fuel-injected ones"], "why": "A dry-sump engine stores its oil in a separate tank and pumps it through the engine; a wet-sump engine carries its oil in a sump built into the engine itself.", "src": "OEG 2E-18 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-026", "t": "fuel", "q": "During a long climb on a hot day, which gauge gives the most direct and immediate indication that the cylinders are overheating?", "a": "Cylinder head temperature (CHT)", "d": ["Oil temperature", "Carburetor air temperature (CAT)", "Outside air temperature (OAT)"], "why": "Oil temperature lags and only indirectly reflects engine heating, though you can use it if it's all you have. CHT reads cylinder temperature directly and right away.", "src": "OEG 2E-19 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-027", "t": "fuel", "q": "Which of these could cause an abnormally high oil temperature indication?", "a": "A blocked oil cooler", "d": ["Oil too viscous for cold-weather operation", "Cowl flaps left open during cruise", "A very rich mixture setting"], "why": "High readings can come from a plugged oil line, low oil quantity, a blocked oil cooler, or a faulty gauge. Unusually low readings can mean improper oil viscosity in cold weather.", "src": "OEG 2E-20 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-028", "t": "fuel", "q": "On a cold winter flight, one engine's oil temperature stays unusually low. Which possible cause does this suggest?", "a": "Oil of improper viscosity for the cold", "d": ["Low oil quantity in that engine", "A plugged oil line on that engine", "A partially blocked oil cooler on that engine"], "why": "Low oil quantity, a plugged line, and a blocked cooler all drive oil temperature up. A low reading in cold weather may point to oil of the wrong viscosity.", "src": "OEG 2E-20 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-029", "t": "fuel", "q": "In a basic hydraulic system, which component controls the direction of fluid flow?", "a": "The selector valve", "d": ["The relief valve", "The reservoir", "The hydraulic filter"], "why": "A basic system has a reservoir, a pump (hand, electric, or engine-driven), a filter to keep fluid clean, a selector valve to direct flow, a relief valve for excess pressure, and an actuator.", "src": "OEG 2E-21 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-030", "t": "fuel", "q": "What is the job of the relief valve in a basic hydraulic system?", "a": "To relieve excess pressure in the system", "d": ["To direct fluid to extend or retract an actuator", "To keep the hydraulic fluid clean", "To store the fluid supply for the pump"], "why": "The relief valve gives the system an outlet when pressure gets too high. The selector valve directs flow, the filter cleans the fluid, and the reservoir holds it.", "src": "OEG 2E-21 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-031", "t": "fuel", "q": "In a hydraulic system, what is a servo?", "a": "A cylinder and piston that turn fluid power into work", "d": ["A valve that limits the maximum system pressure", "A pump that turns engine power into fluid pressure", "A sensor that commands the hydraulic selector valve"], "why": "A servo is a piston working inside a cylinder: fluid pressure on the piston produces the force that moves a system such as the landing gear or a flight control.", "src": "OEG 2E-22 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-032", "t": "fuel", "q": "Why does a hydraulic landing gear system use a double-acting servo and a selector valve?", "a": "The gear must be driven both ways: extension and retraction", "d": ["It lets the gear free-fall by gravity if the pump fails", "It lets two hydraulic pumps share the work of raising the gear", "It keeps pressure from exceeding the relief valve setting"], "why": "A single-acting servo applies fluid force in one direction; a double-acting servo works in both. The selector valve chooses the direction, which gear extension and retraction require.", "src": "OEG 2E-23 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-033", "t": "fuel", "q": "What is the most widely used hydraulic fluid in small aircraft, and what color is it?", "a": "Mineral-based MIL-H-5606, dyed red", "d": ["Phosphate-ester fluid, dyed purple", "Vegetable-based fluid, dyed blue", "Mineral-based MIL-H-5606, dyed blue"], "why": "MIL-H-5606 is mineral-based, smells like penetrating oil, and is dyed red. Always use the fluid your POH/AFM specifies.", "src": "OEG 2E-24 · FAA-H-8083-25 · FAA-H-8083-31"},
{"id": "o4-034", "t": "fuel", "q": "MIL-H-83282 hydraulic fluid is also used in small aircraft. How does it compare with MIL-H-5606?", "a": "It is fire-resistant and is also dyed red", "d": ["It is fire-resistant and is dyed purple", "It is vegetable-based and is dyed blue", "It is a phosphate ester and is dyed green"], "why": "MIL-H-83282 is a newer, fire-resistant fluid, and like MIL-H-5606 it is dyed red, so color alone won't tell them apart. Use the fluid your POH/AFM specifies.", "src": "OEG 2E-24 · FAA-H-8083-25 · FAA-H-8083-31"},
{"id": "o4-035", "t": "fuel", "q": "In light twins that have hydraulic systems, what do those systems most commonly operate?", "a": "The landing gear, and in a few airplanes, the flaps", "d": ["The primary flight controls and the elevator trim tabs", "The gyroscopic flight instruments", "The fuel boost and transfer pumps"], "why": "Not every twin has a hydraulic system. Those that do commonly use it for the landing gear, and a few for the flaps. Know how your system works and how it can fail.", "src": "OEG 2E-25 · FAA-H-8083-25 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-036", "t": "elec", "q": "What is the main advantage of an alternator over a DC generator?", "a": "It produces enough current even at low engine RPM", "d": ["It produces direct current without needing any rectifier", "It needs no voltage regulator to control its output", "It can bring a completely dead battery back up in flight"], "why": "Most DC generators can't carry the full load at low RPM, so the battery makes up the difference. An alternator produces AC that is converted to DC, and its output stays more constant over a wide RPM range.", "src": "OEG 2F-1 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-037", "t": "elec", "q": "In a generator-equipped airplane, you taxi at low RPM for a long time with lights and radios on. What is the likely result?", "a": "The load is drawn from the battery, which can be quickly depleted", "d": ["The generator overcharges the battery at low RPM", "The voltage regulator turns off the radios automatically", "The generator easily carries the load because the demand is so low"], "why": "DC generators often can't produce enough current at low RPM, so the battery supplies the shortfall and can run down. Alternators avoid this by producing adequate output even at slower engine speeds.", "src": "OEG 2F-1 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-038", "t": "elec", "q": "What is the purpose of alternator/generator paralleling circuitry in a twin?", "a": "To match outputs so both units share the load equally", "d": ["To connect both alternators to the battery only for engine start", "To let one alternator run the system while the other stays off", "To trip a failed alternator off the bus automatically"], "why": "Paralleling circuitry matches the output of each engine's alternator or generator so the load is split equally rather than one unit carrying most of it.", "src": "OEG 2F-2 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-039", "t": "elec", "q": "What does 'load shedding' mean in a multi-engine airplane's electrical system?", "a": "Reducing electrical load when running on one alternator", "d": ["Moving electrical load from the battery onto the alternators", "Dumping excess alternator output to ground through a resistor", "Automatically balancing the load between both alternators"], "why": "When one unit fails, the remaining one may not support the full load, so the pilot turns off nonessential equipment. The POH/AFM gives the system description and limits.", "src": "OEG 2F-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-040", "t": "elec", "q": "In cruise, one alternator fails and the remaining one is near its rated load. After isolating the failed unit, what should you do?", "a": "Shed nonessential loads such as backup radios and unneeded lights", "d": ["Turn off the battery master to protect the battery", "Reset the failed alternator's breaker until it stays in", "Increase RPM so the remaining alternator can carry the entire load"], "why": "Normally the failed unit is isolated and the other powers the system, but you may have to shed load: backup radios, unneeded interior or exterior lights, and deice equipment if it isn't needed. Watch the electrical load indications.", "src": "OEG 2F-4 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-041", "t": "elec", "q": "How do circuit breakers differ from fuses in protecting aircraft circuits?", "a": "They do the same job but can be reset instead of replaced", "d": ["Breakers guard against overvoltage; fuses against overcurrent", "Breakers protect avionics only; fuses protect everything else", "Breakers trip at a lower current, so they protect better"], "why": "Both protect circuits and equipment from electrical overload. A breaker can be manually reset rather than replaced, and placards at the panel identify each circuit by name and amperage limit.", "src": "OEG 2F-5 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-042", "t": "elec", "q": "In flight, the circuit breaker for a nonessential cabin light pops. What is the recommended action?", "a": "Leave it out; the light isn't needed to finish the flight safely", "d": ["Reset it right away; breakers are designed to be reset in flight", "Reset it once after it cools, since one reset is always acceptable", "Hold the breaker in by hand so the circuit stays powered"], "why": "Don't reset a tripped breaker in flight unless an approved AFM procedure calls for it or, in the PIC's judgment, the circuit is needed to complete the flight safely. Repeated resets can damage the circuit or cause a fire or explosion.", "src": "OEG 2F-6 · FAA-H-8083-30 · AC 120-80"},
{"id": "o4-043", "t": "elec", "q": "A zero-center ammeter shows a minus (discharge) indication. What does this mean?", "a": "The battery is supplying more current than it receives", "d": ["The battery is being charged at a high rate", "The alternator is carrying the whole load with power to spare", "The voltage regulator is allowing an overcharge"], "why": "A plus indication shows the battery's charging rate; a minus indication means the battery is supplying more than is being put back. The ammeter also shows whether the alternator/generator is producing enough power.", "src": "OEG 2F-7 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-044", "t": "elec", "q": "In flight, your zero-center ammeter suddenly shows a full-scale minus deflection. What does this indicate?", "a": "A malfunction of the alternator/generator", "d": ["A malfunction of the voltage regulator circuit", "A fully charged battery", "A tripped avionics circuit breaker"], "why": "A full-scale minus deflection points to an alternator/generator malfunction. A full-scale positive deflection points to a regulator malfunction.", "src": "OEG 2F-7 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-045", "t": "elec", "q": "What does a full-scale positive (charge) deflection on a zero-center ammeter indicate?", "a": "A malfunction of the voltage regulator", "d": ["A malfunction of the alternator/generator", "A fully charged battery isolated from the bus", "An electrical load beyond the alternator's capacity"], "why": "A pegged positive reading means the regulator is letting excessive charge through. A pegged negative reading means the alternator/generator has failed, and an excessive load shows as a discharge.", "src": "OEG 2F-7 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-046", "t": "elec", "q": "With all electrical equipment turned off, what does a loadmeter indicate?", "a": "Only the charging current the battery is demanding", "d": ["The battery's remaining capacity as a percentage", "Zero, since a loadmeter shows only accessory load", "The alternator's maximum available output"], "why": "A loadmeter's scale starts at zero and shows the load on the alternator/generator as a percentage of its capacity, from accessories plus battery charging. With everything off, only battery charging current remains.", "src": "OEG 2F-8 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-047", "t": "elec", "q": "In a 12-volt aircraft electrical system, about what voltage should the alternator system put out, and why?", "a": "About 14 volts, so it stays above battery voltage and charges it", "d": ["About 12 volts, so it exactly matches the battery voltage", "About 24 volts, so it can charge two 12-volt batteries wired in series", "About 10 volts, so the battery is never overcharged"], "why": "The voltage regulator stabilizes alternator/generator output to control the battery's charge rate. Output must be higher than battery voltage; a 12-volt battery is fed by roughly 14 volts.", "src": "OEG 2F-9 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-048", "t": "elec", "q": "What is an electrical bus bar?", "a": "A power distribution point to which several circuits connect", "d": ["A switch that connects two separate electrical buses", "A protective device that opens the circuit when current is too high", "A relay that connects the battery to the starter"], "why": "A bus bar is often a solid metal strip with terminals that distributes power to many circuits. The switch that connects buses is a bus tie.", "src": "OEG 2F-10 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-049", "t": "elec", "q": "In a twin with split buses, the left generator fails and its bus loses power. What does closing the bus tie do?", "a": "Lets the operating generator power both buses", "d": ["Isolates the failed generator from the battery", "Transfers the dead bus to the standby battery", "Parallels the failed generator with the good one"], "why": "A bus tie is a switch connecting two or more bus bars. It is typically closed when one generator fails so the remaining generator can feed both buses.", "src": "OEG 2F-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-050", "t": "elec", "q": "What does an AHRS (attitude and heading reference system) provide?", "a": "Attitude, heading, and yaw information from three-axis sensors", "d": ["Airspeed, altitude, and vertical speed from pitot-static inputs", "Terrain alerts from GPS position and a terrain database", "Steering commands shown as command bars on the PFD"], "why": "An AHRS uses three-axis sensors to supply attitude, heading, and yaw, replacing the mechanical gyros with more reliable and accurate sensors. Air data comes from the ADC.", "src": "OEG 2G-1 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-051", "t": "elec", "q": "Which inputs does an air data computer (ADC) process?", "a": "Pitot pressure, static pressure, and temperature", "d": ["Pitot pressure, static pressure, and magnetic heading", "GPS position, static pressure, and engine RPM", "Attitude rates, pitot pressure, and fuel flow"], "why": "From pitot, static, and temperature inputs, the ADC computes precise altitude, indicated and true airspeed, vertical speed, and air temperature.", "src": "OEG 2G-1 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-052", "t": "elec", "q": "In a glass cockpit, how do the PFD and MFD roles differ?", "a": "PFD replaces the six-pack; MFD shows maps, nav, and terrain data", "d": ["PFD shows engine data; MFD shows attitude and airspeed", "PFD shows the moving map; MFD shows the six traditional instruments", "PFD is the standby display; MFD is the primary display"], "why": "The PFD replaces the six traditional instruments with one display (horizon, airspeed, altitude, vertical speed, trend, trim, rate of turn). The MFD presents configurable navigation, moving map, and terrain information alongside it.", "src": "OEG 2G-1 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-053", "t": "elec", "q": "What does a flight director (FD) do?", "a": "Shows steering commands, as command bars, for the pilot to follow", "d": ["Moves the control surfaces to fly the selected path", "Stores the database of routes, approaches, and departures for the GPS", "Compares the airplane's path with a terrain database"], "why": "The FD computer processes the selected navigation source and the airplane's parameters and shows command bars or crossbars; the pilot keeps the nose aligned with them. Moving the controls is the autopilot's job.", "src": "OEG 2G-1 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-054", "t": "elec", "q": "Which function belongs to a flight management system (FMS)?", "a": "Programming routes and computing fuel, time remaining, and range", "d": ["Sensing attitude and heading with solid-state three-axis sensors", "Measuring Earth's magnetic field to determine heading", "Computing true airspeed from pitot and static pressure"], "why": "An FMS holds a database for programming routes, approaches, and departures, feeds navigation data to the FD/autopilot, and computes values such as fuel consumption, time remaining, and range.", "src": "OEG 2G-1 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-055", "t": "elec", "q": "How does TAWS (terrain awareness and warning system) determine a terrain threat?", "a": "It compares GPS position and altitude with a terrain database", "d": ["It paints the nearby terrain using returns from the weather radar", "It relies on ATC radar to issue low-altitude alerts", "It senses nearby high terrain with the magnetometer"], "why": "TAWS checks where the airplane is and where it is heading, from GPS and altitude data, against a detailed database of terrain and obstacles.", "src": "OEG 2G-1 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-056", "t": "elec", "q": "In an electronic flight instrument system, what does the magnetometer do?", "a": "Senses Earth's magnetic field and sends heading data to the AHRS", "d": ["Senses aircraft rotation and sends attitude data to the PFD", "Senses static pressure and sends altitude data to the ADC", "Senses GPS signals and sends position data to the MFD"], "why": "It senses Earth's magnetic field to work out heading and sends that digitally to the AHRS, which in turn drives the heading display on the PFD.", "src": "OEG 2G-2 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-057", "t": "elec", "q": "In flight, the magnetometer fails but the rest of the AHRS keeps working. What information will you lose?", "a": "Heading information", "d": ["Attitude information", "Airspeed and altitude", "GPS position"], "why": "The magnetometer is the AHRS's heading source, so losing it removes heading information. Airspeed and altitude come from the ADC.", "src": "OEG 2G-10 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-058", "t": "elec", "q": "When you power up an FMS/RNAV unit, how do you verify the navigation database's effective dates?", "a": "Check the dates on the start-up screen shown during the self-test", "d": ["Check the latest avionics entry in the airplane's maintenance logbook", "Check the dates printed in the current Chart Supplement", "Ask ATC for the current database cycle on initial contact"], "why": "The database's effective dates are typically displayed on a start-up page as the system runs its self-test. Confirm the database is current before relying on it.", "src": "OEG 2G-3 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-059", "t": "elec", "q": "Why do some AHRS units have to be initialized on the ground before departure?", "a": "To set the reference attitude for measuring later changes", "d": ["To spin the gyro rotor up to its full operating speed", "To load and verify the current navigation database cycle", "To calibrate the pitot-static inputs to field elevation"], "why": "Initialization establishes the reference attitude that all later attitude changes are measured against. Some systems must do this on the ground; others can initialize while taxiing or even in flight.", "src": "OEG 2G-4 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-060", "t": "elec", "q": "Which standby instruments are typically provided in an advanced avionics aircraft?", "a": "Attitude indicator, airspeed indicator, and altimeter", "d": ["Turn coordinator, heading indicator, and VSI", "Airspeed indicator, altimeter, and VSI", "Attitude indicator, heading indicator, and turn coordinator"], "why": "Aircraft with electronic flight instruments must carry a minimal set of backups, usually conventional round dials: attitude, airspeed, and altitude.", "src": "OEG 2G-5 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-061", "t": "elec", "q": "Your PFD goes blank in flight. In a system with reversion capability, what can the remaining display show?", "a": "Primary flight and engine instruments", "d": ["Only the moving map and the flight plan pages", "Only the engine instruments", "Only the terrain and traffic pages"], "why": "Many systems offer reversion, putting the primary flight and engine instruments on the remaining display. Know what your system does, and what else a display failure may take with it.", "src": "OEG 2G-6 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-062", "t": "elec", "q": "Besides losing a screen, what else can a display failure cause in some integrated avionics systems?", "a": "Partial loss of navigation, communication, and GPS capability", "d": ["Loss of the standby attitude indicator and the standby altimeter", "Automatic disconnection of both alternators", "Loss of power to the electric fuel pumps"], "why": "In some systems, radios and GPS are tied to a particular display, so its failure can partially remove nav, comm, and GPS capability. Your POH/AFM explains your system.", "src": "OEG 2G-7 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-063", "t": "elec", "q": "Red Xs appear over the airspeed, altitude, and vertical speed on your PFD, but the attitude display is normal. What has failed?", "a": "The air data computer (ADC)", "d": ["The AHRS", "The magnetometer", "The flight management system"], "why": "Airspeed, altitude, and vertical speed come from the ADC, so red Xs on those indicate an ADC failure. A red X on the attitude indicator would point to the AHRS.", "src": "OEG 2G-8 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-064", "t": "elec", "q": "A red X appears over the attitude indicator on your PFD. Which component has most likely failed?", "a": "The AHRS", "d": ["The ADC", "The magnetometer", "The standby battery"], "why": "Attitude comes from the AHRS, so an inoperative (red X) attitude display indicates an AHRS failure. An ADC failure removes airspeed, altitude, and VS; a magnetometer failure removes heading.", "src": "OEG 2G-9 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-065", "t": "elec", "q": "In an aircraft with electronic flight instruments, what is the standby battery for?", "a": "Backup power after a charging failure exhausts the main battery", "d": ["Extra cranking power for engine starts in cold weather", "Powering the avionics during engine start to protect them", "Sharing the electrical load with the main battery in normal flight"], "why": "It stays charged and unused in normal operation, ready to take over if the charging system fails and the main battery is then run down.", "src": "OEG 2G-11 · FAA-H-8083-15"},
{"id": "o4-066", "t": "elec", "q": "In a typical electronic flight instrument system, when does the standby battery come online?", "a": "When main battery voltage drops to about 19 volts, with its switch in ARM", "d": ["As soon as either alternator fails, with its switch in ARM", "When main battery voltage drops to about 19 volts, with its switch OFF", "When main battery voltage drops to about 12 volts, with its switch in ARM"], "why": "It comes online automatically once the main battery is depleted to a set value, about 19 volts in the typical system, and generally only if the standby battery switch is in ARM. Check your POH/AFM for specifics.", "src": "OEG 2G-11 · FAA-H-8083-15"},
{"id": "o4-067", "t": "elec", "q": "A two-axis autopilot typically controls which surfaces?", "a": "Ailerons and elevator, or ailerons and rudder", "d": ["Ailerons only, through a single roll servo", "Elevator and rudder, but never the ailerons", "Ailerons, elevator, and rudder through three servos"], "why": "A single-axis autopilot controls only the ailerons (roll). Two-axis adds the elevator or rudder. A three-axis autopilot uses three servos for ailerons, elevator, and rudder to control all three axes.", "src": "OEG 2G-12 · FAA-H-8083-15"},
{"id": "o4-068", "t": "elec", "q": "In a basic autopilot system, what does the amplifier do?", "a": "Boosts the gyro signals enough to operate the servos", "d": ["Senses what the airplane is doing about each axis", "Moves the control surfaces in response to commands", "Lets the pilot fly the airplane through the autopilot"], "why": "Gyros sense what the airplane is doing, servos move the control surfaces, the amplifier strengthens the gyro signals enough to drive the servos, and a controller allows manual control through the autopilot.", "src": "OEG 2G-13 · FAA-H-8083-31"},
{"id": "o4-069", "t": "elec", "q": "What does a position-based autopilot use to sense deviations?", "a": "The attitude gyro, sensing departure from a reference position", "d": ["The turn-and-bank sensor, sensing roll and yaw rates", "The magnetometer, sensing changes in the magnetic field", "The air data computer, sensing changes in pitot-static pressure"], "why": "A position-based autopilot uses the attitude gyro to measure how far the airplane has moved from a reference, such as wings level, a pitch attitude, or a heading. A rate-based system uses the turn-and-bank sensor instead.", "src": "OEG 2G-14 · FAA-H-8083-15"},
{"id": "o4-070", "t": "elec", "q": "What sensor does a rate-based autopilot use, and about which axes does it use rate information?", "a": "Turn-and-bank sensor; the vertical (yaw) and longitudinal (roll) axes", "d": ["Turn-and-bank sensor; the longitudinal (roll) and lateral (pitch) axes", "Turn-and-bank sensor; the lateral (pitch) and vertical (yaw) axes", "Attitude gyro; the longitudinal (roll) and the lateral (pitch) axes"], "why": "A rate-based system uses the turn-and-bank sensor and works on rate of movement about the vertical axis (heading change or yaw) and the longitudinal axis (roll).", "src": "OEG 2G-14 · FAA-H-8083-15"},
{"id": "o4-071", "t": "elec", "q": "What are the most common features available on light-aircraft autopilots?", "a": "Altitude hold and heading hold", "d": ["Airspeed hold and autothrottle", "Autoland and rollout guidance", "Envelope protection and yaw damping"], "why": "Altitude and heading hold are the most common. More advanced systems add vertical speed and/or indicated airspeed hold, and most autopilots can also track navaids.", "src": "OEG 2G-15 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-072", "t": "elec", "q": "Your autopilot starts wandering in heading and pitch, the attitude and heading indicators drift, and the suction gauge reads low. What should you suspect?", "a": "A pneumatic system failure letting the gyros spin down", "d": ["An air data computer failure affecting the altimeter and VSI", "A magnetometer failure affecting heading only", "A pitot tube blockage affecting the airspeed"], "why": "With vacuum- or pressure-driven gyros, a pneumatic failure lets the gyros spin down and wander. If they feed the autopilot or flight director, the result is erratic movement or false indications.", "src": "OEG 2G-16 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-073", "t": "elec", "q": "What do autopilot systems normally incorporate so a malfunctioning autopilot can be disabled?", "a": "A disconnect safety feature that disengages it manually or automatically", "d": ["A servo fuse that blows when the pilot pushes against the controls", "A caging knob that locks the servos in their last position", "A timer that disengages the autopilot after a set period"], "why": "Autopilots normally have a disconnect safety feature that can drop the system off automatically or on the pilot's command. Systems vary widely, so learn the disconnect procedure in your AFM/POH autopilot supplement.", "src": "OEG 2G-17 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-074", "t": "elec", "q": "Can most autopilot systems be manually overpowered by the pilot?", "a": "Yes, most autopilots can be manually overridden", "d": ["No, the servos lock the controls until it is disengaged", "No, only hydraulic autopilots can be overridden by hand", "Yes, but only in roll; the pitch servo cannot be overpowered"], "why": "Most autopilots can be manually overridden. Check your POH/AFM supplement for your system's specifics.", "src": "OEG 2G-18 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o4-075", "t": "elec", "q": "Your autopilot has envelope protection, and it is engaged and enabled. You select a climb rate the airplane cannot sustain. What will it do?", "a": "Reduce the angle of attack to keep the airplane from stalling", "d": ["Hold the selected climb rate until the airplane eventually stalls", "Disconnect and leave the airplane trimmed nose-up", "Add power automatically to maintain the climb rate"], "why": "Envelope protection lowers the angle of attack to avoid a stall even if the pilot selects an excessive climb rate or attitude. Know whether yours has it and whether it is enabled; it may be disabled to demonstrate required maneuvers.", "src": "OEG 2G-19 · AFM/POH supplements"},
{"id": "o4-076", "t": "elec", "q": "In general, may the autopilot be used during single-engine operations in a light twin?", "a": "Most systems prohibit autopilot use with one engine inoperative", "d": ["Most systems allow it once the airplane is trimmed for one engine", "Most systems require it, to reduce workload after a failure", "Most systems allow it, but only with the yaw damper engaged"], "why": "Most autopilot systems prohibit single-engine use. The POH/AFM and the autopilot supplement govern.", "src": "OEG 2G-20 · POH/AFM"},
{"id": "o4-077", "t": "elec", "q": "What does a yaw damper do?", "a": "Moves the rudder to counter yaw sensed by a gyro or accelerometer", "d": ["Moves the ailerons to keep the wings level in turbulence", "Trims the rudder to cancel asymmetric thrust after an engine failure", "Moves the elevator to damp out pitch oscillations"], "why": "A yaw damper is a servo that drives the rudder in response to a sensed yaw rate, minimizing turbulence-induced motion about the vertical axis. Most can be engaged independently of the autopilot.", "src": "OEG 2G-21 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-078", "t": "elec", "q": "When should a yaw damper normally be off?", "a": "For takeoff and landing", "d": ["During cruise in turbulence", "Whenever the autopilot is disengaged", "During climbs and descents"], "why": "Yaw dampers should be off for takeoff and landing, and there may be added restrictions during single-engine operation. Most work independently of the autopilot, so autopilot off doesn't mean yaw damper off.", "src": "OEG 2G-21 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o4-079", "t": "elec", "q": "Does an airplane always have to remain stationary while its AHRS initializes?", "a": "Not always; some can initialize while taxiing or even in flight", "d": ["Yes; every AHRS must align motionless before the airplane taxis", "No; every AHRS aligns only after takeoff, in wings-level flight", "No; an AHRS needs no initialization, since it has no spinning gyros"], "why": "It depends on the system. Some AHRS units must finish initializing on the ground before departure; others can do it during taxi or in flight. Either way, initialization sets the reference attitude used for later changes.", "src": "OEG 2G-4 · FAA-H-8083-16"},
{"id": "o4-080", "t": "elec", "q": "What is the purpose of the voltage regulator in an aircraft electrical system?", "a": "To stabilize alternator output and so control the battery charge rate", "d": ["To convert the alternator's AC output into DC for the bus", "To divide the electrical load equally between both alternators", "To open a circuit when its current exceeds the placarded limit"], "why": "The regulator holds generator/alternator output steady, slightly above battery voltage, which sets how fast the battery charges. Rectifiers convert AC to DC, paralleling circuitry shares load, and breakers or fuses protect circuits.", "src": "OEG 2F-9 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-001", "t": "instr", "q": "What are the two major parts of a pitot-static system?", "a": "Impact (pitot) pressure chamber and lines; static pressure chamber and lines", "d": ["Impact pressure chamber and lines; vacuum manifold and suction lines", "Static pressure chamber and lines; alternate static valve and cabin vent", "Pitot tube and pitot heater; aneroid capsule and calibrated leak"], "why": "The system has two halves: one senses ram (impact) pressure, the other senses undisturbed outside (static) pressure. Vacuum lines belong to the gyro instrument system, not the pitot-static system.", "src": "OEG 2H-1 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-002", "t": "instr", "q": "Which flight instruments are connected to the pitot-static system?", "a": "Airspeed indicator, altimeter, and vertical speed indicator", "d": ["Airspeed indicator, attitude indicator, and altimeter", "Altimeter, heading indicator, and vertical speed indicator", "Airspeed indicator, turn coordinator, and altimeter"], "why": "The airspeed indicator, altimeter and VSI are pressure instruments. The attitude indicator, heading indicator and turn coordinator are gyroscopic and run on vacuum/pressure or electric power.", "src": "OEG 2H-2 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-003", "t": "instr", "q": "The static port becomes completely blocked while the pitot tube stays clear. Which instruments are affected?", "a": "Altimeter, vertical speed indicator, and airspeed indicator", "d": ["Altimeter and vertical speed indicator, but not airspeed", "Airspeed indicator only; altimeter and VSI read normally", "Altimeter only; the VSI and airspeed indicator read normally"], "why": "All three pitot-static instruments use static pressure. The altimeter and VSI use only static pressure, and the airspeed indicator compares pitot pressure with static pressure, so a blocked static port corrupts it too.", "src": "OEG 2H-2, 2H-4 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-004", "t": "instr", "q": "What ice protection may be installed for the pitot tube, and where are its operating procedures found?", "a": "Electric pitot heat; use it as directed in the AFM/POH", "d": ["An alcohol spray ring; use it as directed in the AIM", "A small pneumatic boot; cycle it as directed in the AIM", "Engine bleed air; it operates automatically in icing"], "why": "Many airplanes have an electrically heated pitot tube for flight in visible moisture. The specific procedures for pitot heat come from the airplane's AFM/POH.", "src": "OEG 2H-3 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-005", "t": "instr", "q": "How does an airspeed indicator work?", "a": "A differential gauge comparing pitot pressure with static pressure", "d": ["A pressure gauge comparing pitot pressure with a sealed aneroid capsule", "A rate gauge sensing static pressure change through a calibrated leak", "A pressure gauge comparing static pressure with a sealed 29.92 inHg reference"], "why": "The airspeed indicator shows the difference between impact (pitot) pressure and undisturbed static pressure, calibrated in mph, knots, or both. A calibrated leak is how a VSI works; sealed aneroids are the altimeter's element.", "src": "OEG 2H-4 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-006", "t": "instr", "q": "Why do many airplanes sample static pressure at two locations, such as both sides of the fuselage?", "a": "So sideslip or lateral movement doesn't produce erroneous static readings", "d": ["So one port can act as the alternate static source if the other ices", "To double static pressure and make the airspeed indicator more sensitive", "So the altimeter and VSI each have their own dedicated port"], "why": "Static ports may be on the pitot-static head, the fuselage sides, or the vertical fin. Dual locations cancel out the pressure errors that lateral movement of the airplane would otherwise cause. The alternate static source is a separate, pilot-selected source.", "src": "OEG 2H-5 · FAA-H-8083-15"},
{"id": "o5-007", "t": "instr", "q": "In most non-pressurized airplanes, where does the alternate static source take its air from?", "a": "From inside the cabin of the airplane", "d": ["From an unpressurized baggage area", "From a flush port on the vertical fin", "From the pitot tube's drain hole"], "why": "Most non-pressurized airplanes plumb the alternate static source to the cabin. A non-pressurized baggage compartment is the usual location in pressurized airplanes.", "src": "OEG 2H-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-008", "t": "instr", "q": "In a pressurized airplane, where is the alternate static source usually plumbed?", "a": "To a non-pressurized baggage compartment", "d": ["To the pressurized cabin behind the panel", "To a port beside the cabin outflow valve", "To the engine induction air plenum"], "why": "Pressurized cabin air is above outside pressure, so it would give false static readings. If an airplane's alternate source is inside the pressurized cabin, you may have to depressurize (and descend to a safe unpressurized altitude) to get correct indications.", "src": "OEG 2H-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-009", "t": "instr", "q": "In IMC the static port ices over and the altimeter stops moving. How is the alternate static source normally put into use?", "a": "The pilot opens an alternate static valve in the flight deck", "d": ["It opens automatically when static pressure stops changing", "It activates whenever the pitot heat switch is turned on", "A mechanic must connect it; it can't be selected in flight"], "why": "The alternate static source is a manual selection: the pilot opens a valve or fitting in the flight deck. Pitot heat protects the pitot tube (and heated static ports where fitted) but does not select alternate static.", "src": "OEG 2H-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-010", "t": "instr", "q": "Unpressurized airplane in level flight: you select the cabin alternate static source. What indications should you typically expect?", "a": "Altimeter and airspeed read slightly high; VSI shows a momentary climb", "d": ["Altimeter and airspeed read slightly low; VSI shows a momentary descent", "Altimeter reads high and airspeed reads low; VSI stays at zero", "No change, because cabin pressure equals outside static pressure"], "why": "Cabin static pressure is usually a little lower than outside static pressure. The altimeter reads that as higher altitude, the airspeed indicator sees a bigger pitot-static difference, and the VSI briefly shows a climb. Check the POH for your airplane's corrections.", "src": "OEG 2H-7 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-011", "t": "instr", "q": "Why does a cabin-vented alternate static source usually make the altimeter read higher than actual?", "a": "Cabin pressure is usually lower than the outside static pressure", "d": ["Cabin pressure is usually higher than the outside static pressure", "Warm cabin air expands the altimeter's aneroid capsules", "The alternate line contains a restrictor that delays readings"], "why": "Airflow around the fuselage tends to lower the pressure inside an unpressurized cabin. The altimeter interprets lower pressure as greater altitude, and the same effect makes airspeed read high.", "src": "OEG 2H-7 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-012", "t": "instr", "q": "What is the sensing element in a sensitive altimeter?", "a": "A stack of evacuated, corrugated bronze aneroid capsules", "d": ["A single diaphragm fed with pitot pressure", "A diaphragm vented to the case through a calibrated leak", "A gyro rotor that precesses as static pressure changes"], "why": "Outside air pressure squeezes the sealed (evacuated) capsules against their own springiness, so their thickness changes with pressure. Stacking several capsules increases the movement over the instrument's range.", "src": "OEG 2H-8 · FAA-H-8083-15"},
{"id": "o5-013", "t": "instr", "q": "What does a sensitive altimeter actually measure?", "a": "Absolute air pressure, shown as height above the set pressure level", "d": ["Pitot minus static pressure, displayed as feet above sea level", "The rate of static pressure change, totaled over time to give height", "Air density, corrected for temperature by the altimeter setting"], "why": "The altimeter is an aneroid barometer. It measures absolute static pressure and displays it as height above the pressure level set in the altimeter setting window.", "src": "OEG 2H-8 · FAA-H-8083-15"},
{"id": "o5-014", "t": "instr", "q": "How is a vertical speed indicator plumbed to sense climbs and descents?", "a": "Diaphragm gets static directly; case gets static via a calibrated leak", "d": ["Diaphragm gets pitot pressure; case gets static pressure directly", "Diaphragm gets static via a calibrated leak; case is vented to the cabin", "Diaphragm is sealed and evacuated; case gets static pressure directly"], "why": "When altitude changes, pressure inside the diaphragm changes at once while case pressure lags behind through the restricted orifice. The VSI displays that pressure difference as a rate of climb or descent.", "src": "OEG 2H-9 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-015", "t": "instr", "q": "Which flight instruments have most commonly been gyroscopically driven?", "a": "Attitude and heading indicators, and some turn coordinators", "d": ["Attitude indicator, altimeter, and vertical speed indicator", "Heading indicator, airspeed indicator, and magnetic compass", "Turn coordinator, vertical speed indicator, and altimeter"], "why": "Attitude and heading indicators are the classic gyro instruments, and some turn coordinators are gyroscopic too. Many modern airplanes no longer use vacuum gyros, so know which system your airplane has.", "src": "OEG 2H-10 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-016", "t": "instr", "q": "Why are gyroscopic instruments usually powered by separate electrical and pneumatic sources?", "a": "So no single failure can leave the pilot unable to finish the flight", "d": ["Because electric gyros spin too slowly for an attitude indicator", "Because regulations prohibit vacuum power for the turn coordinator", "So the alternator carries less load during instrument flight"], "why": "Gyro instruments are crucial for instrument flight, so manufacturers build in redundancy. Splitting the power sources means one failure still leaves usable gyro information to conclude the flight safely.", "src": "OEG 2H-11 · FAA-H-8083-15"},
{"id": "o5-017", "t": "instr", "q": "Typical light twin: vacuum-driven attitude and heading indicators, electric turn coordinator. All vacuum is lost in IMC. Which gyro instrument stays reliable?", "a": "The turn coordinator", "d": ["The attitude indicator", "The heading indicator", "The standby vacuum attitude indicator"], "why": "In the usual arrangement the vacuum system powers the attitude and heading indicators while the electrical system powers the turn coordinator. A total vacuum loss leaves the electric turn coordinator as the working gyro.", "src": "OEG 2H-11 · FAA-H-8083-15"},
{"id": "o5-018", "t": "instr", "q": "How does a vacuum system spin the gyro inside an instrument?", "a": "Suction pulls air across vanes on the rotor, like a waterwheel", "d": ["A flexible shaft from the engine accessory case turns the rotor", "Engine oil pressure is forced through a small turbine on the rotor", "A small electric motor in the case spins the rotor directly"], "why": "Suction draws air across the rotor's vanes so it spins very fast, the way water turns a waterwheel. A common vacuum source is a vane-type pump driven by the engine and mounted on its accessory case.", "src": "OEG 2H-12 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-019", "t": "instr", "q": "What vacuum (suction) range is usually required for vacuum-driven gyro instruments?", "a": "About 4.5 to 5.5 inHg", "d": ["About 1.5 to 2.5 inHg", "About 8.0 to 10.0 inHg", "About 15 to 20 inHg"], "why": "Installations differ, but the typical requirement is roughly 4.5 to 5.5 inHg of suction. Use the operating range in your own airplane's POH.", "src": "OEG 2H-12 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-020", "t": "instr", "q": "How is a vacuum pump failure typically shown in the flight deck?", "a": "Low reading on the suction gauge; some airplanes add a warning light", "d": ["A red X on the attitude indicator; some airplanes add a warning chime", "An OFF flag on the turn coordinator; some airplanes add a warning light", "Lower manifold pressure on the engine that drives the failed pump"], "why": "A vacuum (suction) gauge marked with the normal range is generally provided, and some airplanes also have a light that comes on when vacuum drops below the acceptable level. A red X is how electronic displays flag a failed attitude source.", "src": "OEG 2H-13 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-021", "t": "instr", "q": "In a pressure-type (rather than vacuum) gyro system, what does the gyro pressure gauge measure?", "a": "The pressure drop across the instruments", "d": ["Pump discharge pressure upstream of the regulator", "The difference between cabin and outside pressure", "Suction at the dry air pump inlet filter"], "why": "In a pressure system, filtered and regulated air is pushed through the instruments to spin the gyros, then exhausted from the cases. The gauge reads the pressure drop across the instruments, not raw pump output.", "src": "OEG 2H-14 · FAA-H-8083-15"},
{"id": "o5-022", "t": "instr", "q": "In a typical twin's pressure gyro system, which sequence describes the airflow?", "a": "Pumps, regulator, inline filters, manifold check valve, instruments", "d": ["Pumps, instruments, regulator, manifold check valve, inline filters", "Regulator, pumps, manifold check valve, inline filters, instruments", "Pumps, manifold check valve, instruments, inline filters, regulator"], "why": "Two dry air pumps (with inlet filters) feed a regulator that bleeds off excess air to hold the desired pressure. The air then passes inline filters and a manifold check valve before driving the gyros and exhausting from the cases.", "src": "OEG 2H-14 · FAA-H-8083-15"},
{"id": "o5-023", "t": "instr", "q": "Typical twin with a vacuum/pressure pump on each engine: the right engine fails and is feathered. What must you do to keep the gyros powered?", "a": "Nothing; a check valve isolates that side and the left pump takes over", "d": ["Move the vacuum selector valve to the left pump position at once", "Turn on the standby electric vacuum pump to replace the right pump", "Let the right engine windmill unfeathered so its pump keeps turning"], "why": "Normally, if either engine or either pump fails, a check valve automatically isolates the inoperative side and the instruments run on air from the operating system. No manual pump selection is needed.", "src": "OEG 2H-15 · FAA-H-8083-15"},
{"id": "o5-024", "t": "env", "q": "In a typical light twin, how does ventilation air get into the cabin?", "a": "Through ram-air ducts and skin vents, via the heating ducts", "d": ["Through bleed air taken from each engine's turbocharger outlet", "Through the cowl flaps, then ducted from the engine compartment", "Through dedicated ducts kept separate from the heating system"], "why": "Ram-air inlets in the leading, lower or upper surfaces (or other skin vents) admit fresh air, which usually travels through the same ducts used for heating and cooling. Some airplanes add recirculating fans or blowers.", "src": "OEG 2I-1 · FAA-H-8083-31"},
{"id": "o5-025", "t": "env", "q": "In a typical combustion heater, how is the ventilating air heated?", "a": "It enters at the burner-head end and passes over hot radiator surfaces", "d": ["It passes through the combustion chamber and mixes with the exhaust", "It flows through a shroud around the engine exhaust muffler", "It is compressed by the turbocharger, which raises its temperature"], "why": "Ram or blower air flows over the heater's heated radiator surfaces and then through a plenum into the distribution ducts. The exhaust-shroud heater is the simpler design found in light single-engine trainers.", "src": "OEG 2I-2 · FAA-H-8083-31"},
{"id": "o5-026", "t": "env", "q": "What should a pilot flying an airplane with a fuel-fired cabin heater understand about it?", "a": "Its failure modes, overheat/fire behavior, and fuel shutoff", "d": ["Its heat output rating, part number, and overhaul interval", "Its thermostat settings, blower switch, and cabin comfort range", "Its failure modes and how to reset its overheat switch in flight"], "why": "Fuel-fired heaters are more complex than shroud heaters and usually have overtemp protection. Know how the heater is fired, how it fails, what happens in an overheat or fire, and how to shut off its fuel. The overheat switch is not reachable in flight.", "src": "OEG 2I-2 · FAA-H-8083-31"},
{"id": "o5-027", "t": "env", "q": "With a typical combustion heater, what does the cabin temperature control knob adjust?", "a": "A thermostat sensing duct air temperature aft of the heater", "d": ["A valve metering fuel flow continuously to the burner", "The speed of the blower that feeds ventilating air to the heater", "The trip temperature of the heater's overheat switch"], "why": "The knob sets a thermostat that monitors duct air downstream (aft) of the heater, so the heater cycles off when the air gets warmer than the setting and back on when it cools. The overheat switch is a separate safety device, not a comfort control.", "src": "OEG 2I-3 · FAA-H-8083-31"},
{"id": "o5-028", "t": "env", "q": "With the cabin heat thermostat set, the duct air temperature rises above the setting. What normally happens?", "a": "The thermostat shuts the heater down until the air cools to the setting", "d": ["The overheat switch trips and must be inspected and reset on the ground", "Fuel flow increases to stabilize the combustion chamber temperature", "The ventilation blower stops to reduce airflow through the heater"], "why": "This is normal thermostat cycling, not an overheat. The overheat (thermal) switch is a separate protection against dangerous temperatures and, once tripped, needs inspection and a reset.", "src": "OEG 2I-3 · FAA-H-8083-31"},
{"id": "o5-029", "t": "env", "q": "What is a combustion heater?", "a": "A small gasoline-burning furnace that heats cabin air", "d": ["An exhaust shroud that heats ram air around the muffler", "An electric resistance heater powered from the main bus", "A heat exchanger that uses hot engine oil to warm the cabin"], "why": "It is essentially a small gasoline-fired furnace that warms air for the cabin and for windshield defogging. Most are thermostat-controlled and carry their own hour meter for maintenance tracking.", "src": "OEG 2I-4 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-030", "t": "env", "q": "Why do most combustion heaters have their own hour meter?", "a": "To record heater time in service for maintenance", "d": ["To compute heater fuel burn for the fuel log", "To limit each heater run to one hour", "To record overheat events for the mechanic"], "why": "A separate meter records how long the heater itself has run, so heater maintenance can be based on its own time in service rather than engine or airframe time.", "src": "OEG 2I-4 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-031", "t": "env", "q": "Planning a 3-hour winter flight in a twin with a typical combustion heater running the whole way: about how much fuel will the heater use, and from where?", "a": "About 1.5 to 3 gallons, drawn from the airplane's own fuel system", "d": ["None; the heater has its own separate fuel reservoir", "About 9 to 12 gallons, drawn from the airplane's own fuel system", "About 1.5 to 3 gallons, drawn from a separate heater tank"], "why": "Heaters usually burn about 0.5 to 1 gal/hr, supplied from the engines' fuel system, typically from one specific tank by gravity or a fuel pump. Check the POH for your airplane's tank and rate, and include it in fuel planning.", "src": "OEG 2I-5 · FAA-H-8083-31"},
{"id": "o5-032", "t": "env", "q": "What cool-down does a typical combustion heater need after it is turned off?", "a": "15 seconds of outside airflow in flight; 2 minutes of fan on the ground", "d": ["2 minutes of outside airflow in flight; 15 seconds of fan on the ground", "5 minutes of outside airflow in flight; 10 minutes of fan on the ground", "No cool-down in flight; 30 seconds of fan on the ground"], "why": "Typical heaters need at least 15 seconds of outside airflow through the unit in flight, or at least 2 minutes of ventilation fan on the ground. Skipping the cool-down usually trips the thermal (overheat) switch.", "src": "OEG 2I-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-033", "t": "env", "q": "After landing, you switch off the combustion heater and immediately shut down without running the ventilation fan. What is the likely result?", "a": "The thermal switch trips and the heater stays off until it is reset", "d": ["Nothing; the cool-down requirement applies only to in-flight shutdowns", "The heater runs its blower automatically at the next engine start", "The thermostat resets to its lowest setting for the next flight"], "why": "On the ground the fan should run about 2 minutes after shutoff. Without that cool-down, residual heat usually trips the thermal switch, and the heater stays inoperative until the switch is reset.", "src": "OEG 2I-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-034", "t": "env", "q": "Which conditions cause a combustion heater's automatic controls to cut off its fuel?", "a": "Too little combustion or ventilating air, or (on some) ignition failure", "d": ["Cabin temperature below the setting or a thermostat set to maximum", "Outside air below freezing or visible moisture at the ram-air inlet", "Engine oil temperature below the green arc on either engine"], "why": "Automatic controls stop fuel flow when there isn't enough combustion air or ventilating air, and on some heaters when the ignition isn't working. Other controls limit how fast the chamber heats and cap the maximum output temperature.", "src": "OEG 2I-7 · FAA-H-8083-31"},
{"id": "o5-035", "t": "env", "q": "In flight, the combustion heater quits and won't restart; you suspect its overheat (thermal) switch tripped. What should you do?", "a": "Leave it off; the switch is on the unit and needs ground inspection", "d": ["Reset the overheat switch from the cockpit, then restart the heater", "Cycle the heater switch repeatedly until the heater relights", "Set the thermostat to maximum to override the tripped switch"], "why": "The thermal switch sits on the heater itself, out of reach in flight, and a pilot or mechanic has to inspect the unit for heat damage before it is reset.", "src": "OEG 2I-7 · FAA-H-8083-31"},
{"id": "o5-036", "t": "env", "q": "Why is a thorough preflight of the nose baggage compartment important in many twins?", "a": "Bags, latches, and systems there (heater, oxygen) must be secure", "d": ["It holds the static system drains, which must be checked for water", "Bags there don't affect CG, so only the latches need checking", "Its door is the emergency exit, so it must be left unlatched"], "why": "Baggage must be secured and within limits, latches fastened so the door can't open in flight, and in many airplanes the heater or oxygen bottles are reached through this compartment. Loose bags can damage or interfere with those systems.", "src": "OEG 2I-8 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-037", "t": "env", "q": "A rental twin has deice boots, heated props, and a heated windshield. May you fly it into known icing?", "a": "Only if the AFM/POH and placards approve it for known icing", "d": ["Yes; installed ice protection means it is approved for known icing", "Yes, as long as every ice protection system works on the preflight", "Only below 10,000 feet MSL with all ice protection switched on"], "why": "Ice protection equipment, however complete it looks, doesn't by itself mean the airplane is certificated for icing. The AFM/POH, placards and manufacturer determine approval; an airplane certificated for it is FIKI-approved.", "src": "OEG 2J-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-038", "t": "env", "q": "What is the difference between anti-icing and deicing equipment?", "a": "Anti-icing keeps ice from forming; deicing removes ice already formed", "d": ["Anti-icing removes ice already formed; deicing keeps ice from forming", "Anti-icing protects only the engines; deicing protects the airframe", "Both remove ice, but anti-icing uses fluid and deicing uses heat"], "why": "Anti-icing equipment keeps ice off protected surfaces to begin with (pitot heat, prop heat). Deicing equipment, such as pneumatic boots, sheds ice that has already accumulated.", "src": "OEG 2J-2, 2J-4 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-039", "t": "env", "q": "Which group consists entirely of anti-icing equipment?", "a": "Pitot heat, heated static ports, prop alcohol slingers, windshield heat", "d": ["Pitot heat, wing deice boots, tail deice boots, windshield heat", "Wing deice boots, tail deice boots, prop heat, alternate air door", "Pitot heat, wing deice boots, alternate static, prop alcohol slingers"], "why": "Typical anti-icing items are heated pitot tubes, heated or non-icing static ports and fuel vents, prop electrothermal boots or alcohol slingers, windshield alcohol or electric heat, defoggers, and heated stall warning lift detectors. Pneumatic wing and tail boots are deicing equipment.", "src": "OEG 2J-2 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-040", "t": "env", "q": "No POH/AFM guidance says otherwise. You're about to climb into visible moisture near 0°C in a twin with pitot heat and prop heat. When should you turn them on?", "a": "Before entering the suspected icing conditions", "d": ["When ice first appears on the windshield", "Once about 1/4 inch of ice has built up", "When the airspeed begins to decay in the climb"], "why": "Anti-icing equipment works by stopping ice before it forms, so it is most effective switched on before you fly into suspected icing (visible moisture near freezing), unless the POH/AFM says otherwise. Waiting for ice to appear defeats its purpose.", "src": "OEG 2J-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-042", "t": "env", "q": "On a typical deice-equipped twin, which areas does airframe surface deicing equipment (boots or fluid) commonly cover?", "a": "Wing leading edges and vertical and horizontal tail leading edges", "d": ["Wing leading edges only; the tail surfaces are left unprotected", "The pitot tube, static ports, and stall warning lift detector", "Engine induction inlets, fuel tank vents, and antennas"], "why": "Surface deicing commonly protects the leading edges of both the wing and the tail (vertical and horizontal), usually with inflatable boots or alcohol-based fluid and occasionally with heated surfaces. Pitot tubes, static ports and stall warning detectors are protected by anti-icing heat instead.", "src": "OEG 2J-4 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-043", "t": "env", "q": "How do pneumatic deice boots operate?", "a": "They inflate with pump air to break ice off, then deflate with vacuum", "d": ["They stay inflated continuously so ice cannot bond to the surface", "They heat electrically to melt the ice as soon as it starts to form", "They inflate with hydraulic fluid, then drain back to the reservoir"], "why": "When the pilot activates them, air from the pneumatic pumps inflates the boots for a few seconds and cracks off the ice; vacuum then pulls them back flat. The pilot watches the ice and cycles them as the POH/AFM directs.", "src": "OEG 2J-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-044", "t": "env", "q": "Why shouldn't pneumatic deice boots be thought of as anti-icing equipment?", "a": "They're built to shed ice after it forms, not keep it from forming", "d": ["They're built to melt ice with heat, not keep it from forming", "They're built as backups that work only if anti-icing fails", "They're built to protect the propellers, not the wings or tail"], "why": "Pneumatic boots crack off ice that has already accumulated on the wing and tail leading edges; they don't use heat and don't prevent ice from forming. The pilot watches the buildup and cycles them as the POH/AFM directs.", "src": "OEG 2J-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-045", "t": "env", "q": "Besides surface ice protection, what other equipment is typically required for flight in icing conditions?", "a": "Alternate induction air, alternate static, ice-tolerant antennas", "d": ["A combustion heater, a supplemental oxygen system, and a yaw damper", "An autopilot with altitude hold, a radar altimeter, and an HSI", "A second alternator, a propeller synchrophaser, and windshield wipers"], "why": "Ice can block the normal induction and static sources and damage antennas, so icing-capable airplanes carry an alternate induction air source, an alternate static source, and ice-tolerant antennas.", "src": "OEG 2J-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-046", "t": "env", "q": "In level cruise in IMC near freezing (constant-speed props), manifold pressure slowly drops with throttle and RPM unchanged. Likely cause and action?", "a": "Induction icing; select alternate air (or carb heat if carbureted)", "d": ["Propeller icing; increase RPM to sling the ice off the blades", "Static port icing; open the alternate static source valve", "Fuel vent icing; switch to the opposite tank and boost pump on"], "why": "With a constant-speed prop the governor holds RPM, so ice blocking the normal induction source first shows up as lost manifold pressure. Select alternate air on a fuel-injected engine (carb heat if carbureted); some injected engines switch automatically.", "src": "OEG 2J-7 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-047", "t": "env", "q": "With a fixed-pitch propeller, what is the first indication of ice blocking the normal induction air source?", "a": "A loss of engine RPM", "d": ["A rise in engine RPM", "A rise in oil temperature", "A drop in oil pressure"], "why": "With a fixed-pitch prop, restricted induction airflow reduces power and RPM falls. With a constant-speed prop the governor holds RPM, so the loss appears as lower manifold pressure instead.", "src": "OEG 2J-7 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-048", "t": "env", "q": "How is a static port protected from icing on airplanes equipped for it?", "a": "Electric heating elements around the port", "d": ["Alcohol spray nozzles just ahead of the port", "A small pneumatic boot surrounding the port", "Warm cabin air ducted behind the port"], "why": "Some airplanes have electric heater elements around the static ports so ice can't cover them. Not all do, particularly where the port's location makes icing unlikely, so know whether yours is heated.", "src": "OEG 2J-8, 2H-5 · FAA-H-8083-15"},
{"id": "o5-049", "t": "env", "q": "You're flying with the autopilot engaged where icing is possible, and the POH gives no guidance on autopilot use in ice. What is recommended?", "a": "Hand-fly; the autopilot can hide the trim changes ice causes", "d": ["Keep it engaged; it handles the extra ice drag better than a pilot", "Use altitude hold only, since it prevents an ice-induced stall", "Keep it engaged but turn off the boots to reduce servo loads"], "why": "Unless the POH/AFM recommends otherwise, the autopilot shouldn't be used in icing. It quietly trims out the changes ice causes, so the pilot may not notice ice building to a dangerous level.", "src": "OEG 2J-9 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o5-050", "t": "env", "q": "Unpressurized twin cruising at a cabin altitude of 13,500 ft for 50 minutes. What does 14 CFR 91.211 require?", "a": "Required flight crew must use oxygen for the time beyond 30 minutes", "d": ["Required flight crew must use oxygen for the entire 50 minutes", "Every occupant must be provided oxygen for the entire 50 minutes", "Nothing; oxygen is not required until above 14,000 feet"], "why": "Above 12,500 ft up to 14,000 ft cabin altitude, the required crew must use oxygen for any part of the flight at those altitudes lasting more than 30 minutes. Continuous crew use starts above 14,000 ft; all occupants must be provided oxygen above 15,000 ft.", "src": "OEG 2K-1 · 14 CFR 91.211"},
{"id": "o5-051", "t": "env", "q": "Unpressurized twin with passengers, cruising at a cabin altitude of 14,500 ft. What oxygen is required?", "a": "Crew must use it the entire time; passengers need not be provided it", "d": ["Crew must use it after 30 minutes; passengers need not be provided it", "Crew and passengers must all be provided it the entire time", "Crew must use it after 30 minutes; passengers must be provided it"], "why": "Above 14,000 ft cabin altitude the required crew must use oxygen the whole time they are at that altitude. The requirement to provide oxygen to every occupant begins only above 15,000 ft.", "src": "OEG 2K-1 · 14 CFR 91.211"},
{"id": "o5-052", "t": "env", "q": "Above what cabin pressure altitude must every occupant be provided supplemental oxygen?", "a": "15,000 feet", "d": ["12,500 feet", "14,000 feet", "18,000 feet"], "why": "Above 15,000 ft cabin altitude each occupant must be provided oxygen. The 12,500 ft (after 30 minutes) and 14,000 ft (continuous) thresholds apply to the required flight crew.", "src": "OEG 2K-1 · 14 CFR 91.211"},
{"id": "o5-053", "t": "env", "q": "What are the three basic components of an aircraft oxygen system?", "a": "Storage containers, a delivery system, and a mask or cannula", "d": ["Storage containers, a pressure gauge, and a rebreather bag", "A regulator, a flow indicator, and a demand valve", "A compressor, a storage tank, and a diluter valve"], "why": "Whether portable or installed, most systems consist of storage (containers), a delivery system, and the mask or nasal cannula the user breathes from.", "src": "OEG 2K-2 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-054", "t": "env", "q": "Oxygen systems are often classified by the type of regulator used. Which list names those types?", "a": "Diluter-demand, pressure-demand, continuous-flow, electrical pulse-demand", "d": ["Diluter-demand, pressure-demand, continuous-flow, molecular sieve generator", "Gaseous ABO, liquid LOX, sodium chlorate candle, molecular sieve", "Continuous-flow, rebreather-bag, chemical candle, electrical pulse-demand"], "why": "The regulator-based types are diluter-demand, pressure-demand, continuous-flow and electrical pulse-demand. Gaseous, liquid, chemical candles and molecular sieve generators are storage methods, not regulator types.", "src": "OEG 2K-3 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-055", "t": "env", "q": "At about what pressure is aircraft gaseous oxygen usually stored?", "a": "1,800 to 2,200 psi", "d": ["400 to 600 psi", "50 to 100 psi", "5,000 to 6,000 psi"], "why": "Aircraft oxygen is usually kept in high-pressure containers at 1,800 to 2,200 psi. Portable equipment typically consists of the container, a regulator, a mask outlet and a pressure gauge.", "src": "OEG 2K-4 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-056", "t": "env", "q": "How does a diluter-demand oxygen system work, and to about what altitude can it be used safely?", "a": "Mixes oxygen with air to hold partial pressure; safe to about 40,000 ft", "d": ["Supplies oxygen under positive pressure; safe above 40,000 ft", "Flows pure oxygen steadily into a rebreather bag; safe to 25,000 ft", "Pulses oxygen only on inhalation; safe to about 18,000 ft"], "why": "A diluter-demand regulator mixes oxygen with air to keep oxygen partial pressure constant as altitude changes, using a tight-sealing demand mask. It is safe up to 40,000 ft; positive-pressure delivery is the pressure-demand system.", "src": "OEG 2K-5 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-057", "t": "env", "q": "What distinguishes a pressure-demand oxygen system from a diluter-demand system?", "a": "It delivers oxygen under positive pressure above about 34,000 ft cabin altitude", "d": ["It mixes more cabin air with oxygen above about 34,000 ft cabin altitude", "It delivers oxygen only during inhalation, below about 34,000 ft cabin altitude", "It needs no tight mask seal, since oxygen flow is continuous at all altitudes"], "why": "Above about 34,000 ft cabin altitude the regulator supplies oxygen to the mask under pressure, which pressurizes the user's lungs. That positive pressure is what makes pressure-demand systems safe above 40,000 ft.", "src": "OEG 2K-6 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-058", "t": "env", "q": "How does a continuous-flow oxygen system with a rebreather bag work, and where is it mainly used?", "a": "Constant flow into a bag that mixes it with exhaled gas; airline passengers", "d": ["Oxygen delivered only during inhalation; flight crews of light twins", "Oxygen delivered under positive pressure to the mask; military aircrews", "Oxygen diluted with cabin air on each demand; airline flight crews"], "why": "A continuous-flow system supplies a constant flow of pure oxygen into a rebreather bag, where it mixes with exhaled gases to give a healthy oxygen/air mix at the mask. It is used mainly in airline passenger cabins.", "src": "OEG 2K-7 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-059", "t": "env", "q": "Why does an electrical pulse-demand oxygen system use less oxygen than a continuous-flow system?", "a": "It senses inhalation and delivers oxygen only early in each breath", "d": ["It recirculates exhaled oxygen through a rebreather bag on the mask", "It mixes a larger share of cabin air into each breath as you climb", "It stores the oxygen as a liquid, so each cylinder holds much more"], "why": "The unit detects the start of each inhalation and releases oxygen then, so nothing is wasted during the rest of the breathing cycle.", "src": "OEG 2K-8 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-060", "t": "env", "q": "How do most electrical pulse-demand units compensate for climbing to a higher altitude?", "a": "An internal barometer increases the oxygen delivered per pulse", "d": ["The pilot manually resets a flow meter for each new altitude", "The unit switches to continuous flow above 10,000 feet", "A cabin altitude switch opens a second oxygen cylinder"], "why": "Most pulse-demand systems have a built-in barometer that automatically increases the amount of oxygen in each pulse as altitude increases.", "src": "OEG 2K-8 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-061", "t": "env", "q": "Which oxygen storage method is a solid-state (chemical) source?", "a": "Sodium chlorate oxygen candles", "d": ["Liquid aviator's breathing oxygen (LOX)", "Gaseous aviator's breathing oxygen (ABO)", "Molecular sieve oxygen generators (MSOG)"], "why": "The four storage types are gaseous ABO, liquid LOX, sodium chlorate candles (solid-state oxygen) and molecular sieve oxygen generators.", "src": "OEG 2K-9 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-062", "t": "env", "q": "In the PRICE oxygen system check, what do the letters stand for?", "a": "Pressure, Regulator, Indicator, Connections, Emergency", "d": ["Pressure, Reservoir, Inspection, Cannula, Endurance", "Purity, Regulator, Indicator, Cylinder, Emergency", "Pressure, Regulator, Inflow, Connections, Exhaust"], "why": "PRICE: enough Pressure and quantity for the flight; Regulator working (with compatible fittings for continuous flow); flow Indicator shows steady flow; Connections secure; Emergency readiness and passenger briefing.", "src": "OEG 2K-10 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-063", "t": "env", "q": "During a PRICE check you put on the mask and look for a steady flow of oxygen. Which step is this?", "a": "Indicator", "d": ["Pressure", "Regulator", "Connections"], "why": "The Indicator step uses the flow indicator, on the regulator or in the delivery tube, to confirm oxygen is flowing. Pressure checks quantity, Regulator checks function and compatibility, and Connections checks lines, couplings and mask.", "src": "OEG 2K-10 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-064", "t": "env", "q": "What does the Emergency step of the PRICE oxygen check involve?", "a": "Having oxygen ready for emergencies and briefing passengers on it", "d": ["Carrying a spare oxygen bottle and mask reserved for emergencies", "Checking the emergency locator transmitter before using oxygen aloft", "Planning an emergency descent route and altitude before takeoff"], "why": "Oxygen equipment should be ready for hypoxia, decompression sickness, smoke and fumes, or a rapid decompression, and passengers should be briefed on where it is and how to use it.", "src": "OEG 2K-10 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-065", "t": "env", "q": "In a typical pressurized airplane, which areas form the sealed, pressurized unit?", "a": "The cabin, flight compartment, and baggage compartments", "d": ["The cabin and flight compartment, plus the wheel wells", "The flight compartment only; the cabin is just ventilated", "The cabin, flight compartment, and engine nacelles"], "why": "A typical system seals the cabin, flight compartment and baggage compartments into one unit that can hold air at a higher pressure than outside.", "src": "OEG 2L-1 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-066", "t": "env", "q": "How is the cabin commonly pressurized in a piston-engine pressurized airplane?", "a": "Air from each engine's turbocharger, via a sonic venturi (flow limiter)", "d": ["Compressor bleed air from each engine, through a flow control valve", "A cabin supercharger driven off the engine vacuum pump drive pad", "Ram air trapped by closing the outflow valve, raising cabin pressure"], "why": "Piston airplanes may take air from each engine's turbocharger through a sonic venturi that limits flow. Turbine airplanes use compressor bleed air, and some older turbine models used superchargers.", "src": "OEG 2L-2 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-067", "t": "env", "q": "A typical pressurization system holds about what cabin altitude at the airplane's maximum design cruising altitude?", "a": "About 8,000 feet", "d": ["About 5,000 feet", "About 10,000 feet", "About 12,500 feet"], "why": "Roughly 8,000 ft cabin altitude at the maximum design cruising altitude is typical. That keeps cabin altitude from changing abruptly while still letting air flow through the cabin at a reasonable rate.", "src": "OEG 2L-3 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-068", "t": "env", "q": "Below your maximum design cruising altitude, the cabin altitude climbs well above 8,000 ft. What does this most likely indicate?", "a": "A leak, or the system isn't generating enough pressure", "d": ["Normal operation; cabin altitude always tracks airplane altitude", "Warmer-than-standard outside air reducing air density", "The alternate static source has been left open"], "why": "A system that can't hold about 8,000 ft or lower at or below its maximum design cruising altitude most likely has a leak or isn't producing enough pressure.", "src": "OEG 2L-3 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-069", "t": "env", "q": "In a pressurized airplane, what does the term cabin pressure (cabin altitude) mean?", "a": "Cabin pressure stated as an equivalent altitude above sea level", "d": ["The airplane's indicated altitude while the cabin is pressurized", "The difference between cabin and outside pressure, stated in feet", "The highest altitude at which the cabin can stay pressurized"], "why": "Cabin altitude states the pressure inside the cabin as the altitude that has that same pressure. The pressure difference between cabin and outside is the differential pressure.", "src": "OEG 2L-4 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-070", "t": "env", "q": "What is differential pressure in a pressurized airplane?", "a": "The difference between cabin pressure and outside air pressure", "d": ["The difference between pitot pressure and static air pressure", "The difference between bleed air pressure and cabin pressure", "The difference between the two engines' turbocharger outputs"], "why": "In general it is the pressure difference across a wall; in pressurization systems it is cabin pressure compared with outside atmospheric pressure.", "src": "OEG 2L-5 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-071", "t": "env", "q": "Your pressurized twin loses pressurization at FL240. What is the primary danger?", "a": "Hypoxia", "d": ["Carbon monoxide poisoning", "Spatial disorientation", "Hyperventilation"], "why": "Decompression at altitude exposes everyone to very low pressure, so hypoxia is the main threat and oxygen must be used quickly to avoid unconsciousness. Evolved gas decompression sickness is a further risk.", "src": "OEG 2L-6 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-072", "t": "env", "q": "Besides hypoxia, what other hazard can occupants face in a high-altitude decompression?", "a": "Evolved gas decompression sickness", "d": ["Carbon monoxide poisoning", "Spatial disorientation", "Motion sickness from turbulence"], "why": "A rapid loss of cabin pressure can let dissolved gas come out of solution in the body (evolved gas decompression sickness), in addition to the hypoxia threat.", "src": "OEG 2L-6 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-073", "t": "env", "q": "At FL250 in a pressurized piston twin, the cabin rapidly depressurizes. What are the best immediate actions?", "a": "Get on oxygen and begin an emergency descent, terrain permitting", "d": ["Begin a normal descent; use oxygen only if symptoms appear", "Descend at 500 fpm to protect ears and sinuses; don oxygen if needed", "Hold altitude, troubleshoot, then use oxygen if the cabin stays high"], "why": "Using oxygen promptly keeps everyone conscious, and an emergency descent (if terrain allows) brings the airplane down to where hypoxia stops being a threat. Follow the AFM/POH for the airplane's own procedure.", "src": "OEG 2L-6, 2L-7 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-074", "t": "env", "q": "What does portable oxygen equipment usually consist of?", "a": "A container, regulator, mask outlet, and pressure gauge", "d": ["A container, rebreather bag, demand valve, and flow meter", "A compressor, storage tank, diluter valve, and mask", "A container, chlorate candle, filter, and nasal cannula"], "why": "A typical portable setup is a container, a regulator, a mask outlet and a pressure gauge. The oxygen itself is normally held in high-pressure containers at about 1,800 to 2,200 psi.", "src": "OEG 2K-4 · FAA-H-8083-25"},
{"id": "o5-075", "t": "env", "q": "Why do many multi-engine airplanes use fuel-fired cabin heaters instead of the exhaust-shroud heaters common in light singles?", "a": "They provide more heating capacity for larger cabins", "d": ["They are simpler and need less maintenance than shroud heaters", "They need no overheat or fire protection systems at all", "They burn no fuel from the airplane's own fuel tanks"], "why": "Fuel-fired heaters can deliver more heat, which suits the larger cabins of twins. They are more complex than shroud heaters, usually have overtemp protection, and normally draw fuel from the airplane's own fuel system.", "src": "OEG 2I-2 · FAA-H-8083-31"},
{"id": "o6-001", "t": "aero", "q": "In a light twin whose propellers both turn clockwise as seen from the cockpit, on which side of each propeller disc is the descending blade?", "a": "On the right side of each propeller disc", "d": ["On the left side of each propeller disc", "On the outboard side of each propeller disc", "On the inboard side of each propeller disc"], "why": "Clockwise rotation viewed from behind puts the descending blade on the right of every engine. That places the right engine's descending blade outboard (long arm from the CG) and the left engine's inboard (short arm).", "src": "OEG 3A-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-002", "t": "aero", "q": "With both propellers turning clockwise as seen from the cockpit, why does P-factor make a left-engine failure produce more yaw than a right-engine failure?", "a": "The right engine's descending blade acts on a longer arm from the CG", "d": ["The left engine's descending blade acts on a longer arm from the CG", "The ascending blade produces more thrust at high angles of attack", "The right engine is rated for more horsepower than the left"], "why": "P-factor shifts each engine's effective thrust toward its descending (right-side) blade. On the right engine that point is farthest from the CG, so when the right engine is the one left running, the yawing moment is largest.", "src": "OEG 3A-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-003", "t": "aero", "q": "Under what conditions does P-factor shift a propeller's effective thrust toward the descending blade?", "a": "Under power at positive AOA, strongest at low airspeed and high AOA", "d": ["Under power at any AOA, strongest at high airspeed and low AOA", "Only when the prop is windmilling, strongest at high airspeed and low AOA", "At idle power and negative AOA, strongest in a steep descent"], "why": "At a positive angle of attack the descending blade meets the air at a larger angle and makes more thrust than the ascending blade. The effect peaks at low airspeed and high AOA, the takeoff and climb regime where an engine failure hurts most.", "src": "OEG 3A-1, 3A-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-004", "t": "aero", "q": "What is meant by the critical engine of a multi-engine airplane?", "a": "The engine whose loss most hurts performance and control", "d": ["The engine that drives the airplane's only hydraulic pump", "The engine that runs hottest during a full-power climb", "The engine that must be started first on every flight"], "why": "The critical engine is defined by how badly its loss hurts controllability and performance, especially at takeoff, climb-out and high angles of attack. The OEG ties it to the airplane's design, engine placement and propeller rotation.", "src": "OEG 3A-2 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-005", "t": "aero", "q": "You fly a twin whose propellers both turn clockwise from the cockpit. Which engine failure after liftoff would be hardest to control?", "a": "The left engine", "d": ["The right engine", "Neither; both failures are identical", "Whichever engine is on the upwind side"], "why": "All four PAST factors make losing the left engine worse: the remaining right engine's thrust and slipstream lift act on longer arms, its torque adds roll to the left, and its spiraling slipstream misses the tail.", "src": "OEG 3A-2, 3A-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-006", "t": "aero", "q": "The mnemonic PAST lists the four factors that make an engine critical. What are they?", "a": "P-factor, Accelerated slipstream, Spiraling slipstream, Torque", "d": ["P-factor, Asymmetric drag, Spiraling slipstream, Trim tab", "Propeller drag, Accelerated airflow, Sideslip angle, Torque", "Power, Asymmetric thrust, Sideslip angle, Thrust-line offset"], "why": "PAST = P-factor, Accelerated slipstream, Spiraling slipstream and Torque. In a conventional twin with both propellers turning clockwise (seen from the cockpit), each one makes loss of the left engine worse.", "src": "OEG 3A-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-007", "t": "aero", "q": "Which two PAST factors act mainly as yawing moments?", "a": "P-factor and spiraling slipstream", "d": ["Accelerated slipstream and torque", "P-factor and torque", "Accelerated slipstream and spiraling slipstream"], "why": "P-factor offsets the thrust line and spiraling slipstream pushes on the vertical tail, so both cause yaw. Accelerated slipstream (induced lift on the wing) and torque (reaction to prop rotation) cause roll.", "src": "OEG 3A-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-008", "t": "aero", "q": "Both propellers turn clockwise as seen from the cockpit. How does accelerated slipstream make the left engine critical?", "a": "Right-engine slipstream lift is centered farther outboard, giving more roll", "d": ["Right-engine slipstream misses the tail, giving more yaw", "Left-engine slipstream lift is centered farther outboard, giving more roll", "Right-engine slipstream strikes the stabilator, giving more pitch"], "why": "Because of P-factor, the prop wash and its induced lift are strongest behind the descending (right-side) blade. On the right wing that lift acts farther from the longitudinal axis, so with the left engine out the roll toward the dead engine is greater.", "src": "OEG 3A-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-009", "t": "aero", "q": "Both propellers clockwise (seen from the cockpit), RIGHT engine fails. How does spiraling slipstream affect the yaw toward the dead engine?", "a": "The left engine's slipstream hits the tail from the left and opposes it", "d": ["The left engine's slipstream hits the tail from the right and adds to it", "The left engine's slipstream misses the tail and has no effect on it", "The right engine's windmilling slipstream hits the tail and adds to it"], "why": "The left engine's spiraling slipstream wraps around the fuselage and strikes the left side of the vertical tail, yawing the nose left. In a right-engine failure that partly cancels the yaw to the right, which is one reason the right engine is not critical.", "src": "OEG 3A-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-010", "t": "aero", "q": "Both propellers clockwise (seen from the cockpit), LEFT engine fails. What does the operating right engine's spiraling slipstream do?", "a": "It passes clear of the vertical tail and gives no help against the yaw", "d": ["It strikes the tail from the left and opposes the yaw to the left", "It strikes the rudder from the right side and doubles its effectiveness", "It flows over the left wing and restores the lost induced lift"], "why": "Only the left engine's slipstream reaches the tail. With the left engine failed, the right engine's slipstream drifts away and nothing offsets the yaw toward the dead engine.", "src": "OEG 3A-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-011", "t": "aero", "q": "With both propellers turning clockwise as seen from the cockpit, which way does engine torque roll the airplane, and when does that hurt most?", "a": "Left; it adds to the roll toward the dead engine when the left engine fails", "d": ["Right; it adds to the roll toward the dead engine when the right engine fails", "Left; it adds to the roll toward the dead engine when the right engine fails", "Neither way; torque from the two engines always cancels out"], "why": "A clockwise propeller produces a counterclockwise (left) rolling reaction. If the left engine fails, the right engine's torque rolls the airplane left toward the dead engine; if the right engine fails, the left engine's torque rolls away from it.", "src": "OEG 3A-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-012", "t": "aero", "q": "What does Vmc mean?", "a": "Minimum speed for directional control with the critical engine inoperative", "d": ["Minimum speed for a positive rate of climb with the critical engine inoperative", "Minimum speed for an intentional engine cut during training", "Minimum speed for the best climb gradient with one engine out"], "why": "Vmc is the slowest airspeed at which control can be maintained with the critical engine out and the other at full power. A positive climb is not part of it; the training-cut speed is Vsse and the best-gradient speed is Vxse.", "src": "OEG 3A-4 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-013", "t": "aero", "q": "Is the actual Vmc of a given twin a single fixed speed?", "a": "No; it varies with power, weight, CG, configuration and density altitude", "d": ["Yes; it is fixed by the size and travel of the rudder and vertical fin", "Yes; it is always exactly the red-line speed shown on the indicator", "No; but it changes only with the airplane's weight and nothing else"], "why": "The red line is one value found under a specific worst-case set of conditions. In flight, actual Vmc moves with power, propeller condition, weight, CG, density altitude, sideslip/bank and configuration.", "src": "OEG 3A-4, 3A-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-014", "t": "aero", "q": "Which statement about Vmc as found during certification is correct?", "a": "It addresses directional control only; there is no climb requirement", "d": ["It is the speed that gives the best single-engine rate of climb", "It sets the optimum bank angle and ball position for climb", "It guarantees the airplane can hold altitude on one engine at that speed"], "why": "Certification Vmc answers one narrow question: the lowest speed at which directional control can be kept under a single, tightly defined set of conditions. It tells you nothing about climb capability or the best attitude, bank or configuration for climbing.", "src": "OEG 3A-4, 3A-5 · 14 CFR 23.149"},
{"id": "o6-015", "t": "aero", "q": "For certification (14 CFR 23.149), takeoff Vmc may not exceed what value?", "a": "1.2 Vs1, with Vs1 determined at maximum takeoff weight", "d": ["1.1 Vs1, with Vs1 determined at maximum takeoff weight", "1.2 Vso, with Vso determined at maximum landing weight", "1.3 Vs1, with Vs1 determined at minimum flying weight"], "why": "The rule caps takeoff Vmc at 1.2 times the clean stall speed measured at maximum takeoff weight, which keeps Vmc reasonably close to the stall speed.", "src": "OEG 3A-5 · 14 CFR 23.149"},
{"id": "o6-016", "t": "aero", "q": "During Vmc certification, what is the maximum rudder pedal force allowed to maintain control?", "a": "150 pounds", "d": ["100 pounds", "180 pounds", "200 pounds"], "why": "At Vmc the pilot must be able to keep control with no more than 150 lb of rudder pedal force, without reducing power on the operating engine.", "src": "OEG 3A-5 · 14 CFR 23.149"},
{"id": "o6-017", "t": "aero", "q": "During Vmc certification, the pilot must be able to prevent a heading change of more than how much?", "a": "20 degrees", "d": ["5 degrees", "10 degrees", "45 degrees"], "why": "During the maneuver the airplane must not assume a dangerous attitude, and it must be possible to hold the heading change to 20 degrees or less.", "src": "OEG 3A-5 · 14 CFR 23.149"},
{"id": "o6-018", "t": "aero", "q": "During Vmc certification, which action must NOT be necessary to keep control at Vmc?", "a": "Reducing power on the operating engine", "d": ["Applying rudder toward the operating engine", "Using aileron to hold the wings near level", "Holding up to 150 pounds of rudder pedal force"], "why": "The airplane must be controllable at Vmc with the operating engine still at full power. Rudder, aileron and pedal force up to 150 lb are all allowed.", "src": "OEG 3A-5 · 14 CFR 23.149"},
{"id": "o6-019", "t": "aero", "q": "What configuration is used when Vmc is determined for certification?", "a": "Gear retracted, flaps in takeoff position, trimmed for takeoff", "d": ["Gear extended, flaps retracted, trimmed for cruise", "Gear extended, flaps in takeoff position, trimmed for landing", "Gear retracted, flaps fully extended, trimmed for Vyse"], "why": "Vmc is found in the configuration right after liftoff: gear up, takeoff flaps, takeoff trim, and propeller controls in the takeoff position. Gear up removes the stabilizing effect an extended gear may give.", "src": "OEG 3A-5 · 14 CFR 23.149"},
{"id": "o6-020", "t": "aero", "q": "What power is set when Vmc is determined for certification?", "a": "Maximum available takeoff power initially on each engine", "d": ["Normal recommended cruise power initially on each engine", "75 percent power on the operating engine, idle on the other", "Power for level flight at Vyse initially on each engine"], "why": "Both engines start at maximum available takeoff power, then the critical engine is failed. Maximum power on the remaining engine gives the greatest asymmetric thrust and therefore the highest (most conservative) Vmc.", "src": "OEG 3A-5 · 14 CFR 23.149"},
{"id": "o6-021", "t": "aero", "q": "What weight and CG are used when Vmc is determined for certification?", "a": "The most unfavorable weight and CG position", "d": ["Maximum takeoff weight at the forward CG limit", "Maximum landing weight at a mid-range CG", "Basic empty weight at the forward CG limit"], "why": "Certification uses the worst case for control. For a typical light twin that means light weight (Vmc rises as weight drops) and the aft-most CG (shortest rudder arm).", "src": "OEG 3A-5, 3A-9, 3A-10 · 14 CFR 23.149"},
{"id": "o6-022", "t": "aero", "q": "Where must the airplane be relative to the ground when Vmc is determined for certification?", "a": "Airborne, with ground effect negligible", "d": ["On the takeoff roll, with the nosewheel on the runway", "Airborne, within one wingspan of the runway", "At or above 10,000 feet density altitude"], "why": "Published Vmc is an airborne value with no help from ground effect or nosewheel steering, so it represents the controllability problem just after liftoff.", "src": "OEG 3A-5 · 14 CFR 23.149"},
{"id": "o6-023", "t": "aero", "q": "Where are the propeller controls during Vmc certification?", "a": "In the recommended takeoff position throughout", "d": ["Pulled to the low-RPM stop after the engine cut", "Moved to feather on the failed engine after the cut", "Set to the recommended cruise RPM throughout"], "why": "All propeller controls stay in the takeoff position for the whole test, matching the situation right after takeoff. The critical engine's propeller is therefore left windmilling, which raises Vmc.", "src": "OEG 3A-5 · 14 CFR 23.149"},
{"id": "o6-024", "t": "aero", "q": "Which list contains only factors that affect Vmc?", "a": "Power, CG position, density altitude, sideslip", "d": ["Power, CG position, density altitude, wind velocity", "Weight, flap position, runway slope, sideslip", "Weight, gear position, propeller condition, headwind"], "why": "The OEG lists power, propeller (windmilling or feathered), weight, CG, density altitude, sideslip, flap position and gear position. Wind and runway slope do not change an airspeed-based control limit.", "src": "OEG 3A-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-025", "t": "aero", "q": "Generally, how do conditions that lower Vmc affect the airplane?", "a": "They improve controllability but reduce performance", "d": ["They improve both controllability and performance", "They reduce controllability but improve performance", "They reduce both controllability and performance"], "why": "High density altitude lowers Vmc because the engine makes less power; extended gear and flaps may help control but add drag. Feathering is an exception that helps both, which is why it is done promptly.", "src": "OEG 3A-7 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-026", "t": "aero", "q": "Single-engine approach: dead engine feathered, good engine at reduced power, gear and landing flaps down. How does actual Vmc compare with published Vmc?", "a": "It is typically lower than the published Vmc", "d": ["It is typically higher than the published Vmc", "It is exactly equal to the published Vmc", "It becomes equal to the clean stall speed"], "why": "Published Vmc assumes a windmilling propeller, maximum power and takeoff configuration. Feathering, reducing power and extending gear and flaps all reduce the yaw problem, so the effective Vmc is usually lower.", "src": "OEG 3A-7 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-027", "t": "aero", "q": "How does a forward CG affect Vmc, and why?", "a": "Lowers it, because the rudder's moment arm is longer", "d": ["Raises it, because the rudder's moment arm is shorter", "Lowers it, because the operating engine makes less thrust", "No effect, because CG affects only pitch stability"], "why": "Moving the CG forward lengthens the distance from the CG to the rudder, so the same rudder force makes a bigger yawing moment and control can be held at a lower speed.", "src": "OEG 3A-7, 3A-10 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-028", "t": "aero", "q": "In the OEG's sample light-twin figures, about how much does a windmilling propeller add to the single-engine descent rate?", "a": "About 400 fpm", "d": ["About 100 fpm", "About 200 fpm", "About 1,000 fpm"], "why": "The sample figures: windmilling propeller about 400 fpm, gear extended about 300 fpm, flaps 150 to 550 fpm, and flight control deflections up to about 300 fpm. Check your own AFM, since values vary by airplane.", "src": "OEG 3A-8 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-029", "t": "aero", "q": "In the OEG's sample light-twin figures, extended flaps add how much to the single-engine descent rate?", "a": "About 150 to 550 fpm, depending on setting", "d": ["About 25 to 50 fpm, depending on setting", "About 50 to 100 fpm, depending on setting", "About 1,000 to 1,500 fpm, depending on setting"], "why": "Flaps cover the widest range in the sample because the penalty grows with deflection. The same sample lists about 400 fpm for a windmilling propeller and about 300 fpm for extended gear.", "src": "OEG 3A-8 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-030", "t": "aero", "q": "After an engine failure you are near Vmc with full power on the good engine. What would reducing that engine's power do to Vmc?", "a": "Lower it, because the asymmetric thrust decreases", "d": ["Raise it, because less air flows over the tail", "Raise it, because the airplane will decelerate", "Nothing; Vmc is a fixed value set during certification"], "why": "Vmc rises as power on the operating engine increases, because the rudder must balance a bigger yawing moment. Reducing that power shrinks the moment, which is why it is the first step in a Vmc recovery.", "src": "OEG 3A-9 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-031", "t": "aero", "q": "An engine fails on climb-out and its propeller is still windmilling. Compared with after you feather it, Vmc is:", "a": "Higher, because windmilling drag adds to the yaw", "d": ["Lower, because the windmilling prop makes a little thrust", "The same, because only the good engine's thrust matters", "Lower, because it keeps air flowing over that wing"], "why": "A windmilling propeller makes a lot of drag on the dead-engine side, adding to the yaw and reducing directional control. Feathering removes most of that drag and lowers Vmc.", "src": "OEG 3A-9, 3A-13 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-032", "t": "aero", "q": "With the airplane banked toward the operating engine, does Vmc go up or down as weight decreases?", "a": "Up, since a smaller weight component helps the rudder", "d": ["Down, since a lighter airplane needs less lift", "Down, since stall speed decreases with weight", "No change, since weight never affects Vmc in any attitude"], "why": "In a bank toward the good engine, a component of the airplane's weight acts sideways with the horizontal lift component and helps the rudder fight the asymmetric thrust. A lighter airplane gets less of that help, so Vmc rises; this is why light weight is the most unfavorable weight.", "src": "OEG 3A-9, 3A-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-033", "t": "aero", "q": "You depart a sea-level airport on a very cold day in a normally aspirated twin. How might actual Vmc compare with the published value?", "a": "Higher, because denser air lets the good engine make more power", "d": ["Lower, because denser air makes the rudder more effective", "Lower, because stall speed is lower in dense air", "Unchanged, because the published Vmc already covers all temperatures"], "why": "Vmc increases as density altitude decreases. A cold day at sea level is below standard density altitude, so the operating engine makes more power and more yaw than in the published case.", "src": "OEG 3A-9, 3A-12 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-034", "t": "aero", "q": "Per the OEG, why isn't extending the gear and flaps a good way to lower Vmc after an engine failure?", "a": "Any Vmc benefit is outweighed by the loss of climb performance", "d": ["Extending gear and flaps always raises Vmc well above red line", "Their effect on Vmc is published, and it is always very small", "They lower Vmc only while the dead engine's prop is windmilling"], "why": "Gear and flaps may help directional control, but their drag greatly reduces climb. Their effect on Vmc is also not determined in certification, so there is no published value to rely on.", "src": "OEG 3A-9 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-035", "t": "aero", "q": "In a normally aspirated twin, which of these would make actual Vmc higher than the published red line?", "a": "Holding wings level rather than banking slightly", "d": ["Feathering the dead engine's propeller promptly", "Reducing power somewhat on the operating engine", "Operating at a high density altitude airport"], "why": "Vmc increases significantly as bank toward the operating engine is reduced. Feathering, reducing power and high density altitude all lower Vmc.", "src": "OEG 3A-9 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-036", "t": "aero", "q": "In a normally aspirated twin, which of these would LOWER actual Vmc?", "a": "Moving the CG forward", "d": ["Reducing the airplane's weight", "Increasing power on the operating engine", "Departing on a cold day at sea level"], "why": "A forward CG lengthens the rudder arm. Lighter weight, more power on the good engine and denser air all raise Vmc.", "src": "OEG 3A-9, 3A-10 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-038", "t": "aero", "q": "Solo, low fuel, CG near the aft limit, slight bank toward the good engine. Compared with a heavy, forward-CG flight, Vmc will be:", "a": "Higher, since light weight and aft CG both raise Vmc", "d": ["Lower, since light weight reduces the engine's thrust", "Lower, since an aft CG lengthens the rudder arm", "The same, since Vmc is a fixed published speed"], "why": "Vmc increases as weight is reduced and as the CG moves aft. This light, aft-loaded case is close to the most unfavorable condition used in certification.", "src": "OEG 3A-9, 3A-10, 3A-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-039", "t": "aero", "q": "According to the OEG, under what condition does airplane weight affect Vmc?", "a": "In banked flight, where it adds to the horizontal lift component", "d": ["In wings-level flight, where it changes the rudder's moment arm", "On the takeoff roll, through nosewheel steering friction", "Only above the single-engine service ceiling, in level flight"], "why": "The OEG says weight does not affect Vmc in straight-and-level flight. In a bank toward the operating engine, a heavier airplane gets more help against the yaw, so Vmc is lower when heavy and higher when light.", "src": "OEG 3A-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-040", "t": "aero", "q": "For twins certificated under CAR 3 or early 14 CFR Part 23, at what weight was Vmc determined?", "a": "The weight was not specified", "d": ["Maximum certificated takeoff weight", "Maximum landing weight", "Minimum flying weight"], "why": "Current Part 23 requires the most unfavorable weight. Older certifications did not specify one, so their published Vmc may not reflect a lightly loaded airplane.", "src": "OEG 3A-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-041", "t": "aero", "q": "You are at 8,000 feet density altitude in a normally aspirated twin. Compared with the sea-level red line, actual Vmc is:", "a": "Lower, because the operating engine makes less power", "d": ["Higher, because the rudder is less effective in thin air", "Higher, because true airspeed is greater at altitude", "The same, because Vmc is an indicated airspeed"], "why": "Non-turbocharged engines lose power with altitude, so the thrust moment the rudder must oppose is smaller and control can be held at a lower airspeed.", "src": "OEG 3A-12 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-042", "t": "aero", "q": "One engine inoperative, wings level, ball centered. What is the airplane doing?", "a": "Sideslipping moderately toward the inoperative engine", "d": ["Flying at zero sideslip with best climb performance", "Sideslipping moderately toward the operating engine", "Flying coordinated with the rudder near neutral"], "why": "Holding wings level with the ball centered takes a lot of rudder toward the good engine, and the result is a moderate sideslip toward the dead engine. Climb performance suffers and Vmc rises well above published.", "src": "OEG 3A-14 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-043", "t": "aero", "q": "Why is Vmc significantly higher than published when the wings are held level with one engine out?", "a": "No horizontal lift component helps the rudder oppose the thrust", "d": ["The ailerons are fully deflected and add a lot of adverse yaw", "Holding the wings level shifts the CG aft of the certification limit", "The windmilling propeller makes more drag with wings level"], "why": "Banking toward the good engine tilts lift so part of it helps the rudder. With wings level the rudder must do all the work alone, so more airspeed is needed for control.", "src": "OEG 3A-14 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-044", "t": "aero", "q": "In a normally aspirated twin, how do Vmc and stall speed (Vs) change as altitude increases?", "a": "Vmc decreases while Vs stays the same, so the gap closes", "d": ["Both decrease at about the same rate, so the gap holds", "Vmc stays the same while Vs increases, so the gap closes", "Vmc increases while Vs decreases, so the gap widens"], "why": "Engine power falls with altitude, so Vmc drops, while indicated stall speed does not change. The margin of a few knots at sea level shrinks until, at some altitude, Vmc and Vs are the same.", "src": "OEG 3A-15 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-045", "t": "aero", "q": "Why can a Vmc demonstration be hazardous at a high density altitude?", "a": "The airplane may stall before reaching Vmc and roll into a spin", "d": ["Vmc rises with density altitude, so control may be lost at a higher speed", "The rudder loses effectiveness at any speed in thin air", "The operating engine overheats at high power and altitude"], "why": "With normally aspirated engines Vmc falls as altitude rises while stall speed does not, so at some density altitude the airplane stalls before it loses directional control. A stall under asymmetric thrust brings a strong roll and a likely spin, so the demo should not be flown in those conditions.", "src": "OEG 3A-16 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-046", "t": "aero", "q": "During a Vmc demonstration the stall warning sounds before any loss of directional control. What does this indicate?", "a": "Vmc has fallen to near or below the stall speed", "d": ["The rudder travel is limited by a rigging problem", "The CG is forward of the forward CG limit", "The operating engine is producing too much power"], "why": "With normally aspirated engines Vmc drops with altitude while stall speed stays put, so the two converge. A stall warning first means you are at that point, where a stall under asymmetric thrust can become a spin; recover immediately.", "src": "OEG 3A-16, 3A-17 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-047", "t": "aero", "q": "Vmc demo with the left engine simulated failed: stall warning sounds before Vmc. What is the correct recovery?", "a": "Reduce angle of attack and reduce throttle on the right engine", "d": ["Add full power on the right engine and hold the nose up", "Reduce angle of attack and advance the left engine's throttle", "Hold the pitch and apply full rudder toward the left engine"], "why": "The stall warning means you are near the critical AOA, so lower the nose; reducing the operating (right) engine's power removes the roll and yaw that could turn a stall into a spin.", "src": "OEG 3A-17 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o6-048", "t": "aero", "q": "After recovering from a stall indication during a Vmc demo, when may power be reapplied on the operating engine?", "a": "Once level flight is regained at an AOA that won't produce a stall", "d": ["Immediately, even while the stall warning is still sounding", "Only after full power has been restored on the simulated failed engine", "Only after descending at least 1,000 feet below entry altitude"], "why": "Power on the good engine comes back after the airplane is back in level flight at a lower angle of attack, and at an AOA that will not produce a stall again.", "src": "OEG 3A-17 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o6-050", "t": "aero", "q": "Late in a flight, with most fuel burned, an engine fails. Compared with the same failure at gross weight, what is different?", "a": "Climb performance is better, but Vmc is higher", "d": ["Climb performance is better, and Vmc is lower", "Climb performance is poorer, but Vmc is lower", "Climb performance is poorer, and Vmc is higher"], "why": "Less weight helps a power-starved airplane climb on one engine, but a lighter airplane is more easily pushed out of controlled flight, so the realized Vmc goes up.", "src": "OEG 3A-18 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-051", "t": "aero", "q": "What technique does the OEG recommend for turns with one engine inoperative?", "a": "Shallow bank, in trim, avoiding sideslip", "d": ["Steep bank, to keep the turn as short as possible", "Rudder-only flat turns, keeping the wings level", "Turns only toward the inoperative engine"], "why": "Bank angles should be kept small because higher bank raises the risk of a Vmc condition, and the turn should be flown in trim without sideslip to protect both control and performance.", "src": "OEG 3A-19 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-052", "t": "aero", "q": "With one engine inoperative, a turn in which direction is more likely to lead to a Vmc condition?", "a": "Toward the inoperative engine", "d": ["Toward the operating engine", "Either direction equally, at any bank angle", "Neither direction, if the ball stays centered"], "why": "Turning toward the good engine keeps the horizontal lift component helping the rudder. Turning toward the dead engine works against the rudder and increases the chance of reaching Vmc.", "src": "OEG 3A-19 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-053", "t": "aero", "q": "What is the main operational advantage of counter-rotating propellers?", "a": "There is no critical engine, since torque and P-factor are balanced", "d": ["There is no Vmc, since asymmetric thrust cannot occur", "The airplane will always climb on one engine, even at maximum gross weight", "Zero sideslip occurs with wings level and the ball centered"], "why": "With counter-rotation both thrust lines sit the same distance from the centerline, so losing either engine gives the same yaw. Asymmetric thrust, a Vmc and the need for zero-sideslip technique still exist: the counter-rotating Seneca still has a Vmc of 80 MPH.", "src": "OEG 3A-20 · FAA-H-8083-3 · POH 2-2, 3-11"},
{"id": "o6-054", "t": "aero", "q": "In a twin whose left propeller turns clockwise and right turns counterclockwise (from the cockpit), where are the descending blades?", "a": "Inboard on both engines, nearer the fuselage", "d": ["Outboard on both engines, nearer the wingtips", "On the right side of both propeller discs", "Inboard on the left engine, outboard on the right"], "why": "The left (clockwise) propeller's descending blade is on its right side, and the right (counterclockwise) propeller's is on its left side; both are inboard. Equal thrust-line arms are why neither engine is critical. This is the Seneca's arrangement.", "src": "OEG 3A-20 · FAA-H-8083-3 · POH 2-2"},
{"id": "o6-055", "t": "eo", "q": "If the manufacturer gives no approach speed, what is the slowest speed a twin should fly on final?", "a": "Vyse until short final with landing assured, and never below Vmc", "d": ["Vmc until short final with landing assured, and never below Vs", "Vy until over the threshold, and never below Vyse", "Vxse until short final with landing assured, and never below Vs"], "why": "The final approach is flown with power at the recommended speed. With none given, stay at or above blue line until the landing is assured, and never below red line.", "src": "OEG 3A-21 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-056", "t": "eo", "q": "How should power be handled in a twin's round-out, and are full-stall landings desirable?", "a": "Reduce residual power gradually to idle; full-stall landings are undesirable", "d": ["Cut both throttles at the threshold; full-stall landings are preferred", "Hold approach power until touchdown; full-stall landings are preferred", "Reduce residual power gradually to idle; full-stall landings are preferred"], "why": "Power is eased to idle in the round-out. High wing loading and the drag of two windmilling propellers leave little float, and full-stall landings are generally undesirable in twins.", "src": "OEG 3A-21 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-057", "t": "eo", "q": "How is the best single-engine climb performance obtained?", "a": "At Vyse with maximum power, minimum drag and minimum sideslip", "d": ["At Vxse with maximum power, gear down and wings level", "At Vyse with reduced power and flaps at the takeoff setting", "At Vmc plus 5 with maximum power and the ball centered"], "why": "Fly blue line with maximum available power, flaps and gear up, the dead engine feathered, and the airplane at zero sideslip. Sideslip is a major hidden source of drag.", "src": "OEG 3A-22 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o6-058", "t": "eo", "q": "Does flying Vyse guarantee that a twin can climb with one engine inoperative?", "a": "No; it gives the best rate available, which may be zero or negative", "d": ["Yes, as long as the dead engine's propeller is promptly and fully feathered", "Yes, at any weight at or below the maximum takeoff weight", "No; only Vxse can guarantee a positive climb on one engine"], "why": "Vyse gives the most altitude gain (or least loss) per unit time on one engine, but it cannot create performance that isn't there. Before takeoff, know whether the airplane can reasonably be expected to keep climbing after a failure.", "src": "OEG 3A-22 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o6-059", "t": "aero", "q": "With one engine inoperative, which instrument directly shows that the airplane is at zero sideslip?", "a": "None; fly a predetermined bank angle and ball position", "d": ["The inclinometer, when the ball is centered", "The turn coordinator, when it shows no rate of turn at all", "The heading indicator, when the heading is steady"], "why": "With asymmetric thrust the centered ball no longer marks zero sideslip, and no panel instrument shows it directly. Without a yaw string, the pilot flies a known bank angle and ball position.", "src": "OEG 3A-23 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-060", "t": "aero", "q": "Which two control inputs, used together, achieve zero sideslip with one engine inoperative?", "a": "Rudder toward the good engine plus a small bank toward the good engine", "d": ["Rudder toward the dead engine plus a small bank toward the good engine", "Rudder toward the good engine with the wings held exactly level", "Aileron toward the dead engine with the rudder held neutral"], "why": "Rudder yaw and the horizontal lift component from a slight bank both oppose the asymmetric thrust. Either one alone is wrong; the right combination gives zero sideslip and best climb.", "src": "OEG 3A-24 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-062", "t": "aero", "q": "Besides the best single-engine rate of climb, what does Vyse provide?", "a": "The minimum rate of sink with one engine inoperative", "d": ["The steepest climb gradient with one engine inoperative", "The lowest rudder force needed to hold heading", "The minimum control speed at high density altitude"], "why": "When the airplane can't climb on one engine, flying Vyse gives the slowest descent. The steepest gradient is Vxse.", "src": "OEG 3A-25 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-063", "t": "aero", "q": "The red radial line on a light twin's airspeed indicator marks Vmc under what condition?", "a": "Airborne at sea level with the critical engine inoperative", "d": ["On the ground roll at sea level with both engines running", "Airborne at the single-engine service ceiling", "Airborne at gross weight with the dead engine feathered"], "why": "The red line is the minimum control speed, airborne at sea level, with the critical engine inoperative. Actual Vmc differs with altitude, weight, CG, bank and configuration.", "src": "OEG 3A-26 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o6-064", "t": "aero", "q": "What three problems must a pilot deal with immediately when one engine of a light twin fails?", "a": "Loss of climb performance, and yaw and roll toward the dead engine", "d": ["Loss of climb performance, and yaw and roll toward the good engine", "Loss of electrical power, yaw toward the dead engine, and pitch-up", "Loss of hydraulic pressure, roll toward the dead engine, and pitch-down"], "why": "Asymmetric thrust yaws the nose toward the dead engine, loss of induced airflow over that wing rolls the airplane toward it, and climb performance drops by at least 80 percent.", "src": "OEG 3B-1 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o6-065", "t": "aero", "q": "Losing one engine of a light twin reduces climb performance by at least how much?", "a": "80 percent", "d": ["50 percent", "30 percent", "65 percent"], "why": "Losing half the power costs far more than half the climb, because climb depends on excess power, and most of the remaining engine's power is needed just to stay aloft.", "src": "OEG 3B-1 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o6-066", "t": "aero", "q": "Which of these indicates you are reaching Vmc with one engine inoperative?", "a": "Full rudder is applied and the airplane still yaws toward the dead engine", "d": ["The ball moves slightly toward the operating engine with rudder applied", "Rudder pressure becomes lighter as the airspeed decreases", "The airplane begins to yaw toward the operating engine"], "why": "Signs of approaching Vmc: increasing yaw, rapidly fading control effectiveness, stall warning or buffet, and full rudder reached while the nose still yaws toward the dead engine. A ball slightly toward the good engine is normal at zero sideslip.", "src": "OEG 3B-2 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-067", "t": "aero", "q": "As a twin with one engine inoperative slows toward Vmc, what happens to control effectiveness?", "a": "It decays rapidly while the yawing tendency increases", "d": ["It improves, because angle of attack is increasing", "It stays constant until exactly Vmc, then is lost", "It improves, because the good engine's thrust drops"], "why": "Rudder and aileron power depend on airspeed, so control fades quickly as the airplane slows while the asymmetric yaw grows. Stall warning or buffet may also appear.", "src": "OEG 3B-2 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-068", "t": "aero", "q": "Why does a twin with one engine inoperative become directionally uncontrollable below Vmc?", "a": "Rudder power falls with airspeed until it can't balance thrust", "d": ["The vertical tail stalls whenever the wing reaches critical AOA", "The windmilling propeller stops, and its drag sharply increases", "Aileron adverse yaw overpowers the rudder at low airspeed"], "why": "Fully deflected, the rudder's yawing power depends on the airflow over it. Below a certain airspeed its moment can no longer equal the good engine's thrust moment, and directional control is lost.", "src": "OEG 3B-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-069", "t": "aero", "q": "Which type of twin does not have the asymmetric-thrust control problem after an engine failure?", "a": "A twin with centerline (inline) thrust", "d": ["A twin with counter-rotating propellers", "A twin with turbocharged engines", "A twin with automatic propeller feathering"], "why": "Only centerline thrust removes the yawing moment. Counter-rotation removes the critical engine, and autofeather removes windmilling drag, but both still leave asymmetric thrust and a Vmc.", "src": "OEG 3B-3 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-070", "t": "aero", "q": "Why does a twin roll toward the inoperative engine?", "a": "That wing loses slipstream lift, aided by the yaw", "d": ["The stopped engine's weight pulls that wing down", "The dead engine's fuel tank stays full and heavier", "The good engine's wing carries more drag than lift"], "why": "Loss of power removes the induced airflow from the propeller over that wing, cutting its lift. The yaw toward the dead engine also changes the lift distribution in the same direction.", "src": "OEG 3B-4 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o6-071", "t": "aero", "q": "How are the roll and yaw toward the inoperative engine balanced?", "a": "Bank toward the operating engine and rudder opposite the yaw", "d": ["Bank toward the dead engine and rudder in the direction of yaw", "Hold wings level and use rudder toward the dead engine", "Bank toward the operating engine with the rudder neutral"], "why": "Aileron to bank into the good engine counters the roll, and rudder opposite the yaw (toward the good engine) counters the yaw.", "src": "OEG 3B-4 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o6-072", "t": "aero", "q": "What causes a spin?", "a": "Exceeding the critical AOA with yaw or sideslip present", "d": ["Reaching Vmc with full rudder deflection applied", "Exceeding the critical AOA in fully coordinated flight", "Excessive airspeed in a steep descending turn"], "why": "A spin needs a stall plus yaw or sideslip at or beyond the stall. In a twin that yaw can come from rudder input or from asymmetric thrust.", "src": "OEG 3B-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-073", "t": "aero", "q": "When must spin awareness be greatest in multi-engine flying?", "a": "Vmc demos, stalls, slow flight, and low-speed asymmetric thrust", "d": ["Steep turns, emergency descents, and high-speed cruise flight", "Short-field landings, crosswind takeoffs, and ground taxiing", "Cruise climbs, high-altitude cruise, and instrument holding"], "why": "Any condition of low speed, high angle of attack and large asymmetric thrust is a spin setup.", "src": "OEG 3B-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-074", "t": "aero", "q": "Are single-engine stalls part of multi-engine training?", "a": "No; they are not part of any multi-engine training curriculum", "d": ["Yes; they are required on the multi-engine practical test", "Yes; but only with the critical engine's propeller feathered", "Yes; but only at or above 5,000 feet AGL"], "why": "A stall with asymmetric thrust is a likely spin entry, and twins generally recover from spins poorly, so single-engine stalls are not practiced.", "src": "OEG 3B-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-075", "t": "aero", "q": "An instructor pulls one throttle to idle at a speed just above Vmc during a nose-high climb. What is the main hazard?", "a": "Sudden asymmetric thrust near the stall can develop into a spin", "d": ["The idled engine will shock-cool rapidly and crack a cylinder head", "The propeller will feather and then fail to unfeather", "The gear warning horn will sound and distract the pilot"], "why": "A simulated engine failure introduced at too low a speed is the training procedure most likely to degrade into a spin: sudden asymmetric thrust at high AOA near the stall. Never introduce one below Vsse (or Vyse if none is published).", "src": "OEG 3B-7 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-076", "t": "aero", "q": "Below what speed should a simulated engine failure never be introduced?", "a": "Vsse, or Vyse if no Vsse is published", "d": ["Vmc, or Vs if no Vmc is published", "Vxse, or Vx if no Vxse is published", "Vy, or Vyse if no Vy is published"], "why": "Vsse is the safe, intentional one-engine-inoperative speed. Where none is published, use blue line (Vyse).", "src": "OEG 3B-7 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-077", "t": "aero", "q": "Which spin phase lasts from the stall and start of rotation until the spin is fully developed?", "a": "The incipient phase", "d": ["The entry phase", "The developed phase", "The recovery phase"], "why": "Entry is when the pilot supplies the stall and yaw; incipient runs from the stall and onset of rotation until the spin stabilizes; developed is the stabilized spin.", "src": "OEG 3B-8 · AC 61-67"},
{"id": "o6-078", "t": "aero", "q": "What characterizes a fully developed, steady-state spin?", "a": "Rotation, airspeed and vertical speed stable each turn", "d": ["Rotation rate and airspeed are increasing every turn", "Rotation has stopped but AOA is still above critical", "A nose-high attitude with the wings unstalled"], "why": "Once fully developed, the spin settles into a repeating pattern: rotation rate, airspeed and descent rate stop changing from one turn to the next, and the path is nearly vertical.", "src": "OEG 3B-8 · AC 61-67"},
{"id": "o6-079", "t": "aero", "q": "Practicing slow flight in a twin, the airplane stalls and starts rotating to the left. What recovery do most manufacturers recommend?", "a": "Both throttles idle, full right rudder, full forward elevator, ailerons neutral", "d": ["Both throttles idle, full left rudder, full forward elevator, ailerons neutral", "Right throttle full, full right rudder, full aft elevator, ailerons neutral", "Both throttles full, full right rudder, neutral elevator, ailerons full left"], "why": "Apply all four inputs together and hold them until the rotation stops; then use rudder, back pressure and power to return to normal flight. Expect a large altitude loss, so act at once. The Seneca AFM (POH 3-17) words the elevator step differently: release all back pressure, and push the wheel full forward only if the nose doesn't drop.", "src": "OEG 3B-9 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-080", "t": "aero", "q": "Are multi-engine airplanes approved for intentional spins?", "a": "No, and their spin recovery is generally very poor", "d": ["Yes, most light twins are approved with both engines idle", "Yes, in the utility category at reduced weight", "No, but their spin recovery is generally excellent"], "why": "No multi-engine airplane is spin-approved and recovery characteristics are poor, so the emphasis is on spin avoidance and awareness.", "src": "OEG 3B-9 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-081", "t": "eo", "q": "Why should the failed engine's propeller be feathered as soon as possible?", "a": "A windmilling prop near flat pitch makes enough drag to threaten control", "d": ["A windmilling prop will soon overspeed and damage the failed engine", "A feathered prop is easier to restart than a windmilling one in flight", "A windmilling prop drains the battery through the failed alternator"], "why": "At small blade angles near flat pitch, a high-RPM windmilling propeller makes enormous drag. That raises Vmc and kills climb performance, and can make the airplane uncontrollable.", "src": "OEG 3B-10 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-082", "t": "eo", "q": "A windmilling propeller at low blade angles can increase parasite drag by up to about how much?", "a": "As much as the parasite drag of the basic airplane", "d": ["About 5 percent of the airplane's parasite drag", "About 10 percent of the airplane's parasite drag", "About one-quarter of the airplane's parasite drag"], "why": "In that blade-angle range a windmilling propeller can roughly double the airplane's parasite drag, which is why feathering is so urgent.", "src": "OEG 3B-10 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-083", "t": "aero", "q": "With one engine inoperative, what does banking toward the operating engine accomplish?", "a": "Part of the weight offsets the rudder's side force", "d": ["It increases the thrust from the operating engine's propeller", "It lets the pilot center the ball without using any rudder", "It moves the CG forward and lengthens the rudder's arm"], "why": "The rudder input that counters the yaw also creates a side force that causes a sideslip. A slight bank uses part of the airplane's weight to cancel that force; at the right bank, sideslip is zero, Vmc is lower and climb is best.", "src": "OEG 3B-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-084", "t": "aero", "q": "Reducing bank away from the operating engine increases Vmc by approximately how much?", "a": "About 3 knots per degree of bank", "d": ["About 1 knot per degree of bank", "About 5 knots per degree of bank", "About 10 knots per degree of bank"], "why": "The OEG gives about 3 knots of Vmc increase for each degree of bank reduced. Over a few degrees that adds up quickly.", "src": "OEG 3B-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-085", "t": "aero", "q": "After an engine failure you bank about 10 degrees toward the good engine and control is now solid. What next?", "a": "Reduce bank to the zero-sideslip angle to recover climb performance", "d": ["Hold 10 degrees, since more bank always improves the single-engine climb", "Roll wings level and center the ball with rudder to cut drag", "Increase the bank further, since that lowers Vmc even more"], "why": "Extra bank beyond zero sideslip can add rudder authority at first, but it drastically reduces climb. Once control is established, return to the zero-sideslip bank.", "src": "OEG 3B-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-086", "t": "aero", "q": "Climbing at blue line on one engine, wings level, ball centered, climb is poor. What change best improves climb?", "a": "Bank slightly toward the good engine; let the ball move toward it", "d": ["Hold wings level and add more rudder to keep the ball exactly centered", "Bank 10 to 15 degrees toward the good engine, ball centered", "Bank slightly toward the dead engine to unload the rudder"], "why": "Wings level with the ball centered puts the airplane in a sideslip toward the dead engine. A small bank toward the good engine, with the ball about a third to a half off center toward it, gives zero sideslip and the best climb.", "src": "OEG 3B-11, 3B-12 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-087", "t": "aero", "q": "In typical light twins, what bank toward the operating engine gives zero sideslip?", "a": "About 1.5 to 2.5 degrees", "d": ["About 0 degrees (wings level)", "About 8 to 10 degrees", "About 15 to 20 degrees"], "why": "The exact angle varies by model, power and airspeed. OEG 3B-12 gives 1.5–2.5°; the OEG's later exam tip (after 3C-37) rounds it to about 2–3°, so think 'about 2°'. The airplane's own AFM may specify a value; the Seneca POH 3-11, for example, says to maintain 5° toward the operating engine.", "src": "OEG 3B-12 · FAA-H-8083-3 · POH 3-11"},
{"id": "o6-088", "t": "aero", "q": "At zero sideslip with one engine inoperative, where is the inclinometer ball typically?", "a": "One-third to one-half a ball width toward the operating engine", "d": ["Exactly centered in the inclinometer", "A full ball width toward the inoperative engine", "One-third to one-half a ball width toward the inoperative engine"], "why": "In a slight bank toward the good engine the ball sits toward the low (operating-engine) side, about a third to a half of its width off center.", "src": "OEG 3B-12 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-089", "t": "aero", "q": "Why can the zero-sideslip bank angle differ for a left vs a right engine failure in some twins?", "a": "Without counter-rotating props, P-factor differs for each engine", "d": ["The fuel load in the two wings is never exactly equal in flight", "The airspeed indicator is calibrated for one side only", "The left engine is always rated for more horsepower than the right one"], "why": "Zero sideslip varies with model, power and airspeed. If the propellers don't counter-rotate, P-factor gives the two engines different thrust-line arms, so the required bank differs slightly with which engine failed.", "src": "OEG 3B-12 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-090", "t": "aero", "q": "Flight tests show that holding wings level with the ball centered can raise Vmc by up to about how much?", "a": "As much as 20 knots in some airplanes", "d": ["As much as 3 knots in some airplanes", "As much as 5 knots in some airplanes", "As much as 50 knots in some airplanes"], "why": "Without the bank toward the good engine, the rudder works alone against the asymmetric thrust and Vmc can rise dramatically. Do not confuse this with the roughly 3 knots per degree of bank figure.", "src": "OEG 3B-13 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o6-091", "t": "aero", "q": "During Vmc certification, how much bank may the manufacturer use to hold straight flight, and in which direction?", "a": "Up to 5 degrees toward the operating engine", "d": ["Up to 5 degrees toward the inoperative engine", "None; the wings must be held exactly level", "Up to 15 degrees toward the operating engine"], "why": "The certification rule allows no more than 5° of bank while holding straight flight at Vmc, and the AFH specifies it is toward the good engine. The OEG's list of conditions leaves this out, but examiners expect it; it is also why wings-level flight gives a higher Vmc than published.", "src": "FAA-H-8083-3 · 14 CFR 23.149"},
{"id": "o6-092", "t": "aero", "q": "Why does Vmc certification limit the bank toward the operating engine to 5 degrees?", "a": "More bank lowers Vmc, so the limit stops an unrealistically low value", "d": ["More bank raises Vmc, so the limit stops an unrealistically high value", "More bank would exceed the 150-pound rudder pedal force limit", "More bank would overstress the vertical fin during the test"], "why": "Banking toward the good engine lets part of the lift help the rudder, and Vmc drops sharply as bank increases. Capping the bank at 5° keeps manufacturers from publishing a Vmc that pilots could not realistically achieve.", "src": "FAA-H-8083-3 · 14 CFR 23.149"},
{"id": "o6-093", "t": "aero", "q": "Per the FAA Airplane Flying Handbook, where are the cowl flaps when Vmc is determined for certification?", "a": "In the takeoff position on both engines", "d": ["In the cruise position on both engines", "Closed on the failed engine, open on the other", "Open on the failed engine, closed on the other"], "why": "The AFH adds cowl flaps in the takeoff position to the other takeoff-configuration items (gear up, takeoff flaps, takeoff trim, propellers at takeoff setting), so the test matches the airplane just after liftoff. Closing the dead engine's cowl flap is a later step in the engine-out drill, not a test condition.", "src": "FAA-H-8083-3 · 14 CFR 23.149"},
{"id": "o6-094", "t": "aero", "q": "Certification Vmc tests are flown at several altitudes. How is the published Vmc obtained?", "a": "Results are extrapolated to a single sea-level value", "d": ["It is the value measured at the highest test altitude", "It is the average of the values from all test altitudes", "It is referenced to 5,000 feet density altitude"], "why": "With normally aspirated engines Vmc is highest at sea level with takeoff power, so the AFH says test results are reduced to one sea-level value (AFM V-speeds are for sea-level, standard-day conditions unless noted). Denser air than that gives a higher actual Vmc; higher density altitude gives a lower one.", "src": "FAA-H-8083-3 · 14 CFR 23.149"},
{"id": "o7-001", "t": "eo", "q": "According to the OEG, what are the three major causes of fatalities in engine-out emergencies?", "a": "Loss of directional control, climb performance, and flying airspeed", "d": ["Feathering the wrong engine, fuel exhaustion, and structural overload", "Loss of directional control, engine fire, and failure to feather", "Gear left down, flaps left down, and windmilling propeller drag"], "why": "Engine-out accidents usually come down to losing control (Vmc), losing the ability to climb, or letting airspeed decay into a stall, alone or in combination. The other choices are specific errors that lead to those outcomes.", "src": "OEG 3C-1 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-002", "t": "eo", "q": "When is an engine failure during takeoff considered most critical?", "a": "The 2–3 seconds right after liftoff, while accelerating to climb speed", "d": ["The first 2–3 seconds of the takeoff roll, while the airplane is well below Vmc", "The moment of rotation, as the nosewheel leaves the runway", "Just after gear retraction, while passing about 400 feet AGL at Vyse"], "why": "Right after liftoff the airplane is slow, low and still accelerating toward climb speed. It may be controllable, but its one-engine climb performance is far below optimum, so it may not be able to keep flying.", "src": "OEG 3C-2 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o7-003", "t": "eo", "q": "Most light twins are controllable just above Vmc. Why is an engine failure right after liftoff still so dangerous?", "a": "Performance is so poor that continued flight may be impossible", "d": ["The airplane is below Vso and will stall almost immediately", "The rudder has no authority until the gear is retracted", "The propeller cannot be feathered until the airplane reaches Vyse"], "why": "Being able to control the airplane does not mean it can climb. Close to Vmc and still accelerating, it is far from its best single-engine climb condition.", "src": "OEG 3C-2 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o7-004", "t": "eo", "q": "To reduce the risk of an engine failure on takeoff, what should your pre-takeoff briefing (or self-briefing) cover?", "a": "Your plan of action if an engine fails during takeoff or climb", "d": ["The planned cruise altitude, power setting, and expected fuel burn", "Passenger seat belts, emergency exits, and the no-smoking rule", "The departure frequency, transponder code, and first navigation fix"], "why": "The OEG's first mitigation is a briefing that commits the PIC to a plan for an engine failure or other emergency at any point in the takeoff and initial climb. That way the response isn't being invented under stress.", "src": "OEG 3C-3 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o7-005", "t": "eo", "q": "Which performance figures does the OEG say you should know before a multi-engine takeoff?", "a": "Accelerate-stop/go distances, single-engine service ceiling, OEI climb rate", "d": ["Cruise true airspeed, fuel flow at 65% power, and maximum range", "Landing distance, maximum demonstrated crosswind, and maneuvering speed", "Two-engine service ceiling, never-exceed speed, and best glide speed"], "why": "These numbers, along with terrain and obstacles in the flight path, tell you whether you can stop or continue if an engine fails. The OEG adds that you should allow generous margins for real-world performance.", "src": "OEG 3C-3 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o7-006", "t": "eo", "q": "You are departing a short runway at a high density altitude airport. Which preparation best supports a fast decision if an engine fails?", "a": "Know runway length, accelerate-stop distance, and an obstacle-free path", "d": ["Know the two-engine climb rate, planned cruise altitude, and cruise power", "Know the nearest VOR frequency, the destination ATIS, and the squawk code", "Know the best glide speed, maximum landing weight, and Vfe"], "why": "The OEG says to know your options before takeoff: runway length, a direction of flight free of obstacles, and the stop and go distances. Two-engine and cruise figures tell you nothing about the engine-out case.", "src": "OEG 3C-3 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o7-007", "t": "eo", "q": "Are most light twins certificated under 14 CFR Part 23 required to hold altitude on one engine in the takeoff configuration?", "a": "No; many aren't required to in any configuration, even at sea level", "d": ["Yes, they must hold altitude at sea level and maximum gross weight", "Yes, they must hold altitude up to their single-engine service ceiling", "Yes, but only with the gear retracted and the flaps at takeoff"], "why": "Part 23 does not require a light twin to maintain altitude in the takeoff configuration with an engine inoperative, and many need not do so in any configuration. Never assume the second engine guarantees a climb.", "src": "OEG 3C-4 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-008", "t": "eo", "q": "What common belief about losing an engine in a light twin does the OEG call mistaken?", "a": "That it will still perform at least half as well on one engine", "d": ["That the airplane will yaw toward the inoperative engine", "That a windmilling propeller adds a significant amount of drag", "That Vmc can vary depending on which engine has failed"], "why": "Climb comes from power beyond what level flight needs, so losing half the power costs far more than half the climb. For performance (not control) in the takeoff or landing configuration, think of a light twin as a single whose power is split between two engines.", "src": "OEG 3C-4 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-009", "t": "eo", "q": "In the OEG's typical engine-failure checklist, which memory items come right after maintaining control and Vyse?", "a": "Mixtures rich, propellers high RPM, throttles full power", "d": ["Identify, verify, and feather the failed engine", "Fuel selector to crossfeed, boost pumps on, mags checked", "Cowl flap closed, alternator off, mixture to cutoff"], "why": "After control and airspeed, you push everything forward (mixtures, props, throttles) for maximum available power, as the Seneca POH 3-11 also does. Then you clean up drag and identify the engine. Crossfeed and mags are troubleshooting steps, and the last choice is securing.", "src": "OEG 3C-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-010", "t": "eo", "q": "Which sequence matches the OEG's typical engine-failure memory flow?", "a": "Control, power up, clean up, identify, verify, fix or feather", "d": ["Control, identify, verify, feather, then power up and clean up", "Identify, verify, control, clean up, feather, then power up", "Control, clean up, feather, identify, verify, then power up"], "why": "Control and Vyse come first, then maximum power (mixtures, props, throttles), then drag cleanup, then identify, verify with the throttle, and fix or feather. The OEG's generic table lists gear before flaps. Your school's Seneca memory items hold blue line, 105 MPH, then mixtures, props and throttles forward, flaps up, gear up and fuel pumps on (the AFM, POH 3-11, says airspeed above 90 MPH).", "src": "OEG 3C-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-011", "t": "eo", "q": "Why does the memory flow set full power and clean up drag before you identify the failed engine?", "a": "To get the best performance with the least risk of losing control", "d": ["Because full power makes the gauges reveal the failed engine", "Because the propeller can only feather at full throttle", "Because the gear must be up before the prop can feather"], "why": "The steps before Fix or Feather are there to put the airplane in its most favorable performance condition, the one least likely to end in loss of control, while you work out which engine quit.", "src": "OEG 3C-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-012", "t": "eo", "q": "An engine quits at 600 ft AGL on climb-out. You've completed control, power up, clean up, identify, and verify. What now?", "a": "Feather it immediately; secure it as time allows", "d": ["Run the restart checklist first, then decide whether to feather", "Leave it windmilling so a restart will be easier later", "Switch its fuel selector to crossfeed and wait to see"], "why": "At low altitude the OEG's Fix-or-Feather choice is to feather the affected engine immediately, because a windmilling prop adds a lot of drag when you can least afford it. Troubleshooting belongs at higher altitude, when there is time.", "src": "OEG 3C-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-013", "t": "eo", "q": "An engine fails at 7,000 ft in cruise with plenty of terrain clearance. After control, power up, identify, and verify, what is appropriate?", "a": "If time allows, try to find and fix the problem before feathering", "d": ["Feather it at once; troubleshooting is never done in flight in a twin", "Descend to pattern altitude first, then begin any troubleshooting", "Shut off both boost pumps and wait for it to recover"], "why": "With altitude and time, the Fix-or-Feather step allows an attempt to fix the problem. If that fails, you run a diagnosis or securing checklist. Control remains the first priority throughout.", "src": "OEG 3C-5 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-014", "t": "aero", "q": "During a Vmc demonstration, the nose keeps yawing toward the dead engine despite full rudder. What should you do immediately?", "a": "Retard the operating engine's throttle while lowering the pitch attitude", "d": ["Advance the operating engine's throttle while raising the pitch attitude", "Feather the windmilling engine while holding full rudder", "Hold full rudder and aileron, and raise the nose to slow down further"], "why": "Reducing power on the operating engine removes the asymmetric thrust that is causing the yaw. Lowering the nose restores airspeed and reduces the angle of attack.", "src": "OEG 3C-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-015", "t": "aero", "q": "When recovering from a Vmc demonstration, what condition do you establish before setting symmetrical power?", "a": "Straight flight on the entry heading at Vsse or Vyse", "d": ["A climbing turn toward the good engine at Vxse", "Straight flight on the entry heading at Vmc", "Level flight on any heading at stall warning speed"], "why": "You recover to straight flight on the original heading at Vsse or Vyse, then set power symmetrically. The recovery does not include adding power to the windmilling engine by itself.", "src": "OEG 3C-6 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-016", "t": "aero", "q": "When Vmc is at or below stall speed, or density altitude prevents a true demo, what technique allows a safe Vmc demonstration?", "a": "Limit rudder travel artificially to simulate maximum available rudder", "d": ["Perform the demonstration at a higher density altitude instead", "Feather the inoperative propeller to lower its windmilling drag", "Extend the gear and flaps so the stall happens at a higher speed"], "why": "Blocking the rudder makes directional control run out at a speed well above the stall. Higher density altitude and feathering both lower Vmc, which makes the problem worse.", "src": "OEG 3C-7 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-017", "t": "aero", "q": "When rudder travel is artificially limited to demonstrate Vmc, at about what speed should the demonstration be done?", "a": "About 20 knots above Vs", "d": ["About 5 knots above Vs", "At Vs, with the stall warning sounding", "At Vso, in the landing configuration"], "why": "Running out of (limited) rudder about 20 knots above the stall keeps the demo clear of a stall with asymmetric power, which could become a spin, while still showing the loss of directional control.", "src": "OEG 3C-7 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-018", "t": "aero", "q": "During a Vmc demo you notice the stall signs late, and the airplane begins to roll and yaw. What should you do?", "a": "Pull both throttles back, lower the nose, get above Vmc (ideally Vsse)", "d": ["Add full power on the good engine and pull the nose up to hold altitude", "Feather the windmilling engine, then raise the nose to slow down", "Hold full rudder and aileron against the roll and wait to reach Vyse"], "why": "If nothing is done, the asymmetric thrust overpowers the controls and the airplane can roll over, stall and spin. Taking the power off and reducing the angle of attack stops this. Once level above Vmc you can bring power back on the good engine.", "src": "OEG 3C-8 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-019", "t": "aero", "q": "Why does the OEG suggest reducing both throttles, not just the operating one, when recovering from a botched Vmc demo?", "a": "To avoid any confusion about which throttle to reduce", "d": ["Because the windmilling engine adds yaw when at idle", "Because both throttles must be closed in order to feather", "To let the propellers slow to their start-lock speed"], "why": "When roll and yaw are developing fast, pulling both throttles guarantees that the power causing the problem comes off. Once the airplane is stable, you reapply power on the good engine.", "src": "OEG 3C-8 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-021", "t": "eo", "q": "Per the OEG, why should a twin not become airborne before its airspeed reaches and exceeds Vmc?", "a": "If an engine failed, directional control would be impossible", "d": ["The landing gear cannot be retracted until the airspeed passes Vmc", "The stall warning system does not function below Vmc", "The propellers cannot be feathered at speeds below Vmc"], "why": "Below Vmc, full rudder can't hold heading against an engine failure, so the OEG wants Vmc or more at lift-off. The Seneca's 25° short-field technique (70 MPH) and your school's 75 MPH flaps-up rotation lift off below the 80 MPH Vmc; if an engine fails there, retard the operating engine's throttle and lower the nose at once (POH 6-7).", "src": "OEG 3C-9 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-022", "t": "aero", "q": "Why does the OEG advise against practicing stalls with one engine inoperative, actual or simulated?", "a": "Risk of a Vmc loss of control, and a lot of altitude to recover", "d": ["The operating engine may overheat at a high angle of attack", "The stall warning does not work with an engine out", "A feathered propeller can unfeather itself in a stall"], "why": "Stalling with asymmetric power invites a Vmc roll and possible spin, and the recovery can take a lot of altitude.", "src": "OEG 3C-10 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-023", "t": "aero", "q": "In a steep turn, the inboard (lower-wing) engine suddenly fails. What tendency should you expect?", "a": "The high-wing engine tends to roll the airplane further into the bank", "d": ["The airplane tends to roll back out of the bank toward wings level on its own", "The nose pitches up sharply while the bank becomes shallower", "No rolling tendency develops; yaw and bank cancel each other"], "why": "With the low engine dead, the operating engine on the high wing tends to add roll into the turn, which can grow into a Vmc encounter. If the high-wing engine fails instead, the low engine tends to roll the airplane back toward level.", "src": "OEG 3C-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-024", "t": "eo", "q": "What should be your first action if an engine loses power during a steep turn?", "a": "Reduce the bank and return to level flight", "d": ["Increase back pressure to hold altitude", "Steepen the bank toward the dead engine", "Feather immediately while holding the bank"], "why": "Stall and Vmc risk are higher in a steep turn, so roll level right away. If the airplane is rolling into the turn, the OEG adds that you can reduce power on the operating engine (or both) until you are level.", "src": "OEG 3C-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-025", "t": "eo", "q": "Your left engine suddenly quits in cruise. What is your first priority?", "a": "Maintain aircraft control and airspeed", "d": ["Identify and feather the failed engine", "Declare an emergency with ATC", "Attempt an immediate engine restart"], "why": "Every engine-out procedure starts with flying the airplane: control and airspeed come before identifying, feathering, troubleshooting or talking to ATC.", "src": "OEG 3C-12 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-026", "t": "eo", "q": "After a power loss, the airplane yaws left and your left foot is idle. Before feathering the left engine, how do you verify?", "a": "Retard the left throttle", "d": ["Pull the left mixture to cutoff", "Turn the left magnetos off", "Compare the left and right MP gauges"], "why": "Closing the suspected throttle can be undone: if it's the wrong engine, the yaw increases and you push the throttle back up. Cutting the mixture or mags on the wrong engine could leave you with no power, and gauges can mislead.", "src": "OEG 3C-13 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-027", "t": "eo", "q": "Verifying a suspected failed engine, you retard its throttle and immediately need a clear change in rudder to hold heading. What does that tell you?", "a": "That engine was still making power; don't feather it", "d": ["That engine has definitely failed; feather it now", "Both engines are losing power at the same time", "Its propeller has already gone to feather on its own"], "why": "Retarding the throttle of a truly dead engine changes nothing, so no control change is needed. If you suddenly need different rudder, that engine was still contributing power: put the throttle back and re-identify before feathering anything.", "src": "OEG 3C-14 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-028", "t": "eo", "q": "What does 'dead foot, dead engine' mean?", "a": "The foot not pressing rudder is on the failed engine's side", "d": ["The foot pressing hardest is on the failed engine's side", "Press the rudder on the failed side to stop the yaw", "Take both feet off the rudders to feel the yaw"], "why": "You press rudder on the good engine's side to stop the yaw toward the dead engine. The idle ('dead') foot is on the side of the failed engine.", "src": "OEG 3C-14 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-029", "t": "eo", "q": "After a loud bang and a sudden yaw on climb-out, you're holding firm right rudder and your left foot is idle. Which engine most likely failed?", "a": "The left engine", "d": ["The right engine", "Neither; rudder pressure alone can't show it", "Both; it is a symmetrical power loss"], "why": "The airplane yaws toward the dead engine, so you need rudder on the opposite side. Right rudder with an idle left foot means the left engine. Then verify by retarding the left throttle.", "src": "OEG 3C-14 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-030", "t": "eo", "q": "Why can engine gauges mislead you when identifying a failed engine?", "a": "A windmilling prop can show high RPM, and MP can read near normal", "d": ["The failed engine's gauges always drop to zero immediately", "Both engines' gauges share a single sensor, so they always read the same", "Engine gauges are only accurate above about 5,000 feet density altitude"], "why": "Depending on whether the failure is partial or complete, the dead engine's MP can read close to the good engine's, and a windmilling prop keeps RPM up. A dead engine can look alive, so identify it with control inputs.", "src": "OEG 3C-15 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-031", "t": "eo", "q": "The right engine fails. Which way will the airplane yaw, and which rudder do you apply?", "a": "Yaws right; apply left rudder immediately", "d": ["Yaws left; apply right rudder immediately", "Yaws right; apply right rudder immediately", "Yaws left; apply left rudder immediately"], "why": "The good left engine's thrust swings the nose toward the dead right engine, and left rudder stops that yaw.", "src": "OEG 3C-16 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-032", "t": "eo", "q": "An engine fails while you are in IMC. What should get priority in your scan?", "a": "The flight instruments, holding heading and airspeed", "d": ["The engine gauges, to find out which engine has failed", "The fuel gauges, to rule out fuel starvation", "The GPS, to locate the nearest airport"], "why": "You identify the engine the same way as in VMC, but in IMC you keep scanning the flight instruments to hold heading (mainly with rudder) and airspeed. Rudder pressure still tells you which engine quit.", "src": "OEG 3C-17 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-033", "t": "eo", "q": "Immediately after an engine fails in IMC, before you correct, which way does the turn coordinator ball move?", "a": "Toward the operative engine", "d": ["Toward the inoperative engine", "It stays centered", "Back and forth, with no trend"], "why": "The airplane yaws toward the dead engine and the ball swings the other way, toward the good engine. Rudder on the good-engine side stops the yaw.", "src": "OEG 3C-17 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-034", "t": "eo", "q": "An engine fails after takeoff. The airplane is under control and can climb, but trees ahead must be cleared. Which speed should you fly?", "a": "Vxse", "d": ["Vyse", "Vsse", "Vmc"], "why": "Vxse gives the most altitude gain over the shortest distance on one engine, which is what clearing an obstacle needs. Vyse gives the best rate of climb, not the best angle.", "src": "OEG 3C-18 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-035", "t": "eo", "q": "An engine fails just before liftoff and you close both throttles to reject. Per the OEG, what do you use to keep the airplane on the runway?", "a": "Rudder, nosewheel steering and brakes, aggressively if needed", "d": ["Rudder alone, since braking would skid you toward the dead engine", "Power on the good engine plus rudder to hold the centerline", "Ailerons into the dead engine plus light, gentle braking"], "why": "With both throttles closed, directional control comes from rudder, nosewheel steering and brakes, and the OEG warns they may need to be used hard. Adding power on one engine would bring back the very yaw you are fighting.", "src": "OEG 3C-19 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-036", "t": "eo", "q": "In a rejected takeoff after an engine failure, what is the primary objective?", "a": "Keep control of the airplane as it decelerates", "d": ["Stop the airplane in the shortest possible distance", "Exit the runway at the nearest taxiway", "Secure the failed engine before the airplane stops"], "why": "Aggressive rudder, steering and braking may be needed, but what matters is staying in control while you slow down; the shortest possible stop is secondary.", "src": "OEG 3C-19 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-038", "t": "eo", "q": "Just after liftoff, with the gear still down and runway remaining, an engine fails. What should you do?", "a": "Close both throttles and land on the remaining runway or overrun", "d": ["Raise the gear, feather the failed engine, and then climb out at Vyse", "Feather first, then decide whether to land or continue", "Stay in ground effect and accelerate to Vyse before climbing out"], "why": "With the gear still down, keep straight, close both throttles, lower the nose to hold airspeed, and make a normal landing ahead. The chances of cleaning up, feathering and accelerating under control are minimal. The Seneca POH 3-12a agrees when adequate runway remains.", "src": "OEG 3C-21 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-039", "t": "eo", "q": "In a twin with a single engine-driven hydraulic pump, why does losing that engine on takeoff matter for the gear?", "a": "Raising the gear requires a windmilling engine or a hand pump", "d": ["The gear still retracts normally using the other engine's pump", "The gear can only be raised by using the emergency extension system", "The gear raises electrically, so there is no effect"], "why": "With the pump's engine dead, the only ways to raise the gear are to let that engine windmill or to use a hand pump. Neither is practical during a takeoff emergency, which is one more reason to land ahead. (The Seneca's gear pump is electric, POH 2-4, so this case doesn't apply to it.)", "src": "OEG 3C-21 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-040", "t": "eo", "q": "An engine fails just after liftoff with the gear up. At Vyse with full power on the good engine, you are still sinking. What should you do?", "a": "Land under control ahead, descending at Vyse if needed", "d": ["Raise the nose to hold altitude, letting speed bleed off", "Turn back toward the runway while holding your altitude", "Slow to Vxse to stop the descent and hold altitude"], "why": "Near or above the single-engine ceiling you must land on essentially whatever lies ahead, or keep going ahead in a descent at Vyse. Trying to hold altitude by bleeding off airspeed is almost always fatal.", "src": "OEG 3C-22 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-041", "t": "eo", "q": "Per the OEG, what is almost invariably fatal after an engine failure on takeoff?", "a": "Staying airborne by bleeding off airspeed to hold altitude", "d": ["Landing straight ahead in an off-airport field", "Descending straight ahead at Vyse with the good engine at full power", "Landing with the landing gear still retracted"], "why": "The killer is insisting on flight the airplane can't sustain. A controlled landing, even off-airport or gear up, is far more survivable than a stall or Vmc roll.", "src": "OEG 3C-22 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-042", "t": "eo", "q": "After an engine failure on takeoff with the gear up and adequate climb performance, what are the four areas of concern, in order?", "a": "Control, configuration, climb, checklist", "d": ["Aviate, navigate, communicate, checklist", "Identify, verify, feather, secure", "Climb, configuration, control, checklist"], "why": "Control comes first, using aileron and rudder aggressively if needed. Then the memory items configure the airplane for climb, then the bank is reduced to the best-climb angle, and last come the printed checklist and the securing checklist.", "src": "OEG 3C-23 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-044", "t": "eo", "q": "Shortly after liftoff an engine fails, and even with full power on the other you can't climb. What speed gives the slowest sink rate?", "a": "Vyse", "d": ["Vxse", "Vsse", "Vmc"], "why": "Vyse is the best single-engine rate-of-climb speed, so when a climb isn't possible it gives the least rate of sink (drift-down).", "src": "OEG 3C-24 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-045", "t": "eo", "q": "After an engine failure on departure, what two bank angles are used, in order?", "a": "First a bank for directional control, then a zero-sideslip bank for climb", "d": ["First a zero-sideslip bank for control, then a steeper bank for best climb", "First wings level for control, then a bank toward the dead engine for climb", "First a bank toward the dead engine, then wings level for climb"], "why": "The first bank toward the good engine helps the rudder overcome the yaw from asymmetric thrust. Once you have control, you move to the shallower zero-sideslip bank, a technique for coordinated flight and best climb rather than a fixed number.", "src": "OEG 3C-25 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-046", "t": "eo", "q": "In a typical light twin, per the OEG, what bank helps the rudder right after an engine failure, while you pitch for Vyse?", "a": "5 to 10 degrees toward the operating engine", "d": ["2 to 3 degrees toward the operating engine", "15 to 20 degrees toward the operating engine", "5 to 10 degrees toward the inoperative engine"], "why": "Per the OEG, 5–10° toward the good engine helps the rudder at first; the smaller zero-sideslip bank (about 2°: 2–3° in the exam tip, 1.5–2.5° in OEG 3B-12) follows for best climb. Never bank toward the dead engine. The Seneca POH 3-11 says to maintain 5° of bank toward the operating engine.", "src": "OEG 3C-37 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-047", "t": "eo", "q": "In a typical light twin, per the OEG, what bank and ball position give zero sideslip for best climb at Vyse?", "a": "About 2–3° bank, ball slightly toward the operating engine", "d": ["About 5–10° bank toward the operating engine, ball centered", "Wings level, ball centered", "About 2–3° bank, ball slightly toward the inoperative engine"], "why": "A small bank toward the good engine, with the ball slightly toward that side, cancels the sideslip and minimizes drag; wings level with the ball centered leaves a sideslip. 2–3° is the OEG exam tip's figure; OEG 3B-12 gives 1.5–2.5°, so think 'about 2°'. The Seneca POH 3-11 instead calls for 5° of bank toward the operating engine.", "src": "OEG 3C-37 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-048", "t": "eo", "q": "Per the ACS, what minimum altitude must be reached before an evaluator may simulate an engine failure after liftoff?", "a": "400 feet AGL", "d": ["200 feet AGL", "500 feet AGL", "1,000 feet AGL"], "why": "The ACS sets 400 feet AGL as the minimum, along with at least Vsse, Vxse or Vyse. The OEG recommends the same limits for practice.", "src": "OEG 3C-26 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o7-049", "t": "eo", "q": "Besides altitude, what airspeed must be reached before an engine failure is simulated after liftoff?", "a": "At least Vsse, Vxse, or Vyse", "d": ["At least Vmc", "At least rotation speed (Vr)", "At least 1.1 times Vso"], "why": "The evaluator may not simulate a failure until the airplane is at least 400 feet AGL and at least at the safe single-engine speed (Vsse), Vxse or Vyse. Vmc or rotation speed leaves no margin.", "src": "OEG 3C-26 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o7-050", "t": "eo", "q": "Right after takeoff, why does the OEG value altitude over airspeed in excess of Vyse?", "a": "Extra speed can't easily be converted to needed altitude or distance", "d": ["Extra speed raises Vmc and so reduces your margin of directional control", "Extra speed overheats both engines during the initial climb", "Extra speed keeps the failed propeller from feathering fully"], "why": "Once at a safe height a twin can hold level on one engine far more easily than it can climb, and spare altitude can be cashed in for speed or glide distance. Spare airspeed can't be turned into the height you need nearly as readily.", "src": "OEG 3C-27 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-051", "t": "eo", "q": "An engine fails just after gear retraction, near the single-engine ceiling, with no runway left. What should you do with the gear?", "a": "Leave it up until committed to a landing; extending it costs climb", "d": ["Lower it immediately; a twin should never be landed with the gear up", "Lower it to stabilize the airplane, then try to climb", "Cycle it down and up again to confirm the hydraulic system still works"], "why": "Lowering the gear can cost the climb you badly need, so wait until you are positioned to land. Controlled off-airport engine-out landings, even gear up, usually turn out well. The Seneca POH 3-13 likewise says not to extend the gear until certain of making the field.", "src": "OEG 3C-28 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-052", "t": "eo", "q": "Compared with a failure at low altitude, what does an engine failure in cruise give you, and what stays the same?", "a": "Time to diagnose and fix it; control is still the top priority", "d": ["Time to diagnose it; you never need to feather an engine in cruise", "Better control; calling ATC now comes before flying", "No extra time; you must still feather at once, just as down low"], "why": "Cruise speed improves control and altitude buys time, so diagnosis and a fix may be possible. Flying the airplane still comes first.", "src": "OEG 3C-29 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-053", "t": "eo", "q": "If an en route engine failure can't be corrected, what does the OEG recommend?", "a": "Do the manufacturer's single-engine procedure; land as soon as possible", "d": ["Continue on to the planned destination as long as fuel permits", "Keep attempting restarts until the failed engine is running normally again", "Climb to the single-engine service ceiling and hold there"], "why": "If you can't fix the problem, run the manufacturer's single-engine procedure and get on the ground as soon as possible. Repeated restarts and pressing on to the destination add risk.", "src": "OEG 3C-29 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-054", "t": "eo", "q": "Once the immediate emergency is handled, what is the main problem after an en route engine failure?", "a": "Keeping enough altitude to reach the nearest suitable airport", "d": ["Keeping the failed engine warm enough for a later restart attempt", "Maintaining the planned cruise airspeed and ETA to the destination", "Keeping fuel balanced between the two tanks"], "why": "Whether you can hold enough altitude to reach a suitable airport depends on density altitude, gross weight, and the elevation of terrain and obstructions.", "src": "OEG 3C-30 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-055", "t": "eo", "q": "An engine fails at 11,000 ft, above the airplane's single-engine absolute ceiling. What will happen, and what speed should you fly?", "a": "It slowly loses altitude; fly Vyse to minimize the descent", "d": ["It holds altitude; fly Vyse to maintain it", "It slowly loses altitude; fly Vxse to minimize the descent", "It holds altitude; fly Vmc to minimize drag"], "why": "Above the single-engine absolute ceiling the airplane can't hold altitude and slowly drifts down. Vyse keeps the rate of altitude loss as low as possible.", "src": "OEG 3C-30 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-056", "t": "eo", "q": "Per the OEG, many en route power losses come from what cause, with what simple fix?", "a": "Fuel starvation; select another fuel tank", "d": ["Magneto failure; switch both magnetos off", "Induction icing; lean the mixture fully", "Oil starvation; reduce the power to idle"], "why": "Fuel starvation is a common cause, and switching tanks often brings the power back; in the Seneca, the POH 3-11 restore-power note includes crossfeed. A methodical check of gauges and switches, alternate air or carb heat, single magnetos, mixture and boost pump also helps.", "src": "OEG 3C-31 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-057", "t": "eo", "q": "En route, one engine's fuel flow and pressure begin to fluctuate and you suspect fuel vapor. What does the OEG suggest?", "a": "Turn on that engine's electric fuel boost pump", "d": ["Pull that engine's mixture to cutoff briefly", "Close that engine's cowl flap to warm it up", "Turn that engine's magnetos off and back on"], "why": "When vapor is suspected, running the boost pump can clear the fuel flow and pressure fluctuations. An engine that runs rough may also run smoothly on one magneto or at lower power.", "src": "OEG 3C-31 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-058", "t": "eo", "q": "A turbocharged engine loses power at high altitude and you suspect the turbocharger has failed. What might restore some power?", "a": "Lean the mixture further and/or descend to a lower altitude", "d": ["Enrich the mixture fully and climb to a higher altitude", "Close the throttle partway and select alternate air", "Increase propeller RPM and climb to a higher altitude"], "why": "Without boost, the engine gets too much fuel for the thin air, so leaning helps. At a lower altitude it runs better as what is now a normally aspirated engine.", "src": "OEG 3C-32 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-059", "t": "eo", "q": "After feathering a failed engine, which actions normally complete securing it?", "a": "Fuel (mixture, pump, selector), ignition and alternator off; cowl flap closed", "d": ["Fuel on (pump, selector); ignition and alternator off; cowl flap open", "Fuel (mixture, pump, selector) and ignition off; alternator on; cowl flap open", "Fuel and ignition left on for a restart; alternator off; cowl flap closed"], "why": "Securing cuts off fuel, spark and electrical load to the dead engine, and closing its cowl flap reduces drag. Pressurized twins may also have a bleed-air valve to close. The Seneca POH 3-11 feathering procedure covers the same items.", "src": "OEG 3C-33 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-060", "t": "eo", "q": "An engine was shut down in flight because of an oil leak. Why is an attempted restart risky?", "a": "More engine damage, a worse leak, or an in-flight fire", "d": ["The propeller will automatically re-feather on restart", "The starter will not engage when oil pressure is low", "The restart will discharge the battery below minimums"], "why": "An engine shut down for a mechanical reason can be damaged further if it's restarted, and an oil or fuel leak can get worse and start a fire. Leave it secured unless there is a compelling reason.", "src": "OEG 3C-34 · SAIB CE-05-51"},
{"id": "o7-061", "t": "eo", "q": "During a restart attempt, the prop comes out of feather but the engine won't start, and the prop can't be re-feathered. Why?", "a": "Windmilling RPM is too low, so the start locks block feathering", "d": ["The unfeathering accumulator must be recharged before feathering", "The prop governor stays locked at its high-RPM stop after unfeathering", "The feathering spring is damaged by every unfeathering cycle"], "why": "If the prop windmills below the start-lock disengagement speed, the locks stay engaged and it can't return to feather; the windmilling drag may make level flight impossible. The Seneca can be feathered only above 800 RPM (POH 3-11).", "src": "OEG 3C-34 · SAIB CE-05-51"},
{"id": "o7-062", "t": "eo", "q": "What is the result if the propeller is left windmilling after a failed in-flight restart?", "a": "Significant drag; the airplane may be unable to hold level flight", "d": ["Less drag than a feathered prop, so climb performance improves", "About the same drag as a feathered prop, so nothing changes", "Only a small loss of cruise speed, with no effect on climb"], "why": "A windmilling prop produces far more drag than a feathered one, which can turn a manageable single-engine cruise into a forced descent.", "src": "OEG 3C-34 · SAIB CE-05-51"},
{"id": "o7-063", "t": "eo", "q": "Citing SAIB CE-05-51, what risk does the OEG warn of when shutting down and restarting an engine for training?", "a": "A faulty restart may leave a dead engine: a real emergency", "d": ["Feathering at altitude may damage the propeller governor", "A restart using the starter may overheat the battery", "A feathered prop may unfeather itself during the descent"], "why": "A practice shutdown can turn into an actual engine-out emergency if the restart goes wrong. Always follow the manufacturer's restart checklist and weigh all the hazards before shutting down for training.", "src": "OEG 3C-34 · SAIB CE-05-51"},
{"id": "o7-064", "t": "eo", "q": "Per the OEG, how should a typical light twin's single-engine traffic pattern compare with a normal two-engine pattern?", "a": "Essentially the same altitudes, airspeeds, and key positions", "d": ["Lower and tighter, to minimize the time spent flying on one engine", "A long, flat, low final to stay close to the runway", "Flown at Vxse throughout for a steeper descent"], "why": "The OEG treats an OEI pattern as basically a normal one: similar altitudes, speeds and key points, and no long, flat, low final. The Seneca POH 3-13 adds: carry extra altitude and speed on the approach.", "src": "OEG 3C-35 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-065", "t": "eo", "q": "Per the OEG, in a typical light twin with adequate performance, when should the gear be confirmed down on a single-engine approach?", "a": "No later than abeam the intended point of landing", "d": ["No later than the start of the turn onto the base leg", "No later than 500 feet AGL on the final approach", "Only once over the runway threshold"], "why": "Per the OEG, with adequate performance the gear can go down on downwind and should be confirmed down by the abeam point. The Seneca POH 3-13 differs: don't extend the gear until certain of making the field.", "src": "OEG 3C-35 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-066", "t": "eo", "q": "Per the OEG, what is the minimum airspeed on a single-engine approach until the landing is assured?", "a": "Vyse", "d": ["Vxse", "Vmc", "1.3 Vso"], "why": "Hold at least Vyse on downwind, base and final, because it keeps the best performance available until the landing is certain; slow to 1.3 Vso or the POH speed only after that. The Seneca POH 3-13 final approach speed is 105 mph, its Vyse.", "src": "OEG 3C-35 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-067", "t": "eo", "q": "On base with one engine out, your airspeed is decaying and the sink rate is high. What does the OEG say about more flaps?", "a": "Delay further flap extension until closer to the runway", "d": ["Extend full flaps now to steepen the approach", "Extend flaps and slow below Vyse to cut the sink", "Retract all flaps and climb back to pattern altitude"], "why": "Decaying airspeed or a high sink rate means performance is inadequate, so hold off on more flaps until closer to the runway; Vyse stays the minimum. The Seneca POH 3-13 says no flaps until certain of making the field, and 25° rather than full.", "src": "OEG 3C-35 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-068", "t": "eo", "q": "In a typical light twin, per the OEG, once a single-engine landing is assured, to what speed do you slow?", "a": "1.3 Vso, or the POH/AFM recommended speed", "d": ["Vmc, to minimize the total landing distance", "Vxse, to steepen the final descent angle", "Vsse, to keep a safety margin"], "why": "Hold Vyse until the landing is assured, then slow to 1.3 Vso or the speed the POH/AFM recommends; final flaps can wait, or you can land with partial flaps. The Seneca POH 3-13 gives 105 mph for the single-engine final approach.", "src": "OEG 3C-35 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-069", "t": "eo", "q": "Which approach profile should be avoided on a single-engine final?", "a": "A long, flat, low approach", "d": ["A normal 3° glidepath", "A slightly steeper than normal approach", "Following the VASI glidepath"], "why": "A normal 3° path is preferred, ideally using a VASI or similar aid, and slightly steeper is acceptable. A long, flat, low approach needs more power and leaves no margin if you come up short.", "src": "OEG 3C-35 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-070", "t": "eo", "q": "Besides a long, flat approach, what does the OEG say to avoid on a single-engine final?", "a": "Large, sudden power applications or reductions", "d": ["Landing with only partial flaps, rather than full flaps", "Using a VASI or other visual glidepath aid", "Delaying the final flap setting"], "why": "Big, abrupt power changes on one engine cause big yaw changes and make control harder. Landing with partial flaps, delaying final flaps and using a VASI are all acceptable.", "src": "OEG 3C-35 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-071", "t": "eo", "q": "Per the OEG, when should a typical light twin be configured and stabilized for a single-engine instrument approach?", "a": "Before reaching the final approach fix", "d": ["At the decision altitude or MDA", "Just after crossing the final approach fix", "At the missed approach point"], "why": "Configuring before the FAF (or on downwind for a VFR pattern) lets you stabilize, reduces Vmc effects and frees your attention for flying. The Seneca POH 3-13 is more conservative: no gear or flaps until certain of making the field.", "src": "OEG 3C-36 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-072", "t": "eo", "q": "Per the OEG, are single-engine go-arounds recommended in a light twin?", "a": "No; on final with gear and flaps down, you're committed to land", "d": ["Yes; they are safer than landing long on a single engine", "Yes, as long as the airspeed stays above Vmc throughout", "No; Part 23 prohibits go-arounds with an engine inoperative"], "why": "A light twin generally can't climb on one engine with gear and flaps down, and cleaning up while holding Vyse costs a lot of altitude. The Seneca POH 3-13 also says to avoid one if at all possible and gives a procedure only for when it can't be avoided.", "src": "OEG 3C-37 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-073", "t": "eo", "q": "On short final with one engine out, gear and flaps down, a vehicle enters the runway. Per the OEG, what is the best choice?", "a": "Land on another runway, a taxiway, or the grass", "d": ["Go around at full power on the operating engine", "Retract the flaps and climb away at Vxse", "Slow to Vmc and extend the final approach"], "why": "Once configured on final with one engine, you are committed to land. If the intended runway is blocked, land on another runway, a taxiway or the infield rather than attempt a single-engine go-around.", "src": "OEG 3C-37 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-074", "t": "eo", "q": "Roughly how much altitude can a light twin lose while retracting gear and flaps at Vyse in a single-engine go-around?", "a": "500 feet or more", "d": ["About 50 feet", "About 100 feet", "None, if Vyse is held"], "why": "Losses of 500 feet or more are not unusual. If the gear went down by an alternate (emergency) method, it may not retract at all, which wipes out nearly all climb ability.", "src": "OEG 3C-37 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-075", "t": "eo", "q": "You start a go-around from short final with the gear down and full flaps. Unless the POH says otherwise, what do you retract first?", "a": "The flaps (at least partially), then the gear", "d": ["The gear first, then the flaps", "Gear and flaps together, immediately", "The gear first; flaps after 1,000 feet AGL"], "why": "After setting climb attitude and power, bring the flaps up partially or to the takeoff setting. Retract the gear once a positive rate of climb is established and you are sure the airplane will stay airborne. Your school's Seneca go-around does the same: flaps to 25°, gear up on a positive rate, then the rest of the flaps.", "src": "OEG 3C-38 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-076", "t": "eo", "q": "Why are the flaps generally retracted before the gear during a go-around?", "a": "Full flaps add more drag than gear, and gear down helps if you touch down", "d": ["The gear adds more drag than full flaps do, and the flaps improve the climb", "The gear motor cannot run while the flaps are still moving", "Retracting the gear first makes the airplane pitch down sharply near the ground"], "why": "On most airplanes full flaps cause more drag than the gear. Leaving the gear down and locked also protects you if the airplane touches down as the go-around begins.", "src": "OEG 3C-38 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-077", "t": "eo", "q": "Why should flaps be retracted in small increments during a go-around?", "a": "A sudden full retraction loses lift and can settle the airplane", "d": ["The flap motor overheats if it is run continuously for long", "Small increments keep the airspeed safely below Vfe", "Small increments keep the gear warning horn from sounding"], "why": "Raising the flaps a little at a time lets the airplane accelerate as lift is reduced. Dumping them all at once can make it sink back toward the ground.", "src": "OEG 3C-38 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-078", "t": "eo", "q": "In a very low go-around, the airplane settles onto the runway and bounces. How does the OEG view this?", "a": "Not a big danger if kept straight at a steady, safe pitch", "d": ["An emergency that calls for an immediate power reduction", "A cue to retract the landing gear at once to reduce drag", "A cue to pull the nose up sharply and climb away quickly"], "why": "Go-around power brings the airplane to flying speed quickly and softens any second contact with the runway. Keep it straight, hold the pitch, and raise the gear only once you are sure it will stay airborne.", "src": "OEG 3C-38 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o7-079", "t": "eo", "q": "Per the OEG, if a second pilot is aboard, what should you agree on before takeoff to reduce engine-failure risk?", "a": "Pilot flying and pilot not flying duties in an emergency", "d": ["Which pilot will do the cruise fuel and power planning", "That both pilots share the controls during any emergency", "That the second pilot stays hands-off and silent throughout"], "why": "Settling in advance who flies and who handles checklists, radios and callouts prevents both pilots grabbing the controls, or neither acting, when an engine quits.", "src": "OEG 3C-3 · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-001", "t": "acs", "q": "Per the OEG's chapter 4 overview, which flight maneuver is added on the Commercial multi-engine test that the Private test does not require?", "a": "Accelerated stalls", "d": ["Power-on stalls", "Emergency descent", "Steep turns"], "why": "The Private and Commercial multi-engine task lists are nearly the same; Commercial tolerances are a little tighter, and the accelerated stall (CA.VII.D) is the added maneuver. Steep turns are on both tests, just at 50° instead of 45° for Commercial.", "src": "OEG 4 · ACS CA.VII.D"},
{"id": "o8-002", "t": "acs", "q": "In the ACS Taxiing task, when should the brake check be performed?", "a": "Immediately after the airplane begins moving", "d": ["Before engine start, with the parking brake set", "At the hold-short line during the run-up", "Just before turning onto the runway"], "why": "The standard calls for checking the brakes right as the airplane starts to roll, so a problem is found while speed is low and you are still on the ramp.", "src": "OEG 4A · ACS PA.II.D/CA.II.D · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-003", "t": "acs", "q": "Ground issues a taxi route different from the one you almost always get at your home field, and you start taxiing the usual way. Which ACS taxi risk is this?", "a": "Expectation bias about taxi instructions", "d": ["Division of attention during preflight checks", "Wake turbulence from departing traffic", "Disembarking passengers on the ramp"], "why": "Hearing what you expect rather than what was said is expectation bias, a listed taxi risk alongside route or runway changes and runway incursions. Reading back and following the airport diagram mitigate it.", "src": "OEG 4A · ACS PA.II.D/CA.II.D · AC 91-73"},
{"id": "o8-004", "t": "acs", "q": "Per the ACS Before Takeoff Check, what should you consider when positioning the airplane for the run-up?", "a": "The wind and any nearby aircraft or buildings", "d": ["The departure runway heading and the current ATIS", "The hold-short line and the taxi route", "The field elevation and the current density altitude"], "why": "The task asks you to position the airplane with the wind and anything behind or around you in mind, so prop blast and run-up don't endanger others.", "src": "OEG 4B · ACS PA.II.F/CA.II.F · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-005", "t": "acs", "q": "In the run-up area you weigh runway length, surface condition, obstructions, and the airplane's characteristics. Which ACS before-takeoff risk are you addressing?", "a": "A powerplant failure during takeoff", "d": ["Division of attention during the checks", "Wake turbulence from departing traffic", "An unexpected runway change by ATC"], "why": "The Before Takeoff Check lists a potential powerplant failure during takeoff, to be assessed against airplane characteristics, runway/takeoff path length, surface, environment, and obstructions — the basis of your engine-failure plan.", "src": "OEG 4B · ACS PA.II.F/CA.II.F · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-006", "t": "acs", "q": "On a Private multi-engine checkride, what is the ACS airspeed tolerance for the Vy climb after a normal takeoff?", "a": "+10/−5 knots", "d": ["±5 knots", "±10 knots", "+5/−0 knots"], "why": "Private applicants must hold the manufacturer's recommended speed or Vy within +10/−5 knots. ±5 knots is the Commercial standard.", "src": "OEG 4C · ACS PA.IV.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-007", "t": "acs", "q": "On a Commercial multi-engine checkride, what airspeed tolerance applies to the Vy climb after a normal takeoff?", "a": "±5 knots", "d": ["+10/−5 knots", "±10 knots", "+5/−0 knots"], "why": "Commercial tightens the takeoff climb standard to ±5 knots of Vy (or the recommended speed); +10/−5 is the Private standard.", "src": "OEG 4C · ACS CA.IV.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-008", "t": "acs", "q": "After a normal takeoff, how long does the ACS require you to hold Vy within tolerance?", "a": "Until reaching a safe maneuvering altitude", "d": ["Until passing 50 feet above the runway surface", "Until the landing gear is up and the flaps retracted", "Until reaching 1,000 feet AGL"], "why": "The task says to maintain Vy to a safe maneuvering altitude. The 50-foot point is where a short-field takeoff transitions from Vx to Vy.", "src": "OEG 4C · ACS PA.IV.A/CA.IV.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-009", "t": "acs", "q": "The wind is calm on your checkride day. How does the ACS say your crosswind takeoff and landing knowledge will be evaluated?", "a": "Through oral testing", "d": ["It is waived for that checkride", "You must return on a crosswind day", "By flying a simulated crosswind correction"], "why": "The normal takeoff and landing tasks note that if no crosswind exists, the crosswind elements are evaluated orally — so be ready to explain the technique.", "src": "OEG 4C/4D · ACS PA.IV.A/CA.IV.A, PA.IV.B/CA.IV.B · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-010", "t": "acs", "q": "If the manufacturer publishes no approach speed, what does the ACS say to fly on a normal approach?", "a": "Not more than 1.3 times Vso", "d": ["Not more than 1.2 times Vso", "Not more than 1.5 times Vs1", "Blue line (Vyse) plus the gust factor"], "why": "Absent a published speed, the ACS standard is no more than 1.3 times the stalling speed or minimum steady flight speed in the landing configuration (Vso), with the gust factor applied. Separately, the OEG's twin technique (3A-21) is to fly no slower than Vyse until short final with the landing assured.", "src": "OEG 4D · ACS PA.IV.B/CA.IV.B · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-011", "t": "acs", "q": "The POH approach speed is 90 knots and the wind is calm. On a Private checkride, what airspeed range meets the ACS on a normal approach?", "a": "85 to 100 knots", "d": ["85 to 95 knots", "80 to 100 knots", "90 to 100 knots"], "why": "The Private standard is +10/−5 knots of the published approach speed: 90 − 5 = 85 and 90 + 10 = 100. A Commercial applicant would need 85 to 95 (±5).", "src": "OEG 4D · ACS PA.IV.B · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-012", "t": "acs", "q": "A Private applicant's normal landing touches down 300 feet past the specified point, with no drift and aligned with centerline. How does this compare to the ACS?", "a": "Within tolerance; Private allows up to 400 feet beyond", "d": ["Outside tolerance; Private allows only up to 200 feet beyond", "Outside tolerance; Private allows only 100 feet", "Within tolerance; there is no touchdown distance limit"], "why": "For a normal landing the Private standard is touchdown within 400 feet beyond or on the specified point, and Commercial is 200 feet. The short-field standards are tighter: 200 feet (Private) and 100 feet (Commercial).", "src": "OEG 4D · ACS PA.IV.B · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-013", "t": "acs", "q": "On a Commercial checkride, what is the ACS touchdown tolerance for a normal landing?", "a": "Within 200 feet beyond or on the specified point", "d": ["Within 400 feet beyond or on the specified point", "Within 100 feet beyond or on the specified point", "Within 250 feet beyond or on the specified point"], "why": "Commercial halves the Private normal-landing allowance: 200 feet instead of 400. The short-field Commercial standard is tighter still at 100 feet.", "src": "OEG 4D · ACS CA.IV.B · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-014", "t": "acs", "q": "On a normal approach at 300 feet AGL you are 15 knots fast and can't get stabilized. What does the ACS expect?", "a": "Execute a timely go-around", "d": ["Slip to bleed off the extra speed", "Land long and use firm braking", "Lower full flaps and continue"], "why": "The task requires a timely go-around whenever the approach can't be flown within tolerance or anything else makes the approach or landing unsafe. Being 15 knots fast exceeds even the Private +10 limit.", "src": "OEG 4D · ACS PA.IV.B/CA.IV.B · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-015", "t": "acs", "q": "How does the ACS want takeoff power set for a short-field takeoff?", "a": "Hold the brakes, set power, confirm it, then release", "d": ["Release the brakes first, then advance smoothly", "Set takeoff power during a rolling start", "Set half power, release brakes, then add the rest"], "why": "The task says to apply brakes while setting power for maximum performance and to confirm takeoff power before brake release, after lining up using the maximum available takeoff area.", "src": "OEG 4E · ACS PA.IV.E/CA.IV.E · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-016", "t": "acs", "q": "On a short-field takeoff, how long must you hold the recommended obstacle-clearance speed or Vx?", "a": "Until clear of the obstacle, or 50 feet AGL", "d": ["Until reaching 400 feet AGL", "Until the landing gear is fully retracted", "Until reaching a safe maneuvering altitude"], "why": "Hold Vx (or the recommended obstacle speed) until clear of the obstacle, or until 50 feet AGL when the obstacle is simulated, then pitch for Vy. Vy is then held to a safe maneuvering altitude.", "src": "OEG 4E · ACS PA.IV.E/CA.IV.E · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-017", "t": "acs", "q": "On a Commercial checkride, what airspeed tolerance applies to Vx during the short-field takeoff climb?", "a": "±5 knots", "d": ["+10/−5 knots", "+5/−0 knots", "±10 knots"], "why": "Commercial requires ±5 knots of Vx or the recommended obstacle-clearance speed; the Private standard is +10/−5 knots.", "src": "OEG 4E · ACS CA.IV.E · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-018", "t": "acs", "q": "On a Private checkride, what is the ACS touchdown tolerance for a short-field landing?", "a": "Within 200 feet beyond or on the specified point", "d": ["Within 400 feet beyond or on the specified point", "Within 100 feet beyond or on the specified point", "Within 50 feet beyond or on the specified point"], "why": "The Private short-field standard is 200 feet beyond or on the point (or threshold markings/numbers), with minimum float and no side drift. 400 feet is the Private normal-landing figure.", "src": "OEG 4F · ACS PA.IV.F · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-019", "t": "acs", "q": "A Commercial applicant's short-field landing touches down 150 feet past the specified point, with no drift and minimum float. How does this compare to the ACS?", "a": "Outside; the Commercial limit is 100 feet beyond", "d": ["Within; the Commercial limit is 200 feet beyond", "Within; the Commercial limit is 400 feet beyond", "Within, since there was no drift and minimum float"], "why": "Commercial short-field landings must touch down within 100 feet beyond or on the point. 200 feet would satisfy only the Private short-field standard.", "src": "OEG 4F · ACS CA.IV.F · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-020", "t": "acs", "q": "When you apply takeoff power on a go-around/rejected landing, what climb speed does the ACS have you pitch for?", "a": "Vx or Vy, as appropriate", "d": ["Vyse, in all cases", "Vxse until clear of obstacles", "Vmc plus 5 knots"], "why": "The go-around task is a two-engine maneuver: apply takeoff power right away and pitch for Vx or Vy as the situation needs (+10/−5 Private, ±5 Commercial), then hold Vy to a safe maneuvering altitude.", "src": "OEG 4G · ACS PA.IV.N/CA.IV.N · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-021", "t": "acs", "q": "During a go-around, a departing airplane is climbing out below you over the runway. What does the ACS expect you to do?", "a": "Move to the side of the runway to avoid it", "d": ["Stay over the centerline and climb steeply", "Reduce power and hold altitude above it", "Continue the approach and land behind it"], "why": "The task includes moving to the side of the runway or landing area when needed to keep the conflicting traffic in sight and avoid it.", "src": "OEG 4G · ACS PA.IV.N/CA.IV.N · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-022", "t": "acs", "q": "Per the OEG's exam tip, the ACS Go-Around/Rejected Landing task is not intended to be evaluated in what configuration?", "a": "Single-engine configuration", "d": ["Full-flap landing configuration", "Gear-down approach configuration", "Normal two-engine configuration"], "why": "The go-around task is meant for both engines operating. Single-engine go-around risk shows up instead under the engine-inoperative approach tasks.", "src": "OEG 4G · ACS PA.IV.N/CA.IV.N · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-023", "t": "acs", "q": "Which is an ACS risk-management item for the Go-Around/Rejected Landing task?", "a": "Delayed recognition of the need to go around", "d": ["Fuel management during single-engine operation", "Management of compressed gas containers", "Airport-specific security procedures"], "why": "Go-around risks include delayed recognition, delayed performance at low altitude, power application, configuring the airplane, and a go-around after accepting LAHSO. The others belong to the single-engine instrument, oxygen, and parking tasks.", "src": "OEG 4G · ACS PA.IV.N/CA.IV.N · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-024", "t": "acs", "q": "What bank angle does the ACS require for steep turns on a Private multi-engine checkride?", "a": "Approximately 45°", "d": ["Approximately 50°", "Approximately 30°", "Approximately 60°"], "why": "Private steep turns are coordinated 360° turns at about 45° of bank; Commercial applicants use about 50°.", "src": "OEG 4H · ACS PA.V.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-025", "t": "acs", "q": "What bank angle does the ACS require for steep turns on a Commercial multi-engine checkride?", "a": "Approximately 50°", "d": ["Approximately 45°", "Approximately 55°", "Approximately 60°"], "why": "Commercial steep turns call for about 50° of bank, 5° steeper than the Private 45°.", "src": "OEG 4H · ACS CA.V.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-026", "t": "acs", "q": "Per the OEG's ACS excerpt, what altitude and airspeed tolerances apply during steep turns (same for Private and Commercial)?", "a": "Altitude ±100 feet and airspeed ±10 knots", "d": ["Altitude ±50 feet and airspeed ±5 knots", "Altitude ±100 feet and airspeed +10/−5 knots", "Altitude ±200 feet and airspeed ±10 knots"], "why": "Hold the entry altitude within ±100 feet and airspeed within ±10 knots throughout the 360° turn.", "src": "OEG 4H · ACS PA.V.A/CA.V.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-027", "t": "acs", "q": "In ACS steep turns, what are the bank-angle and rollout-heading tolerances (same for Private and Commercial)?", "a": "Bank ±5°; roll out on the entry heading ±10°", "d": ["Bank ±10°; roll out on the entry heading ±10°", "Bank ±5°; roll out on the entry heading ±5°", "Bank ±10°; roll out on the entry heading ±20°"], "why": "Bank must stay within ±5° of the target, and the rollout must be within ±10° of the entry heading.", "src": "OEG 4H · ACS PA.V.A/CA.V.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-028", "t": "acs", "q": "If the manufacturer gives no recommended steep-turn airspeed, what does the ACS specify?", "a": "Not more than Va", "d": ["Anywhere in the green arc", "Vyse plus 10 knots", "At or above Va"], "why": "Staying at or below Va keeps the structure protected because the wing stalls before the limit load factor is exceeded. Remember Va decreases as weight decreases.", "src": "OEG 4H · ACS PA.V.A/CA.V.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-029", "t": "acs", "q": "Terrain below is 1,000 feet MSL. On a multi-engine checkride, what is the lowest altitude at which slow flight may be completed?", "a": "4,000 feet MSL", "d": ["2,500 feet MSL", "3,000 feet MSL", "3,500 feet MSL"], "why": "Multi-engine slow flight must be completed no lower than 3,000 feet AGL: 1,000 + 3,000 = 4,000 MSL. The 1,500 feet AGL figure (2,500 MSL here) is the single-engine-airplane standard.", "src": "OEG 4I · ACS PA.VII.A/CA.VII.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-030", "t": "acs", "q": "How does the ACS define the airspeed to fly during slow flight?", "a": "Where more AOA or load factor, or less power, gives a stall warning", "d": ["About 5 knots above the published power-off stall speed", "1.3 times Vso, with the airplane in the landing configuration", "The lowest speed at which altitude can be held with full power"], "why": "Slow flight is flown just above the stall warning: any further increase in angle of attack or load factor, or reduction in power, would trigger the horn or buffet. Then fly straight, turns, climbs, and descents without a warning.", "src": "OEG 4I · ACS PA.VII.A/CA.VII.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-031", "t": "acs", "q": "What are the ACS slow-flight tolerances on a Private checkride?", "a": "Altitude ±100 ft, heading ±10°, airspeed +10/−0 kt, bank ±10°", "d": ["Altitude ±50 ft, heading ±10°, airspeed +5/−0 kt, bank ±5°", "Altitude ±100 ft, heading ±10°, airspeed ±10 kt, bank ±5°", "Altitude ±100 ft, heading ±20°, airspeed +10/−5 kt, bank ±10°"], "why": "Private: ±100 feet, ±10° heading, +10/−0 knots, ±10° bank. The airspeed tolerance has no minus side because going slower means a stall warning.", "src": "OEG 4I · ACS PA.VII.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-032", "t": "acs", "q": "What are the ACS slow-flight altitude, airspeed, and bank tolerances on a Commercial checkride?", "a": "Altitude ±50 ft, airspeed +5/−0 kt, bank ±5°", "d": ["Altitude ±100 ft, airspeed +10/−0 kt, bank ±10°", "Altitude ±50 ft, airspeed ±5 kt, bank ±10°", "Altitude ±100 ft, airspeed +5/−0 kt, bank ±5°"], "why": "Commercial tightens slow flight to ±50 feet, +5/−0 knots, and ±5° bank; heading stays ±10° at both levels.", "src": "OEG 4I · ACS CA.VII.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-033", "t": "acs", "q": "During slow flight a gust briefly sounds the stall horn, and you immediately correct. How does the ACS treat this?", "a": "Not unsatisfactory, because you recognized and corrected it", "d": ["Unsatisfactory, since any stall warning fails the task", "Acceptable only on the Private, not the Commercial, test", "Acceptable only if you then do a full stall recovery"], "why": "The ACS note says a momentary warning caused by turbulence, promptly recognized and corrected, is not a failure. Continual deviation, no correction, or not recognizing it would be.", "src": "OEG 4I · ACS PA.VII.A/CA.VII.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-034", "t": "acs", "q": "For an ACS power-off stall, how is the airplane configured before inducing the stall?", "a": "Approach or landing configuration, in a stabilized descent", "d": ["Takeoff or departure configuration, in a stabilized climb", "Cruise configuration, in straight-and-level flight", "Takeoff configuration, with one engine at idle and windmilling"], "why": "Power-off stalls simulate the approach and landing phase: set approach or landing configuration, establish a stabilized descent, then pitch smoothly to induce the stall.", "src": "OEG 4J · ACS PA.VII.B/CA.VII.B · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-035", "t": "acs", "q": "On a power-off stall (Private or Commercial), when does the ACS have you recover?", "a": "Promptly after a full stall occurs", "d": ["At the first indication of the stall warning", "Before any buffet can be felt", "Only after the nose drops below the horizon"], "why": "Acknowledge the impending-stall cues, then recover promptly once the full stall occurs, using the POH/AFM procedure.", "src": "OEG 4J · ACS PA.VII.B/CA.VII.B · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-036", "t": "acs", "q": "During a turning power-off stall on a Commercial checkride, what bank limits apply while inducing the stall?", "a": "Specified bank not over 20°, held within ±5°", "d": ["Specified bank not over 20°, held within ±10°", "Specified bank not over 30°, held within ±5°", "Specified bank not over 45°, held within ±10°"], "why": "The bank may not exceed 20°; it must be held within ±5° for Commercial or ±10° for Private. In straight flight, heading is held within ±10°.", "src": "OEG 4J · ACS CA.VII.B · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-037", "t": "acs", "q": "How do the ACS stall tasks treat altitude lost during the recovery?", "a": "No set altitude-loss value; holding altitude isn't required", "d": ["Altitude loss must be held under 100 feet in recovery", "Altitude must be held constant throughout the recovery", "Altitude loss must not exceed the POH's published value"], "why": "The ACS notes for power-off and power-on stalls say evaluators should not require a set altitude loss or require holding altitude; recovery means reducing angle of attack first, and many factors affect the altitude used.", "src": "OEG 4J/4K · ACS PA.VII.B/CA.VII.B, PA.VII.C/CA.VII.C · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-038", "t": "acs", "q": "What is the minimum power setting the ACS specifies for power-on stalls?", "a": "No less than 65 percent power", "d": ["No less than 55 percent power", "No less than 75 percent power", "Full takeoff power only"], "why": "Power is set as the evaluator assigns, but no less than 65 percent, simulating a departure or takeoff stall.", "src": "OEG 4K · ACS PA.VII.C/CA.VII.C · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-039", "t": "acs", "q": "In a high-performance twin, 65% power would need more than 30° nose-up to stall. What does the ACS note allow?", "a": "Reducing power below the ACS guideline", "d": ["Pitching beyond 30° nose-up to reach the stall", "Using a steep bank to stall sooner", "Skipping the power-on stall task entirely"], "why": "The note recognizes that some airplanes need less power to avoid excessively high pitch attitudes (over 30° nose-up), so power may be reduced below the 65% guideline.", "src": "OEG 4K · ACS PA.VII.C/CA.VII.C · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-040", "t": "acs", "q": "For an ACS power-on stall, which configurations may the evaluator specify?", "a": "Takeoff, departure, or cruise", "d": ["Approach or landing only", "Landing, with the gear down", "Cruise, with one engine at idle"], "why": "Power-on stalls represent the takeoff/departure phase, so the airplane is set in takeoff, departure, or cruise configuration. Approach or landing configuration is used for power-off stalls.", "src": "OEG 4K · ACS PA.VII.C/CA.VII.C · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-041", "t": "acs", "q": "On a Commercial checkride, when do you recover from a power-on stall?", "a": "First indication or full stall, as the evaluator specifies", "d": ["Always at a full stall, never at the first indication", "Always at the first stall horn, never at full stall", "When the airspeed decays to the red-line Vmc"], "why": "Commercial applicants recover at the first indication or after a full stall, as the evaluator specifies. Private applicants recover promptly after the full stall.", "src": "OEG 4K · ACS CA.VII.C · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-042", "t": "acs", "q": "Which stall type is listed in the power-on stall risk management but not in the power-off stall list?", "a": "Elevator trim stalls", "d": ["Secondary stalls", "Cross-control stalls", "Accelerated stalls"], "why": "Both lists include secondary, accelerated, and cross-control stalls; the power-on list adds elevator trim stalls — the nose-up trim surprise when full power is added with the airplane trimmed for approach.", "src": "OEG 4K · ACS PA.VII.C/CA.VII.C · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-043", "t": "acs", "q": "In the ACS accelerated stall (Commercial), what bank angle is used while increasing back pressure?", "a": "45°, in a coordinated turn", "d": ["20°, in a coordinated turn", "30°, in a coordinated turn", "60°, in a coordinated turn"], "why": "Establish a coordinated 45° banked turn and increase elevator back pressure smoothly and firmly until an impending stall. 20° is the bank limit for the power-on and power-off stall tasks.", "src": "OEG 4L · ACS CA.VII.D · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-044", "t": "acs", "q": "On the Commercial accelerated stall, what airspeed limit does the ACS impose when setting power?", "a": "Not above Va or other POH/AFM limits", "d": ["Not below Vno, the top of the green arc", "Not below Vyse at any point in the turn", "Not above Vx at the entry"], "why": "Because the stall is induced with extra load factor, the entry must not exceed maneuvering speed (or other POH limits) so the wing stalls before structural limits are reached.", "src": "OEG 4L · ACS CA.VII.D · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-045", "t": "acs", "q": "During the Commercial accelerated stall, when do you acknowledge the cues and recover?", "a": "At the first indication of a stall", "d": ["After the full stall breaks", "After the buffet becomes continuous", "When the airspeed falls below Vs1"], "why": "The accelerated stall is taken only to an impending stall: acknowledge the first indication (buffet, horn) and recover per the POH, then accelerate to Vx or Vy.", "src": "OEG 4L · ACS CA.VII.D · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-046", "t": "acs", "q": "Your training twin has no oxygen equipment. In the ACS Supplemental Oxygen task, what can you still be asked to do?", "a": "Determine oxygen needed and brief passengers", "d": ["Nothing; the entire task is skipped", "Climb above 12,500 ft MSL to show symptoms", "Obtain and install a portable system"], "why": "Operating the equipment applies only if it is installed or available, but determining the oxygen quantity required and briefing passengers are both done for a scenario the evaluator gives.", "src": "OEG 4M · ACS PA.I.G/CA.VIII.A · AC 61-107 · 14 CFR Part 91"},
{"id": "o8-047", "t": "acs", "q": "Which risk-management item is part of the ACS Supplemental Oxygen task?", "a": "Combustion hazards in an oxygen-rich environment", "d": ["Fuel management during single-engine operation", "Airport-specific security procedures", "Wake turbulence in the run-up area"], "why": "Oxygen risks are high-altitude flight, use of supplemental oxygen, management of compressed gas containers, and combustion hazards in an oxygen-rich environment.", "src": "OEG 4M · ACS PA.I.G/CA.VIII.A · AC 61-107"},
{"id": "o8-048", "t": "acs", "q": "The OEG lists the Pressurization task under the Commercial ACS (CA.VIII.B). When do its operate/respond/brief elements apply?", "a": "Only if the equipment is installed", "d": ["On every multi-engine practical test", "Only on Private-level practical tests", "Only when flying above 12,500 feet MSL"], "why": "Each skill element — operating the system, handling simulated malfunctions, and briefing oxygen use after a pressurization failure — is qualified with \"if equipment is installed.\"", "src": "OEG 4N · ACS CA.VIII.B · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-049", "t": "acs", "q": "What bank angle does the ACS specify during an emergency descent, and why?", "a": "30° to 45°, to keep positive load factors", "d": ["15° to 30°, to limit the total altitude lost", "45° to 60°, to steepen the descent", "Wings level, so the airspeed stays below Vne"], "why": "Banking 30° to 45° keeps the load factor positive while the nose is low in a steep descent, instead of pushing over toward zero or negative G.", "src": "OEG 4O · ACS PA.IX.A/CA.IX.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-050", "t": "acs", "q": "What airspeed and level-off tolerances apply to the ACS emergency descent (same for Private and Commercial)?", "a": "Airspeed +0/−10 knots; level off ±100 feet", "d": ["Airspeed ±10 knots; level off ±50 feet", "Airspeed +10/−0 knots; level off ±100 feet", "Airspeed ±5 knots; level off ±200 feet"], "why": "Airspeed may run up to 10 knots below the target but never above it (+0/−10), and you level off at the specified altitude within ±100 feet. These are the same at both certificate levels.", "src": "OEG 4O · ACS PA.IX.A/CA.IX.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-051", "t": "acs", "q": "Calculated Vmc is 70 knots. A simulated engine failure on the takeoff roll should be given before about what speed?", "a": "35 knots", "d": ["53 knots", "65 knots", "70 knots"], "why": "The ACS note says to simulate the failure before 50 percent of calculated Vmc: 50% of 70 = 35 knots. Waiting until near Vmc would leave little margin for control. In the Seneca (Vmc 80 MPH CAS) that means before about 40 MPH.", "src": "OEG 4P · ACS PA.IX.E/CA.IX.E · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-052", "t": "acs", "q": "On the takeoff roll, the evaluator simulates an engine failure. What does the ACS require you to do?", "a": "Close both throttles and stop straight, braking as needed", "d": ["Feather the failed engine and continue the takeoff on one engine", "Retard only the failed engine's throttle and stop", "Rotate early and climb out at Vyse on one engine"], "why": "Before Vmc there is no continuing: close the throttles smoothly and promptly, keep directional control, and use brakes as needed. Leaving one engine at power would yaw you off the runway.", "src": "OEG 4P · ACS PA.IX.E/CA.IX.E · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-053", "t": "acs", "q": "You're climbing at Vyse through 250 feet AGL when the evaluator reaches for a throttle to simulate a failure. Per the ACS, this is:", "a": "Too low; not below 400 feet AGL", "d": ["Acceptable, since you are at or above Vyse", "Acceptable once the gear is retracted", "Too low; not below 1,000 feet AGL"], "why": "The evaluator must not simulate an engine failure after liftoff until at least Vsse/Vxse/Vyse and no lower than 400 feet AGL. Both conditions must be met.", "src": "OEG 4Q · ACS PA.IX.F/CA.IX.F · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-054", "t": "acs", "q": "After a simulated engine failure after liftoff with no obstructions ahead, what speed does the ACS have you establish?", "a": "Vyse", "d": ["Vxse", "Vmc plus 5 knots", "Vy"], "why": "With no obstacles, go to Vyse, the best single-engine rate of climb. Vxse (or Vmc + 5) is only for clearing obstructions; Vy is a two-engine speed.", "src": "OEG 4Q · ACS PA.IX.F/CA.IX.F · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-055", "t": "acs", "q": "An engine fails just after liftoff with obstructions ahead. What speed does the ACS have you hold until they are cleared?", "a": "Vxse or Vmc + 5 knots, whichever is greater", "d": ["Vxse or Vmc + 5 knots, whichever is less", "Vyse, until the obstructions are cleared", "Vmc + 10 knots, then Vxse"], "why": "Fly the greater of Vxse or Vmc + 5 knots to clear obstacles with a control margin, then transition to Vyse.", "src": "OEG 4Q · ACS PA.IX.F/CA.IX.F · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-056", "t": "acs", "q": "After you simulate feathering the propeller in the engine-failure-after-liftoff task, what does the evaluator do?", "a": "Sets zero thrust on the inoperative engine", "d": ["Actually feathers the inoperative engine's propeller", "Restores full power on that engine", "Leaves it at idle with the prop windmilling"], "why": "You only simulate the feather; the evaluator then sets zero thrust to mimic a feathered prop's drag. Leaving it at idle would add windmilling drag that a feathered prop wouldn't have.", "src": "OEG 4Q · ACS PA.IX.F/CA.IX.F · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-057", "t": "acs", "q": "What heading and airspeed tolerances apply in the ACS Engine Failure After Liftoff task (Private or Commercial)?", "a": "Heading ±10° and airspeed ±5 knots", "d": ["Heading ±10° and airspeed ±10 knots", "Heading ±20° and airspeed ±5 knots", "Heading ±5° and airspeed +10/−5 knots"], "why": "Right after liftoff the margins are tightest: ±10° heading and ±5 knots of the target single-engine speed, the same at both certificate levels.", "src": "OEG 4Q · ACS PA.IX.F/CA.IX.F · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-058", "t": "acs", "q": "After a simulated engine failure after liftoff, the airplane can't climb at Vyse. What does the ACS say to do?", "a": "Hold Vyse and return or go to the best landing area", "d": ["Slow to Vxse to get more climb performance", "Hold altitude by slowing gradually toward red-line Vmc", "Attempt an engine restart before turning back to land"], "why": "If no climb is possible at Vyse, keep Vyse (the best you'll get) and return to the departure airport or approach the most suitable landing area. Slowing down only reduces performance and control margin.", "src": "OEG 4Q · ACS PA.IX.F/CA.IX.F · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-059", "t": "acs", "q": "On a simulated engine-inoperative approach and landing, where does the ACS require you to touch down?", "a": "Within the first one-third of the available runway", "d": ["Within 200 feet beyond a specified point", "Within 400 feet beyond a specified point", "Within the first half of the available runway length"], "why": "Unlike the two-engine landings, the single-engine landing has no spot tolerance: touch down in the first third of the runway, with no drift and aligned with centerline.", "src": "OEG 4R · ACS PA.IX.G/CA.IX.G · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-060", "t": "acs", "q": "On a Commercial checkride, the recommended single-engine approach speed is 95 knots. What range meets the ACS until landing is assured?", "a": "90 to 100 knots", "d": ["90 to 105 knots", "85 to 105 knots", "95 to 100 knots"], "why": "Commercial is ±5 knots of the recommended approach speed (95 ± 5). A Private applicant would be allowed +10/−5, or 90 to 105 knots.", "src": "OEG 4R · ACS CA.IX.G · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-061", "t": "acs", "q": "In the ACS Maneuvering with One Engine Inoperative task, what do you do with the propeller of the failed engine?", "a": "Actually feather it, then restart per the manufacturer", "d": ["Simulate feathering; the evaluator sets zero thrust", "Leave it windmilling with the throttle at idle", "Feather it and leave it shut down until landing"], "why": "Here you really feather the prop, secure the engine, and then restart it with the manufacturer's procedure. The evaluator sets zero thrust only in the tasks where feathering is simulated, and the ACS limits where and how high an actual shutdown may be done.", "src": "OEG 4S · ACS PA.X.A/CA.X.A · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-062", "t": "acs", "q": "The manufacturer recommends 4,000 feet AGL for engine-out training. Per the ACS, single-engine demonstration tasks must be completed no lower than:", "a": "4,000 feet AGL", "d": ["3,000 feet AGL", "3,500 feet AGL", "1,500 feet AGL"], "why": "The evaluator must pick an entry altitude so the tasks finish no lower than 3,000 feet AGL or the manufacturer's recommended altitude, whichever is higher — here 4,000 feet. In the Seneca, POH 6-12 says not to do the Vmc demonstration below 3,500 ft AGL, so that task must finish at or above 3,500.", "src": "OEG 4S · ACS PA.X.A/CA.X.A · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-063", "t": "acs", "q": "Per the ACS safety note, where must the airplane be when you perform an actual feather or engine shutdown?", "a": "Where a safe landing at an established airport is possible", "d": ["Directly over the departure runway at pattern altitude", "Within gliding distance of a suitable open field", "Within 10 NM of an airport with an operating tower"], "why": "Tasks that need a real shutdown are flown only where you could still land safely at an established airport if the prop won't unfeather or the engine won't restart.", "src": "OEG 4S · ACS PA.X.A/CA.X.A · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-064", "t": "acs", "q": "After an in-flight shutdown on the checkride, the propeller won't unfeather. Per the ACS note, how should you and the evaluator treat this?", "a": "As a real emergency, by both of you", "d": ["As routine; continue the remaining tasks", "As a chance to demonstrate Vmc", "As a non-event if altitude can be held"], "why": "If the engine can't be unfeathered or restarted in flight, the ACS says both you and the evaluator treat it as a real emergency.", "src": "OEG 4S · ACS PA.X.A/CA.X.A · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-065", "t": "acs", "q": "You're at 2,500 feet AGL. Per the ACS, how should an engine failure be simulated?", "a": "Throttle to idle, then set zero thrust", "d": ["Mixture to cutoff, then an actual feather", "Fuel selector off, then an actual feather", "Throttle to idle, prop left windmilling"], "why": "Below 3,000 feet AGL, no actual shutdown: the failure is simulated by reducing the throttle to idle and then establishing zero thrust.", "src": "OEG 4S · ACS PA.X.A/CA.X.A · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-066", "t": "acs", "q": "What tolerances apply in the ACS Maneuvering with One Engine Inoperative task (Private or Commercial)?", "a": "Altitude ±100 ft (or min sink), airspeed ±10 kt, headings ±10°", "d": ["Altitude ±50 ft (or min sink), airspeed ±5 kt, headings ±5°", "Altitude ±100 ft (or min sink), airspeed ±5 kt, headings ±10°", "Altitude ±200 ft (or min sink), airspeed ±10 kt, headings ±20°"], "why": "Hold altitude within ±100 feet — or the minimum sink rate if altitude can't be held — airspeed within ±10 knots, and selected headings within ±10°.", "src": "OEG 4S · ACS PA.X.A/CA.X.A · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-067", "t": "acs", "q": "Which sequence matches how the ACS lists the steps in Maneuvering with One Engine Inoperative?", "a": "Memory items, identify/verify, feather, find cause, secure, restart", "d": ["Feather, identify/verify, memory items, find cause, secure, restart", "Identify/verify, restart, memory items, feather, secure, find cause", "Memory items, feather, restart, identify/verify, find cause, secure"], "why": "Keep control and do the memory items, identify and verify, feather the correct prop, try to find and fix the cause, secure the engine and watch the good one, then restart per the manufacturer.", "src": "OEG 4S · ACS PA.X.A/CA.X.A · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-068", "t": "acs", "q": "Absent manufacturer guidance, how are the wing flaps and cowl flaps set for the ACS Vmc demonstration?", "a": "Flaps and cowl flaps both set for takeoff", "d": ["Flaps up and cowl flaps closed", "Flaps for landing and cowl flaps open", "Flaps for approach and cowl flaps closed"], "why": "The default setup mirrors the takeoff case: gear up, flaps, cowl flaps and trim set for takeoff, props high RPM, critical engine idle and windmilling, other engine at takeoff power.", "src": "OEG 4T · ACS PA.X.B/CA.X.B · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-069", "t": "acs", "q": "In the ACS Vmc demonstration setup, how are the two engines set?", "a": "Critical engine idle and windmilling; other at max power", "d": ["Critical engine feathered; other at max power", "Critical engine at zero thrust; other at max power", "Critical engine idle and windmilling; other at climb power"], "why": "A windmilling prop creates the most drag, and full power on the good engine the most asymmetric thrust — the worst case that defines Vmc. Feathering or zero thrust would lower Vmc and make the demo unrepresentative.", "src": "OEG 4T · ACS PA.X.B/CA.X.B · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-070", "t": "acs", "q": "Absent manufacturer guidance, how are the landing gear and propeller controls set for the ACS Vmc demonstration?", "a": "Gear retracted; propellers at high RPM", "d": ["Gear extended; propellers at high RPM", "Gear retracted; propellers at cruise RPM", "Gear extended; propellers at low RPM"], "why": "The ACS default is gear up and props full forward (high RPM), matching the takeoff/initial-climb configuration where loss of control is most likely.", "src": "OEG 4T · ACS PA.X.B/CA.X.B · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-071", "t": "acs", "q": "Once configured, what airspeed does the ACS have you establish in a single-engine climb attitude before slowing in the Vmc demo?", "a": "About 10 knots above Vsse", "d": ["About 5 knots above Vsse", "About 5 knots above red-line Vmc", "About 10 knots above red-line Vmc"], "why": "Start in a single-engine climb attitude at roughly Vsse + 10 knots, then slow about 1 knot per second.", "src": "OEG 4T · ACS PA.X.B/CA.X.B · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-072", "t": "acs", "q": "What bank angle does the ACS allow during the Vmc demonstration?", "a": "Not more than 5°, toward the operating engine", "d": ["Not more than 5°, toward the inoperative engine", "Not more than 10°, toward the operating engine", "Wings level, with the ball centered"], "why": "Up to 5° of bank into the good engine helps controllability and performance. Banking toward the dead engine would raise Vmc.", "src": "OEG 4T · ACS PA.X.B/CA.X.B · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-073", "t": "acs", "q": "During the Vmc demonstration, at what rate does the ACS have you reduce airspeed?", "a": "About 1 knot per second", "d": ["About 3 knots per second", "About 5 knots per second", "About 1 knot every 5 seconds"], "why": "Raise the pitch slowly to lose about 1 knot per second, adding rudder as needed to hold heading, so the loss of directional control can be recognized clearly.", "src": "OEG 4T · ACS PA.X.B/CA.X.B · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-074", "t": "acs", "q": "During the Vmc demonstration, what tells you to begin the recovery?", "a": "First loss of directional control, stall warning, or buffet", "d": ["Airspeed decaying to 10 knots below the published Vsse", "The airspeed reaching the red-line Vmc marked on the airspeed indicator", "A heading change of 20° from the entry heading"], "why": "Recover at the first of those three cues. Don't wait for the red line — at altitude, actual Vmc can be below it, so the stall can come first. The 20° figure is the heading limit for completing the recovery, not the cue to start it.", "src": "OEG 4T · ACS PA.X.B/CA.X.B · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-075", "t": "acs", "q": "How does the ACS describe the Vmc demonstration recovery?", "a": "Reduce operating-engine power and lower the nose together", "d": ["Add power on the idle engine while lowering the nose", "Add full power on the good engine and pitch up to hold altitude", "Lower the nose first, then reduce power afterward"], "why": "Recover by simultaneously reducing power on the operating engine (less asymmetric thrust) and lowering the angle of attack to regain speed and control — without adding power on the simulated failed engine.", "src": "OEG 4T · ACS PA.X.B/CA.X.B · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-076", "t": "acs", "q": "During the Vmc demonstration recovery, what heading tolerance does the ACS require (Private or Commercial)?", "a": "Within 20° of the entry heading", "d": ["Within 10° of the entry heading", "Within 5° of the entry heading", "Within 30° of the entry heading"], "why": "The recovery must be completed within 20° of the entry heading, then power is added smoothly and the airplane accelerated to Vsse/Vyse.", "src": "OEG 4T · ACS PA.X.B/CA.X.B · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-077", "t": "acs", "q": "On a Private checkride, you recover from the Vmc demo and accelerate to a Vyse of 88 knots. What range meets the ACS?", "a": "83 to 98 knots", "d": ["83 to 93 knots", "78 to 98 knots", "88 to 98 knots"], "why": "Private allows +10/−5 knots of Vsse/Vyse during the recovery: 88 − 5 = 83, 88 + 10 = 98. Commercial is ±5 (83 to 93).", "src": "OEG 4T · ACS PA.X.B · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-078", "t": "acs", "q": "An applicant plans to start the Vmc demo by pitching steeply nose-up on both engines and then pulling the critical engine to idle. Per the ACS note, this is:", "a": "Hazardous and may cause loss of control", "d": ["Preferred, since it saves altitude", "Acceptable if bank stays under 5°", "Required above 5,000 feet density altitude"], "why": "The ACS specifically warns against this entry: chopping an engine at a high pitch attitude and low speed can produce a sudden loss of control. Reduce power first, then slow gradually.", "src": "OEG 4T · ACS PA.X.B/CA.X.B · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-079", "t": "acs", "q": "In a normally aspirated twin at higher altitudes, why does the ACS stress recovering at the first sign of trouble in the Vmc demo?", "a": "Power loss with altitude can put Vmc below stall speed", "d": ["Vmc rises above Vyse as density altitude increases", "Rudder authority increases as altitude increases", "Stall speed decreases below Vmc at high altitude"], "why": "Less power from the good engine means less yaw, so Vmc decreases with altitude while stall speed doesn't. Where Vmc is below stall speed, a stall with asymmetric power can come first and lead to a spin.", "src": "OEG 4T · ACS PA.X.B/CA.X.B · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-080", "t": "acs", "q": "You do not hold an instrument–airplane rating. Which ACS tasks are not required on your multi-engine checkride?", "a": "OEI flight by instruments and the OEI instrument approach", "d": ["The Vmc demonstration and the OEI maneuvering task", "Engine failure after liftoff and the OEI visual landing", "The OEI visual landing and the OEI instrument approach"], "why": "Both the one-engine-inoperative flight solely by reference to instruments and the instrument approach with an inoperative engine carry a note: not required without an instrument–airplane rating.", "src": "OEG 4U/4V · ACS PA.X.C/CA.X.C, PA.X.D/CA.X.D · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-081", "t": "acs", "q": "On a simulated engine failure in instrument conditions, the airplane can't hold altitude at the best single-engine speed. What does the ACS expect?", "a": "Assess performance and choose a safe-landing plan", "d": ["Hold altitude even if the airspeed decays below Vyse", "Continue to the destination as originally planned", "Unfeather and restart the engine before deciding"], "why": "The task requires assessing the airplane's capability, deciding on an action that ensures a safe landing, and never flying contrary to the engine-inoperative limitations (e.g., trading Vyse for altitude).", "src": "OEG 4U · ACS PA.X.C/CA.X.C · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-082", "t": "acs", "q": "On the final approach segment of a simulated OEI instrument approach, how much course/glidepath deflection does the ACS allow (Private or Commercial)?", "a": "Within ¾-scale deflection", "d": ["Within ½-scale deflection", "Within full-scale deflection", "Within ¼-scale deflection"], "why": "Keep lateral and (if applicable) vertical guidance within ¾-scale deflection. The task's other tolerances are altitude ±100 feet (or minimum sink), airspeed ±10 knots, and heading ±10°.", "src": "OEG 4V · ACS PA.X.D/CA.X.D · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-083", "t": "acs", "q": "On a simulated OEI instrument approach, what should your rate of descent accomplish per the ACS?", "a": "Reach MDA or DA/DH positioned to land straight-in or circling", "d": ["Reach the FAF already level at the MDA or DA/DH", "Stay at the MEA until the runway environment is in sight", "Hold a fixed 1,000 fpm descent all the way to minimums"], "why": "The descent rate must get you to the MDA or DA/DH positioned for a normal descent to the intended runway, straight-in or circling. If circling, you must follow the published criteria for your approach category.", "src": "OEG 4V · ACS PA.X.D/CA.X.D · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "o8-084", "t": "acs", "q": "Which is a skill element of the ACS After Landing, Parking, and Securing task?", "a": "A postflight inspection noting discrepancies and servicing", "d": ["Recomputing the weight and balance for the next planned flight", "Refueling to full tanks before leaving the airplane", "Topping off engine oil to the maximum dipstick mark"], "why": "The task: park in an appropriate area considering nearby people and property, complete the checklists, do a postflight inspection documenting discrepancies and servicing needs, and secure the airplane.", "src": "OEG 4W · ACS PA.XII.A/CA.XI.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-085", "t": "acs", "q": "Your passengers want to walk across the ramp to the FBO while another twin is running up nearby. Which ACS after-landing risk applies most directly?", "a": "Disembarking passengers safely and monitoring them", "d": ["Confirmation or expectation bias in taxi instructions", "Wake turbulence behind departing or landing traffic", "A runway incursion at the hold-short line"], "why": "The parking task's risk list covers activities and distractions, airport-specific security procedures, and getting passengers off the airplane and across the ramp safely.", "src": "OEG 4W · ACS PA.XII.A/CA.XI.A · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-086", "t": "acs", "q": "On a Private checkride, when does the ACS have you recover from a power-on stall?", "a": "Promptly after a full stall occurs", "d": ["At the first indication of a stall, always", "First indication or full stall, as the evaluator says", "Once the nose has dropped through the horizon"], "why": "The Private standard is to acknowledge the impending-stall cues and recover promptly once the full stall occurs. Letting the evaluator choose first indication or full stall is the Commercial standard.", "src": "OEG 4K · ACS PA.VII.C · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "o8-087", "t": "acs", "q": "On a Private checkride, the recommended single-engine approach speed is 95 knots. What range meets the ACS until landing is assured?", "a": "90 to 105 knots", "d": ["90 to 100 knots", "85 to 105 knots", "95 to 105 knots"], "why": "Private allows +10/−5 knots of the recommended engine-inoperative approach speed: 95 − 5 = 90 and 95 + 10 = 105. A Commercial applicant must hold ±5 knots, or 90 to 100.", "src": "OEG 4R · ACS PA.IX.G · FAA-P-8740-66"},
{"id": "p1-002", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "Per the Seneca I specifications, what are the best rate-of-climb speed (Vy) and the best single-engine rate-of-climb speed (Vyse)?", "a": "Both are 105 MPH (91.5 kt)", "d": ["Vy 105 MPH (91.5 kt); Vyse 90 MPH (78 kt)", "Vy 120 MPH; Vyse 105 MPH (91.5 kt)", "Both are 90 MPH (78 kt)"], "why": "POH 1-1 gives 105 MPH (91.5 kt) for both, at either weight. 90 MPH is Vx, and 120 MPH is the en route climb speed suggested in Section 6.", "src": "POH 1-1"},
{"id": "p1-003", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What best angle-of-climb speed (Vx) does the Seneca I POH list?", "a": "90 MPH", "d": ["80 MPH", "85 MPH", "105 MPH"], "why": "POH 1-1 lists Vx as 90 MPH (78 kt). 80 MPH is Vmc, 85 MPH is the 50-ft speed in the flaps-up short-field takeoff, and 105 MPH is Vy.", "src": "POH 1-1"},
{"id": "p1-006", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "At max gross weight (4,200 lb), sea level, standard day, what single-engine rate of climb does the POH spec table give?", "a": "190 fpm", "d": ["230 fpm", "1,360 fpm", "50 fpm"], "why": "POH 1-1: 190 fpm at 4,200 lb (230 fpm at 4,000 lb), against 1,360 fpm with both engines. Losing an engine costs well over 80% of the climb performance.", "src": "POH 1-1"},
{"id": "p1-007", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What is the Seneca I single-engine service ceiling (left engine out) at 4,200 lb, per the POH specifications?", "a": "3,650 ft", "d": ["5,000 ft", "5,200 ft", "6,600 ft"], "why": "POH 1-1: 3,650 ft at 4,200 lb and 5,200 ft at 4,000 lb. 5,000 ft and 6,600 ft are the single-engine absolute ceilings at those weights.", "src": "POH 1-1"},
{"id": "p1-008", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "At 4,200 lb on a standard day, the left engine fails while you are level at 4,500 ft pressure altitude. Once it is feathered and you are clean at Vyse, what climb can you expect per POH 1-1?", "a": "A positive climb of less than 50 fpm", "d": ["About 190 fpm, the sea-level figure", "None; you are above the absolute ceiling", "About 100 fpm, the service ceiling rate"], "why": "At 4,200 lb the single-engine service ceiling (50 fpm) is 3,650 ft and the absolute ceiling (zero climb) is 5,000 ft. At 4,500 ft you are between them, so only a small climb remains.", "src": "POH 1-1"},
{"id": "p1-009", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "POH 1-1 short-field takeoff (flaps 25°) at 4,200 lb: what are the ground run and the distance over a 50-ft barrier?", "a": "800 ft run; 1,235 ft over 50 ft", "d": ["750 ft run; 1,140 ft over 50 ft", "705 ft run; 1,335 ft over 50 ft", "1,235 ft run; 1,800 ft over 50 ft"], "why": "800/1,235 ft at 4,200 lb; 750/1,140 ft is the 4,000-lb figure and 705/1,335 ft is the short-field landing. Use the Section 8 charts for actual conditions.", "src": "POH 1-1"},
{"id": "p1-010", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What short-field landing distances (flaps down) appear in the Seneca I General Specifications?", "a": "705 ft roll; 1,335 ft over a 50-ft barrier", "d": ["800 ft roll; 1,235 ft over a 50-ft barrier", "1,335 ft roll; 2,000 ft over a 50-ft barrier", "750 ft roll; 1,140 ft over a 50-ft barrier"], "why": "POH 1-1 lists 705 ft and 1,335 ft, with a footnote that they apply only to the conditions on the Landing Distance vs Density Altitude chart. 800/1,235 and 750/1,140 are takeoff figures.", "src": "POH 1-1"},
{"id": "p1-011", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "Planning a cruise at 75% power, what total fuel burn for both engines does POH 1-1 list, and what at 65% power?", "a": "20.6 GPH at 75%; 18.3 GPH at 65%", "d": ["10.3 GPH at 75%; 9.2 GPH at 65%", "19.2 GPH at 75%; 16.0 GPH at 65%", "24.0 GPH at 75%; 20.6 GPH at 65%"], "why": "20.6 and 18.3 GPH are totals for both engines (roughly half each per engine). 19.2 GPH is the fuel flow gauge red line in Section 3, not a consumption figure.", "src": "POH 1-1"},
{"id": "p1-015", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Viewed from the cockpit, which way does each Seneca I engine rotate?", "a": "Left clockwise; right counterclockwise", "d": ["Left counterclockwise; right clockwise", "Both clockwise", "Both counterclockwise"], "why": "POH 2-2: the left engine turns clockwise and the right counterclockwise as seen from the cockpit. This counter-rotation removes asymmetric thrust in takeoff and climb.", "src": "POH 2-2"},
{"id": "p1-017", "t": "s-engine", "q": "How many cylinders, what displacement and what compression ratio does each Seneca I engine have?", "a": "Four cylinders, 361 cu in, 8.7:1", "d": ["Four cylinders, 320 cu in, 8.5:1", "Six cylinders, 361 cu in, 8.7:1", "Six cylinders, 540 cu in, 7.3:1"], "why": "POH 1-2: 361.0 cu in, 8.7:1 compression, 5.125-in bore, 4.375-in stroke, 350 lb dry weight. POH 2-2 describes them as four-cylinder, fuel-injected Lycomings.", "src": "POH 1-2 · POH 2-2"},
{"id": "p1-018", "t": "s-engine", "q": "How is fuel delivered to the Seneca I engines?", "a": "Bendix RSA-5 fuel injection metered by engine airflow", "d": ["Marvel-Schebler float carburetors with mixture and carb heat", "Timed direct cylinder injection metered by engine RPM", "Bendix pressure carburetors with accelerator pumps"], "why": "POH 2-2: each engine has a Bendix RSA-5 system that measures the engine's air consumption and uses that airflow to control fuel flow. There is no carburetor and no carb heat.", "src": "POH 2-2"},
{"id": "p1-019", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Is the Seneca I (PA-34-200) turbocharged?", "a": "No; air goes from the filter straight to the fuel servo", "d": ["Yes; both engines have turbochargers with fixed wastegates", "Yes; only the right (LIO-360) engine has a turbocharger", "Yes; the turbos also feed heated air to the alternate air box"], "why": "POH 2-3 describes induction air going from the scoop through the filter directly to the servo regulator, with no turbocharger in the path. Expect power to fall off with altitude.", "src": "POH 2-3"},
{"id": "p1-020", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Where does normal induction air enter each Seneca I engine?", "a": "A scoop on the outboard side of the lower cowl, then a filter", "d": ["A scoop on the inboard side of the upper cowl, then a filter", "The oil cooler scoops on the sides of the cowl", "The dorsal fin air scoop, then a filter"], "why": "POH 2-3: an air scoop on the outboard lower cowl feeds a filter and then the servo regulator. The side-cowl scoops feed the oil cooler (POH 2-2).", "src": "POH 2-3"},
{"id": "p1-021", "t": "s-engine", "q": "How does the Seneca I alternate air system work?", "a": "A manual valve admits unfiltered air heated by the exhaust", "d": ["A spring door opens automatically when the filter clogs", "It routes carburetor heat from a muffler shroud", "A manual valve admits filtered ram air from the nose"], "why": "POH 2-3: a valve in the air box between the filter and servo regulator, worked by the control below the power quadrant, admits air heated by the crossover exhaust tube that bypasses the filter.", "src": "POH 2-3 · POH 6-9"},
{"id": "p1-023", "t": "s-engine", "q": "When does the POH say alternate air should NOT be used on the Seneca I?", "a": "During ground operation and for takeoff", "d": ["Above 10,000 ft, where exhaust heat is too low", "In visible moisture below freezing", "During climb, because it overheats the cylinders"], "why": "POH 2-3: unfiltered alternate air may carry dust on the ground, and the primary induction source should always be used for takeoff. A brief run-up check is allowed unless the air is dusty (POH 6-5).", "src": "POH 2-3 · POH 6-5"},
{"id": "p1-024", "t": "s-engine", "q": "How do the Seneca I cowl flaps operate?", "a": "Manual levers with a lock: open, intermediate, closed", "d": ["Electric switches with an indicator: open or closed", "Manual levers with infinite positions and no lock", "Automatic, driven by a cylinder head thermostat"], "why": "POH 2-2: levers below the throttle quadrant work cowl flaps on the bottom of each nacelle. Depress the lock for each selection; once released, the flap stops at the next position.", "src": "POH 2-2"},
{"id": "p1-025", "t": "s-engine", "q": "In a climb with the left engine's CHT rising, you want its cowl flap open. How do you move the control?", "a": "Depress the lock and push the left lever", "d": ["Depress the lock and pull the left lever", "Pull the left lever; no lock is used", "Push the right lever; the levers are linked"], "why": "The cowl flap placard (POH 2-3) reads PULL–CLOSE and PUSH–OPEN, with separate L and R levers. The lock must be depressed for each selection (POH 2-2).", "src": "POH 2-3 · POH 2-2"},
{"id": "p1-026", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Where is each Seneca I engine's oil cooler, and how is it cooled?", "a": "Forward of the firewall, fed by scoops on the cowl sides", "d": ["Aft of the firewall, cooled by cabin ventilation air", "Behind the spinner, fed by the main cowl nose inlet", "Under the nacelle, cooled by air leaving the cowl flaps"], "why": "POH 2-2: air picked up by scoops on the side of the cowl passes through the oil cooler on the forward side of the firewall and is ducted overboard in the lower cowling.", "src": "POH 2-2"},
{"id": "p1-027", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What is each Seneca I engine's oil capacity, and what minimum quantity does the POH call safe?", "a": "8 quarts capacity; 2 quarts minimum safe", "d": ["8 quarts capacity; 4 quarts minimum safe", "6 quarts capacity; 3 quarts minimum safe", "12 quarts capacity; 8 quarts minimum safe"], "why": "POH 1-2 and 9-7: 8 quarts each, with a minimum safe quantity of 2 quarts. The Section 6 preflight looks for six to eight quarts before flight.", "src": "POH 1-2 · POH 9-7 · POH 6-2"},
{"id": "p1-028", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Operating in summer with temperatures consistently above 90°F, which oil grade does the POH call for?", "a": "SAE 50", "d": ["SAE 40", "SAE 30", "SAE 20"], "why": "POH 9-7: SAE 50 above 60°F, SAE 40 from 30 to 90°F, SAE 30 from 0 to 70°F, SAE 20 below 10°F. Above 90°F only SAE 50 applies.", "src": "POH 9-7"},
{"id": "p1-032", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Describe the Seneca I propellers.", "a": "Hartzell constant-speed, full-feathering, oil and nitrogen", "d": ["Hartzell constant-speed, non-feathering, governor oil pressure only", "McCauley constant-speed, full-feathering, oil and springs", "Hartzell fixed-pitch, counter-rotating, 76-in diameter"], "why": "POH 2-4: constant-speed, controllable-pitch, full-feathering Hartzell propellers operated by oil and nitrogen pressure. Diameter is 76 in (POH 1-2); the limitations allow 74 to 76 in (POH 3-1).", "src": "POH 2-4 · POH 1-2"},
{"id": "p1-033", "t": "s-engine", "q": "In the Seneca I propeller, what does increasing governor oil pressure do to the blades?", "a": "Moves them toward low pitch / high RPM (unfeather)", "d": ["Moves them toward high pitch / low RPM (feather)", "Locks them against the low-pitch stop", "Nothing until engine RPM exceeds 800"], "why": "POH 2-4: oil pressure, supplied by an engine-mounted governor through the prop shaft, drives the prop toward high RPM/unfeather; nitrogen drives it toward low RPM/feather.", "src": "POH 2-4"},
{"id": "p1-034", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What two jobs does the nitrogen charge in the Seneca I propeller dome do?", "a": "Drives blades toward feather and guards against overspeed", "d": ["Drives blades toward high RPM and assists unfeathering in flight", "Stores oil under pressure to unfeather the prop without the starter", "Cools the hub and damps blade vibration at high RPM"], "why": "POH 2-4: nitrogen pressure sends the prop toward low RPM/feather and keeps it from overspeeding. Dry compressed air may be used instead; the charge pressure placard is on the dome and in the spinner.", "src": "POH 2-4"},
{"id": "p1-036", "t": "s-engine", "q": "How do you feather a Seneca I propeller?", "a": "Pull the prop lever fully aft through the low-RPM detent", "d": ["Pull the prop lever back to the low-RPM detent and hold it there", "Press the feather button for that engine, then pull the mixture", "Pull the prop lever fully aft once the RPM is below 800"], "why": "POH 2-4: move the control fully aft through the low-RPM detent into the feather position. It must be done while the engine is still turning above 800 RPM.", "src": "POH 2-4"},
{"id": "p1-037", "t": "s-engine", "q": "About how long does a Seneca I propeller take to feather?", "a": "About 6 seconds", "d": ["About 1–3 seconds", "About 10–12 seconds", "About 30 seconds"], "why": "POH 2-4: feathering takes approximately six seconds. The 1–3 seconds figure is the run-up feather check drop to 1,000 RPM.", "src": "POH 2-4"},
{"id": "p1-042", "t": "s-engine", "q": "According to the Seneca I POH, what does counter-rotation of the propellers accomplish?", "a": "Balanced thrust; no 'critical engine' in single-engine flight", "d": ["Makes the right engine the critical engine instead of the left", "Lowers Vmc below the power-off stall speed in all conditions", "Removes the need for rudder or rudder trim on one engine"], "why": "POH 2-2/2-4: counter-rotation eliminates asymmetric thrust in takeoff and climb and the 'critical engine' factor. Vmc (80 MPH) still applies, and the rudder trim tab exists for single-engine flight.", "src": "POH 2-4 · POH 2-2"},
{"id": "p1-043", "t": "s-gear", "q": "What powers the Seneca I landing gear for normal retraction and extension?", "a": "A reversible, electrically powered hydraulic pump", "d": ["An engine-driven hydraulic pump on the left engine", "A direct electric motor and screw jack", "An engine-driven pump with an electric backup"], "why": "POH 2-4: hydraulic pressure comes from an electrically powered reversible pump. Pressure in one direction retracts the gear; in the other it extends it.", "src": "POH 2-4"},
{"id": "p1-044", "t": "s-gear", "q": "Where is the Seneca I gear selector, and how is it operated?", "a": "Left of the quadrant; pull the wheel knob out, then move it", "d": ["Right of the quadrant; push the wheel knob in, then move it", "On the floor console; lift the lever over a detent", "Left of the quadrant; move it with no pull-out needed"], "why": "POH 2-4: the two-position switch with a wheel-shaped knob is on the panel to the left of the control quadrant and must be pulled out before it can move to UP or DOWN.", "src": "POH 2-4"},
{"id": "p1-045", "t": "s-gear", "q": "Just after selecting gear UP you decide you want it down again. The gear is in transit with the pump running. What does the POH caution?", "a": "Don't reverse until travel completes; it can harm the pump", "d": ["Reverse immediately; the pump stops and reverses automatically", "Pull the emergency knob instead of reversing the selector", "Cycle the master switch off and on, then select DOWN"], "why": "POH 2-4: moving the selector the opposite way before the gear reaches its travel limit is inadvisable because the sudden reversal may harm the electric pump. A full cycle takes 6–7 seconds.", "src": "POH 2-4"},
{"id": "p1-046", "t": "s-gear", "q": "How long does normal Seneca I gear retraction or extension take?", "a": "6 to 7 seconds", "d": ["3 to 4 seconds", "10 to 12 seconds", "About 20 seconds"], "why": "POH 2-4: retraction or extension normally takes six to seven seconds.", "src": "POH 2-4"},
{"id": "p1-047", "t": "s-gear", "q": "What holds the Seneca I landing gear in the retracted position?", "a": "Hydraulic pressure only; there are no mechanical uplocks", "d": ["Mechanical uplock hooks, released by hydraulic pressure", "Over-center links held in place by bungee springs", "Electric solenoid uplocks wired to the up-limit switches"], "why": "POH 2-5: the gear is held up by hydraulic pressure, and POH 6-9 states there are no mechanical uplocks. Lose the pressure and the gear free-falls.", "src": "POH 2-5 · POH 6-9"},
{"id": "p1-048", "t": "s-gear", "q": "In cruise, a leak drains the landing gear hydraulic pressure. What will the gear do?", "a": "Extend by gravity, helped by airloads and springs", "d": ["Stay up, held by its mechanical uplock hooks", "Stay up until the emergency extension knob is pulled", "Extend only the nose gear, while the mains stay retracted"], "why": "POH 2-5: if the system leaks or pressure is relieved, gravity extends the gear, with aerodynamic loads and springs assisting extension and locking.", "src": "POH 2-5"},
{"id": "p1-049", "t": "s-gear", "q": "How is the Seneca I landing gear held locked in the down position?", "a": "Downlock hooks kept engaged by springs", "d": ["Hydraulic pressure trapped by a check valve", "Over-center drag links with no springs", "Electric solenoids energized by the squat switch"], "why": "POH 2-5: when all three gear are down the downlock hooks engage, and a spring keeps force on each hook until hydraulic pressure releases it for retraction.", "src": "POH 2-5"},
{"id": "p1-050", "t": "s-gear", "q": "What does the Seneca I emergency gear extension knob do, and where is it?", "a": "Releases hydraulic pressure; near panel center, under a guard", "d": ["Runs a backup electric pump; near panel center, under a guard", "Releases the mechanical uplock hooks; on the floor console", "Engages a hand crank for the gear; below the pilot's seat"], "why": "POH 2-5: pulling the knob releases the hydraulic pressure holding the gear up so it can free-fall. A guard over the knob (the clip in POH 3-14) prevents inadvertent extension.", "src": "POH 2-5 · POH 3-14"},
{"id": "p1-051", "t": "s-gear", "q": "Before pulling the emergency gear extension knob, where should the gear selector be, and why?", "a": "DOWN, so the pump doesn't try to raise the gear", "d": ["UP, so the downlock hooks are not loaded during free-fall", "Left where it is, to avoid a sudden reversal harming the pump", "UP, so the pump can't run during free-fall"], "why": "POH 2-5 advises placing the selector DOWN first to prevent the pump from trying to raise the gear. The manual extension checklist (POH 3-14) does the same.", "src": "POH 2-5 · POH 3-14"},
{"id": "p1-054", "t": "s-gear", "q": "What cockpit and external indications show the Seneca I gear position?", "a": "Three green, a red light, and a mirror on the left nacelle", "d": ["Three green and a red light, with no external aid", "Three green and a mechanical floor indicator", "Three green, a red light, and a mirror on the right nacelle"], "why": "POH 2-5: three green = down and locked; red = not fully up or fully down; a convex mirror on the left engine nacelle lets the pilot confirm the nose gear.", "src": "POH 2-5"},
{"id": "p1-056", "t": "s-gear", "q": "With gear DOWN selected, one green light stays dark. Per the POH, which three conditions could cause it?", "a": "Gear not locked, bulb burned out, or indicating fault", "d": ["Gear not locked, low hydraulic fluid, or squat switch open", "Bulb burned out, pump failure, or nav lights off", "Gear not locked, low battery, or throttle switch fault"], "why": "POH 2-5 lists these three possibilities for each green light that fails to come on after DOWN is selected. For the nose gear, the mirror on the left nacelle gives an independent check.", "src": "POH 2-5"},
{"id": "p1-058", "t": "s-gear", "q": "With the gear up, the Seneca I gear warning horn sounds when manifold pressure drops below what value on either engine?", "a": "14 in Hg", "d": ["12 in Hg", "15 in Hg", "18 in Hg"], "why": "POH 2-5: gear up and MP reduced below 14 inches on one or both engines. The other horn condition is the gear selector in UP while on the ground.", "src": "POH 2-5"},
{"id": "p1-061", "t": "s-gear", "q": "Where is the Seneca I landing gear safety (squat) switch, and what does it do?", "a": "Left main gear; keeps the pump from retracting on the ground", "d": ["Nose gear strut; keeps the pump from retracting on the ground", "Right main gear; silences the gear warning horn on the ground", "Left main gear; mechanically locks the gear selector in DOWN"], "why": "POH 2-6: with the selector UP on the ground, a safety switch on the left main gear prevents the hydraulic pump from running if the master switch is turned on.", "src": "POH 2-6"},
{"id": "p1-062", "t": "s-gear", "q": "On takeoff, how far must the oleo extend before the safety switch lets the pump retract the gear?", "a": "More than 8 inches", "d": ["More than 2.5 inches", "More than 3.5 inches", "More than 12 inches"], "why": "POH 2-6: when the oleo extends in excess of eight inches, the safety switch closes the circuit so the pump can raise the gear. 3.5 and 2.5 in are the static main and nose strut extensions.", "src": "POH 2-6"},
{"id": "p1-063", "t": "s-gear", "q": "Parked, a passenger bumps the gear selector to UP, and then you turn the master switch on. What happens?", "a": "The pump won't run, but the gear warning horn sounds", "d": ["The gear begins retracting until the squat switch cuts the pump", "Nothing happens; the warning horn works only in flight", "The pump runs, but the downlocks hold the gear"], "why": "POH 2-5/2-6: the left-main safety switch keeps the pump from actuating on the ground, and the horn sounds with the selector UP on the ground. Put the selector back to DOWN.", "src": "POH 2-6 · POH 2-5"},
{"id": "p1-065", "t": "s-gear", "q": "How is the Seneca I steered on the ground, and through what arc?", "a": "Rudder pedals steer the nose gear through a 42° arc", "d": ["Differential braking only; the nose wheel casters", "Rudder pedals steer the nose gear through a 30° arc", "A tiller steers the nose gear through a 60° arc"], "why": "POH 2-6: the nose gear is steerable through a 42-degree arc with the rudder pedals. On retraction the linkage disengages and the wheel straightens; a centering spring prevents shimmy.", "src": "POH 2-6"},
{"id": "p1-066", "t": "s-gear", "q": "A line technician will tow the Seneca with a powered tug. How far may the nose gear be turned?", "a": "No more than 20° either side of center", "d": ["No more than 42° either side of center", "No more than 30° either side of center", "No more than 10° either side of center"], "why": "POH 9-1 caution: turning more than 20° either way with power equipment damages the nose gear and steering mechanism. 42° is the rudder-pedal steering arc.", "src": "POH 9-1"},
{"id": "p1-067", "t": "s-gear", "q": "Where is the Seneca I landing gear hydraulic reservoir?", "a": "Built into the pump, reached through the nose baggage area", "d": ["Behind the rear baggage bulkhead, reached from the cabin", "On the left firewall, reached through the cowl side panel", "Shared with the brake reservoir in the nose compartment"], "why": "POH 2-6: the reservoir is an integral part of the gear hydraulic pump, accessed through a panel in the nose baggage compartment. The brake reservoir is a separate unit.", "src": "POH 2-6"},
{"id": "p1-068", "t": "s-gear", "q": "The gear cycles normally, but the brakes feel spongy. Could the gear hydraulic system be the cause?", "a": "No; the brakes have their own independent reservoir", "d": ["Yes; both systems draw on the gear pump's reservoir", "Yes; the gear pump also pressurizes the brake lines", "No; the brakes are mechanical, worked by cables"], "why": "POH 2-6: the brake reservoir is independent of the gear reservoir and sits behind a panel at the rear top of the nose baggage compartment. Keep it at the marked level.", "src": "POH 2-6 · POH 9-6"},
{"id": "p1-069", "t": "s-gear", "q": "The brake reservoir needs topping off. What fluid does the POH specify?", "a": "MIL-H-5606 petroleum-base hydraulic fluid", "d": ["MIL-H-83282 synthetic-base hydraulic fluid", "Skydrol phosphate-ester hydraulic fluid", "DOT 3 glycol-base automotive brake fluid"], "why": "POH 9-6: the brake system is filled with MIL-H-5606 (petroleum base) fluid, checked periodically or at every 100-hour inspection.", "src": "POH 9-6"},
{"id": "p1-070", "t": "s-gear", "q": "Describe the Seneca I brake assemblies.", "a": "A single-disc, double-puck brake on each main gear", "d": ["A dual-disc, single-puck brake on each main gear", "Single-disc brakes on all three wheels", "A drum brake on each main gear"], "why": "POH 2-6: each main gear strut has a single-disc double-puck brake, actuated by toe brake cylinders and a hand-brake cylinder.", "src": "POH 2-6"},
{"id": "p1-071", "t": "s-gear", "q": "On the Seneca I, where are the toe brakes and the hand brake handle?", "a": "Left pedals (right optional); handle below left center panel", "d": ["Both pedal sets standard; handle on the center floor console", "Right pedals only; handle below the right side of the panel", "Left pedals (right optional); handle on the tunnel by the flap lever"], "why": "POH 2-6: toe brake cylinders are on the left rudder pedals (optional on the right), plus a handle-operated cylinder below and behind the left center of the instrument panel.", "src": "POH 2-6"},
{"id": "p1-072", "t": "s-gear", "q": "How is the Seneca I parking brake set and released?", "a": "Set: pull handle, push button. Release: pull handle only", "d": ["Set: push button, pull handle. Release: push button again", "Set: pull handle, turn it 90°. Release: turn it back and push", "Set: press toe brakes, pull knob. Release: push knob"], "why": "POH 2-6: pull back on the handle and push forward on the button to its left to set. To release, pull aft on the handle without touching the button and let it swing forward.", "src": "POH 2-6"},
{"id": "p1-073", "t": "s-gear", "q": "What tires does the Seneca I use?", "a": "6.00-6 on all three; 6-ply nose, 8-ply mains", "d": ["6.00-6 on all three; 8-ply nose, 6-ply mains", "5.00-5 6-ply nose; 6.00-6 8-ply mains", "6.00-6 on all three; all 8-ply"], "why": "POH 1-3 and 2-6: all three wheels are the same size, 6.00-6, with a six-ply nose tire and eight-ply main tires.", "src": "POH 2-6 · POH 1-3"},
{"id": "p1-074", "t": "s-gear", "q": "During preflight, what pressure should the Seneca I nose tire have?", "a": "31 PSI", "d": ["53 PSI", "42 PSI", "25 PSI"], "why": "POH 1-3 and 9-8 both give 31 PSI for the nose tire, and the school preflight checklist agrees. The main tires take 53 PSI (POH 9-8).", "src": "POH 1-3 · POH 9-8 · School checklist"},
{"id": "p1-075", "t": "s-gear", "q": "Per the POH Section 9 tire inflation guidance, what pressure should the main tires have?", "a": "53 PSI", "d": ["31 PSI", "42 PSI", "60 PSI"], "why": "POH 9-8 (servicing) and the school checklist give 53 PSI for the mains and 31 PSI for the nose. The Section 1 spec table (POH 1-3) prints 50 PSI for the mains; use the Section 9 servicing figure and confirm with your mechanic.", "src": "POH 9-8 · POH 1-3 · School checklist"},
{"id": "p1-076", "t": "s-gear", "q": "What type of shock struts does the Seneca I landing gear use?", "a": "Air-oil (oleo) struts on the nose and main gear", "d": ["Spring-steel main legs with an air-oil nose strut", "Rubber disc stacks on the nose and main gear", "Air-oil main struts with a spring nose strut"], "why": "POH 2-6: the nose and main gear struts are all air-oil assemblies. That is why the preflight checks exposed piston: 3-1/2 in on the mains, 2-1/2 in on the nose (POH 6-2, 9-6).", "src": "POH 2-6"},
{"id": "p1-077", "t": "s-gear", "q": "What circuit breakers protect the Seneca I landing gear system, per its electrical schematic?", "a": "25-amp pump breaker; 5-amp control and warning breaker", "d": ["One 25-amp breaker shared by the pump, lights and horn", "15-amp pump breaker; 5-amp gear light and horn breaker", "An in-line pump fuse; 10-amp control and warning breaker"], "why": "The landing gear electrical schematic (POH 2-8) shows a 25-amp 'landing gear electro-hydraulic pump' breaker and a 5-amp 'landing gear control & warning' breaker.", "src": "POH 2-8"},
{"id": "p1-078", "t": "s-gear", "q": "On the Seneca I hydraulic schematic, what component does the emergency (free fall) control operate?", "a": "The free fall valve", "d": ["The gear-up check valve in the pump", "The high-pressure control valve", "The shuttle valve below the reservoir"], "why": "In the POH 2-7 schematic the free fall control is linked to the free fall valve. Opening it relieves the pressure holding the gear up so gravity, airloads and springs lower it.", "src": "POH 2-7"},
{"id": "p1-079", "t": "s-gear", "q": "Per the gear electrical schematic, what turns on each green gear light?", "a": "A down-limit switch at that gear", "d": ["The hydraulic pressure switch", "The squat switch on the left main gear", "The throttle micro switch"], "why": "The POH 2-8 schematic shows up-limit and down-limit switches at the left, nose and right gear, wired to their lights; POH 2-5 says three greens mean down and locked.", "src": "POH 2-8 · POH 2-5"},
{"id": "p1-080", "t": "s-gear", "q": "If the Seneca I hydraulic pump fails, is there any backup means of retracting the gear?", "a": "No; the backup only releases pressure so the gear falls", "d": ["Yes; a hand pump on the floor beside the pilot's seat", "Yes; pull the emergency knob out, then push it back in", "Yes; airloads raise it once the emergency knob is pushed in"], "why": "POH 2-5: the emergency system works only by relieving hydraulic pressure so the gear can free-fall. No manual retraction means is described; plan to fly with gear down.", "src": "POH 2-5"},
{"id": "p1-082", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "What type of linkage connects the Seneca I cockpit controls to the flight control surfaces?", "a": "Cables", "d": ["Push-pull tubes", "Hydraulic actuators", "Electric servos"], "why": "POH 2-9: dual controls are standard, with a cable system between the controls and the surfaces.", "src": "POH 2-9"},
{"id": "p1-083", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "How is pitch trim provided on the Seneca I?", "a": "Stabilator anti-servo tab, set from the control tunnel", "d": ["Separate elevator trim tab, set by an overhead crank", "Adjustable horizontal stabilizer, set by a jackscrew motor", "Bungee springs on the control column, set by a panel knob"], "why": "POH 2-9: the all-movable stabilator's anti-servo tab also acts as the longitudinal trim tab, worked by a control on the tunnel between the front seats. Your electric pitch trim drives the same trim, and the manual trim can overpower it (AFM electric trim supplement).", "src": "POH 2-9"},
{"id": "p1-084", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "How does the Seneca I stabilator anti-servo tab move, and why?", "a": "Same direction as the stabilator, with more travel, for stability", "d": ["Opposite the stabilator, like a servo tab, to reduce control forces", "Same direction as the stabilator, but only when trim is moved", "Opposite the stabilator, with more travel, to add pitch authority"], "why": "POH 2-2: the tab moves in the same direction as the stabilator but with increased travel, which improves longitudinal stability (adds control feel) and provides trim.", "src": "POH 2-2"},
{"id": "p1-085", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "What is the Seneca I rudder trim tab designed to do?", "a": "Relieve excessive pedal force in single-engine flight", "d": ["Eliminate adverse yaw in normal coordinated turns", "Hold the nose wheel straight for gear retraction", "Counter P-factor during takeoff and climb power only"], "why": "POH 2-2: the rudder trim tab can relieve the pilot of excessive pedal force during single-engine operation. Adverse yaw is handled by the aileron-rudder springs.", "src": "POH 2-2"},
{"id": "p1-086", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "How does the Seneca I design reduce adverse yaw in turns?", "a": "Differential ailerons lightly spring-linked to the rudder", "d": ["Frise-type ailerons combined with an electric yaw damper", "Roll spoilers that deploy with each aileron input", "A rudder trim tab geared to move with the ailerons"], "why": "POH 2-9: the ailerons have differential action and are lightly interconnected with the rudder by springs, reducing adverse yaw and the coordination needed in normal turns.", "src": "POH 2-9"},
{"id": "p1-087", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "What type of wing flaps does the Seneca I have?", "a": "Slotted", "d": ["Split", "Plain", "Fowler"], "why": "POH 2-1 describes slotted wing flaps, mechanically operated by a four-position handle between the front seats.", "src": "POH 2-1"},
{"id": "p1-088", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "How are the Seneca I flaps moved, and what returns them to the retracted position?", "a": "A floor lever with a button; springs retract them", "d": ["A panel switch and electric motor; the motor retracts them", "The gear hydraulic pump; a relief valve retracts them", "A floor lever with a button; only airloads retract them"], "why": "POH 2-9: the flaps are extended manually by the lever on the floor between the front seats (depress the button on its end first). They are aerodynamically balanced for light forces and spring-loaded to return to retracted.", "src": "POH 2-9"},
{"id": "p1-090", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "You've lost all electrical power on approach. Can you still extend the Seneca I flaps?", "a": "Yes; the flaps are fully manual, with no motor", "d": ["No; the flap motor needs bus power", "Only to 10°, using the emergency knob", "Only if the hydraulic pump still has pressure"], "why": "POH 2-9: the flaps are extended manually by a lever, so there is no flap motor. The gear, by contrast, would need the emergency free-fall knob.", "src": "POH 2-9"},
{"id": "p1-091", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "Passengers are about to board and the flaps are at 10°. What does the POH recommend?", "a": "Retract fully; the right flap holds a step only when up", "d": ["Leave them at 10° so the step up onto the wing is lower", "Lower them to 40° so passengers clear the wing walk", "Retract them; either flap can then be used as a boarding step"], "why": "POH 2-9: a past-center lock holds the flap retracted so it can be used as a step on the right side. It won't support a step load unless fully retracted.", "src": "POH 2-9"},
{"id": "p1-093", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "What are the Seneca I wing span and wing area?", "a": "38.88 ft span; 208.7 sq ft area", "d": ["38.88 ft span; 178.0 sq ft area", "28.5 ft span; 208.7 sq ft area", "42.0 ft span; 208.7 sq ft area"], "why": "POH 1-2: span 38.88 ft, area 208.7 sq ft, length 28.5 ft, height 9.9 ft, wing loading 20.1 lb/sq ft and power loading 10.5 lb/hp. Wheel base is 7.0 ft and tread 11.1 ft (POH 1-3).", "src": "POH 1-2 · POH 1-3"},
{"id": "p1-094", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "How many fuel tanks does the Seneca I have, and how are they arranged?", "a": "Four: two interconnected 24.5-gal tanks per wing", "d": ["Two: one 49-gal tank in each wing", "Four: main and auxiliary tanks selected separately", "Six: three interconnected tanks per wing"], "why": "POH 2-10: two 24.5-gallon aluminum tanks in each wing are interconnected, so each wing works as one tank for selection (POH 3-8). Both fill through one neck in the outboard tank, and outboard fuel flows inboard as it is used.", "src": "POH 2-10 · POH 3-8"},
{"id": "p1-095", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "What is the Seneca I total fuel capacity, and how much of it is unusable?", "a": "98 gal total; 5 gal unusable (2.5 per side)", "d": ["98 gal total; 2.5 gal unusable in all", "93 gal total; 5 gal unusable (2.5 per side)", "100 gal total; 7 gal unusable (3.5 per side)"], "why": "POH 1-2 and 2-10: 98 gallons total (49 per wing, POH 9-7) with 2-1/2 unusable gallons per side, leaving 93 gallons usable.", "src": "POH 2-10 · POH 1-2"},
{"id": "p1-098", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "How many fuel pumps does the Seneca I fuel system have?", "a": "Four: an engine-driven and an electric pump per engine", "d": ["Two: one engine-driven pump per engine and no electric pumps", "Three: two engine-driven pumps and one shared electric pump", "Two: one electric pump in each wing, no engine-driven pumps"], "why": "POH 2-10: each engine has an engine-driven pump as the primary supply and an electric pump as a backup in case the engine-driven pump fails.", "src": "POH 2-10"},
{"id": "p1-099", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "Where are the Seneca I electric (auxiliary) fuel pumps located?", "a": "On the aft side of each firewall", "d": ["Inside each inboard fuel tank", "Under the cabin floor by the selectors", "On the forward side of each firewall"], "why": "POH 2-10: an electric pump on the aft side of the firewall for each engine. POH 9-7 puts the gascolators between the selector valves and these boost pumps in the nacelles.", "src": "POH 2-10"},
{"id": "p1-100", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "When does the POH say to use the electric fuel pumps?", "a": "Takeoff and landing, and if an engine-driven pump fails", "d": ["Continuously above 10,000 ft to prevent fuel vapor lock", "Only for engine starting and priming, then turned off", "Whenever either fuel selector is placed on CROSSFEED"], "why": "POH 2-10: the electric pumps back up the engine-driven pumps and should be on for takeoffs and landings in case an engine-driven pump fails then. The switches are on the panel left of the pilot.", "src": "POH 2-10 · POH 3-8"},
{"id": "p1-102", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "Where are the Seneca I fuel selectors, and what positions do they have?", "a": "On the tunnel between the front seats: OFF, ON, CROSSFEED", "d": ["On the floor between the seats: LEFT, RIGHT, BOTH, OFF", "On the left sidewall below the panel: ON, OFF, CROSSFEED", "On the overhead panel: MAIN, AUXILIARY, CROSSFEED, OFF"], "why": "POH 2-10: the selectors are on the tunnel between the pilot and copilot seats, and each lever has OFF, ON and CROSSFEED. Normally each engine draws from its own wing.", "src": "POH 2-10"},
{"id": "p1-103", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "What two purposes does the POH give for the Seneca I crossfeed?", "a": "Extend single-engine range and keep fuel weight balanced", "d": ["Feed both engines in an emergency and aid engine priming", "Transfer fuel between the wing tanks and drain water", "Supply the cabin heater and balance fuel while on the ground"], "why": "POH 2-10: the crossfeed lines let an engine use fuel from the opposite side, to extend single-engine range and to keep the fuel weight balanced.", "src": "POH 2-10"},
{"id": "p1-106", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "What two crossfeed restrictions are stated in the NOTE on POH 2-10?", "a": "Never both selectors on CROSSFEED; no takeoff on CROSSFEED", "d": ["Never both on CROSSFEED; no crossfeed above 10,000 ft", "No takeoff on CROSSFEED; no crossfeed with pumps off", "No crossfeed with both engines running; no landing on ON"], "why": "POH 2-10 NOTE: do not operate with both fuel selectors on CROSSFEED, and do not take off with a selector on CROSSFEED. Section 3 also limits crossfeed to level flight.", "src": "POH 2-10"},
{"id": "p1-107", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "Both engines running in cruise, the left wing has 10 gal more than the right. How can you rebalance using the POH fuel system?", "a": "Right selector to CROSSFEED, left stays ON, until even", "d": ["Left selector to CROSSFEED, right stays ON, until even", "Both selectors to CROSSFEED until even", "Both electric pumps on to transfer fuel across"], "why": "POH 3-9 permits running both engines from the same tank in cruise. With the right selector on CROSSFEED, both engines draw from the left wing until the sides even up; never put both selectors on CROSSFEED (POH 2-10).", "src": "POH 3-9 · POH 2-10"},
{"id": "p1-108", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "With one engine inoperative, can the operating engine use the fuel in both wings?", "a": "Yes, by moving its selector between ON and CROSSFEED", "d": ["No; each wing can feed only its own engine", "Yes, but only with both selectors on CROSSFEED", "Yes, by running both electric pumps to transfer it"], "why": "POH 2-10/3-8: crossfeed lets an engine use the opposite wing's fuel, so the good engine can use both sides (less 2.5 gal unusable per side). Both selectors on CROSSFEED is prohibited.", "src": "POH 2-10 · POH 3-8"},
{"id": "p1-109", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "How is fuel quantity measured and displayed in the Seneca I?", "a": "A sender in each tank; one gauge shows each wing's total", "d": ["One sender per wing; a separate cockpit gauge for each tank", "Sight gauges in each tank, read on preflight", "A digital totalizer that computes quantity from fuel flow"], "why": "POH 2-10: a sender in each of the four tanks electrically transmits the total in each pair of tanks to a single gauge per wing; fuel pressure and fuel flow gauges are also provided.", "src": "POH 2-10"},
{"id": "p1-110", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "Where in the fuel flow path is each Seneca I gascolator (fuel filter) located?", "a": "Between the fuel selector valve and the electric fuel pump", "d": ["Between the engine-driven fuel pump and the fuel injector servo", "Inside each inboard tank, at the fuel outlet", "Between the flow divider and the injector nozzles"], "why": "POH 2-10: one gascolator per side between the selector valve and the electric pump. Its drain is on the inboard side of the nacelle, forward of and below the wing leading edge.", "src": "POH 2-10"},
{"id": "p1-111", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "How many fuel quick drains does the Seneca I have in total, and what are they?", "a": "Eight: 4 tank, 2 gascolator, 2 crossfeed", "d": ["Six: 4 tank, 2 gascolator", "Eight: 4 tank, 2 gascolator, 2 fuel injector", "Ten: 4 tank, 2 gascolator, 4 crossfeed"], "why": "POH 2-10: quick drains for each fuel tank (4), the fuel gascolators (2) and each crossfeed line (2).", "src": "POH 2-10"},
{"id": "p1-112", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "Where are the Seneca I fuel crossfeed line drains?", "a": "Under the belly, opposite the right flap trailing edge", "d": ["Under the belly, opposite the left flap trailing edge", "Under each nacelle, next to the gascolator drain", "Under each wing, just outboard of the tank drains"], "why": "POH 2-10 puts the two crossfeed drains under the fuselage opposite the trailing edge of the right flap (POH 9-7: inboard of the right flap). They are drained on the right-side walk-around (POH 6-2).", "src": "POH 2-10 · POH 9-7"},
{"id": "p1-113", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "What makes up the Seneca I fuel tank vent system?", "a": "Cap vents, a vent link between tanks, filler-neck overflows", "d": ["A single NACA vent under each wingtip and nothing else", "Cap vents only, since the tanks in each wing are not interconnected", "Cap vents plus a vent line routed to the heater fuel pump"], "why": "POH 2-10: a vent in each fuel cap, a vent interconnect between the tanks in each wing, and an overflow line from the top of each filler neck. Check the cap vent on preflight (POH 6-2).", "src": "POH 2-10"},
{"id": "p1-114", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "Using the POH fuel schematic, what is the order of components from the tanks to the cylinders?", "a": "Selector, filter, electric pump, engine pump, injector, divider", "d": ["Filter, selector, engine pump, electric pump, injector, divider", "Selector, electric pump, filter, injector, engine pump, divider", "Selector, filter, engine pump, divider, injector, electric pump"], "why": "POH 2-11 schematic and 2-10 text: selector valve, gascolator (filter), electric pump, engine-driven pump, fuel injector (servo), then the flow divider and nozzles.", "src": "POH 2-11 · POH 2-10"},
{"id": "p1-118", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "In cruise, with plenty of fuel in the right tanks, the right engine's fuel pressure drops and the engine starts to falter. What backup does the fuel system provide?", "a": "Turn on the right electric fuel pump", "d": ["Move the right selector to CROSSFEED", "Pull the right alternate air control", "Feather the right engine at once"], "why": "POH 2-10: each engine's electric pump is the backup for its engine-driven pump (POH 3-8 repeats this). Crossfeed changes the fuel source, not the pump, so it would not cure a failed engine-driven pump.", "src": "POH 2-10 · POH 3-8"},
{"id": "p1-120", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What supplies the varying oil pressure that each Seneca I propeller uses to hold the selected RPM?", "a": "An engine-mounted governor, through the propeller shaft", "d": ["The engine oil pump, through a fixed pressure regulator", "An electric pump in each nacelle, set by the prop lever", "The nitrogen charge in the dome, through a regulator"], "why": "POH 2-4: a governor on each engine supplies oil through the propeller shaft at varying pressures to keep a constant RPM. Oil pressure moves the blades toward high RPM; the dome's nitrogen charge pushes them toward feather.", "src": "POH 2-4"},
{"id": "p1-121", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Why does the POH call for smooth, not-too-rapid throttle movements on the Seneca I engines?", "a": "To avoid engine wear and damage to the dynamic counterweights", "d": ["To avoid flooding the float carburetors and fouling the plugs", "To keep the fuel injection servo from vapor locking at idle", "To protect the alternators and regulators from sudden voltage surges"], "why": "POH 2-3: smooth throttle operation prevents unnecessary engine wear and damage to the engines' dynamic counterweights. The engines are fuel injected, so there are no carburetors to flood.", "src": "POH 2-3"},
{"id": "p1-122", "t": "s-gear", "q": "Which way does the Seneca I nose gear retract, and how does that help a free-fall extension?", "a": "Forward; once it starts down, the airstream pushes it to lock", "d": ["Aft; the springs must overcome the airstream to lock it down fully", "Inboard; gravity alone swings it down and into the locks", "Aft; the airstream then pushes it down and into the downlock"], "why": "POH 2-5: the mains fold inboard toward the centerline and the nose gear retracts forward. Once the nose gear starts down, the airstream pushes on it and helps it lock, along with gravity and springs.", "src": "POH 2-5"},
{"id": "p1-123", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "Where are the Seneca I's standard cabin entry doors?", "a": "A front door on the right and a rear door on the left", "d": ["A front door on the left and a rear door on the right", "Front and rear doors, both on the right side", "Front and rear doors, both on the left side"], "why": "POH 2-1: the fuselage has a front door on the right side and a rear door on the left; a large rear cargo door is available as an option. The right flap can be used as a step only when fully retracted (POH 2-9).", "src": "POH 2-1"},
{"id": "p5-001", "t": "s-elec", "q": "What are the Seneca I's sources of electrical power?", "a": "Two 60-amp alternators and a 12-volt, 35 amp-hour battery", "d": ["Two 70-amp alternators and a 24-volt, 17 amp-hour battery", "One 60-amp alternator on the left engine and a 12-volt, 35 amp-hour battery", "Two 60-amp generators and a 24-volt, 35 amp-hour battery"], "why": "Each engine drives its own 60-ampere alternator. The 12-volt, 35 Ah battery handles starting, power with engines off, and backup for the alternators.", "src": "POH 2-12 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-002", "t": "s-elec", "q": "To what voltage do the Seneca I's voltage regulators hold the electrical bus?", "a": "14.0 volts", "d": ["12.0 volts", "28.0 volts", "24.0 volts"], "why": "Two solid-state regulators keep the bus at 14.0 volts. It is a 14-volt system with a 12-volt battery, not a 28-volt system.", "src": "POH 2-12 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-003", "t": "s-elec", "q": "Where is the Seneca I's battery located?", "a": "In the nose, just forward of the nose baggage compartment", "d": ["In the aft fuselage, behind the rear baggage compartment bulkhead", "In the left engine nacelle, just aft of the engine firewall", "Under the cockpit floor, beneath the front seats"], "why": "The 12-volt battery sits in the nose section just forward of the nose baggage compartment and is reached through that compartment.", "src": "POH 2-12, 9-8 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-004", "t": "s-elec", "q": "Per the POH, how should a run-down Seneca battery be recharged?", "a": "Remove it from the airplane; start at 4 amps and finish at 2 amps", "d": ["Leave it installed and quick-charge it from a ground power cart", "Leave it installed and charge at 10 amps with the master ON", "Run both engines at 2000 RPM until the ammeters read zero"], "why": "Section 2 says to remove the battery from the airplane to charge it. Section 9 gives the rate: start at 4 A, finish at 2 A. Quick charges are not recommended.", "src": "POH 2-12, 9-8"},
{"id": "p5-005", "t": "s-elec", "q": "Your school's nose preflight checks the external power plug. Where on the Seneca is that receptacle?", "a": "Lower left side of the nose", "d": ["Lower right side of the nose", "Left side of the aft fuselage", "Inside the nose baggage compartment"], "why": "POH 2-12 and 9-8 put the external power receptacle on the lower left side of the nose. It is optional on the Seneca I, but your school preflight checks it. It takes a 12–14 volt source; keep the master OFF while plugging in or unplugging.", "src": "POH 2-12, 9-8 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-006", "t": "s-elec", "q": "You are starting with Piper External Power (PEP). Where should the master switch be when plugging in, and during the start?", "a": "OFF while plugging in; ON for cranking and the start", "d": ["ON while plugging in; OFF for cranking and the start", "OFF for both, so the external source powers the starter", "ON for both, so the battery assists the external source"], "why": "Keep the master OFF while inserting or removing the plug to prevent sparking. Then turn it ON and do a normal start. The external source is a 12-volt battery (12–14 V).", "src": "POH 2-12, 6-4 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-008", "t": "s-elec", "q": "What does each alternator's overvoltage relay do?", "a": "Takes its alternator off line if its output voltage runs too high", "d": ["Disconnects the battery from the bus if bus voltage drops too low", "Limits that alternator's output current to its 60-amp rating", "Opens the master solenoid to shed every electrical load at once"], "why": "Some regulator failures let alternator voltage climb with nothing to stop it. The relay shuts that alternator off to protect the electrical and avionics equipment, and the red light shows the trip.", "src": "POH 2-12, 3-7 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-009", "t": "s-elec", "q": "In cruise the left ammeter drops to zero, but the left OVERVOLTAGE light stays out. What does this mean?", "a": "The left alternator isn't producing current; the light shows only relay trips", "d": ["The left alternator is fine, because any alternator failure would light that lamp", "The left ammeter has failed, because the overvoltage light did not come on", "The battery has taken over and the alternator will recover itself"], "why": "A zero reading means that alternator is producing no current. The POH says the red light's only job is to show a tripped overvoltage relay; it may not come on for other alternator failures.", "src": "POH 2-12 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-010", "t": "s-elec", "q": "What do the Seneca I's two ammeters show?", "a": "Each alternator's output current, working as a load meter", "d": ["Battery charge or discharge, on a center-zero scale", "Bus voltage on each side of a split electrical bus", "Current drawn by the avionics bus and the lighting bus"], "why": "There is one ammeter per alternator, and each shows how much current its alternator is producing. Zero means that alternator is producing nothing. A reading near 60 means it is heavily loaded.", "src": "POH 2-12, 3-7 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-011", "t": "s-elec", "q": "One ammeter is reading near 60 amps in cruise. What does the POH recommend?", "a": "Turn off unneeded equipment, since that alternator is being taxed", "d": ["Nothing; about 60 amps is the normal cruise reading", "Turn that alternator off and let the other one carry the whole load", "Increase that engine's RPM so the alternator can produce more"], "why": "A reading near 60 means demand is taxing that alternator, so reduce the load. The alternators already give rated output at low RPM, so adding RPM does not help.", "src": "POH 2-12"},
{"id": "p5-013", "t": "s-elec", "q": "On one engine, why must you watch the electrical load on the remaining alternator?", "a": "An overloaded alternator may burn out or trip its circuit breaker", "d": ["The good alternator's output is cut in half at single-engine power", "The overvoltage relay trips whenever only one alternator is on line", "The battery is disconnected from the bus when a prop is feathered"], "why": "On one engine, one alternator carries everything. The POH warns that overloading it can burn it out or trip its breaker. Reduce the load to 50 A or less after an engine failure.", "src": "POH 2-12, 3-15 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-014", "t": "s-elec", "q": "Engines running, all equipment off except the master, and the two ammeter readings add up to a significant amount. What does that suggest?", "a": "The battery has a low state of charge", "d": ["An overvoltage relay is about to trip", "One alternator is not sharing the load", "A circuit breaker has tripped somewhere"], "why": "With no loads on, the ammeters show only battery charging current plus instrument draw. A large total means the battery is taking a lot of charge. Find out why, or have a mechanic check the battery and system.", "src": "POH 2-16"},
{"id": "p5-017", "t": "s-elec", "q": "Where are the master, magneto, fuel pump, starter, alternator, light and pitot heat switches?", "a": "On the switch panel to the left of the pilot", "d": ["On the lower right panel above the circuit breakers", "On an overhead panel above the windshield", "On the pedestal below the power quadrant"], "why": "Most electrical switches, including the master and the magnetos, are on the switch panel to the pilot's left. The red overvoltage lights are there too.", "src": "POH 2-12"},
{"id": "p5-021", "t": "s-elec", "q": "After lots of restart cranking the battery is weak, and the good alternator has dropped off line. What may bring it back, per the POH?", "a": "Shed heavy loads, operating alternator ON, master OFF briefly, then master ON", "d": ["Leave the master ON and cycle the operating alternator switch several times", "Leave the master ON and turn both alternator switches OFF, then ON", "Turn the master OFF for the rest of the flight to save the battery"], "why": "First check the alternator breakers. Shed heavy loads with their own switches, not the master. Then turn the master off for a short time and back on, and watch the ammeter. If there's no output, repeat with longer waits.", "src": "POH 3-15"},
{"id": "p5-024", "t": "s-elec", "q": "Why does the POH want radio, light and pitot heat switches off before engine start?", "a": "So the system isn't overloaded when the starter engages", "d": ["So the overvoltage relays don't trip during the start", "So the master solenoid contacts don't weld closed", "Because the alternators can't carry any load at idle"], "why": "The starter is the biggest single electrical load. Turning other loads off first avoids an overloaded condition when it is engaged.", "src": "POH 7-1 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-025", "t": "s-elec", "q": "If the master switch is turned OFF with both engines running, what happens?", "a": "The engines keep running; magneto ignition doesn't need ship's power", "d": ["Both engines quit, because the ignition runs off the main bus", "The engines keep running only if the electric fuel pumps are on", "The left engine quits, and the right runs on its own alternator"], "why": "Ignition comes from the engine-driven magnetos, and fuel comes from engine-driven pumps. The external-power start procedure itself has you turn the master off with the engine running.", "src": "POH 6-4 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-026", "t": "s-elec", "q": "Which Seneca I flight instruments are vacuum-driven?", "a": "The attitude indicators and directional gyros", "d": ["The attitude indicators and turn indicators", "The directional gyros and airspeed indicators", "The attitude indicators, directional gyros and turn indicators"], "why": "The vacuum system draws filtered cabin air through the attitude and heading gyros. The turn indicator is electric, which is why the POH uses it as a cross-check when vacuum fails.", "src": "POH 2-16, 3-16 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-027", "t": "s-elec", "q": "How is vacuum supplied in the Seneca I?", "a": "An engine-driven vacuum pump on each engine", "d": ["A single electric vacuum pump in the nose", "One engine-driven pump on the left engine only", "Venturi tubes on each side of the fuselage"], "why": "There is one pump on each engine, plus plumbing, regulators, filters, and a check valve. Either pump alone can run the gyros.", "src": "POH 2-16 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-028", "t": "s-elec", "q": "What vacuum do the Seneca's regulators maintain, and at what RPM?", "a": "5.0 ±0.1 in. Hg at 2000 RPM", "d": ["4.5 ±0.1 in. Hg at 1500 RPM", "5.2 ±0.1 in. Hg at 2700 RPM", "5.0 ±0.5 in. Hg at idle RPM"], "why": "A regulator on the right aft side of each firewall holds 5.0 ±0.1 in. Hg at 2000 RPM. The Operating Tips list what to check before flight if the gauge doesn't show this.", "src": "POH 2-16, 7-1 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-030", "t": "s-elec", "q": "What does a vacuum reading below 4.5 in. Hg indicate?", "a": "Low airflow through the gyros, so their readings may be inaccurate", "d": ["Normal operation; the gauge reads this low in every phase of flight", "Too much suction, which may overspeed and damage the gyros", "Normal single-pump operation, as long as one red button shows"], "why": "Below 4.5 in. Hg, not enough air is moving through the gyro instruments to keep them accurate. Low readings are expected only at low RPM or very high altitude.", "src": "POH 2-16"},
{"id": "p5-031", "t": "s-elec", "q": "The left engine fails and is feathered. What must you do to keep the gyros powered?", "a": "Nothing; a check valve automatically isolates the failed side", "d": ["Select the right pump with the vacuum selector valve on the panel", "Turn on the standby electric vacuum pump with its own switch", "Open the alternate air to restore suction"], "why": "When one pump or one side's hose loses suction, the check valve closes by itself and the other pump supplies vacuum. The red button for that side shows on the gauge. No pilot selection is needed.", "src": "POH 2-16, 3-16 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-033", "t": "s-elec", "q": "On one engine, up to what altitude can a single vacuum pump run a dual set of gyros?", "a": "12,500 ft; above that, keep a high RPM", "d": ["10,000 ft; above that, you must descend", "15,000 ft; above that, open alternate air", "Any altitude, if RPM is at least 2000"], "why": "Each pump alone can drive both sets of gyros up to 12,500 ft. Above that, a high RPM is needed on the remaining pump to keep enough suction.", "src": "POH 2-16 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-035", "t": "s-elec", "q": "Per the Operating Tips, which of these is a normal cause of a low vacuum reading, not a malfunction?", "a": "Low engine RPM, such as on approach or in training maneuvers", "d": ["A dirty or restricted common air filter behind the panel", "A collapsed or broken vacuum line behind the panel", "Worn vacuum pumps that no longer pull full suction"], "why": "A low reading is normal at low RPM and at very high altitude (above 12,000 ft). The dirty filter, damaged lines and worn pumps are items to check before flight.", "src": "POH 7-1"},
{"id": "p5-036", "t": "s-elec", "q": "Where is the Seneca I's pitot source, and which instrument uses it?", "a": "Aluminum mast under the left wing; airspeed indicator only", "d": ["Mast under the right wing; airspeed indicator and altimeter", "Probe on the nose; airspeed indicator only", "Mast under the left wing; airspeed indicator and VSI"], "why": "Pitot (total) pressure comes from the mast under the left wing. It feeds only the airspeed indicator.", "src": "POH 2-18 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-037", "t": "s-elec", "q": "Where are the Seneca I's static sources?", "a": "One on each side of the rear fuselage, connected inside", "d": ["A single flush port on the left side of the rear fuselage", "Built into the pitot mast under the left wing, aft of the inlet", "One on each side of the nose, just below the windshield"], "why": "Two static units, one on each side of the aft fuselage, are tied together inside. A slip or skid raises pressure on one side and lowers it on the other, and the connection averages them out.", "src": "POH 2-18 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-038", "t": "s-elec", "q": "Which Seneca I instruments use static pressure?", "a": "Altimeter, vertical speed indicator and airspeed indicator", "d": ["Altimeter and vertical speed indicator only", "Altimeter, vertical speed indicator and the attitude indicator", "Airspeed indicator and the manifold pressure gauges"], "why": "The altimeter, VSI and airspeed indicator all use static pressure. The airspeed indicator also gets pitot pressure.", "src": "POH 2-18 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-039", "t": "s-elec", "q": "What ice protection does your Seneca's pitot-static system have?", "a": "A heated pitot mast; the static ports are not heated", "d": ["A heated pitot and heated static ports", "Heated static ports only; the pitot is not heated", "None at all; the alternate static is the only backup"], "why": "The pitot mast has a heating element for ice or heavy rain (an option on the Seneca I; your school preflight checks it warm). The static sensors are not heated, because experience shows they are unlikely to ice up.", "src": "POH 2-18 · School checklist · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-040", "t": "s-elec", "q": "Where is the alternate static source control, and what static source does it use?", "a": "Below the panel, right of the power quadrant; cabin air pressure", "d": ["Below the panel, left of the pilot; outside air from the tailcone", "On the left switch panel; cabin air pressure", "Right of the power quadrant; air from the pitot mast"], "why": "With the valve in the alternate position, the altimeter, VSI and airspeed indicator take static pressure from the cabin, so their readings may change slightly.", "src": "POH 2-18 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-041", "t": "s-elec", "q": "With alternate static selected, what does the POH say can change your instrument readings?", "a": "Airspeed, heater and vent settings, and the storm window", "d": ["Only airspeed; the heater and vent settings have no effect", "Outside air temperature, humidity and the altimeter setting", "Pitot heat and the load on the alternators"], "why": "These all change cabin pressure. You can see the effect by switching sources at different speeds and vent settings, including the storm window open, which is allowed only below 150 MPH.", "src": "POH 2-18"},
{"id": "p5-042", "t": "s-elec", "q": "You suspect water in the static system. Per the POH, how do you drain it?", "a": "Select alternate static; its valve is at the system's low point", "d": ["Open the drain valve in the lower left front side panel", "Turn on pitot heat long enough to evaporate the water in the lines", "Blow out the static ports with low-pressure shop air"], "why": "The alternate static selector valve is at the lowest point of the static system, so selecting alternate drains it. The drain in the lower left side panel is for the pitot line. The walk-around says to drain both before flight.", "src": "POH 2-18, 6-2 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-043", "t": "s-elec", "q": "What does the drain in the lower left front side panel remove moisture from?", "a": "The pitot line between the mast and the instrument panel", "d": ["The static lines running from the rear fuselage static ports", "The fuel crossfeed line running under the cabin floor", "The vacuum system's common air filter"], "why": "That drain is for the pitot pressure line. Moisture in the static system is drained by selecting the alternate static source.", "src": "POH 2-18 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-050", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "What provides the cabin and defroster heat in your Seneca?", "a": "A heat exchanger (muff) on each engine's exhaust manifold", "d": ["A Janitrol combustion heater mounted in the nose baggage area", "Electric heating elements under the cabin floor panels", "Air bled from the engine-driven vacuum pumps"], "why": "Each engine has a heater muff around its exhaust manifold. That is your Seneca's only cabin heat; the optional Janitrol combustion heater (aft fuselage) is not installed.", "src": "POH 2-20 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-051", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "Where does air for the exhaust heater muffs come in?", "a": "Through a scoop on the outboard side of each cowling", "d": ["Through the inlets in each wing's leading edge", "Through an inlet at the base of the vertical fin", "Through a scoop on the inboard side of each nacelle"], "why": "Air enters a scoop on the outboard side of each cowling and is heated in the muff. A heat-and-defrost valve on the firewall sends it to the windshield or the cabin.", "src": "POH 2-20 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-052", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "Where does fresh air for the cabin and for the overhead outlets come from?", "a": "Wing leading-edge inlets; the overhead outlets are fed from inlets on the fin", "d": ["Cowling scoops on each engine; the overhead outlets are fed from wing-root inlets", "Nose inlets; the overhead outlets are fed from a roof scoop", "Wing leading-edge inlets; the overhead outlets are fed from the heater muffs"], "why": "Fresh air for the heat mixing box comes in through each wing's leading edge. The overhead outlets take air from inlets on each side of the lower leading edge of the vertical fin.", "src": "POH 2-20 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-053", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "How does the standard heating system control cabin temperature?", "a": "A control box mixes muff-heated air with fresh air from the wing inlets", "d": ["A thermostat cycles the exhaust muffs on and off as needed", "The pilot varies how much exhaust gas is routed through the heater muffs", "A control box mixes muff-heated air with warm air from the oil coolers"], "why": "Heated air from the muffs goes to a temperature-fresh air control box, where it is mixed with fresh air from the wing leading-edge inlets before entering the cabin.", "src": "POH 2-20 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-054", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "Where does heated or fresh air enter the cabin for the occupants?", "a": "An outlet near each occupant's feet, plus adjustable overhead outlets", "d": ["Only through the windshield defroster outlets and the side wall air vents", "Only through adjustable overhead outlets in the ceiling", "Through outlets along each cabin side wall at window level only"], "why": "Each occupant has a floor outlet near the feet for heated or fresh air, as selected on the panel. Overhead outlets give fresh air: turn the rim counterclockwise for more flow, clockwise for less.", "src": "POH 2-20 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-055", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "Where are the standard cabin heat and defroster controls?", "a": "On the right side of the instrument panel", "d": ["On the console between the two front seats", "On the switch panel to the left of the pilot", "On the pedestal below the power quadrant"], "why": "The cabin heat and defroster controls for the standard muff system are on the right side of the instrument panel.", "src": "POH 2-20 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-056", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "How is the defroster blower turned on and off?", "a": "Lever at full on/HI starts it; moving it back about an inch stops it", "d": ["With a separate DEFROST BLOWER switch on the left switch panel", "It runs whenever the master switch is ON and cannot be turned off", "It runs whenever cabin heat is on and stops when heat is off"], "why": "The blower defogs the windshield on the ground. It runs when the defrost lever is at full on/HI, and moving the lever back about an inch shuts it off in flight.", "src": "POH 2-20"},
{"id": "p5-057", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "Per the POH, how do you get maximum windshield defrosting?", "a": "Turn cabin heat off and the defroster full on", "d": ["Turn cabin heat and the defroster both full on", "Turn the defroster on and open the storm window", "Turn the defroster on and select alternate air"], "why": "Shutting off cabin heat sends all available hot air to the windshield outlets.", "src": "POH 2-20"},
{"id": "p5-067", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "When is a Seneca I approved for flight into known icing conditions?", "a": "Only with the complete optional Piper ice protection system installed", "d": ["Whenever pitot heat and the heated windshield panel are both installed", "Whenever the heated pitot and propeller deice both work", "Never; no Seneca I can be approved for known icing"], "why": "Known-icing approval requires the full system: boots, prop deice, electric windshield panel, heated pitot, heated stall warning transmitters, anti-icing fuel vents, governor shields and deflectors, and the wing ice light. Your Seneca has no ice protection system, so flight in icing is prohibited.", "src": "POH 2-22, 3-6, 6-13 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-086", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "How does the Seneca I warn of an approaching stall?", "a": "A light and horn, triggered by two lift detectors on the left wing", "d": ["A horn only, triggered by a single vane on the right wing", "A stick shaker, triggered by an angle-of-attack vane on the nose", "A light only, triggered by a pressure port in the left wing"], "why": "Two lift detectors sit on the leading edge of the left wing, outboard of the nacelle. Heated detectors are part of the optional ice protection system.", "src": "POH 2-27"},
{"id": "p5-087", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "Which lift detector activates the stall warning with the flaps at 25° or 40°?", "a": "The inboard detector", "d": ["The outboard detector", "Both detectors together", "Neither; the warning is disabled at 40°"], "why": "The inboard detector works with the flaps at 25° and 40°. The outboard one works at the other settings (0° and 10°).", "src": "POH 2-27"},
{"id": "p5-088", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "On preflight, with the flaps fully down, you lift the outboard stall vane and the horn stays silent. Is the system faulty?", "a": "Not necessarily; the outboard vane works only with flaps at 0° or 10°", "d": ["Yes; either vane should sound the horn at any flap setting from 0° to 40°", "Yes; the outboard vane is the one that works at 40°", "Not necessarily; the horn works only with an engine running"], "why": "With flaps at 25° or 40°, only the inboard detector is active. The school preflight tests the outboard vane at 0–10° and the inboard vane at 25–40°, which matches the POH.", "src": "POH 2-27 · School checklist"},
{"id": "p5-089", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "How can you tell the stall warning horn from the gear warning horn?", "a": "The two horns have different sounds", "d": ["They sound the same; check which light is on", "The stall horn sounds only with the flaps down", "The gear warning has no horn, only a red light"], "why": "The POH says the stall warning horn sounds different from the landing gear warning horn. The stall warning also has its own light.", "src": "POH 2-27"},
{"id": "p5-094", "t": "s-gear", "q": "Where is the brake fluid reservoir, and when should it be checked?", "a": "Top rear of the nose baggage area; periodically and each 100-hour", "d": ["Behind the left main gear; at each annual inspection only", "Built into the gear hydraulic power pack; at every 50-hour inspection", "Under the pilot's floor panel; checked as part of every preflight"], "why": "It's behind a panel marked \"Brake Reservoir Behind\" at the top rear of the nose baggage compartment. Keep the fluid at the marked level; your school's nose preflight also checks it. The gear system has its own separate reservoir built into the gear pump.", "src": "POH 9-6, 2-6 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-096", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "How does the POH say to tie down the Seneca?", "a": "Ropes to the wing rings and tail skid at about 45°, using bowline knots", "d": ["Ropes straight down from the wing rings and tail skid, using slip knots", "Ropes to the propeller hubs and nose gear fork, using bowlines", "Chains around both main gear struts only, pulled tight at 90° to the ground"], "why": "Head into the wind if possible, lock the controls with the wheel lock or surface blocks, block the wheels, then tie down at about 45°. Use bowlines, never slip knots, and install the pitot cover.", "src": "POH 9-2, 9-3"},
{"id": "p5-097", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Per the POH ground handling section, how should the propellers be set for taxiing?", "a": "Low pitch, high RPM", "d": ["High pitch, low RPM", "Midrange pitch, to cut prop noise", "Feathered on whichever engine isn't needed"], "why": "Taxi with the props in low pitch, high RPM. Avoid high RPM over loose stones or gravel, which can damage the blades.", "src": "POH 9-1"},
{"id": "p5-100", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Ambient temperatures are expected to be about 20°F. Which oil grade does the POH require?", "a": "SAE 30", "d": ["SAE 40", "SAE 50", "SAE 20"], "why": "20°F falls only in the SAE 30 range (0–70°F). SAE 40 starts at 30°F and SAE 20 is for below 10°F.", "src": "POH 9-7 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-101", "t": "s-engine", "q": "How often does the POH recommend oil changes, and when may the interval be extended?", "a": "Every 50 hr; up to double with a full-flow filter changed each 50 hr", "d": ["Every 100 hr; up to 50% longer with any type of oil filter or screen", "Every 25 hr; up to double with a screen-type oil filter", "Every 50 hours; no extension is allowed under any condition"], "why": "Drain and refill every 50 hours, or sooner in bad conditions. With full-flow cartridge filters, the interval can be up to doubled, as long as the filter element is replaced every 50 hours.", "src": "POH 9-7"},
{"id": "p5-103", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What gas does the POH recommend for charging the propeller dome?", "a": "Dry nitrogen", "d": ["Carbon dioxide", "Argon", "Oxygen"], "why": "Dry nitrogen is recommended. Section 2 allows compressed air only if it has no moisture, since moisture may freeze the piston in cold weather.", "src": "POH 9-6, 2-4"},
{"id": "p5-104", "t": "s-engine", "q": "How does the propeller dome pressure change with temperature, per the POH?", "a": "It rises about 1/3 psi for each 1°F increase", "d": ["It rises about 1 psi for each 1°F increase", "It rises about 1/10 psi for each 1°F increase", "It stays fixed; temperature has no effect"], "why": "The Section 9 text gives about 1/3 psi per °F, so allow for temperature and charge to the spinner-cap placard. (The POH's chamber pressure table itself steps about 2 psi per 10°F.)", "src": "POH 9-6"},
{"id": "p5-105", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Per the POH chamber pressure table, what prop dome charge is required at 70°F?", "a": "80 psi", "d": ["72 psi", "86 psi", "66 psi"], "why": "From the table: 70°F needs 80 psi. 30°F needs 72 psi, 100°F needs 86 psi, and 0°F needs 66 psi.", "src": "POH 9-6"},
{"id": "p5-106", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What does the POH say not to do when checking or charging the propeller dome?", "a": "Don't check or charge it with the propeller feathered", "d": ["Don't check it when the engine and propeller are cold", "Don't charge it with dry nitrogen", "Don't check it against the placard in the spinner cap"], "why": "The note under the chamber pressure table says never to check or charge the pressure with the propeller feathered.", "src": "POH 9-6"},
{"id": "p5-111", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "When does the POH say to drain the gascolator quick drains?", "a": "Before the first flight of the day and after each refueling", "d": ["Only at each 50-hour inspection, and only by an A&P mechanic", "Only when you suspect water contamination in the tanks", "After every engine shutdown, before the fuel has time to settle"], "why": "Drain them before the first flight of the day and after refueling to check for contamination. The tank drains are checked the same way.", "src": "POH 9-7 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p5-112", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "You keep finding water while draining a gascolator, even after a full minute of draining. What does the POH say?", "a": "Have a mechanic check the fuel system", "d": ["Keep draining until at least a gallon is out", "Fly anyway; the engine-driven pump will separate the water", "Top off the tank and then drain it again"], "why": "Drain until the contamination stops. If it is still there after a minute of draining, the POH says to have a mechanic check the fuel system.", "src": "POH 9-7"},
{"id": "p5-114", "t": "s-elec", "q": "One alternator is inoperative before departure. What does the POH say about taking off into IFR conditions?", "a": "It is not recommended to take off into IFR with a single alternator", "d": ["It is allowed if the operating alternator stays below 50 amps", "It is allowed if the battery has been fully charged first", "It is allowed once the inoperative alternator switch is OFF"], "why": "Section 6 says an IFR takeoff on a single alternator is not recommended. With only one, another failure leaves the battery as the only source, and the POH wants each alternator held to 50 A in flight so the battery keeps charging.", "src": "POH 6-9"},
{"id": "p5-115", "t": "s-elec", "q": "Both alternators fail in flight. Per the POH, what determines how long the battery can carry the needed equipment?", "a": "The equipment's current draw, how soon you noticed, and battery condition", "d": ["The equipment's current draw, engine RPM, and outside air temperature", "Battery condition, engine RPM, and the alternator switch positions", "Only the battery's 35 amp-hour rating, regardless of the load"], "why": "With both alternators out, the battery is the only source, so turn off all unnecessary equipment. That's why the POH wants the ammeters and warning lights watched closely at night and in instrument flight.", "src": "POH 2-16 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-001", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What is the never-exceed speed (Vne) of the Seneca I?", "a": "217 MPH CAS", "d": ["190 MPH CAS", "207 MPH CAS", "195 MPH CAS"], "why": "Vne is 217 MPH CAS, the radial red line at the top of the yellow arc. 190 MPH is Vno, the top of the green arc.", "src": "POH 3-2 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-002", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What is the maximum structural cruising speed (Vno) of the Seneca I?", "a": "190 MPH CAS", "d": ["217 MPH CAS", "146 MPH CAS", "175 MPH CAS"], "why": "Vno is 190 MPH CAS, the top of the green arc. Above it, in the yellow arc up to 217 MPH, fly only in smooth air.", "src": "POH 3-2 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-003", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What is the design maneuvering speed (Va) at the maximum weight of 4,200 lb?", "a": "146 MPH CAS", "d": ["133 MPH CAS", "125 MPH CAS", "150 MPH CAS"], "why": "The AFM gives 146 MPH at 4,200 lb and 133 MPH at the minimum weight of 2,743 lb. A heavier airplane can fly faster before a gust or abrupt input reaches the load limit.", "src": "POH 3-2 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-004", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "Flying solo near the 2,743 lb minimum weight, you enter moderate turbulence. What rough-air/maneuvering speed does the panel placard give?", "a": "133 MPH", "d": ["146 MPH", "190 MPH", "125 MPH"], "why": "The placard 'Rough air or maneuvering speeds' lists 133 MPH at 2,743 lb and 146 MPH at 4,200 lb. Va drops as weight drops, so the gross-weight figure is too fast when light.", "src": "POH 3-4"},
{"id": "p2-005", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What is the maximum flaps-extended speed (Vfe) of the Seneca I?", "a": "125 MPH CAS", "d": ["150 MPH CAS", "105 MPH CAS", "140 MPH CAS"], "why": "Vfe is 125 MPH CAS, the top of the white arc, and the landing checklist placard repeats 'Flaps Set - 125 MPH Max.' The AFM gives this one limit for every flap setting.", "src": "POH 3-2 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-006", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What are the AFM's maximum gear extended speed and maximum gear retract speed?", "a": "150 MPH extended; 125 MPH retract", "d": ["125 MPH extended; 150 MPH retract", "150 MPH for both", "125 MPH for both"], "why": "The gear may be lowered and flown down up to 150 MPH CAS but should be raised only at or below 125 MPH. The placard by the selector reads 'Gear up 125 MPH max / Down 150 MPH max.'", "src": "POH 3-2, 3-4 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-007", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "You are descending at 140 MPH CAS. Which landing gear operation is within limits?", "a": "Extending the gear, but not retracting it", "d": ["Retracting the gear, but not extending it", "Neither extending nor retracting it", "Both extending and retracting it"], "why": "140 MPH is below the 150 MPH gear-down/extended limit but above the 125 MPH gear-retract limit.", "src": "POH 3-2, 3-4"},
{"id": "p2-008", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What is the Seneca I single-engine minimum control speed (Vmc), and how is it marked?", "a": "80 MPH CAS, radial red line", "d": ["76 MPH CAS, bottom of green arc", "105 MPH CAS, radial blue line", "69 MPH CAS, bottom of white arc"], "why": "The AFM lists Vmc as 80 MPH CAS and marks it with a radial red line on the airspeed indicator. The blue line at 105 MPH is Vyse.", "src": "POH 3-2 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-009", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "The radial blue line on the Seneca I airspeed indicator marks what?", "a": "105 MPH, best single-engine rate of climb", "d": ["90 MPH, best single-engine angle of climb", "80 MPH, single-engine minimum control", "125 MPH, maximum flaps extended"], "why": "The blue radial is Vyse, 105 MPH CAS, the best single-engine rate-of-climb speed and the airspeed your school's engine-failure memory items hold. 90 MPH is Vx; the red radial at 80 MPH is Vmc.", "src": "POH 3-2 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-010", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What range does the green arc on the Seneca I airspeed indicator cover?", "a": "76 to 190 MPH", "d": ["69 to 190 MPH", "80 to 217 MPH", "76 to 217 MPH"], "why": "The green arc (normal operating range) runs from 76 MPH, the flaps-up stall speed at the 4,200-lb gross weight, to 190 MPH, Vno.", "src": "POH 3-2"},
{"id": "p2-011", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What range does the white arc (flaps extended range) cover?", "a": "69 to 125 MPH", "d": ["76 to 125 MPH", "69 to 150 MPH", "65 to 125 MPH"], "why": "The white arc runs from 69 MPH, the 40°-flap stall speed at the 4,200-lb gross weight, up to 125 MPH, Vfe.", "src": "POH 3-2"},
{"id": "p2-012", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What are the Seneca I flight load factor limits with flaps up?", "a": "+3.8 G; no inverted maneuvers approved", "d": ["+4.4 G; inverted maneuvers to -1.76 G", "+3.0 G; inverted maneuvers to -1.2 G", "+3.8 G; inverted maneuvers to -1.0 G"], "why": "The AFM gives a maximum positive load factor of 3.8 G (flaps up) and, in place of a negative figure, states that no inverted maneuvers are approved. +4.4 G is the utility-category value.", "src": "POH 3-2 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-013", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What crosswind figure is placarded on the Seneca I instrument panel?", "a": "Demonstrated crosswind component 15 MPH", "d": ["Maximum crosswind component 15 knots", "Demonstrated crosswind component 17 MPH", "Maximum crosswind component 25 MPH"], "why": "The placard reads 'Demonstrated crosswind component 15 MPH,' the value shown in flight testing. POH 6-11 also gives 15 MPH as the maximum crosswind component for landing. Note the unit: MPH, not knots.", "src": "POH 3-4 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-014", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "Your Seneca has no ice protection system. What kinds of operation does its limitations placard allow?", "a": "VFR and IFR, day and night, non-icing", "d": ["VFR only, day and night, non-icing", "VFR and IFR, day only, non-icing", "VFR and IFR, day and night, known icing"], "why": "The placard approves VFR, IFR, day and night non-icing flight when equipped per FAR 91 or 135. Only a properly equipped airplane's placard adds 'and icing conditions.'", "src": "POH 3-3 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-015", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "Under what category is the Seneca I operated, and are spins approved?", "a": "Normal category; no acrobatic maneuvers, including spins", "d": ["Utility category; spins approved with flaps up", "Normal category; spins approved with gear up only", "Utility category; no acrobatic maneuvers except spins"], "why": "The placard requires operation as a normal category airplane and says no acrobatic maneuvers (including spins) are approved. The spin procedure is for unintentional spins only.", "src": "POH 3-3, 3-17 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-016", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "With the rear cabin and cargo doors removed, what are the maximum speed and the single-engine minimum control speed?", "a": "150 MPH maximum; Vmc 81 MPH", "d": ["125 MPH maximum; Vmc 80 MPH", "150 MPH maximum; Vmc 85 MPH", "190 MPH maximum; Vmc 81 MPH"], "why": "With the doors off the AFM limits speed to 150 MPH and raises Vmc to 81 MPH CAS (normally 80). It is VFR non-icing only, and if an engine fails below 81 MPH, reduce power on the operating engine to keep directional control.", "src": "POH 3-9, 3-17"},
{"id": "p2-017", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What are the vacuum system operating limits for all operations?", "a": "4.5 to 5.2 in. Hg", "d": ["5.0 to 6.0 in. Hg", "4.0 to 5.0 in. Hg", "3.5 to 4.5 in. Hg"], "why": "Section L of the limitations gives 4.5 to 5.2 inches of mercury. Below 4.5 in. Hg the vacuum failure procedure applies.", "src": "POH 3-6"},
{"id": "p2-018", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What does the instrument panel placard say about single-engine stalls?", "a": "Not recommended; can cause 500 ft loss and 15° pitch angle", "d": ["Prohibited; can cause a spin within one turn", "Not recommended; can cause 1,000 ft loss and 30° bank", "Permitted only above 3,500 ft with props unfeathered"], "why": "The placard reads 'Single engine stalls not recommended. Can cause 500 ft. loss of altitude and 15° pitch angle.'", "src": "POH 3-5"},
{"id": "p2-019", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "The AFM's 80 MPH CAS single-engine minimum control speed is stated for what conditions?", "a": "Sea level standard conditions", "d": ["Maximum gross weight at 5,000 ft", "Any altitude and temperature", "The single-engine service ceiling"], "why": "The takeoff and climb engine-failure procedures both state Vmc is 80 MPH CAS under sea level standard conditions. Actual Vmc decreases as density altitude increases.", "src": "POH 3-12a, 3-13"},
{"id": "p2-020", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "Which autopilot limitation appears in every autopilot supplement in this AFM?", "a": "Autopilot OFF for takeoff and landing", "d": ["Maximum autopilot speed 195 MPH CAS", "Flaps limited to 25° or less", "Pilot must hold the wheel lightly"], "why": "All autopilot supplements require the autopilot OFF for takeoff and landing. The AltiMatic IIIB-1 and AutoControl III also prohibit use above 195 MPH CAS and limit flaps to 25°; the AltiMatic V/FD-1/V-1 limit is 207 MPH (180 kt) CAS.", "src": "POH 3-24, 3-25, 3-26"},
{"id": "p2-021", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "At 3,800 lb, power off, gear down, flaps 40°, wings level, about what is the stall speed (Vso)? (chart 8-11)", "a": "About 65 MPH CAS", "d": ["About 69 MPH CAS", "About 72 MPH CAS", "About 58 MPH CAS"], "why": "Chart 8-11 (stalling speed vs weight): the flaps-40 line reads about 65 MPH CAS at 3,800 lb. At 4,200 lb it is 69 MPH, the bottom of the white arc. Stall speed drops about 1 MPH per 100 lb.", "src": "POH 8-11 · POH 3-19"},
{"id": "p2-022", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "At 3,800 lb, power off, gear down, flaps up, wings level, what is the stall speed (Vs), and how close is it to Vmc?", "a": "About 72 MPH CAS, 8 MPH below Vmc", "d": ["About 76 MPH CAS, 4 MPH below Vmc", "About 65 MPH CAS, 15 MPH below Vmc", "About 80 MPH CAS, equal to Vmc"], "why": "Chart 8-11: flaps up, about 72 MPH CAS at 3,800 lb (76 MPH at 4,200 lb, the bottom of the green arc). With Vmc at 80 MPH, there is little margin between losing directional control and stalling.", "src": "POH 8-11 · POH 3-2"},
{"id": "p2-023", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What is the AFM stall speed at 4,200 lb with flaps up in a 60° bank?", "a": "108 MPH CAS", "d": ["98 MPH CAS", "95 MPH CAS", "87 MPH CAS"], "why": "Flaps up, the table rises from 76 (0°) to 78 (20°), 87 (40°), 95 (50°) and 108 MPH CAS at 60° bank. 98 MPH is the 60° figure with 40° flaps.", "src": "POH 3-19"},
{"id": "p2-024", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Which engines does the AFM list for the Seneca I, and on which side?", "a": "IO-360-C1E6 on the left; LIO-360-C1E6 on the right", "d": ["LIO-360-C1E6 on the left; IO-360-C1E6 on the right", "IO-360-C1E6 on both sides", "TIO-360-C1E6 on the left; LTIO-360 on the right"], "why": "The left engine is the IO-360-C1E6 and the right is the LIO-360-C1E6, the opposite-rotation version that gives the Seneca counter-rotating propellers. Both are fuel injected.", "src": "POH 3-1"},
{"id": "p2-025", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What are the Seneca I engine limits for all operations?", "a": "2700 RPM and 200 HP", "d": ["2600 RPM and 200 HP", "2700 RPM and 180 HP", "2575 RPM and 220 HP"], "why": "The AFM engine limit for all operations is 2700 RPM, 200 HP. 2700 RPM is the tachometer red line.", "src": "POH 3-1, 3-2"},
{"id": "p2-026", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What is the minimum fuel grade in the AFM limitations?", "a": "100/130 octane aviation gasoline", "d": ["80/87 octane aviation gasoline", "91/96 octane aviation gasoline", "Any premium automotive gasoline"], "why": "The limitation is 100/130 octane aviation gasoline minimum, and the filler cap placard reads '100/130 aviation grade.' The POH lists no lower alternate grade: POH 9-7 warns that lower grades can cause serious engine damage in a short time.", "src": "POH 3-1, 3-5 · POH 9-7"},
{"id": "p2-027", "t": "s-engine", "q": "With the standard (non-damper) Hartzell propellers, what RPM range must be avoided for continuous operation?", "a": "2200 to 2400 RPM", "d": ["2000 to 2200 RPM", "2400 to 2600 RPM", "1800 to 2000 RPM"], "why": "These props carry a tachometer red arc from 2200 to 2400 RPM (avoid continuous operation), with green arcs at 500–2200 and 2400–2700 RPM. The damper-equipped props have no restricted range (green 500–2700 RPM).", "src": "POH 3-1, 3-2"},
{"id": "p2-028", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What are the oil temperature markings?", "a": "Green 75°–245°F; red line 245°F", "d": ["Green 100°–245°F; red line 245°F", "Green 75°–225°F; red line 225°F", "Green 120°–240°F; red line 260°F"], "why": "Oil temperature is normal from 75° to 245°F, with the maximum red line at 245°F. The cowl flap note also says oil temperature must never exceed 245°F.", "src": "POH 3-1"},
{"id": "p2-029", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What are the oil pressure green and yellow arcs?", "a": "Green 60–90 PSI; yellow 25–60 PSI", "d": ["Green 25–60 PSI; yellow 60–90 PSI", "Green 60–90 PSI; yellow 90–100 PSI", "Green 35–90 PSI; yellow 25–35 PSI"], "why": "Green (normal) is 60–90 PSI and yellow (caution) is 25–60 PSI. The maximum red line is 90 PSI; the minimum red line is 25 or 60 PSI depending on the gauge installed.", "src": "POH 3-1"},
{"id": "p2-030", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What are the fuel pressure markings?", "a": "Green 14–35 PSI; red lines at 14 and 35 PSI", "d": ["Green 0.5–8 PSI; red lines at 0.5 and 8 PSI", "Green 25–60 PSI; red lines at 25 and 60 PSI", "Green 14–35 PSI; red line at 35 PSI only"], "why": "Fuel pressure has a green arc of 14 to 35 PSI, with a minimum red line at 14 PSI and a maximum red line at 35 PSI.", "src": "POH 3-2"},
{"id": "p2-031", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What is the maximum (red line) on the fuel flow gauge?", "a": "19.2 GPH", "d": ["14.9 GPH", "20.6 GPH", "16.5 GPH"], "why": "The AFM power plant markings put the fuel flow red line (maximum) at 19.2 GPH.", "src": "POH 3-2"},
{"id": "p2-032", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What are the cylinder head temperature markings?", "a": "Green 200°–475°F; red line 475°F", "d": ["Green 200°–500°F; red line 500°F", "Green 150°–435°F; red line 435°F", "Green 75°–245°F; red line 245°F"], "why": "CHT is normal from 200° to 475°F with a 475°F red line. 75°–245°F is the oil temperature arc.", "src": "POH 3-2"},
{"id": "p2-033", "t": "s-engine", "q": "When does the AFM say the cowl flaps should be open?", "a": "During ground operations and in climbs", "d": ["Only during takeoff and go-around", "During descents and landing", "Only when CHT nears the red line"], "why": "Cowl flaps give manual control of engine temperature. In no case may CHT exceed 475°F or oil temperature 245°F.", "src": "POH 3-7"},
{"id": "p2-034", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Above what engine RPM must the propeller be turning for it to feather?", "a": "800 RPM", "d": ["1,000 RPM", "500 RPM", "1,500 RPM"], "why": "Below about 800 RPM, loss of centrifugal force lets a stop pin engage. The pin keeps the prop from feathering every time the engine is shut down on the ground.", "src": "POH 3-11"},
{"id": "p2-035", "t": "s-engine", "q": "An engine fails and you delay. Before you move the prop control, the engine slows below 800 RPM. What happens?", "a": "Stop pins engage and the prop will not feather", "d": ["The prop feathers normally, just more slowly", "The prop feathers once the mixture is cut off", "The prop feathers when the throttle is closed"], "why": "The props feather only while turning above 800 RPM. If RPM drops below that, the centrifugal stop pins engage and hold the blades out of feather, leaving a draggy unfeathered prop.", "src": "POH 3-11"},
{"id": "p2-036", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What does the AFM give as the cause of a propeller overspeed?", "a": "Loss of the air charge in the prop dome", "d": ["A broken governor control cable", "Cold oil during a winter climb", "Feathering stop pins that fail to engage"], "why": "Losing the dome air charge can let the prop overspeed if the throttle is advanced rapidly or airspeed is abruptly increased. The AFM adds that an overspeeding prop will not feather, so use the overspeed procedure instead.", "src": "POH 3-18"},
{"id": "p2-037", "t": "s-engine", "q": "After recovering from a propeller overspeed, what can cause it to happen again?", "a": "Throttle below 15–20 in. MP above 105 MPH, then adding power", "d": ["Any RPM above 2400 with the prop control fully forward", "Climbing above 10,000 ft at full throttle and 2700 RPM", "Throttle below 15–20 in. MP below 105 MPH, then adding power"], "why": "The AFM warns that retarding the throttle below 15–20 inches MP at speeds above 105 MPH may let the prop overspeed again when power is reapplied. Use the same procedure to regain control.", "src": "POH 3-18"},
{"id": "p2-038", "t": "s-wb", "q": "What are the Seneca I maximum takeoff and maximum landing weights?", "a": "4,200 lb takeoff; 4,000 lb landing", "d": ["4,000 lb takeoff; 4,000 lb landing", "4,200 lb takeoff; 4,200 lb landing", "4,570 lb takeoff; 4,342 lb landing"], "why": "The AFM and the weight placard give 4,200 lb maximum takeoff and 4,000 lb maximum landing weight.", "src": "POH 3-3, 3-4 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-039", "t": "s-wb", "q": "The AFM lists no separate zero-fuel weight. What does the weight placard effectively limit the non-fuel weight to?", "a": "4,000 lb", "d": ["4,200 lb", "3,800 lb", "3,400 lb"], "why": "The weight placard says 'All weight in excess of 4000 pounds must consist of fuel.' So the airplane, occupants and baggage without fuel cannot exceed 4,000 lb, which acts like a maximum zero-fuel weight.", "src": "POH 3-4 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-040", "t": "s-wb", "q": "What is the Seneca I aft CG limit?", "a": "94.6 in aft of datum at all weights", "d": ["87.9 in aft of datum at all weights", "94.6 in at 4,200 lb, less when lighter", "96.6 in aft of datum at all weights"], "why": "The aft limit is 94.6 inches at 2,780, 3,400 and 4,200 lb. Only the forward limit changes with weight.", "src": "POH 3-3"},
{"id": "p2-041", "t": "s-wb", "q": "What is the forward CG limit at the 4,200 lb maximum weight?", "a": "87.9 in aft of datum", "d": ["80.7 in aft of datum", "82.0 in aft of datum", "94.6 in aft of datum"], "why": "The forward limit is 80.7 in at 2,780 lb, 82.0 in at 3,400 lb and 87.9 in at 4,200 lb, varying in a straight line between points.", "src": "POH 3-3"},
{"id": "p2-042", "t": "s-wb", "q": "Your loaded weight is 3,800 lb. Using straight-line variation, what is the approximate forward CG limit?", "a": "About 85.0 in aft of datum", "d": ["82.0 in aft of datum", "87.9 in aft of datum", "About 83.5 in aft of datum"], "why": "3,800 lb is halfway between 3,400 lb (82.0 in) and 4,200 lb (87.9 in), so the limit is about 82.0 + 5.9/2 = 84.95 in. The AFM says to vary limits in a straight line between points.", "src": "POH 3-3"},
{"id": "p2-043", "t": "s-wb", "q": "Where is the Seneca I datum?", "a": "78.4 in forward of the wing leading edge, at the inboard tank edge", "d": ["100 in forward of the wing leading edge, measured at the wing root", "78.4 in forward of the firewall face, on the fuselage centerline", "78.4 in aft of the propeller spinner tip, along the thrust line"], "why": "The AFM places the datum 78.4 inches forward of the wing leading edge, measured from the inboard edge of the inboard fuel tank.", "src": "POH 3-3 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-044", "t": "s-wb", "q": "What is the Seneca I usable fuel capacity?", "a": "46.5 gal per side, 93 gal total", "d": ["49 gal per side, 98 gal total", "46.5 gal total in four tanks", "45 gal per side, 90 gal total"], "why": "Usable fuel is 46.5 gallons per side (two interconnected tanks each), 93 gallons total; the filler placard reads 'usable capacity 46.5 gal.' Unusable fuel is 2.5 gallons per side, so 98 gallons is the total including unusable.", "src": "POH 3-3, 3-5"},
{"id": "p2-045", "t": "s-wb", "q": "What are the Seneca I baggage compartment limits?", "a": "100 lb forward and 100 lb aft", "d": ["100 lb forward and 200 lb aft", "200 lb forward and 100 lb aft", "150 lb forward and 150 lb aft"], "why": "Both the nose compartment door placard and the aft baggage closeout placard say 100 lb maximum. The aft placard adds 'No heavy objects on hat shelf.'", "src": "POH 3-4 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-046", "t": "s-normal", "q": "What flap settings does the AFM give for normal takeoff and landing?", "a": "Takeoff 0°; landing 40°", "d": ["Takeoff 10°; landing 40°", "Takeoff 25°; landing 25°", "Takeoff 0°; landing 25°"], "why": "Section II lists takeoff 0° and landing 40°. 25° is the AFM's recommendation for a single-engine landing, not a normal one.", "src": "POH 3-7 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-047", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "How are the Seneca I flaps operated, and what does each handle notch give?", "a": "Manually; 10°, 25° and 40°", "d": ["Electrically; 10°, 25° and 40°", "Manually; 15°, 25° and 40°", "Hydraulically; 10°, 20° and 40°"], "why": "The flaps are manually operated: first notch 10°, second 25°, third 40°.", "src": "POH 3-7"},
{"id": "p2-048", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Per your school's go-around checklist, what comes right after throttles full forward and pitching up 10–12°?", "a": "Flaps retract to 25°", "d": ["Landing gear up", "Flaps fully retracted", "Cowl flaps closed"], "why": "School go-around: throttles full forward, pitch up 10–12°, flaps to 25°, landing gear up once the VSI shows a positive rate of climb, the remaining flaps below 125 MPH, then adjust the cowl flaps. (The printed AFM, POH 3-7, lists positive climb, flaps, then gear.)", "src": "School checklist · POH 3-7"},
{"id": "p2-049", "t": "s-normal", "q": "What does the AFM preflight check of the alternator system confirm?", "a": "OV lights work; ammeters about equal at 1500+ RPM", "d": ["Each ammeter reads 60 A with all electrical loads on", "OV lights stay out with both alternator switches off", "Battery voltage rises as each alternator switch cycles"], "why": "The check confirms the overvoltage lights operate and both ammeters show roughly equal outputs with both engines at 1500 RPM or more. Both alternator switches stay ON for normal operation.", "src": "POH 3-8"},
{"id": "p2-050", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Except during engine cranking, what is the maximum output for each alternator?", "a": "60 amperes", "d": ["50 amperes", "70 amperes", "40 amperes"], "why": "Normal output should not exceed 60 A per alternator except while cranking. The emergency procedures limit the load to 50 A when relying on one alternator.", "src": "POH 3-8"},
{"id": "p2-051", "t": "s-normal", "q": "What must you do to use the press-to-test feature of an overvoltage trip light?", "a": "Turn that alternator switch OFF", "d": ["Turn the master switch OFF", "Pull that alternator's circuit breaker", "Reduce that engine to idle RPM"], "why": "The AFM says the alternator switch must be OFF to use the press-to-test feature. The school run-up does this one alternator at a time.", "src": "POH 3-7"},
{"id": "p2-052", "t": "s-normal", "q": "What is the normal fuel system setup for takeoff and landing?", "a": "Selectors ON; electric fuel pumps ON", "d": ["Selectors ON; electric fuel pumps OFF", "Selectors X-FEED; electric fuel pumps ON", "Selectors ON; one electric pump ON"], "why": "For takeoff and landing both selectors are ON and both electric pumps ON. In cruise the selectors stay ON and the pumps go OFF.", "src": "POH 3-8"},
{"id": "p2-053", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Per the AFM, what can a fast taxi turn just before the takeoff run cause if the electric boost pumps are not ON?", "a": "Temporary malfunction of one engine during takeoff", "d": ["Vapor lock in both engines during the climb", "Overheating of the electric fuel pump motors", "Fuel venting overboard through the tank vents"], "why": "The AFM 'Turning takeoffs' note warns that fast taxi turns right before the takeoff run can briefly upset one engine's fuel supply on takeoff unless the boost pumps are ON. That is one reason the pumps are ON for takeoff and landing.", "src": "POH 3-9"},
{"id": "p2-054", "t": "s-normal", "q": "How are the Seneca I fuel tanks treated for tank selection?", "a": "Each side's two interconnected tanks count as one", "d": ["Each of the four tanks is selected separately", "Inboard tanks feed first, then outboard tanks", "All four tanks feed one common manifold"], "why": "Each engine normally draws from the two interconnected tanks on its own side, and the AFM treats them as a single tank for selection.", "src": "POH 3-8"},
{"id": "p2-055", "t": "s-normal", "q": "With both engines running in cruise, may both engines be fed from the same tank?", "a": "Yes, it is permissible in cruising flight", "d": ["No, crossfeed is for single-engine use only", "Yes, but only for takeoff and landing", "Only with both electric pumps ON"], "why": "The AFM says it is permissible to operate both engines from the same tank in cruising flight. Crossfeed is used in level flight only.", "src": "POH 3-9"},
{"id": "p2-056", "t": "s-normal", "q": "When does the gear warning horn sound, per the AFM?", "a": "At low throttle settings with the gear up", "d": ["Below 125 MPH with the gear up", "When flaps pass 25° with the gear up", "Whenever airspeed is below 105 MPH"], "why": "The horn sounds at low throttle settings with the gear up. On serial numbers 34-7250046 and up, which includes your Seneca (s/n 34-7350315), the red gear light also comes on whenever the horn sounds.", "src": "POH 3-9"},
{"id": "p2-057", "t": "s-normal", "q": "What maximum altitude loss does the AFM give for a power-off stall?", "a": "Up to 450 ft, clean or with gear down and 40° flaps", "d": ["Up to 550 ft clean; 400 ft with gear and flaps down", "Up to 250 ft in either configuration", "Up to 500 ft clean; 300 ft with gear and flaps down"], "why": "The AFM says a power-off stall may lose up to 450 ft with gear and flaps up and also up to 450 ft with gear down and 40° flaps.", "src": "POH 3-19"},
{"id": "p2-058", "t": "s-normal", "q": "What maximum altitude loss does the AFM give for a power-on stall, clean versus gear down and 40° flaps?", "a": "Up to 550 ft clean; up to 400 ft with gear and flaps down", "d": ["Up to 450 ft clean; up to 550 ft with gear and flaps down", "Up to 400 ft clean; up to 550 ft with gear and flaps down", "Up to 550 ft in either configuration"], "why": "Power-on stalls may lose up to 550 ft with gear and flaps up and up to 400 ft with gear down and 40° flaps.", "src": "POH 3-19"},
{"id": "p2-059", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "When is the Seneca I stall warning system inoperative?", "a": "With the master switch off", "d": ["With the flaps beyond 25°", "With the pitot heat off", "With the gear down and locked"], "why": "The stall warning is electric, and the AFM states it is inoperative with the master switch off.", "src": "POH 3-19"},
{"id": "p2-072", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "With coordinated controls, how does the AFM say you detect a dead engine?", "a": "Loss of thrust; nose yaws toward the dead engine", "d": ["Loss of thrust; nose yaws toward the good engine", "RPM drop; nose pitches down sharply", "Loss of thrust; roll away from the dead engine"], "why": "The AFM lists two cues: loss of thrust, and the nose yawing toward the dead engine. The good engine's thrust swings the nose toward the failed side.", "src": "POH 3-11"},
{"id": "p2-073", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Per your school's engine-failure memory items, the first step is 'maintain direction and airspeed' at what speed?", "a": "105 MPH (blue line)", "d": ["80 MPH (red line)", "120 MPH (en route climb)", "76 MPH (bottom of green arc)"], "why": "School memory items: maintain direction and airspeed, blue line 105 MPH (Vyse); then mixtures, props and throttles forward, flaps up, gear up, fuel pumps on, identify and verify. (The printed AFM, POH 3-11, says to maintain airspeed above 90 MPH.)", "src": "School checklist · POH 3-11"},
{"id": "p2-074", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Per the AFM feathering procedure, after mixtures, props and throttles forward, what are the next three steps?", "a": "Flaps retract, gear retract, electric fuel pumps ON", "d": ["Identify, verify, then feather the inoperative engine", "Gear retract, flaps retract, cowl flaps open on both", "Electric fuel pumps ON, identify, then gear retract"], "why": "The AFM order is mixtures, props, throttles forward; flaps up; gear up; electric pumps ON; then identify, retard the throttle to verify, and feather.", "src": "POH 3-11 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-075", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Why does the AFM have you retard the throttle of the suspected inoperative engine before feathering?", "a": "To verify it is the failed engine before feathering", "d": ["To cut drag from the windmilling prop before feathering", "To keep the failed engine from overspeeding its prop", "To slow the prop below 800 RPM so it can feather"], "why": "'Throttle of inoperative engine - retard to verify.' Feathering the wrong (good) engine would leave you with no power, so confirm first.", "src": "POH 3-11"},
{"id": "p2-076", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "You retard the throttle of the engine you think failed, and yaw and power noticeably change. What does that tell you?", "a": "Wrong engine; re-advance it and re-identify", "d": ["Engine verified dead; feather it", "Governor failed; pull the prop to low RPM", "Engine flooded; pull its mixture to cutoff"], "why": "Pulling back the dead engine's throttle changes nothing. A change in yaw or power means that engine was producing thrust, so restore it and identify again before feathering.", "src": "POH 3-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "p2-077", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Immediately after feathering the propeller, what is the next AFM step?", "a": "Mixture of the inoperative engine to idle cutoff", "d": ["Magnetos of the inoperative engine OFF", "Fuel selector of the inoperative engine OFF", "Electric fuel pump of the inoperative engine OFF"], "why": "After 'Propeller - feather' comes 'Mixture - idle cut off,' then trim, 5° bank, inoperative-engine pump OFF, magnetos OFF, cowl flaps, alternator OFF, electrical load, fuel OFF.", "src": "POH 3-11"},
{"id": "p2-078", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "After feathering, how much bank does the AFM say to hold, and which way?", "a": "5° toward the operating engine", "d": ["5° toward the inoperative engine", "10° toward the operating engine", "Wings level with the ball centered"], "why": "Banking toward the good engine lets the horizontal component of lift help the rudder counter the yaw, reducing sideslip and drag.", "src": "POH 3-11 · FAA-H-8083-3"},
{"id": "p2-079", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "If circumstances permit, the AFM suggests trying to restore power before feathering. Which set matches its suggestions?", "a": "Mixture as required, boost pump on, crossfeed, L or R mag, alt air on", "d": ["Mixture lean, boost pump off, selector off, both mags, alt air off", "Mixture rich, boost pump off, selector on, both mags, carb heat on", "Mixture cutoff, boost pump on, crossfeed, mags off, alt air on"], "why": "The AFM note lists: mixture as required, fuel boost pump on, fuel selector crossfeed, magnetos L or R only, alternate air on.", "src": "POH 3-11 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-080", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Why does the AFM direct feathering the propeller of a failed engine?", "a": "Single-engine performance decreases if it is not feathered", "d": ["A windmilling prop will overspeed the propeller governor", "Feathering is required before any in-flight restart", "It keeps the stop pins from engaging as the engine slows"], "why": "A windmilling prop creates large drag. The AFM states single-engine performance will decrease if the inoperative prop is not feathered.", "src": "POH 3-11"},
{"id": "p2-081", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Per your school's air-start checklist, what is the first step to unfeather and restart an engine?", "a": "Fuel selector of the inoperative engine ON", "d": ["Starter engaged until the prop windmills", "Mixture of the inoperative engine rich", "Propeller control full forward"], "why": "School air start: fuel selector (inop engine) ON, its fuel pump OFF, throttle open 1/4 inch, prop control full forward, mixture rich, magnetos ON, starter until the prop windmills, throttle reduced until the engine is warm, zero thrust set, alternator ON, cowl flaps open when warm.", "src": "School checklist · POH 3-12"},
{"id": "p2-082", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "In the Seneca I AFM unfeathering procedure, what gets the feathered propeller turning?", "a": "Engaging the starter until the prop windmills", "d": ["An unfeathering accumulator, as the prop moves forward", "Diving until the prop windmills above 800 RPM", "Turning the electric fuel pump on for 3 seconds"], "why": "The AFM procedure uses the starter: 'Starter - engage till prop windmills.' It has no accumulator step.", "src": "POH 3-12 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-083", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Per your school's air-start checklist, where does the propeller control go when unfeathering?", "a": "Full forward (high RPM)", "d": ["In the feather detent until the engine fires", "Full aft (low RPM) until the engine is warm", "Left where it was before the engine failed"], "why": "The school air-start checklist sets the propeller control full forward, then engages the starter until the prop windmills. (The printed AFM, POH 3-12, says forward to the cruise RPM position.)", "src": "School checklist · POH 3-12"},
{"id": "p2-084", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Single-engine cruise: you want fuel from the tank on the dead engine's side. How are the selectors set?", "a": "Operating engine X-FEED; inoperative engine OFF", "d": ["Operating engine ON; inoperative engine X-FEED", "Both selectors X-FEED", "Operating engine X-FEED; inoperative engine ON"], "why": "The operating engine's selector goes to X-FEED and the dead engine's to OFF; electric pumps stay OFF unless the operating engine's driven pump fails. Crossfeed exists to increase single-engine range and is used in level flight only.", "src": "POH 3-12 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-085", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "For a single-engine landing, how does the AFM set up the fuel system?", "a": "Operating ON, inop OFF, operating electric pump ON", "d": ["Operating X-FEED, inop OFF, both electric pumps OFF", "Both selectors ON, both electric pumps ON for landing", "Operating ON, inop OFF, all electric pumps OFF"], "why": "For landing: operating engine selector ON, inoperative engine OFF, and the operating engine's electric pump ON.", "src": "POH 3-12 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-086", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "An engine fails during the takeoff roll at 85 MPH CAS. What does the AFM say to do?", "a": "Close both throttles immediately; stop straight ahead", "d": ["Continue the takeoff and feather once safely airborne", "Close only the failed engine's throttle and stop", "Rotate early at 90 MPH and climb out at 105 MPH"], "why": "If the failure happens on the ground roll before 100 MPH CAS, the AFM says close both throttles immediately and stop straight ahead.", "src": "POH 3-12a · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-087", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "After an abort below 100 MPH, the remaining runway is inadequate to stop. What does the AFM add?", "a": "Max braking, master OFF, fuel selectors OFF", "d": ["Gear up, feather both props, flaps up", "Max braking, mixtures rich, props forward", "Flaps up, master ON, electric pumps ON"], "why": "With throttles closed: apply maximum braking, master switch OFF, fuel selectors OFF, and continue straight ahead, turning to avoid obstacles as needed.", "src": "POH 3-12a · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-088", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "An engine fails just after liftoff, gear still down, at 102 MPH CAS, with plenty of runway left. What does the AFM say?", "a": "Close both throttles, land, and stop straight ahead", "d": ["Retract the gear and continue the climb at 105 MPH", "Feather the dead engine and climb straight ahead", "Lower the nose to 105 MPH and circle back to land"], "why": "At or above 100 MPH with gear down, if adequate runway remains: close both throttles immediately, land if airborne, and stop straight ahead.", "src": "POH 3-12a · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-089", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "You decide to continue after an engine failure on takeoff. What order does the AFM give?", "a": "Hold heading and airspeed, gear up once climbing, feather", "d": ["Feather, then gear up, then hold heading and airspeed", "Gear up immediately, feather, then establish the climb", "Hold heading, feather, then raise the gear at 500 ft AGL"], "why": "If continuing: maintain heading and airspeed, retract the gear when a climb is established, then feather. The AFM bases the continue/abort decision on loading, density altitude, obstructions, weather and pilot competence.", "src": "POH 3-12a · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-090", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "An engine fails in the climb at 78 MPH CAS. What does the AFM say to do first?", "a": "Reduce power on the good engine to keep directional control", "d": ["Full power on both engines and feather the dead engine", "Raise the nose to hold altitude, then feather the dead engine", "Feather the dead engine first, then lower the nose"], "why": "Below the 80 MPH Vmc, full power on the good engine can yaw the airplane beyond rudder authority. Reduce that power as needed to keep directional control.", "src": "POH 3-13 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-091", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Below Vmc after an engine failure, you have reduced power on the good engine. What does the AFM say next?", "a": "Lower the nose toward 105 MPH, then feather", "d": ["Feather first, then lower the nose to hold 90 MPH", "Hold the attitude, add rudder trim, then feather", "Lower the nose to 80 MPH and hold it there"], "why": "The AFM: reduce nose attitude to accelerate toward the 105 MPH single-engine best rate-of-climb speed, then feather the inoperative engine.", "src": "POH 3-13"},
{"id": "p2-092", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "An engine fails in the climb at 95 MPH CAS. What are the AFM steps?", "a": "Maintain control, adjust toward 105 MPH, feather", "d": ["Reduce good-engine power, lower nose, feather", "Maintain control, slow to 90 MPH, feather", "Feather, then accelerate to 120 MPH"], "why": "Above 80 MPH: maintain directional control, adjust airspeed toward 105 MPH (Vyse), and feather the inoperative engine.", "src": "POH 3-13 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-093", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Per the AFM (POH 3-13), when should the landing gear and wing flaps be extended for a single-engine landing?", "a": "Not until certain of making the field", "d": ["At the final approach fix, as usual", "Gear on downwind, flaps on base", "Gear at 1,000 ft AGL, flaps at 500 ft AGL"], "why": "The AFM says do not extend the landing gear or lower the flaps until certain of making the field. Their drag on one engine can make a go-around impossible.", "src": "POH 3-13 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-094", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "What final approach speed and flap setting does the AFM recommend for a single-engine landing?", "a": "105 MPH with 25° flaps", "d": ["90 MPH with 40° flaps", "105 MPH with 40° flaps", "87 MPH with 25° flaps"], "why": "A final approach at 105 MPH using 25° rather than full flaps puts the airplane in the best configuration for a go-around, should one be needed.", "src": "POH 3-13 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-095", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Why does the AFM recommend 25° rather than full flaps for a single-engine landing?", "a": "It leaves the airplane best set up for a go-around", "d": ["40° flaps exceed the single-engine flap speed limit", "Full flaps blank the rudder at low airspeeds", "The stall speed is lower with 25° than with 40°"], "why": "Less flap means less drag if power must be added. Even so, the AFM says a single-engine go-around should be avoided if at all possible and may be impossible under some loading and density altitude conditions.", "src": "POH 3-13"},
{"id": "p2-096", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Per the AFM single-engine go-around, after mixture, prop and throttle forward, what comes next?", "a": "Flaps retract, then landing gear retract", "d": ["Landing gear retract, then flaps retract", "Cowl flaps open, then landing gear retract", "Trim set, then flaps retract to 25°"], "why": "AFM sequence: mixture, prop, throttle forward; flaps up; gear up; airspeed 105 MPH (Vyse); trim set; cowl flap as required on the operating engine.", "src": "POH 3-13 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-097", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Before a daytime manual gear extension, the AFM has you turn the navigation lights OFF. Why?", "a": "Gear lights dim with nav lights on, hard to see by day", "d": ["The nav lights share a circuit with the hydraulic gear pump", "It frees electrical power for the emergency gear extension", "The gear warning horn is disabled with the nav lights on"], "why": "The AFM notes the gear indicator lights are dimmed while the navigation lights are on, so in daylight you might not see three greens. The other pre-checks are circuit breakers, master ON and alternators.", "src": "POH 3-9, 3-14"},
{"id": "p2-098", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "What is the maximum airspeed for manual (emergency) gear extension?", "a": "100 MPH", "d": ["125 MPH", "150 MPH", "105 MPH"], "why": "The AFM says reduce power with airspeed not to exceed 100 MPH, then put the selector DOWN, pull the emergency knob and check three greens.", "src": "POH 3-14 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-099", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "After an emergency gear extension due to a malfunction, how long should the knob stay pulled out?", "a": "Until the airplane is on jacks and the gear systems are checked", "d": ["Until the landing rollout is complete and the airplane is parked", "Until the red unsafe light goes out after touchdown", "Until the next 100-hour inspection is completed"], "why": "The AFM warning says leave the control out until the airplane is on jacks to check the gear's hydraulic and electrical systems. The placard says 'See A.F.M. before re-engagement.'", "src": "POH 3-14, 3-4 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-100", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "When does the red landing gear unsafe light illuminate on your Seneca (s/n 34-7350315)?", "a": "Gear in transit, or whenever the gear horn sounds", "d": ["Only when the gear is up and locked", "Only when a gear leg fails to lock down", "Gear in transit, or whenever the stall horn sounds"], "why": "The light shows the gear in transition. On serial numbers 34-7250046 and up, which includes 34-7350315, it also lights whenever the gear warning horn sounds. It is off when the gear is fully up, or down and locked.", "src": "POH 3-9, 3-14 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-101", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "After a normal gear extension the red unsafe light stays on. What does the AFM say to do?", "a": "Recycle the landing gear", "d": ["Pull the emergency gear knob immediately", "Land; it is only an indication fault", "Pull the gear pump circuit breaker"], "why": "The red light means the gear is between fully up and down-and-locked. The AFM says to recycle the gear if the light stays on.", "src": "POH 3-14 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-102", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Why does the AFM say to leave the flaps up for a gear-up landing?", "a": "To reduce wing and flap damage", "d": ["To lower the touchdown speed", "To keep the stall warning working", "To keep the CG within limits"], "why": "Gear-up landing: approach with power at normal speed, flaps up to limit damage, close throttles just before touchdown, master and ignition OFF, fuel selectors OFF, touch down at minimum speed.", "src": "POH 3-14 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-103", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Per the AFM, both overvoltage lights come on. You turn off all loads except the master, and both alternator switches OFF. What next?", "a": "Turn each alternator on briefly, one at a time, watching ammeters", "d": ["Turn both alternators on together and compare the two ammeters", "Leave both alternators off and land on battery power alone", "Cycle the master switch off, then back on after 10 seconds"], "why": "The AFM has you briefly switch each alternator ON, one at a time, to compare outputs, then keep the one showing the least output.", "src": "POH 3-14 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-104", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Per the AFM both-overvoltage-lights procedure, which alternator do you turn back ON?", "a": "The one showing the LEAST output", "d": ["The one showing the MOST output", "Either one; they are identical", "The one on the critical engine"], "why": "A failed regulator drives its alternator's output up uncontrollably, so the lower-output alternator is the healthy one. Then keep the load at or below 50 A.", "src": "POH 3-14, 3-7 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-105", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "After an overvoltage trip or loss of one alternator, what electrical load limit does the AFM give?", "a": "Do not exceed 50 amperes", "d": ["Do not exceed 60 amperes", "Do not exceed 30 amperes", "Do not exceed 70 amperes"], "why": "The electrical failure procedures repeatedly limit output to 50 A. 60 A is the normal maximum per alternator.", "src": "POH 3-15"},
{"id": "p2-106", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Per your school checklist, one overvoltage light comes on. After loads off and that alternator OFF, what next?", "a": "Alternator ON; if more than 50 amps, OFF; load 50 A max", "d": ["Reset its circuit breaker, then leave it ON at any load", "Turn the other alternator OFF and keep this one ON", "Leave it OFF and the master OFF for the rest of the flight"], "why": "School checklist (Overvoltage Light — One): all electrical load off, master on, that alternator off, then on — if it shows more than 50 amps, off — and keep electrical equipment to no more than 50 amps. (The printed AFM, POH 3-15, switches it on only momentarily to confirm excessive output, then leaves it off.)", "src": "School checklist · POH 3-15"},
{"id": "p2-107", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "One alternator loses output (no overvoltage light). What does the AFM say to do?", "a": "Load 50 A max, check breakers, cycle that alternator OFF then ON", "d": ["Load 60 A max, check breakers, cycle the master OFF then ON", "Turn off the good alternator, then reset both overvoltage relays", "Shed all electrical loads and land immediately"], "why": "If cycling the switch does not restore output, keep the load at or below 50 A to continue, and get maintenance before further flight.", "src": "POH 3-15 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-108", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "What warning does the AFM give when both alternators are inoperative?", "a": "Compass error may exceed 10°", "d": ["Fuel gauges will read inaccurately", "Vacuum instruments will be unreliable", "Gear position lights will not work"], "why": "The AFM warns that compass error may exceed 10° with both alternators inoperative.", "src": "POH 3-15"},
{"id": "p2-109", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "What does the red button annunciator in the vacuum system indicate?", "a": "A feathered engine or failed vacuum pump", "d": ["Vacuum above the 5.2 in. Hg upper limit", "A clogged gyro instrument air filter", "Low oil pressure to that engine's prop governor"], "why": "Each engine drives a vacuum pump. The red button shows when one pump is not supplying vacuum, whether because it failed or its engine is feathered.", "src": "POH 3-16"},
{"id": "p2-110", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Vacuum reads below 4.5 in. Hg. What does the AFM say to do?", "a": "Increase RPM to 2700; descend if needed to hold 4.5", "d": ["Reduce RPM to 2200 and climb to cooler, denser air", "Shut down the engine with the failed vacuum pump", "Turn on the standby electric vacuum pump and continue"], "why": "Raise RPM to 2700, descend if possible to where 4.5 in. Hg can be held, and use the electric turn indicator to monitor the attitude and heading indicators.", "src": "POH 3-16"},
{"id": "p2-111", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Engine fire in flight. What is the first AFM step for the affected engine?", "a": "Fuel selector OFF", "d": ["Throttle CLOSE", "Mixture IDLE CUT OFF", "Propeller FEATHER"], "why": "AFM order for the affected engine: fuel selector OFF, throttle close, propeller feather, mixture idle cutoff; then heater OFF and defroster OFF (in all cases of fire), and land immediately if terrain permits. Your school checklist adds cowl flaps OPEN after the defroster.", "src": "POH 3-16 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-112", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Engine fire in flight: after fuel selector OFF and throttle closed, what comes next?", "a": "Propeller FEATHER, then mixture IDLE CUT OFF", "d": ["Mixture IDLE CUT OFF, then propeller FEATHER", "Magnetos OFF, then cowl flaps OPEN", "Propeller FEATHER, then master switch OFF"], "why": "The AFM feathers the prop before the mixture goes to idle cutoff, then turns off the heater and defroster and lands immediately if terrain permits.", "src": "POH 3-16 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-113", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "An engine catches fire during start, before it is running. What does the AFM say to do?", "a": "Mixture idle cutoff, throttle open, keep cranking", "d": ["Stop cranking, fuel selector OFF, evacuate", "Mixture rich, throttle closed, keep cranking", "Master OFF, magnetos OFF, evacuate"], "why": "Cranking with the mixture at idle cutoff and the throttle open tries to pull the fire into the engine. If it persists more than a few seconds, use external extinguishing with fuel selectors OFF.", "src": "POH 3-16 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-114", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "A fire starts during engine start, but the engine has started and is running. What does the AFM say?", "a": "Keep it running to pull the fire into the engine", "d": ["Shut it down at once with the mixture cutoff", "Reduce to idle and turn the electric fuel pump off", "Shut it down, then restart it to clear the fire"], "why": "Keep the engine running to try to draw the fire in. If the fire lasts more than a few seconds, put it out by the best available external means, with fuel selectors OFF and mixture at idle cutoff.", "src": "POH 3-16 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-115", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "In an unintentional spin, what is the AFM's first recovery step?", "a": "Retard both throttles to idle", "d": ["Full rudder opposite the rotation", "Control wheel full forward", "Ailerons opposite the rotation"], "why": "AFM order: throttles idle, full opposite rudder, release back pressure (push full forward if the nose doesn't drop), ailerons neutral, hold until rotation stops, neutralize rudder, smooth pull-out.", "src": "POH 3-17 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-116", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Why does the AFM warn against abrupt control movement in the dive after spin recovery?", "a": "The positive limit load factor may be exceeded", "d": ["It can overspeed both propellers in the dive", "It can unport the fuel pickups in the tanks", "It can restart the rotation in the opposite direction"], "why": "Pull out of the dive with smooth back pressure; an abrupt pull can exceed the 3.8 G positive limit maneuvering load factor.", "src": "POH 3-17"},
{"id": "p2-117", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "An engine fails in icing conditions. What does the AFM say to try first?", "a": "Select alternate air and attempt a restart", "d": ["Feather immediately and descend out of the ice", "Close the cowl flaps to warm it, then attempt a restart", "Increase RPM to 2700 to shed ice from the prop"], "why": "Induction icing may be the cause, so select ALTERNATE AIR and try to restart. If it won't restart, feather and follow the icing engine-out steps.", "src": "POH 3-17 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-118", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "The engine won't restart after a failure in icing. What minimum airspeed does the AFM specify?", "a": "105 MPH CAS, including on final", "d": ["90 MPH CAS, then 80 MPH on final", "105 MPH CAS, then 90 MPH on final", "120 MPH CAS, then 105 MPH on final"], "why": "Maintain at least 105 MPH CAS, descending if needed to hold it; reduce electrical loads, avoid further icing, land as soon as practical, keep 105 MPH on final, and use 25° rather than full flaps. Gear and flaps wait until certain of making the field.", "src": "POH 3-17"},
{"id": "p2-120", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "What is the first step if a propeller overspeeds?", "a": "Close the throttle", "d": ["Pull the prop control to feather", "Pull the mixture to idle cutoff", "Raise the nose to slow to Vx"], "why": "Overspeed procedure: close throttle, slow to best rate-of-climb speed, prop control back to low RPM, slowly add throttle until the governor engages, then set power. Continue at reduced speed and power and land as soon as practical.", "src": "POH 3-18 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-121", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Propeller overspeed: after closing the throttle and slowing to best rate-of-climb speed, what is next?", "a": "Pull the prop control back to low RPM", "d": ["Move the prop control to feather", "Advance the throttle to re-engage the governor", "Shut down the engine with mixture cutoff"], "why": "With the prop control at low RPM, slowly increase throttle until the governor takes hold, then slowly bring prop and throttle up to the desired setting.", "src": "POH 3-18 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-124", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "Per the FAA-approved AFM limitations, what is the maximum speed for extending even the first notch (10°) of flaps?", "a": "125 MPH, the single Vfe for every setting", "d": ["160 MPH, from the staged flap table", "140 MPH, the second-notch figure", "150 MPH, the gear-extended limit"], "why": "The AFM limitations give one maximum flaps-extended speed, 125 MPH CAS, which is also the top of the white arc and the placarded figure. POH 6-10 prints higher staged speeds for 10° and 25°, but that is not the approved limitation; POH 7-1 also says flaps may be lowered up to 125 MPH.", "src": "POH 3-2, 3-5"},
{"id": "p2-125", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What is the placarded maximum speed for opening the storm window?", "a": "150 MPH", "d": ["125 MPH", "133 MPH", "105 MPH"], "why": "The storm window placard reads 'Do not open above 150 MPH.' 150 MPH is also the gear-extended limit and the doors-removed maximum speed.", "src": "POH 3-6"},
{"id": "p2-126", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Per the instrument panel placard, when must the oil cooler winterization plate be removed?", "a": "When ambient temperature exceeds 50°F", "d": ["When ambient temperature exceeds 32°F", "When oil temperature exceeds 245°F", "When ambient temperature exceeds 70°F"], "why": "The placard says to remove the winterization plate when the ambient temperature exceeds 50°F. Left on in warm weather, it restricts oil cooling.", "src": "POH 3-6"},
{"id": "p2-127", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Where are the Seneca I circuit breakers, and how does the AFM say to reset one?", "a": "Lower right of the panel; push in the reset button", "d": ["Lower left of the panel; pull out, then push in", "Overhead panel; toggle the breaker off, then on", "Lower right of the panel; cycle the master switch"], "why": "All circuit breakers are grouped in the lower right corner of the instrument panel and are reset by pushing in the button. The Operating Tips (POH 7-1) add a two to five minute cooling period before resetting.", "src": "POH 3-8 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-128", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Single-engine cruise with the electric pumps OFF: when does the AFM say the operating engine's electric fuel pump must be used?", "a": "If its engine-driven fuel pump fails", "d": ["Whenever crossfeed is selected", "Whenever the dead engine is feathered", "Whenever above 10,000 ft density altitude"], "why": "In single-engine cruise the electric pumps stay OFF, from the same side or on crossfeed, except that an engine-driven pump failure requires the electric pump on the operating engine's side. It goes back ON for the landing.", "src": "POH 3-8, 3-12 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p2-129", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Per the AFM, how does removing the rear cabin and cargo doors affect climb and cruise performance?", "a": "Both are reduced by about 5 percent", "d": ["Both are reduced by about 15 percent", "Climb drops 5 percent; cruise is unchanged", "No change; only Vmc rises to 81 MPH"], "why": "All climb and cruise performance drops about five percent with the doors off. The doors-off limits also include 150 MPH maximum, 81 MPH Vmc and VFR non-icing only.", "src": "POH 3-19, 3-9"},
{"id": "p2-130", "t": "s-airframe", "q": "If the electric pitch trim malfunctions, how does its AFM supplement say to disengage it?", "a": "Operate the push-button trim switch on the instrument panel", "d": ["Depress the center bar on the control wheel switch", "Pull the stabilator trim cable release knob", "Cycle both alternator switches OFF, then ON"], "why": "The supplement says to disengage electric trim with the push-button trim switch on the panel, and in an emergency it can be overpowered with the manual trim wheel. Pressing the center bar is part of normal operation, not a disconnect.", "src": "POH 3-23 · OEG App 2"},
{"id": "p4-001", "t": "s-normal", "q": "On the walk-around, how much strut piston should be exposed under static load on the main and nose gear?", "a": "Main gear 3-1/2 inches; nose gear 2-1/2 inches", "d": ["Main gear 2-1/2 inches; nose gear 3-1/2 inches", "Main gear 4-1/2 inches; nose gear 3-1/2 inches", "Main gear 3 inches; nose gear 2 inches"], "why": "POH 6-2 calls for 3-1/2 inches of exposed piston on each main gear and 2-1/2 inches on the nose gear at static load. Your school checklist agrees (3.5 in and 2.5 in).", "src": "POH 6-2"},
{"id": "p4-002", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What oil quantity does the POH walk-around call for in each engine?", "a": "Six to eight quarts", "d": ["Four to six quarts", "Eight to ten quarts", "Five to seven quarts"], "why": "POH 6-2: open the nacelle doors, check oil quantity (six to eight quarts), then secure both inspection doors. The school checklist uses the same 6–8 qt.", "src": "POH 6-2"},
{"id": "p4-003", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "How many fuel drains does the POH walk-around have you drain on the right side?", "a": "Five: two tank, one gascolator and two crossfeed drains", "d": ["Three: two tank drains and one gascolator drain", "Four: two tank drains, one gascolator, one crossfeed drain", "Two: one tank drain and one gascolator drain"], "why": "POH 6-2 lists two fuel tank drains under the wing, one gascolator drain near the bottom of the nacelle, and two crossfeed drains on the bottom of the fuselage inboard of the flap. The left side is then inspected the same way.", "src": "POH 6-2"},
{"id": "p4-004", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Per POH 6-3 and your school checklist, how is each engine primed for a normal start?", "a": "Pump on, mixture rich until fuel flow shows, then cut-off", "d": ["Pump the throttle three times with the mixture full rich", "Hold a primer switch for 3 seconds with the electric pumps off", "Crank with the mixture full rich and the electric pump on"], "why": "Both prime the same way: with the electric fuel pump on, move the mixture to rich until a fuel flow is indicated and stabilized, then back to idle cut-off; the mixture is advanced again as the engine starts. Your school then turns that engine's pump OFF before cranking (POH 6-3 leaves both pumps on until both engines are running).", "src": "POH 6-3 · School checklist"},
{"id": "p4-005", "t": "s-engine", "q": "After a normal start on a mild day, one engine shows no oil pressure after 30 seconds. Per the POH, what do you do?", "a": "Shut the engine down and have it checked", "d": ["Run it at 1500 RPM to help build pressure", "Wait up to two minutes for the oil to warm", "Continue and recheck pressure during run-up"], "why": "POH 6-3: oil pressure should come up within 30 seconds (it may take somewhat longer only in very cold weather). If there is no indication, shut down the engine and have it checked.", "src": "POH 6-3"},
{"id": "p4-006", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "Per your school's starting checklist, when is each engine's electric fuel pump turned off?", "a": "Right after priming, before the starter is engaged", "d": ["After the before-takeoff run-up, then check fuel pressure", "As soon as that engine's oil pressure starts rising", "Not until climbing through 1,000 ft AGL after takeoff"], "why": "School start: electric fuel pump ON, mixture rich until fuel flow shows, then idle cut-off (priming), electric fuel pump OFF, clear the prop, engage the starter, and advance the mixture as it starts. (POH 6-3 instead leaves both pumps on until both engines are running.)", "src": "School checklist · POH 6-3"},
{"id": "p4-007", "t": "s-engine", "q": "How does the POH hot start differ from the normal start?", "a": "The electric fuel pumps stay off, so the engine is not primed", "d": ["The throttle is full forward and the mixture stays in cut-off", "The engine is primed for twice as long as normal", "The propellers are set full aft to ease cranking"], "why": "Hot start: mixture idle cut-off, throttle open 1/2 inch, props forward, electric pumps OFF, crank and advance the mixture as it starts. If it won't start that way, use the normal procedure, which includes priming. Full throttle with mixture cut-off is the flooded start.", "src": "POH 6-3"},
{"id": "p4-008", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Which setup matches the POH flooded-start procedure?", "a": "Mixture cut-off, throttle full forward, electric pump off", "d": ["Mixture rich, throttle open 1/2 inch, electric pump on", "Mixture cut-off, throttle closed, electric pump on", "Mixture rich, throttle full forward, electric pump off"], "why": "Flooded start (POH 6-4): mixture idle cut-off, throttle full forward, prop forward, electric pump off, then crank. When the engine fires, retard the throttle and advance the mixture slowly.", "src": "POH 6-4"},
{"id": "p4-009", "t": "s-engine", "q": "An engine fails to start after 30 seconds of cranking. What does the POH say to do before trying again?", "a": "Let the starter cool for several minutes", "d": ["Release and immediately re-engage the starter", "Crank another 30 seconds with the pumps on", "Wait 30 seconds, then crank continuously"], "why": "POH 6-4: limit cranking to 30-second periods; if the engine doesn't start, allow a cooling period of several minutes. Engaging the starter immediately after releasing it may damage the starter mechanism.", "src": "POH 6-4"},
{"id": "p4-010", "t": "s-normal", "q": "After a start with Piper External Power, you remove the cable, turn the master on, and the ammeters show no alternator output. What does the POH say?", "a": "Do not attempt the flight", "d": ["Depart; the alternators will come on line in flight", "Reconnect external power, then depart VFR only", "Depart day VFR on the battery alone"], "why": "POH 6-4: master OFF before plugging in the jumper cable and again before removing it; then master ON and check the ammeters. The POH says do not attempt flight if there is no indication of alternator output.", "src": "POH 6-4"},
{"id": "p4-011", "t": "s-normal", "q": "You need to make a right turn while taxiing. How does the POH suggest using differential power?", "a": "More power on the left engine, less on the right", "d": ["More power on the right engine, less on the left", "Equal power on both, with right brake only", "Left engine at idle, right engine at 1500 RPM"], "why": "POH 6-5: turn with rudder pedal motion and differential power rather than brakes, with more power on the engine on the outside of the turn. In a right turn, the left engine is on the outside.", "src": "POH 6-5"},
{"id": "p4-012", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "How does the POH have you check the fuel crossfeed during taxi?", "a": "Each selector to CROSSFEED briefly while the other stays ON", "d": ["Both selectors to CROSSFEED together for about one minute", "Both selectors OFF briefly, then both to CROSSFEED", "Each selector OFF briefly while the other stays on CROSSFEED"], "why": "POH 6-5: place each selector on CROSSFEED for a short time with the other selector ON, then return both to ON. Do not attempt takeoff with a selector on CROSSFEED.", "src": "POH 6-5"},
{"id": "p4-013", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "Lined up for takeoff, you notice the left fuel selector is still on CROSSFEED. What does the POH say?", "a": "Return it to ON; never take off with a selector on CROSSFEED", "d": ["Leave it; crossfeed is fine if the fuel quantities are balanced", "Leave it; that's acceptable as long as both electric pumps are on", "Leave it; crossfeed gives that engine a second fuel source"], "why": "POH 6-5 says plainly: do not attempt takeoff with a selector on CROSSFEED. Both selectors go to ON for takeoff (pretakeoff check item 3).", "src": "POH 6-5"},
{"id": "p4-014", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "Why does the POH say to taxi with the electric fuel pumps off?", "a": "To check that the engine-driven fuel pumps are working", "d": ["To prevent vapor lock in the fuel lines on a hot ramp", "To reduce the electrical load on the alternators at low RPM", "To keep the engines from flooding while idling on the ground"], "why": "POH 6-5: the electric fuel pump should be off during taxi to check the operation of the engine-driven fuel pump. The autopilot should also be off during taxi.", "src": "POH 6-5"},
{"id": "p4-015", "t": "s-engine", "q": "On a cold day, how does the POH say you know an engine is warm enough to accept run-up power?", "a": "It shows no hesitation when the throttle is advanced", "d": ["Oil temperature reaches the middle of the green arc", "Cylinder head temperature reaches at least 200°F", "It idles smoothly at 600 RPM with no roughness"], "why": "POH 6-5: before advancing the throttle for the mag and prop checks on a cold day, make sure the engine will accept power. If there is no hesitation when the throttle is advanced, it is warm enough.", "src": "POH 6-5"},
{"id": "p4-016", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Per the POH, at what RPM is the feather check done, and what result is expected?", "a": "At 1500 RPM; RPM drops to 1000 within 1–3 seconds", "d": ["At 2000 RPM; RPM drops 200–300 within 1–3 seconds", "At 1000 RPM; the propeller feathers and stops", "At 1500 RPM; RPM drops to 800 within 5–7 seconds"], "why": "Run-up steps c–d: throttles to 1500 RPM, bring the prop control fully back to the feather position and then full forward. RPM should drop to 1000 in 1–3 seconds. The 200–300 RPM drop at 2000 RPM is the governor check.", "src": "POH 6-5"},
{"id": "p4-017", "t": "s-engine", "q": "During the feather check, RPM takes about 6 seconds to fall to 1000. What does the POH say this may indicate?", "a": "Low prop dome pressure, risking overspeed or loss of feathering", "d": ["A worn governor, risking RPM surges and hunting in the climb", "Excess dome nitrogen pressure, risking a prop stuck in high pitch", "Low oil quantity, risking oil starvation of the prop governor"], "why": "POH 6-5: if more than 1–3 seconds is needed, propeller dome pressure may be excessively low, with a danger of propeller overspeed or loss of feathering capability. Operating tip 14 (7-2) also links sluggish RPM control to low dome nitrogen pressure.", "src": "POH 6-5"},
{"id": "p4-018", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Per the POH, how is the governor exercised on the first flight of the day?", "a": "Three times at 2000 RPM, each with a 200–300 RPM drop", "d": ["Once at 1500 RPM, pulling back for a single 500 RPM drop", "Three times at 1500 RPM, cycling the props fully to feather", "Twice at 2200 RPM, each with a 100–200 RPM drop"], "why": "Run-up steps e–f (6-5): throttles to 2000 RPM, retard the prop control until a 200–300 RPM drop shows, three times on the first flight of the day. To confirm it is governing, pull for a 100–200 RPM drop, then add throttle: RPM should stay the same as MP rises. (Your school checklist cycles the props full back–full forward three times at 1500 RPM; per the POH the feather check is at 1500 RPM and the three governor cycles are at 2000 RPM.)", "src": "POH 6-5"},
{"id": "p4-019", "t": "s-engine", "q": "During run-up you select alternate air ON. What should happen, and when should you skip this check?", "a": "RPM drops from the heated air; skip it in dusty conditions", "d": ["RPM rises as the air filter is bypassed; skip it in heavy rain", "MP rises from the added ram air; skip it below freezing", "No change is expected on the ground; check it only in flight"], "why": "POH 6-5: there should be a drop in RPM when alternate air is ON, since heated air is being supplied. Do not check alternate air on the ground if dusty air conditions prevail.", "src": "POH 6-5"},
{"id": "p4-020", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What magneto drop limits does the POH give for the run-up?", "a": "Normal 100 RPM, maximum 175 RPM, max differential 50 RPM", "d": ["Normal 50 RPM, maximum 125 RPM, max differential 50 RPM", "Normal 100 RPM, maximum 150 RPM, max differential 75 RPM", "Normal 125 RPM, maximum 175 RPM, max differential 25 RPM"], "why": "POH 6-5: normal drop 100 RPM, maximum drop 175 RPM, maximum differential drop 50 RPM. Your school checklist uses the same numbers.", "src": "POH 6-5"},
{"id": "p4-021", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Right engine mag check: left mag drops 160 RPM, right mag drops 95 RPM. Per the POH, is that acceptable?", "a": "No; the 65 RPM difference exceeds the 50 RPM limit", "d": ["Yes; both drops are within the 175 RPM maximum", "Yes; only a single drop over 175 RPM is disqualifying", "No; any drop over the normal 100 RPM is unacceptable"], "why": "Each drop is under the 175 RPM maximum, but the difference between magnetos (160 − 95 = 65 RPM) exceeds the 50 RPM maximum differential. 100 RPM is the normal drop, not a limit.", "src": "POH 6-5"},
{"id": "p4-023", "t": "s-engine", "q": "What is the POH's normally recommended takeoff power at sea level?", "a": "Full throttle at 2700 RPM", "d": ["Full throttle at 2600 RPM", "28 in. MP at 2575 RPM", "25 in. MP at 2500 RPM"], "why": "POH 6-6: the normally recommended procedure for sea-level takeoff is full throttle at 2700 RPM. 25 in./2500 RPM is your school's cruise-climb setting, not takeoff power.", "src": "POH 6-6"},
{"id": "p4-024", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Departing an airport at 4,000 ft density altitude, the POH has you lean at full throttle to about what fuel-flow mark?", "a": "About 14.0", "d": ["About 14.9", "About 13.3", "About 15.5"], "why": "POH 6-6: at a high elevation, apply full throttle and lean until the fuel flow pointer stabilizes at the mark for the density altitude: about 14.9 at 2,000 ft, 14.0 at 4,000 ft and 13.3 at 6,000 ft. If the engine overheats, enrich until temperature returns to normal.", "src": "POH 6-6"},
{"id": "p4-025", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "Why does the POH warn against a fast turn onto the runway followed by an immediate takeoff, especially with low fuel?", "a": "Fuel can move away from the tank outlet and interrupt flow", "d": ["Side loads can overstress the nose gear shimmy damper", "The gyros may not yet have erected fully for departure", "The prop governors may not stabilize before rotation"], "why": "POH 6-7 says to avoid it, especially with a low fuel supply. Operating tip 10 (7-1) explains that the tank shape lets fuel move away from the outlet in some maneuvers; extreme running turning takeoffs can interrupt fuel flow and cause a temporary power loss.", "src": "POH 6-7 · POH 7-1"},
{"id": "p4-026", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "In the POH normal (flaps up) takeoff, at what airspeed do you ease back to let the airplane lift off?", "a": "80–85 MPH", "d": ["70–75 MPH", "90–95 MPH", "100–105 MPH"], "why": "POH 6-7: accelerate to 80–85 MPH and ease back on the wheel enough to let the airplane lift off, then accelerate to the best rate of climb speed, 105 MPH, or higher.", "src": "POH 6-7"},
{"id": "p4-027", "t": "s-normal", "q": "On a normal takeoff, when does the POH say to retract the landing gear?", "a": "When a gear-down landing is no longer possible on the runway", "d": ["As soon as the VSI shows a positive rate of climb", "At 500 ft AGL, once at or above 105 MPH", "Immediately at lift-off, to cut drag as early as possible"], "why": "Every POH takeoff technique retracts the gear when a gear-down landing is no longer possible on the runway. Operating tip 2 (7-1): don't retract prematurely, because the airplane may settle back onto the ground.", "src": "POH 6-7 · POH 7-1"},
{"id": "p4-028", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "For a flaps-up (0°) short-field takeoff, what rotation speed does your school's takeoff briefing use?", "a": "75 MPH", "d": ["70 MPH", "85 MPH", "90 MPH"], "why": "School takeoff briefing: short-field Vr 75 MPH with flaps 0° and 70 MPH with flaps 25°; then Vx 90 MPH over obstacles or Vy 105 MPH. 75 MPH is below the 80 MPH Vmc, so if an engine fails right after lift-off, do what POH 6-7 mandates below Vmc: retard the operating engine's throttle and lower the nose immediately. (POH 6-7's flaps-up technique rotates at 80 MPH.)", "src": "School briefing · POH 6-7"},
{"id": "p4-029", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "For the 25° flap short-field takeoff, what are the POH rotation speed and speed at 50 ft?", "a": "Rotate 70 MPH; about 80 MPH at 50 ft", "d": ["Rotate 80 MPH; about 85 MPH at 50 ft", "Rotate 65 MPH; about 75 MPH at 50 ft", "Rotate 60 MPH; about 70 MPH at 50 ft"], "why": "POH 6-7: use 25° (second notch) for the shortest ground roll and greatest clearance over 50 ft. Trim slightly nose up from the takeoff range, rotate firmly at 70 MPH, about 80 MPH at 50 ft. Holding it on the runway too long with 25° flaps can cause a wheelbarrowing tendency.", "src": "POH 6-7"},
{"id": "p4-030", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Why does the POH call its flaps-up short-field takeoff (rotate at 80 MPH) the safest technique when the obstacle margin is wide?", "a": "It's best configured to hold altitude after gear-up if an engine fails", "d": ["It gives the shortest ground roll of the three POH takeoff techniques", "It gives the greatest clearance height over a 50-ft obstacle of any method", "It allows the gear to be raised as soon as the airplane lifts off"], "why": "POH 6-7: with flaps up, if an engine fails the airplane is in the best flight configuration to sustain altitude immediately after the gear is raised, and it rotates at 80 MPH, the Vmc, rather than below it. The 25° technique (rotate 70 MPH) gives a shorter roll and better obstacle clearance but is momentarily below Vmc, and so is your school's 75 MPH flaps-0 rotation.", "src": "POH 6-7"},
{"id": "p4-031", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Just after a 25° flap short-field lift-off, below 80 MPH, the left engine fails. What does the POH say is mandatory?", "a": "Retard the good engine's throttle and lower the nose at once", "d": ["Hold full right rudder and keep climbing at Vx, 90 MPH", "Feather the left propeller first, then lower the nose to 90 MPH", "Raise the gear and flaps at once, then pitch for Vyse, 105 MPH"], "why": "The 25° technique is momentarily below Vmc (80 MPH). POH 6-7: if an engine fails while below Vmc, it is mandatory that the throttle on the operating engine be retarded and the nose lowered immediately to maintain control.", "src": "POH 6-7"},
{"id": "p4-032", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Shortly after takeoff the main cabin door pops partly open. What does the POH recommend first?", "a": "Fly the airplane normally and return to land, then close it", "d": ["Slow to 90 MPH and have the pilot close it right away", "Climb to pattern altitude, then close it yourself while flying", "Declare an emergency and land straight ahead off-airport"], "why": "POH 6-8: fly the airplane in a normal manner and return for a landing to close the door on the ground. Closing it in flight is only for when a landing can't be made, and it takes a second person aboard.", "src": "POH 6-8"},
{"id": "p4-033", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "If a door must be closed in flight, what airspeed does the POH specify, and what do you do next?", "a": "100–110 MPH; open the storm window", "d": ["90–100 MPH; close the cabin vents", "110–125 MPH; open the storm window", "Below 100 MPH; unlatch the side latch"], "why": "POH 6-8: maintain 100–110 MPH, open the storm window, pull the door closed with the upper latch properly positioned, then close the upper latch. Someone besides the pilot must do it. If it can't be closed, stay above 100 and below 125 MPH to prevent buffeting. (Your school checklist says slow to 100 MPH and close the cabin vents first; per the POH the range is 100–110 MPH.)", "src": "POH 6-8"},
{"id": "p4-034", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "As density altitude increases, how do the Seneca's best angle and best rate of climb speeds change?", "a": "Vy decreases slightly and Vx increases slightly", "d": ["Both increase slightly", "Vy increases slightly and Vx decreases slightly", "Both remain fixed in CAS"], "why": "POH 6-8: Vx is 90 MPH and Vy is 105 MPH at sea level. Vy decreases slightly and Vx increases slightly with increased density altitude.", "src": "POH 6-8"},
{"id": "p4-035", "t": "s-normal", "q": "What does the POH say about a time limit on full-power operation after takeoff?", "a": "None, but reduce to climb power any time after 500 ft AGL", "d": ["Five minutes at full power, then reduce to climb power", "One minute above 2600 RPM, then reduce to climb power", "None; keep full power until reaching cruise altitude"], "why": "POH 6-8: hold Vy with full power to about 500 ft AGL. There is no time limit on full power, but it burns a lot of fuel and is rarely needed, so reduce to a climb power setting any time after 500 ft AGL.", "src": "POH 6-8"},
{"id": "p4-036", "t": "s-engine", "q": "Reducing from takeoff to climb power, in what order does the POH have you move the levers?", "a": "Throttles first, then propeller controls", "d": ["Propeller controls first, then throttles", "Mixtures first, then throttles", "Throttles and props together"], "why": "POH 6-8: when reducing power, retard the throttles first, then the propeller controls. The descent guidance (6-10) is the same: if prop speed is reduced, do it after the throttles. This avoids high MP at low RPM.", "src": "POH 6-8 · POH 6-10"},
{"id": "p4-037", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What en route climb speed does the POH suggest for visibility, climb performance and engine cooling?", "a": "120 MPH", "d": ["105 MPH", "110 MPH", "130 MPH"], "why": "POH 6-8: an en route climb speed of 120 MPH provides good visibility, climb performance and engine cooling. 105 MPH is Vy.", "src": "POH 6-8"},
{"id": "p4-038", "t": "s-engine", "q": "During climb, what cylinder head temperature does the POH say must not be exceeded at any time?", "a": "475°F", "d": ["435°F", "400°F", "500°F"], "why": "POH 6-8: monitor CHT in the climb and keep it below 475° at all times. Closed cowl flaps give better climb performance, but adjust them as needed for cooling. 435°F and 400°F are the cruise targets for engine life.", "src": "POH 6-8"},
{"id": "p4-039", "t": "s-engine", "q": "For maximum engine service life, what cylinder head temperatures does the POH recommend in cruise?", "a": "Below 435°F high-performance, below 400°F economy cruise", "d": ["Below 475°F high-performance, below 435°F economy cruise", "Below 400°F high-performance, below 350°F economy cruise", "Below 450°F high-performance, below 425°F economy cruise"], "why": "POH 6-8: for maximum service life keep CHT below 435°F in high-performance cruise and below 400°F in economy cruise. 475°F is the never-exceed value.", "src": "POH 6-8"},
{"id": "p4-040", "t": "s-engine", "q": "In cruise, both CHTs are creeping toward the limit. Which actions does the POH list to bring them down?", "a": "Enrich the mixture, open cowl flaps, reduce power", "d": ["Lean the mixture, close cowl flaps, increase RPM", "Lean the mixture, open cowl flaps, add power", "Enrich the mixture, close cowl flaps, add power"], "why": "POH 6-8: reduce high cylinder head temperatures by enriching the mixture, opening the cowl flaps, reducing power, or any combination of these. Extra fuel and more cooling air both carry heat away.", "src": "POH 6-8"},
{"id": "p4-041", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "After takeoff, how does the POH have you turn off the electric fuel pumps?", "a": "One at a time above 500 ft AGL, checking fuel pressure stays green", "d": ["Both together at 1,000 ft AGL, checking that fuel flow stays steady", "Both together after level-off at cruise altitude, checking fuel flow", "One at a time after gear-up, checking manifold pressure holds steady"], "why": "POH 6-8: the electric fuel pumps may be turned off one at a time above 500 ft AGL, watching that fuel pressure stays in the green as each goes off. After cruise level-off, check that they are off (6-9).", "src": "POH 6-8"},
{"id": "p4-042", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Power Setting Table: for 65% power at 4,000 ft pressure altitude, standard temperature, 2400 RPM, what MP do you set?", "a": "22.0 in. Hg", "d": ["24.8 in. Hg", "24.4 in. Hg", "19.5 in. Hg"], "why": "POH 8-16 (Lycoming IO-360-C table): 65% (130 HP) at 4,000 ft is 22.0 in. at 2400 RPM or 24.8 in. at 2100 RPM. At that altitude 24.4 in. is 75% at 2400 RPM and 19.5 in. is 55% at 2400 RPM.", "src": "POH 8-16"},
{"id": "p4-043", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Cruising where the OAT is 20°F above standard, how do you correct the table manifold pressure to hold the same power?", "a": "Add about 0.32 in. Hg", "d": ["Subtract about 0.32 in. Hg", "Add about 0.16 in. Hg", "No correction is needed"], "why": "POH 8-16: correct MP about 0.16 in. Hg for each 10°F the inlet air differs from standard: add for warmer, subtract for colder. 20°F warmer is 2 × 0.16 = 0.32 in.", "src": "POH 8-16"},
{"id": "p4-044", "t": "s-normal", "q": "School procedure (not from the POH): what cruise-climb power and speed does your school checklist use above 1,000 ft AGL?", "a": "25 in. Hg and 2500 RPM at 120 MPH", "d": ["Full throttle and 2700 RPM at 105 MPH", "22 in. Hg and 2400 RPM at 120 MPH", "25 in. Hg and 2700 RPM at 105 MPH"], "why": "School checklist (Cruise Climb): 120 MPH, 25 in. Hg / 2500 RPM, cowl flaps as required, electric pumps off one at a time. This fits the POH (6-8), which only says to reduce to a climb power setting any time after 500 ft AGL and suggests 120 MPH en route; the POH gives no specific climb MP/RPM.", "src": "School checklist · POH 6-8"},
{"id": "p4-045", "t": "s-engine", "q": "After flying through rain above freezing, you enter below-freezing air. What induction-icing risk does the POH describe, and the fix?", "a": "Water in the air filter may freeze; select alternate air early", "d": ["Carburetor ice may form; apply full carburetor heat now", "Injector nozzles may ice over; switch the selectors to crossfeed", "None; a fuel-injected engine can't suffer induction icing"], "why": "POH 6-9: moisture collected in the air filter may freeze in the colder air; snow or freezing rain can also ice the filter. Alternate air is controlled manually, not automatically, and supplies heated air, so it is excellent protection if applied soon enough. The engines are fuel injected, so there is no carburetor heat.", "src": "POH 6-9"},
{"id": "p4-046", "t": "s-normal", "q": "What in-flight electrical load per alternator does the POH recommend, and why?", "a": "50 amps each, to ensure battery charging current", "d": ["60 amps each, the alternators' rated output", "40 amps each, to avoid overvoltage relay trips", "50 amps total across both, to protect the bus"], "why": "POH 6-9: the alternators can put out 60 amps, but limiting loads to 50 amps each assures battery charging current. The POH also says taking off into IFR with a single alternator is not recommended, and to watch the ammeters, especially at night or in IFR.", "src": "POH 6-9"},
{"id": "p4-047", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "How does the POH recommend managing fuel on a long cruise leg?", "a": "Feed the engines symmetrically; use crossfeed to even up if needed", "d": ["Run one side down first, then crossfeed from the other side", "Leave one selector on crossfeed so the sides balance automatically", "Burn the outboard tanks first, then switch to the inboard tanks"], "why": "POH 6-9: there is one combined fuel tank per engine (two interconnected tanks each side, POH 3-3), so feed symmetrically to land with about the same fuel on each side; crossfeed can even it up. Also track time and fuel used against the gauges.", "src": "POH 6-9"},
{"id": "p4-048", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "The fuel flow gauge reads well above actual consumption, or the two engines read unevenly. What does the POH suggest?", "a": "A clogged or restricted fuel injector nozzle", "d": ["A failing or worn engine-driven fuel pump", "A partially blocked or iced-over fuel tank vent", "An overly rich mixture setting in cruise"], "why": "POH 6-9: an indication considerably higher than the fuel actually being used, or an asymmetric indication, may mean a clogged fuel nozzle that should be cleaned. Operating tip 8 (7-1): a high fuel pressure indication on the fuel flow indicator is a possible sign of restricted nozzles.", "src": "POH 6-9 · POH 7-1"},
{"id": "p4-049", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Planning a long overwater leg, you consider a hydraulic failure. Gear down, TAS is about what fraction of gear-up TAS?", "a": "About 75%", "d": ["About 50%", "About 90%", "About 95%"], "why": "POH 6-9: there are no mechanical uplocks, so a hydraulic malfunction lets the gear free-fall to the down position. With the gear down, TAS is about 75% of the gear-up TAS at a given power, so plan for the lost speed and range.", "src": "POH 6-9"},
{"id": "p4-050", "t": "s-normal", "q": "What does the POH say to do with the mixtures and propellers during descent?", "a": "Enrich mixtures as you descend; props may stay at cruise RPM", "d": ["Keep mixtures leaned; set props full forward at top of descent", "Lean further to prevent fouling; reduce RPM before the throttles", "Mixtures full rich at top of descent; props back to 2100 RPM"], "why": "POH 6-10: when power is reduced for descent, enrich the mixtures as altitude decreases. The props may be left at the cruise setting; if prop speed is reduced, do it after the throttles have been retarded.", "src": "POH 6-10"},
{"id": "p4-051", "t": "s-normal", "q": "What does the POH suggest checking on every approach by retarding the throttles?", "a": "That the gear warning horn sounds", "d": ["That the stall warning horn sounds", "That the props stay on the governors", "That fuel pressure stays in the green"], "why": "POH 6-10: flying with the gear horn inoperative is not advisable, since it's easy to forget the gear, especially on a single-engine approach or with distractions. Having power fully off before touchdown also gives the horn a chance to blow if the gear isn't locked down.", "src": "POH 6-10"},
{"id": "p4-052", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "Per the POH, what pattern speeds are flown for a normal approach?", "a": "115 downwind, 110 base and turn to final, 95 final (MPH)", "d": ["110 downwind, 100 base and turn to final, 90 final (MPH)", "120 downwind, 105 base and turn to final, 90 final (MPH)", "105 downwind, 100 base and turn to final, 87 final (MPH)"], "why": "POH 6-10: slow to about 115 MPH before entering the pattern and hold it on downwind; 110 MPH on base and in the turn to final; 95 MPH on final, or 90 MPH if lightly loaded. (Your school checklist uses 110/100/90; per the POH it's 115/110/95.)", "src": "POH 6-10"},
{"id": "p4-053", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Per the POH landing check, where are the props set on downwind, and when do they go full forward?", "a": "2500 RPM on downwind; full forward as power comes off on short final", "d": ["Full forward on downwind, and left there all the way through landing", "2400 RPM on downwind; full forward only once the mains are on the ground", "2700 RPM on downwind; back to 2500 RPM as power comes off on final"], "why": "POH 6-10 landing check item 7: propellers set to 2500 RPM. When power is reduced on close final, the prop controls may be advanced full forward for maximum power in case of a go-around. (Your school's GUMPS sets props full forward on the approach.)", "src": "POH 6-10"},
{"id": "p4-055", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Why does the POH suggest waiting until you're off the runway to retract the flaps after a normal landing?", "a": "It avoids grabbing the gear handle by mistake", "d": ["The flaps act as an air brake during the rollout", "It keeps the stall warning from sounding", "Raising flaps on the roll overloads the tires"], "why": "POH 6-11: unless extra braking is needed or there is a strong crosswind or gusty air, wait until turning off the runway to retract the flaps. This avoids reaching for the gear handle instead of the flap handle and keeps your attention on the landing roll.", "src": "POH 6-11"},
{"id": "p4-056", "t": "s-normal", "q": "For maximum braking after touchdown, the POH says to:", "a": "Retract the flaps, hold back pressure, and apply brakes", "d": ["Keep flaps down, push the wheel forward, and brake", "Retract the flaps, push the wheel forward, and brake", "Keep flaps down, hold back pressure, and brake"], "why": "POH 6-11: maximum braking comes from retracting the flaps, applying back pressure to the wheel and applying the brakes. The short-field landing uses the same technique immediately after touchdown.", "src": "POH 6-11"},
{"id": "p4-057", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "What approach speed and flap setting does the POH give for a short-field landing?", "a": "Full flaps (40°) at 87 MPH CAS", "d": ["25° flaps at 87 MPH CAS", "Full flaps (40°) at 95 MPH CAS", "Full flaps (40°) at 80 MPH CAS"], "why": "POH 6-11: approach with full flaps at 87 MPH CAS; immediately after touchdown raise the flaps, apply back pressure and brake. 95 MPH is the normal final approach speed.", "src": "POH 6-11"},
{"id": "p4-058", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Which description matches the POH normal landing technique?", "a": "Full flaps, partial power until just before touchdown, nose held up", "d": ["25° flaps, power off over the threshold, nosewheel lowered right away", "Full flaps, power off on base leg, nosewheel touching down first", "10° flaps, partial power all the way to touchdown, nose held level"], "why": "POH 6-11: approach with full flaps (40°) and partial power until shortly before touchdown, and hold the nose up as long as possible before and after the main wheels touch.", "src": "POH 6-11"},
{"id": "p4-059", "t": "s-normal", "q": "What flap setting and approach speed does the POH recommend for a crosswind or high-wind landing?", "a": "0–25° of flaps, with higher than normal speed", "d": ["Full 40° flaps, at normal approach speed", "10° flaps only, at lower than normal speed", "25–40° flaps, at lower than normal speed"], "why": "POH 6-11: approach faster than normal with zero to 25° of flaps; crab into the wind until the flare, then lower the upwind wing and use rudder to align with the runway; raise flaps right after touchdown. Avoid prolonged sideslips with a low fuel indication.", "src": "POH 6-11"},
{"id": "p4-061", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "With the tank in use not full, what slip or skid do the POH operating tips say must be avoided?", "a": "Prolonged slips or skids that lose more than 2,000 ft", "d": ["Any slip or skid that loses more than 500 ft of altitude", "Any slip or skid held for longer than 30 seconds", "Prolonged slips or skids that lose more than 1,000 ft"], "why": "Operating tip 10 (7-1): the tank shape can let fuel move away from the outlet. Prolonged slips or skids causing more than 2,000 ft of altitude loss, or other radical maneuvers, can interrupt fuel flow when the tank in use is not full.", "src": "POH 7-1"},
{"id": "p4-062", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Per the POH post-landing check after leaving the runway, how are the flaps, cowl flaps and electric pumps set?", "a": "Flaps up, cowl flaps fully open, electric pumps off", "d": ["Flaps up, cowl flaps closed, electric pumps on", "Flaps 10°, cowl flaps fully open, electric pumps on", "Flaps up, cowl flaps closed, electric pumps off"], "why": "POH 6-11 post landing: wing flaps retract, cowl flaps fully open, electric fuel pumps off. Open cowl flaps help cool the engines on the ground.", "src": "POH 6-11"},
{"id": "p4-063", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Per the POH, which sequence matches the shutdown checklist?", "a": "Radios/electrical off, mixtures cut-off, mags off, master off, brake on", "d": ["Mags off, mixtures cut-off, master off, radios/electrical off, brake off", "Mixtures cut-off, radios/electrical off, master off, mags off, brake off", "Master off, mixtures cut-off, radios/electrical off, mags off, brake on"], "why": "POH 6-11: radio and electrical equipment off, mixture controls idle cut-off, magneto switches off, master switch off, parking brake on. (Your school checklist ends with parking brake OFF and a tie-down; the POH says on.)", "src": "POH 6-11"},
{"id": "p4-064", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "Flaps up, in level flight, the airspeed indicator reads 80 MPH (zero instrument error). What is the CAS per the POH correction table?", "a": "82 MPH", "d": ["80 MPH", "78 MPH", "85 MPH"], "why": "POH 6-12 table, flaps 0°: 80 IAS = 82 CAS (70 = 72, 90 = 92). All POH speeds are CAS unless noted. Below 90 MPH IAS the table is valid only in level flight, using power as needed to avoid rapid altitude changes.", "src": "POH 6-12"},
{"id": "p4-066", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "As defined in POH Section 6, Vmc (80 MPH) assumes the inoperative engine's propeller is:", "a": "Windmilling", "d": ["Feathered", "Stopped but not feathered", "Set at zero thrust"], "why": "POH 6-12: Vmc is the CAS below which the airplane can't be controlled with one engine at takeoff power at sea level density altitude and the other engine windmilling, the high-drag case. Vmc for the Seneca is 80 MPH.", "src": "POH 6-12"},
{"id": "p4-067", "t": "s-speeds", "q": "As a safety margin in single-engine training or emergencies, the POH says to keep indicated airspeed above:", "a": "90 MPH", "d": ["80 MPH", "100 MPH", "85 MPH"], "why": "POH 6-12: never fly below Vmc (80 MPH) on one engine, and as a precaution keep indicated airspeed above 90 MPH in single-engine training or emergencies. That is a floor, not a target: your school's engine-failure memory items hold blue line, 105 MPH.", "src": "POH 6-12"},
{"id": "p4-068", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Per the POH, what is the minimum height for performing the Vmc demonstration?", "a": "3,500 ft AGL", "d": ["3,000 ft AGL", "5,000 ft AGL", "1,500 ft AGL"], "why": "POH 6-12: the demonstration approaches an uncontrolled flight condition and should not be performed below 3,500 ft above the ground. Your school's pre-maneuver checklist uses 3,000 ft AGL for other maneuvers and 3,500 for the Vmc demo.", "src": "POH 6-12"},
{"id": "p4-069", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "During the Vmc demo, how does the POH say to recover?", "a": "Immediately reduce power on the operating engine and lower the nose", "d": ["Immediately add full power on the operating engine and hold the nose up", "Feather the idling engine's prop at once and apply full rudder", "Lower the nose only, keeping full power on the good engine"], "why": "POH 6-12: initiate recovery by immediately reducing power on the operating engine and promptly lowering the nose. Reducing power removes the asymmetric thrust; lowering the nose regains airspeed.", "src": "POH 6-12"},
{"id": "p4-070", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Per the POH, why is the altitude where stall speed and Vmc coincide the most critical for a Vmc demo?", "a": "Loss of directional control and the stall coincide; a spin may follow", "d": ["Asymmetric thrust is at its maximum value at that altitude", "Vmc reaches its highest value at that altitude, leaving no margin", "The stall warning horn may not sound at that altitude"], "why": "POH 6-12: Vmc is highest at low altitude and decreases with altitude because the operating engine makes less power, so higher up the airplane approaches a stall before Vmc. Where the two coincide, directional control is lost as the airplane stalls and a spin could result.", "src": "POH 6-12"},
{"id": "p4-075", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Your Seneca has no approved deice equipment, and you pick up ice in cloud. What does the POH warning direct?", "a": "Take immediate action to fly out of the icing conditions", "d": ["Continue the flight using alternate air and pitot heat", "Hold the altitude and cycle the props to shed ice", "Extend flaps to lower the stall speed and continue"], "why": "POH 6-9 warning: flight in icing is prohibited unless equipped with approved deicing equipment, and if icing is encountered, act immediately to fly out of it. Hazards: reduced performance, lost forward visibility, longitudinal control difficulty from increased control sensitivity, and impaired engine and fuel system operation.", "src": "POH 6-9"},
{"id": "p4-078", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Why does AD 99-14-01 prohibit autopilot use when severe icing cues exist?", "a": "It can mask the feel of adverse changes in handling", "d": ["It draws too much current with the deice system running", "It cannot hold altitude with ice on the tail", "Its servos may freeze and jam in severe icing conditions"], "why": "Per the AD, an operating autopilot may mask tactile cues of adverse handling changes. Its use is prohibited when any cue exists, or when unusual lateral trim needs or autopilot trim warnings occur in icing. If it's engaged, hold the wheel firmly and disengage it.", "src": "AD 99-14-01"},
{"id": "p4-079", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Which weather does AD 99-14-01 list as conducive to severe in-flight icing?", "a": "Visible rain or splashing droplets below 0°C ambient", "d": ["Dry snow at temperatures below −10°C ambient", "Visible moisture between 0°C and +10°C ambient", "Ice crystals at temperatures below −20°C ambient"], "why": "The AD lists visible rain below 0°C and droplets that splash or splatter on impact below 0°C. Severe icing may form as cold as −18°C, but extra vigilance is warranted near freezing with visible moisture.", "src": "AD 99-14-01"},
{"id": "p4-081", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "In icing, you notice an unusual roll response and uncommanded control-wheel movement. What does AD 99-14-01 direct?", "a": "Reduce the angle of attack", "d": ["Increase the angle of attack", "Engage the autopilot wing leveler", "Extend the flaps to the first notch"], "why": "The AD: if an unusual roll response or uncommanded roll control movement is observed, reduce the angle of attack. Also avoid abrupt maneuvering and keep the autopilot off.", "src": "AD 99-14-01"},
{"id": "p4-082", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "After an ELT test with the switch in ON, you move it straight to ARM. What happens, per the POH?", "a": "It keeps transmitting; select OFF before ARM", "d": ["It stops transmitting and re-arms normally", "It stops after one minute and then re-arms", "It runs a battery test, then re-arms"], "why": "POH 6-14 note: if the switch has been in ON, OFF must be selected before ARM; going directly from ON to ARM, the transmitter continues to transmit. To check for accidental activation, listen on 121.5 MHz for an oscillating tone.", "src": "POH 6-14"},
{"id": "p4-083", "t": "s-normal", "q": "In a busy pattern where high speeds are needed, up to what airspeed does the POH permit lowering the landing gear?", "a": "150 MPH", "d": ["125 MPH", "140 MPH", "160 MPH"], "why": "Operating tip 3 (7-1) and POH 6-10: the gear may be extended at speeds up to 150 MPH. 125 MPH is the maximum gear retraction speed and the flap limit.", "src": "POH 7-1 · POH 6-10"},
{"id": "p4-085", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Before resetting a popped circuit breaker, how long does the POH say to let it cool?", "a": "Two to five minutes", "d": ["About 30 seconds", "Ten to fifteen minutes", "No wait; reset it once right away"], "why": "Operating tip 5 (7-1): allow a two to five minute cooling-off period before attempting to reset any circuit breaker.", "src": "POH 7-1"},
{"id": "p4-087", "t": "s-engine", "q": "How does the POH recommend simulating an engine failure at low altitude in training?", "a": "Retard the throttle slowly to idle", "d": ["Pull the mixture to idle cut-off", "Turn the fuel selector to OFF", "Snap the throttle closed for realism"], "why": "Operating tip 15 (7-2): pulling a mixture or shutting off the fuel at low altitude isn't worth the risk. Retard the throttle slowly to idle instead; a fast power reduction may harm the engine.", "src": "POH 7-2"},
{"id": "p4-088", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Per your school checklist, what is the two-engine go-around sequence from the landing configuration?", "a": "Full throttle, pitch up, flaps 25°, gear up on a positive rate, rest of flaps", "d": ["Full throttle, pitch up, gear up, flaps 25°, rest of flaps on a positive rate", "Full throttle, flaps fully up, pitch up, gear up on a positive rate of climb", "Pitch up, flaps 25°, full throttle, rest of flaps, then gear up at 500 ft"], "why": "School go-around: throttles full forward, pitch up 10–12°, flaps retract to 25°, landing gear up with a positive rate of climb on the VSI, remaining flaps below 125 MPH, cowl flaps adjust. (The printed AFM, POH 3-7, lists positive climb, flaps, then gear.)", "src": "School checklist · POH 3-7"},
{"id": "p4-089", "t": "s-fuel", "q": "Per the POH downwind landing check, how are the fuel selectors, electric fuel pumps and mixtures set?", "a": "Selectors ON, electric pumps on, mixtures rich", "d": ["Selectors ON, electric pumps off, mixtures rich", "Selectors CROSSFEED, electric pumps on, mixtures rich", "Selectors ON, electric pumps on, mixtures as leaned"], "why": "POH 6-10 landing check: seat backs erect, belts fastened, fuel selectors ON, cowl flaps as required, electric fuel pumps on, mixtures rich, props 2500 RPM, gear down (three green and nose wheel in mirror), flaps as required (125 MPH maximum).", "src": "POH 6-10"},
{"id": "p4-090", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Climbing into an overcast, why do the POH operating tips say to turn off the anti-collision lights?", "a": "Reflected light can cause spatial disorientation", "d": ["They overload the bus when the pitot heat is on", "Their flashes interfere with the VOR receivers", "Ice forms on the hot lenses inside the cloud"], "why": "Tip 12 (7-1): anti-collision lights should be off when flying through overcast and clouds, since reflected light can produce spatial disorientation, and strobes should not be used while taxiing near other aircraft. A panel placard (3-4) says the same for strobes in cloud, fog or haze.", "src": "POH 7-1 · POH 3-4"},
{"id": "p4-091", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Per the POH approach and landing guidance, when and how do you confirm the gear is down?", "a": "Downwind and again on final: three greens and nose wheel in the mirror", "d": ["On the base leg only: three greens and the gear horn silent at idle power", "On downwind only: gear handle down and the red light out", "On short final only: three greens and the hydraulic pump quiet"], "why": "POH 6-10: check the gear on the downwind leg and again on final, using the three green lights on the panel and the external mirror to see that the nose gear is extended. Tip 6 (7-1): always determine gear position from the gear position lights.", "src": "POH 6-10 · POH 7-1"},
{"id": "p4-092", "t": "s-normal", "q": "In extreme turbulence or strong vertical currents with the autopilot engaged, which autopilot mode does the POH say not to use?", "a": "Altitude hold", "d": ["Heading hold", "Wing leveler", "Course (VOR) intercept"], "why": "POH 6-12 (Rough Air Flight): reduce power to slow slightly below maneuvering speed (133 MPH at light weight to 146 MPH at 4,200 lb), and when using the autopilot in extreme turbulence or strong vertical currents, don't use the altitude-hold mode.", "src": "POH 6-12"},
{"id": "p4-094", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Why does the POH walk-around have you extend and retract the flaps before starting the engines?", "a": "So you can hear any noise that might indicate binding", "d": ["So the hydraulic system can bleed air from the flap lines", "So the trim tabs can be checked for alignment", "So the flap motor runs while the battery is still fresh"], "why": "POH 6-2 (in cabin, item 11): extend and retract the flaps to check operation before engine start, when it's quiet enough to hear any noise that might indicate binding. The Seneca's flaps are manually operated (3-7); trim is set to neutral (item 10) to check tab alignment.", "src": "POH 6-2 · POH 3-7"},
{"id": "p4-095", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "The POH says the ELT battery must be replaced after two years of shelf or service life. When else should it be replaced?", "a": "After emergency use, or once test time totals more than one hour", "d": ["After any test, or once test time totals more than 15 minutes", "Only at the annual inspection, whatever the use or test time", "After emergency use, or once test time totals 30 minutes"], "why": "POH 6-14: the battery's useful life is four years, but to meet FAA rules it is replaced after two years of shelf or service life, and also if the ELT has been used in an emergency or accumulated test time exceeds one hour. The replacement date is marked on the transmitter label.", "src": "POH 6-14"},
{"id": "p4-097", "t": "s-normal", "q": "Per the POH operating tips, how should you trim the airplane for takeoff?", "a": "So only very light back pressure is needed to lift off", "d": ["Full nose-up, so the airplane lifts off by itself", "Neutral, so the stabilator is streamlined for the roll", "Nose-down, so the nose wheel stays firmly on the runway"], "why": "Tip 1 (7-1): learn to trim for takeoff so only a very light back pressure on the wheel is needed to lift the airplane off. The 6-7 techniques set the stabilator trim in the takeoff range, or slightly nose up from it for the 25° flap short-field method.", "src": "POH 7-1 · POH 6-7"},
{"id": "r4-001", "t": "s-emerg", "q": "Per your school checklist, how do you set up the Seneca for an emergency descent?", "a": "Throttles closed, props full forward, gear down, 140 MPH", "d": ["Throttles closed, props full aft, gear up, 190 MPH", "Throttles closed, props full forward, gear down, 160 MPH", "Throttles closed, props full forward, gear up, flaps 40°, 125 MPH"], "why": "School checklist (Emergency Descent): throttles closed, props full forward, mixtures as required, landing gear down, 140 MPH (120 MPH listed for a demo), cowl flaps closed. 140 MPH keeps the extended gear under its 150 MPH limit; 160 MPH would exceed it. The POH itself has no emergency descent procedure.", "src": "School checklist · POH 3-2"},
{"id": "p3-002", "t": "s-wb", "q": "What arm does the Seneca loading form use for the pilot and front passenger?", "a": "85.5 in", "d": ["93.6 in", "118.1 in", "78.4 in"], "why": "The sample loading form lists the pilot and front passenger at 85.5 in aft of datum. 93.6 in is usable fuel, 118.1 in is the center seats, and 78.4 in is the datum-to-leading-edge distance.", "src": "POH 4-7"},
{"id": "p3-003", "t": "s-wb", "q": "What arms does the loading form give for the center (middle) seats and the rear seats (seats 5 and 6)?", "a": "Center seats 118.1 in; rear seats 155.7 in", "d": ["Center seats 155.7 in; rear seats 178.7 in", "Center seats 85.5 in; rear seats 118.1 in", "Center seats 118.1 in; rear seats 178.7 in"], "why": "Center passengers are at 118.1 in and rear passengers at 155.7 in (the optional jump seat also uses 118.1 in). 178.7 in is the aft baggage compartment, behind the rear seats.", "src": "POH 4-7"},
{"id": "p3-004", "t": "s-wb", "q": "What arm is used for usable fuel on the Seneca loading form?", "a": "93.6 in", "d": ["103.0 in", "85.5 in", "118.1 in"], "why": "Usable fuel (93 gallons maximum on the form) is entered at 93.6 in. The 103.0-in arm belongs to the 5 gallons of unusable fuel, which is already part of licensed empty weight.", "src": "POH 4-7 · POH 4-5"},
{"id": "p3-005", "t": "s-wb", "q": "What are the arms of the forward (nose) and aft baggage compartments?", "a": "22.5 in forward and 178.7 in aft", "d": ["22.5 in forward and 155.7 in aft", "-7.8 in forward and 178.7 in aft", "43.2 in forward and 178.7 in aft"], "why": "Forward baggage is at 22.5 in and aft baggage at 178.7 in, the two extremes of the loading form. Because the aft compartment has the longest arm, its weight moves the CG aft the most per pound.", "src": "POH 4-7"},
{"id": "p3-006", "t": "s-wb", "q": "Where are the Seneca I's two baggage compartments, and how is each one reached?", "a": "Nose, via a left-side door; aft of seats 5 and 6, via the rear cabin door", "d": ["Nose, via a right-side door; aft of seats 5 and 6, via the rear cabin door", "Nose, via a left-side door; in each engine nacelle, via a top hatch", "Aft of seats 5 and 6 only, via a separate right-side baggage door"], "why": "The nose compartment (15.3 cu ft) has its own baggage door on the left side. The aft compartment (20 cu ft) behind seats 5 and 6 is reached through the left rear cabin door and can be reached in flight. Each is limited to 100 lb, and the aft placard warns against heavy objects on the hat shelf.", "src": "OEG App 2 · POH 2-26 · POH 1-2 · POH 3-4"},
{"id": "p3-008", "t": "s-wb", "q": "What are the Seneca I forward CG limits at 2,780 lb and at 3,400 lb?", "a": "80.7 in at 2,780 lb and 82.0 in at 3,400 lb", "d": ["82.0 in at 2,780 lb and 87.9 in at 3,400 lb", "80.7 in at both weights", "78.4 in at 2,780 lb and 80.7 in at 3,400 lb"], "why": "Forward limits: 80.7 in at 2,780 lb, 82.0 in at 3,400 lb, 87.9 in at 4,200 lb, varying in a straight line between those points. The aft limit is 94.6 in at all weights.", "src": "POH 3-3 · POH 4-9"},
{"id": "p3-009", "t": "s-wb", "q": "Your loading works out to 4,200 lb with the CG at 87.0 in. Is it within limits?", "a": "No; it is forward of the 87.9-in forward limit at that weight", "d": ["Yes; it is aft of the 80.7-in forward limit", "Yes; only the 94.6-in aft limit applies at gross weight", "No; it is aft of the aft limit at that weight"], "why": "The forward limit moves aft as weight increases, reaching 87.9 in at 4,200 lb. A CG of 87.0 in is fine at light weights but forward of the envelope at gross weight; move load aft or reduce weight.", "src": "POH 3-3 · POH 4-9"},
{"id": "p3-010", "t": "s-wb", "q": "At the 4,000-lb maximum landing weight, about where is the Seneca's forward CG limit?", "a": "About 86.4 in, on a straight line between 82.0 in and 87.9 in", "d": ["82.0 in, because the 3,400-lb limit applies up to gross", "87.9 in, because the gross-weight limit applies above 3,400 lb", "About 85.0 in, halfway between the 3,400-lb and 4,200-lb limits"], "why": "The limits vary in a straight line between the tabulated points. 4,000 lb is three-quarters of the way from 3,400 lb (82.0 in) to 4,200 lb (87.9 in): 82.0 + 0.75 x 5.9 = about 86.4 in. 85.0 in is the limit at 3,800 lb.", "src": "POH 3-3 · POH 4-9"},
{"id": "p3-012", "t": "s-wb", "q": "Which of these weight limits does the Seneca I POH NOT list?", "a": "A separate maximum ramp (taxi) weight", "d": ["A maximum takeoff weight", "A maximum landing weight", "A maximum weight for each baggage compartment"], "why": "The POH lists a 4,200-lb maximum (takeoff) weight, a 4,000-lb maximum landing weight and 100 lb per baggage compartment, and a placard requires all weight above 4,000 lb to be fuel, which works as a zero-fuel limit. No ramp weight is given, so the 4,200-lb maximum applies on the ramp too.", "src": "OEG App 2 · POH 3-3 · POH 3-4 · POH 1-2"},
{"id": "p3-013", "t": "s-wb", "q": "You take off at exactly 4,200 lb. About how much fuel must you burn before you are at maximum landing weight?", "a": "About 200 lb, roughly 33 gallons", "d": ["About 100 lb, roughly 17 gallons", "None; 4,200 lb is also the landing limit", "About 400 lb, roughly 67 gallons"], "why": "Maximum landing weight is 4,000 lb, so 200 lb must be burned. At the POH's 6 lb/gal that is about 33 gallons.", "src": "POH 3-3 · POH 4-8"},
{"id": "p3-014", "t": "s-wb", "q": "Per N56307's 2022 weight and balance report, what is the airplane's corrected empty weight?", "a": "2,831.0 lb", "d": ["3,389.0 lb", "2,625 lb", "2,743 lb"], "why": "The 11/18/2022 report weighed N56307 at 3,389.0 lb with its usable fuel aboard, then removed 558 lb of usable fuel to get a corrected empty weight of 2,831.0 lb. 2,625 lb is the POH's approximate standard empty weight; 2,743 lb is the minimum weight on the maneuvering-speed placard.", "src": "N56307 W&B report 11/18/2022 · POH 1-2"},
{"id": "p3-015", "t": "s-wb", "q": "What empty-weight CG does N56307's 2022 weight and balance report show?", "a": "85.00 in aft of datum", "d": ["86.42 in aft of datum", "82.00 in aft of datum", "80.70 in aft of datum"], "why": "The corrected empty weight of 2,831.0 lb has a moment of 240,641.4 in-lb, so the CG is 85.00 in. 86.42 in is the as-weighed CG with usable fuel aboard; 82.0 in is the basic CG in the POH plotter sample; 80.7 in is the forward limit at 2,780 lb.", "src": "N56307 W&B report 11/18/2022"},
{"id": "p3-016", "t": "s-wb", "q": "What useful load does N56307's 2022 weight and balance report list?", "a": "1,369.0 lb", "d": ["1,575 lb", "1,169.0 lb", "811.0 lb"], "why": "Useful load is maximum weight minus empty weight: 4,200 - 2,831.0 = 1,369.0 lb. 1,575 lb is the POH's approximate figure for a standard airplane; 1,169 lb (4,000 - 2,831) is the most non-fuel load the 4,000-lb placard allows; 811 lb (4,200 - 3,389) is what remains with full usable fuel.", "src": "N56307 W&B report 11/18/2022 · POH 4-6"},
{"id": "p3-017", "t": "s-wb", "q": "N56307 (2,831.0 lb at 85.00 in) carries 380 lb in front, 250 lb center, 180 lb rear, 60 gal, 50 lb nose and 45 lb aft baggage. Result?", "a": "4,096 lb at 91.20 in, within the envelope", "d": ["3,880 lb at 89.52 in, within the envelope", "4,096 lb at 91.20 in, aft of the aft CG limit", "4,654 lb at 91.49 in, over maximum takeoff weight"], "why": "Weight: 2,831.0 + 380 + 250 + 180 + 360 (60 gal x 6) + 50 + 45 = 4,096 lb. Moment: 240,641.4 + 32,490 + 29,525 + 28,026 + 33,696 + 1,125 + 8,041.5 = 373,544.9 in-lb, so CG = 91.20 in, between the forward limit (about 87.1 in at 4,096 lb) and the 94.6-in aft limit; without fuel it weighs 3,736 lb, under the 4,000-lb placard limit. 3,880 lb is the POH plotter sample's answer; 4,654 lb comes from wrongly starting at the as-weighed 3,389 lb.", "src": "N56307 W&B report 11/18/2022 · POH 4-7 · POH 3-3"},
{"id": "p3-018", "t": "s-wb", "q": "N56307 takes off at 4,096 lb, CG within limits, and must return to land 10 minutes later. Which limit is the concern?", "a": "It will still be over the 4,000-lb maximum landing weight", "d": ["None; a takeoff inside the envelope is good for landing too", "The CG will have moved aft of the 94.6-in limit as fuel burned", "None; the 4,200-lb maximum weight also applies to landing"], "why": "Takeoff at 4,096 lb is legal, but maximum landing weight is 4,000 lb, so about 96 lb (16 gal) must burn off first. Even at the 19.2-GPH red-line fuel flow on both engines, 10 minutes burns only about 6 gal. The POH says fuel burn does not significantly affect the CG.", "src": "POH 3-3 · POH 3-2 · POH 4-10"},
{"id": "p3-019", "t": "s-wb", "q": "What does full usable fuel (93 gal) weigh for Seneca loading purposes?", "a": "558 lb", "d": ["588 lb", "623 lb", "480 lb"], "why": "The loading graph uses 6 lb/gal, so 93 usable gallons weigh 558 lb. 588 lb would be all 98 gallons, but the 5 unusable gallons are already in the empty weight; 623 lb would use Jet-A's 6.7 lb/gal.", "src": "POH 4-8 · POH 3-3"},
{"id": "p3-020", "t": "s-wb", "q": "N56307 (useful load 1,369 lb) is filled with all 93 gal of usable fuel. About how much is left for people and bags?", "a": "About 811 lb", "d": ["About 1,369 lb", "About 1,169 lb", "About 781 lb"], "why": "1,369 - 558 (93 gal x 6 lb/gal) = 811 lb: four 170-lb adults and about 130 lb of bags, not seven people. The POH warns you cannot fill the airplane with seven adults and full fuel. 781 lb wrongly subtracts all 98 gal; the unusable fuel is already in the empty weight. CG must still be checked.", "src": "OEG App 2 · N56307 W&B report 11/18/2022 · POH 4-1"},
{"id": "p3-021", "t": "s-wb", "q": "Per the POH, what happens to the Seneca's weight and CG as fuel is burned?", "a": "CG changes little; it moves down the fuel line and stays in the envelope", "d": ["The CG moves well aft, because the fuel tanks are forward of the CG", "The CG moves well forward, often past the forward limit for landing", "Nothing changes, because the fuel tanks sit right at the datum"], "why": "The POH states that fuel burn-off and gear movement do not significantly affect the CG; in the plotter sample the point follows the fuel line down and stays in the envelope for landing. Gear retraction changes the moment by only -32 in-lb.", "src": "OEG App 2 · POH 4-10 · POH 4-11 · POH 4-9"},
{"id": "p3-022", "t": "s-wb", "q": "In the Seneca POH, what does 'basic weight' include?", "a": "Empty weight plus unusable fuel and full oil", "d": ["Empty weight plus full usable fuel and oil", "Empty weight plus the pilot and full oil", "Empty weight only, without unusable fuel or oil"], "why": "Piper defines basic weight as empty weight plus unusable fuel and full oil capacity. On the form, licensed empty weight adds the unusable fuel (5 gal, 30 lb at 103.0 in), and basic weight then adds the oil (16 qt, 30 lb at 49.0 in).", "src": "POH 4-1 · POH 4-6"},
{"id": "p3-023", "t": "s-wb", "q": "How is unusable fuel handled on the Seneca weight and balance form?", "a": "5 gal (30 lb) at 103.0 in, added to reach licensed empty weight", "d": ["5 gal (30 lb) at 93.6 in, entered on the form with the usable fuel", "2.5 gal (15 lb) at 103.0 in, ignored as negligible for W&B", "16 qt (30 lb) at 49.0 in, added to reach the basic weight"], "why": "The form adds 30 lb of unusable fuel at 103.0 in (moment 3,090) to the as-weighed empty weight to get licensed empty weight. The 2.5-gallon figure is per side; 16 qt at 49.0 in is the oil entry.", "src": "POH 4-5 · POH 4-6"},
{"id": "p3-024", "t": "s-wb", "q": "On the Seneca form, how is useful load (normal category) defined?", "a": "Gross weight (4,200 lb) minus licensed empty weight", "d": ["Gross weight minus basic weight and full fuel", "Landing weight (4,000 lb) minus licensed empty weight", "Gross weight minus empty weight and the pilot"], "why": "Useful load = 4,200 lb - licensed empty weight. The POH notes that useful load covers fuel, oil, baggage, cargo and passengers.", "src": "POH 4-6 · POH 4-2"},
{"id": "p3-025", "t": "s-wb", "q": "After new equipment is installed in the Seneca, who must compute the new basic weight and CG, and where is it recorded?", "a": "The mechanic doing the work, in the aircraft logbook", "d": ["The pilot, on the weight and balance plotter only", "Piper, by issuing a revised POH weight page", "The owner, at the next annual inspection"], "why": "The POH says the mechanic responsible for the work must compute a new basic weight and CG and enter them in the aircraft logbook. The owner should make sure it is done and update the plotter, and the current values should always be used.", "src": "POH 4-1"},
{"id": "p3-026", "t": "s-wb", "q": "When weighing the Seneca per the POH, how is the fuel handled?", "a": "Defuel, open all drains, then run each engine until it stops", "d": ["Fill all tanks and subtract the usable fuel weight", "Drain the tanks only, leaving fuel in the lines and engines", "Leave 2.5 gal in each wing as unusable fuel"], "why": "The procedure defuels the airplane, drains through all fuel drains and runs each engine until the undrainable fuel is used and it stops. The 5 gallons of unusable fuel are then added back by calculation.", "src": "POH 4-3"},
{"id": "p3-027", "t": "s-wb", "q": "Which is part of the POH's preparation for weighing the Seneca?", "a": "Seats in the 4th notch aft of forward, flaps up, controls neutral", "d": ["Seats in the full forward position, flaps 25 degrees, controls neutral", "Seats full aft, flaps fully down, tow bar removed and stowed", "Seats removed, flaps up, all entrance and baggage doors open"], "why": "Front seats go in the fourth notch aft of the forward position, flaps fully up, controls neutral, tow bar in its proper place and all doors closed. The airplane is weighed in a closed building, leveled by blocking the main oleos extended and deflating the nose tire.", "src": "POH 4-3"},
{"id": "p3-028", "t": "s-wb", "q": "Per the POH, what can happen if the Seneca's CG is too far forward?", "a": "It may be difficult to rotate for takeoff or for landing", "d": ["It may rotate early on takeoff and pitch up in the climb", "Longitudinal stability drops and spin recovery gets harder", "Stall speed rises but handling is otherwise unchanged"], "why": "The POH warns that a forward CG can make it hard to rotate on takeoff or landing. Early rotation, pitch-up, reduced stability and harder spin recovery are the aft-CG problems.", "src": "POH 4-1"},
{"id": "p3-029", "t": "s-wb", "q": "Per the POH, what can happen if the Seneca's CG is aft of the approved limit?", "a": "Early rotation, pitch-up in climb, less stability, harder spin recovery", "d": ["Difficulty rotating for takeoff and a nose-heavy landing flare", "A longer takeoff roll from extra tail-down force, but more stability", "More longitudinal stability and easier spin recovery as CG moves aft"], "why": "With an aft CG the airplane may rotate prematurely or pitch up in the climb, longitudinal stability is reduced, inadvertent stalls and spins become more likely, and spin recovery gets harder as the CG moves aft.", "src": "POH 4-1"},
{"id": "p3-030", "t": "s-wb", "q": "The POH warns of a twin-specific penalty for loading the Seneca heavy. What is it?", "a": "Less single-engine climb performance, losing a twin's main advantage", "d": ["A higher Vmc because rudder authority is reduced at high weight", "A shorter accelerate-stop distance because of better braking traction", "A lower stall speed that masks the approach to an engine-out stall"], "why": "The POH says the heavier the airplane, the less single-engine climb it has, so the pilot may lose one of the advantages of twin-engine flight. The single-engine climb chart shows this: about 190 fpm at 4,200 lb vs. roughly 430 fpm at 3,400 lb at sea level.", "src": "POH 4-1 · POH 8-8"},
{"id": "p3-031", "t": "s-wb", "q": "You remove the center seats and strap a 150-lb crate to the cabin floor. How should you check weight and balance?", "a": "Work weight and moment by hand; the plotter doesn't cover that spot", "d": ["Use the plotter's center-seat slot, since the arm is about the same", "Use the plotter's aft baggage slot, since all cargo counts as baggage", "No check is needed as long as the total stays under 4,200 lb"], "why": "The POH says the plotter does not cover cargo loaded anywhere other than the seats or baggage compartments, and that the computed method should be used when cargo is carried. You need the crate's actual arm.", "src": "POH 4-10 · POH 4-2"},
{"id": "p3-032", "t": "s-wb", "q": "How is the Seneca weight and balance plotter used?", "a": "Plot basic weight and CG, then chain a line along a slot for each load", "d": ["Plot each load from the basic point separately and average them", "Plot total weight only; the plotter computes CG from fuel", "Start at 4,200 lb and subtract each load along its slot"], "why": "Plot the basic weight and CG, put the zero end of a slot on that dot, draw to the load's weight, then start the next slot from the end of that line. The final point gives takeoff weight and CG. Keep the plotter out of the sun; heat can warp it.", "src": "POH 4-10"},
{"id": "p3-033", "t": "s-wb", "q": "In the POH plotter sample (basic 2,615 lb at 82.0 in, six occupants, 95 lb of bags, 60 gal of fuel), what is the result?", "a": "3,880 lb at 89.52 in, within the envelope", "d": ["4,096 lb at 91.20 in, within the envelope", "3,880 lb at 94.80 in, aft of the limit", "4,240 lb at 89.52 in, over maximum weight"], "why": "Loads: 380 lb front, 250 lb middle, 180 lb rear, 50 lb forward and 45 lb aft baggage, and 360 lb of fuel, giving 3,880 lb at 89.52 in, with room for more fuel. 4,096 lb at 91.20 in is the same load on N56307's heavier empty weight (2,831 lb at 85.00 in).", "src": "POH 4-10 · POH 4-11"},
{"id": "p3-034", "t": "s-wb", "q": "Per the POH's CG range and weight instructions, once total weight and total moment are known, what do you do?", "a": "Divide moment by weight, then plot weight and CG on the envelope", "d": ["Divide weight by moment, then plot moment on the envelope", "Subtract the 78.4-in datum offset, then compare with 94.6 in", "Multiply moment by 1,000, then compare with the plotter"], "why": "Add the weights and moments (moments from the loading graph or weight x arm), divide total moment by total weight for the CG, and plot that point on the weight vs. CG graph. If it is inside the envelope, the loading is acceptable.", "src": "POH 4-7"},
{"id": "p3-036", "t": "s-wb", "q": "Why does the battery have a negative arm (-7.8 in) in the Seneca equipment list?", "a": "It is in the nose, forward of the datum", "d": ["It is in the tail cone, aft of the datum", "Its weight is subtracted from empty weight", "Negative arms mark optional equipment"], "why": "Arms are measured aft of the datum, so anything ahead of it gets a negative arm. The 27-lb, 35-amp-hour battery is in the nose, reached through the forward baggage compartment, at -7.8 in.", "src": "POH 4-16 · POH 2-12"},
{"id": "p3-037", "t": "s-wb", "q": "In the Seneca equipment list, what does an 'X' next to an item mean?", "a": "It was installed when the airplane left the factory", "d": ["It is required equipment for night and IFR flight", "It has since been removed from the airplane", "It is inoperative and must be placarded"], "why": "The equipment list notes that items marked 'X' were installed when Piper delivered the airplane. The list gives each item's weight, arm and moment for recomputing empty weight after changes.", "src": "POH 4-13"},
{"id": "p3-038", "t": "s-perf", "q": "What does POH Section 8 say about values found by extrapolating beyond the chart limits?", "a": "Do not use them for flight planning", "d": ["Use them only with an added 25% safety margin", "Use them for takeoff but never for landing", "They are valid below 8,000 ft density altitude"], "why": "A warning at the start of Section 8 says performance derived by extrapolation beyond the charted limits should not be used for flight planning.", "src": "POH Sec. 8 index"},
{"id": "p3-039", "t": "s-perf", "q": "Which Seneca performance charts are entered with density altitude instead of pressure altitude and temperature?", "a": "The climb and cruise charts", "d": ["The takeoff and accelerate-stop charts", "The landing distance charts", "The stall speed charts"], "why": "The multi-engine climb, single-engine climb, cruise speed and range charts have a density-altitude axis, so use the Altitude Conversion Chart (8-1) first. The takeoff, accelerate-stop and landing charts are entered with temperature (degrees F) and pressure altitude directly.", "src": "POH 8-1 · POH 8-7 · POH 8-8 · POH 8-2"},
{"id": "p3-040", "t": "s-perf", "q": "Altitude Conversion Chart example: 25 degrees F at 8,000 ft pressure altitude. What density altitude results, and why?", "a": "7,500 ft, because 25 degrees F is colder than standard", "d": ["8,500 ft, because 25 degrees F is warmer than standard", "8,000 ft, because temperature does not change density altitude", "10,500 ft, because cold air always raises density altitude"], "why": "The chart's printed example gives 7,500 ft. Standard temperature at 8,000 ft is about 31 degrees F, so colder air makes the density altitude lower than the pressure altitude.", "src": "POH 8-1"},
{"id": "p3-041", "t": "s-perf", "q": "What does the flaps-0 takeoff ground run chart (8-2) assume?", "a": "80 MPH lift-off, full power before brake release, short-field effort", "d": ["70 MPH lift-off, full power before brake release, short-field effort", "85 MPH lift-off, rolling takeoff, normal effort", "90 MPH (Vx) lift-off, full power before brake release"], "why": "Chart 8-2: flap setting 0 degrees, lift-off 80 MPH, full power before brake release, short-field effort, paved level dry runway. 70 MPH is the 25-degree-flap lift-off speed. Your school rotates at 75 MPH for the flaps-0 short-field takeoff; the chart's distances assume the 80-MPH lift-off.", "src": "POH 8-2"},
{"id": "p3-042", "t": "s-perf", "q": "Chart 8-2 example: 70 degrees F, 2,000 ft pressure altitude, 3,600 lb, 10 MPH headwind, flaps 0. What is the ground run?", "a": "900 ft", "d": ["1,650 ft", "1,600 ft", "615 ft"], "why": "The printed example on the flaps-0 ground run chart gives 900 ft. 1,650 ft is the accelerate-stop answer for the same conditions; 615 ft is the landing ground roll example.", "src": "POH 8-2"},
{"id": "p3-044", "t": "s-perf", "q": "Chart 8-3 example: 70 degrees F, 4,000 ft pressure altitude, 3,600 lb, 10 MPH headwind, 25 degrees flaps. What is the ground run?", "a": "810 ft", "d": ["1,350 ft", "1,650 ft", "405 ft"], "why": "The printed example on the 25-degree-flap ground run chart gives 810 ft. 1,350 ft is the distance over 50 ft for the same conditions (chart 8-4).", "src": "POH 8-3"},
{"id": "p3-045", "t": "s-perf", "q": "What lift-off and 50-ft barrier speeds do the takeoff distance charts assume with 25-degree flaps (8-4) and with flaps 0 (8-5)?", "a": "Flaps 25: 70 and 80 MPH; flaps 0: 80 and 85 MPH", "d": ["Flaps 25: 80 and 85 MPH; flaps 0: 70 and 80 MPH", "Flaps 25: 70 and 90 MPH; flaps 0: 80 and 105 MPH", "Flaps 25: 65 and 80 MPH; flaps 0: 85 and 90 MPH"], "why": "Chart 8-4 (flaps 25) assumes a 70-MPH lift-off and 80 MPH at 50 ft; chart 8-5 (flaps 0) assumes an 80-MPH lift-off and 85 MPH at 50 ft, both with full power before brake release and short-field effort. Your school rotates at 75 MPH for the flaps-0 short-field takeoff; the chart's distances assume 80 MPH.", "src": "POH 8-4 · POH 8-5 · POH 6-7"},
{"id": "p3-047", "t": "s-perf", "q": "At 70 degrees F, 4,000 ft PA, 3,600 lb and 10 MPH headwind, how do the takeoff distances over 50 ft compare by flap setting?", "a": "1,350 ft with 25 degrees of flaps vs 1,600 ft with flaps up", "d": ["1,600 ft with 25 degrees of flaps vs 1,350 ft with flaps up", "810 ft with 25 degrees of flaps vs 900 ft with flaps up", "1,350 ft with either flap setting"], "why": "Both charts use the same example conditions: 1,350 ft with 25-degree flaps (8-4) and 1,600 ft with flaps 0 (8-5). 810 and 900 ft are ground-run examples, and the 900-ft one is at a different altitude.", "src": "POH 8-4 · POH 8-5"},
{"id": "p3-049", "t": "s-perf", "q": "At 4,200 lb, sea level standard, no wind, about what takeoff distance over 50 ft does the flaps-0 chart (8-5) give?", "a": "About 1,400 ft", "d": ["About 800 ft", "About 2,200 ft", "About 3,000 ft"], "why": "On chart 8-5 the sea-level line meets the standard-temperature curve at about 1,350 to 1,400 ft, read at the 4,200-lb reference line with no wind. That is a little longer than the 1,235 ft with 25-degree flaps.", "src": "POH 8-5"},
{"id": "p3-051", "t": "s-perf", "q": "What does the accelerate-stop chart (8-6) assume?", "a": "Flaps 0; accelerate to 80 MPH, then stop; full power before brake release", "d": ["Flaps 25; accelerate to 70 MPH, then stop; full power before brake release", "Flaps 0; accelerate to 100 MPH, then stop; rolling start", "Flaps 0; accelerate to 105 MPH (Vyse), then stop; rolling start"], "why": "Chart 8-6: flap setting 0, accelerate to 80 MPH and then stop, full power before brake release, paved level dry runway. Note that the takeoff engine-failure procedure is written around 100 MPH CAS; a stop from a higher speed needs more runway than this chart shows.", "src": "OEG App 2 · POH 8-6 · POH 3-12a"},
{"id": "p3-052", "t": "s-perf", "q": "Chart 8-6 example: 70 degrees F, 2,000 ft pressure altitude, 3,600 lb, 10 MPH headwind. What accelerate-stop distance does it give?", "a": "1,650 ft", "d": ["900 ft", "1,100 ft", "3,300 ft"], "why": "The accelerate-stop chart's printed example gives 1,650 ft. Under the same conditions the flaps-0 ground run (chart 8-2) is only 900 ft: accelerating to 80 MPH and then braking to a stop nearly doubles the runway needed.", "src": "OEG App 2 · POH 8-6 · POH 8-2"},
{"id": "p3-053", "t": "s-perf", "q": "You are planning a sea level standard departure at 4,200 lb with no wind. About what accelerate-stop distance does chart 8-6 give?", "a": "About 2,000 ft", "d": ["About 1,000 ft", "About 3,000 ft", "About 4,200 ft"], "why": "On chart 8-6 the sea-level line meets the standard-temperature curve at about 2,000 ft on the 4,200-lb reference line; with no wind that is the answer. Compare about 1,000 ft for the flaps-0 ground run alone.", "src": "OEG App 2 · POH 8-6"},
{"id": "p3-054", "t": "s-perf", "q": "Where do you find the Seneca I's accelerate-go distance?", "a": "Nowhere; the POH has no accelerate-go chart", "d": ["Chart 8-6, below the accelerate-stop curves", "Chart 8-8, the single-engine climb chart", "The Section 1 specifications at 4,200 lb"], "why": "Section 8 contains takeoff ground run, takeoff over 50 ft, accelerate-stop, climb, cruise, stall and landing charts, but no accelerate-go data. The POH leaves the continue-or-stop decision to pilot judgment based on loading, density altitude, obstructions, weather and competence; the single-engine climb chart shows what climb to expect.", "src": "OEG App 2 · POH Sec. 8 index · POH 3-12a"},
{"id": "p3-055", "t": "s-perf", "q": "You plan to depart a wet grass strip with a slight upslope. How do the Seneca takeoff charts apply?", "a": "Actual distances will be longer than the charts show", "d": ["The charts are valid; only a tailwind needs correction", "Use the 25-degree chart, which already allows for grass", "Reduce the chart values by 25%, per the chart note"], "why": "Every takeoff and landing chart assumes a paved, level, dry runway. The POH says chart performance is reduced by uphill gradient, tailwind, and soft, wet, rough or grassy surfaces. The 25% note concerns heavy-duty brakes on landing, not grass.", "src": "POH 6-7 · POH 8-2"},
{"id": "p3-056", "t": "s-perf", "q": "In what order do you work a Seneca takeoff or landing distance chart?", "a": "Temperature up to pressure altitude, across to weight, then headwind", "d": ["Density altitude up to weight, then across to headwind", "Weight up to temperature, across to pressure altitude, then wind", "Headwind first, then weight, then temperature and altitude"], "why": "Each chart is entered with outside air temperature (degrees F), up to the pressure-altitude line, across to the reference line, along the guide lines to the weight, then to the wind scale. The wind scale is headwind only, 0 to 30 MPH.", "src": "POH 8-2 · POH 8-6 · POH 8-13"},
{"id": "p3-057", "t": "s-perf", "q": "What configuration does the multi-engine climb chart (8-7) assume?", "a": "Gear up unless noted, flaps up, cowl flaps open, mixture leaned", "d": ["Gear down, flaps 25 degrees, cowl flaps closed, mixture rich", "Gear up, flaps up, cowl flaps closed, mixture full rich", "Gear up, flaps 10 degrees, cowl flaps half open, mixture leaned"], "why": "Chart 8-7 conditions: gear up except as noted, flaps up, cowl flaps open, mixture leaned per Lycoming instructions. One line shows 4,200 lb with the gear down.", "src": "POH 8-7"},
{"id": "p3-058", "t": "s-perf", "q": "On the multi-engine climb chart, what best rate-of-climb speed is used with the gear down?", "a": "92 MPH CAS at all altitudes", "d": ["105 MPH CAS at sea level, decreasing with altitude", "90 MPH CAS (Vx) at all altitudes", "100 MPH CAS at all altitudes"], "why": "A chart note says the gear-down best rate-of-climb speed is 92 MPH CAS at all altitudes. Gear up, the best rate speed is about 105 MPH at sea level and decreases slightly with altitude.", "src": "POH 8-7"},
{"id": "p3-059", "t": "s-perf", "q": "Chart 8-7 example: 4,000 lb at 5,000 ft density altitude, gear up. What rate and speed?", "a": "1,100 fpm at 103 MPH", "d": ["1,460 fpm at 105 MPH", "350 fpm at 98 MPH", "1,100 fpm at 92 MPH"], "why": "The printed example gives 1,100 fpm at a best rate-of-climb speed of 103 MPH. 1,460 fpm is the sea-level figure at 4,000 lb, and 350 fpm at 98 MPH is the single-engine chart's example.", "src": "POH 8-7"},
{"id": "p3-060", "t": "s-perf", "q": "Per the POH Section 1 figures at 4,200 lb and sea level, about how much of the Seneca's climb rate is lost when one engine fails?", "a": "About 86%, far more than half", "d": ["About 50%, since half the power is lost", "About 25%, since only drag increases", "All of it; it cannot climb at 4,200 lb"], "why": "Section 1 lists 1,360 fpm on two engines and 190 fpm on one at 4,200 lb: (1,360 - 190) / 1,360 = about 86%. Losing half the power costs far more than half the climb, because climb comes only from the power left over after drag is overcome.", "src": "POH 1-1 · POH 8-7 · POH 8-8"},
{"id": "p3-061", "t": "s-perf", "q": "Which conditions does the single-engine climb chart (8-8) assume?", "a": "Gear and flaps up, 5 degree bank toward the good engine, full throttle", "d": ["Gear down, flaps 25 degrees, wings level, full throttle", "Gear and flaps up, wings level and ball centered, 75% power", "Gear and flaps up, 5 degree bank toward the dead engine, full throttle"], "why": "Chart 8-8: one engine inoperative, cowl flap open on the operating engine only, gear and flaps retracted, 5 degrees of bank into the operative engine, full throttle, mixture leaned.", "src": "POH 8-8"},
{"id": "p3-062", "t": "s-perf", "q": "Chart 8-8 example: 3,400 lb at 2,000 ft density altitude, one engine inoperative. What climb and speed?", "a": "350 fpm at 98 MPH", "d": ["1,100 fpm at 103 MPH", "190 fpm at 105 MPH", "50 fpm at 90 MPH"], "why": "The single-engine chart's printed example gives 350 fpm at 98 MPH. Its best-climb speed lines get slightly slower at lighter weights; near 4,000 to 4,200 lb they are about the 105-MPH blue line.", "src": "POH 8-8"},
{"id": "p3-063", "t": "s-perf", "q": "At 4,000 lb, what are the Seneca I's single-engine service ceiling (50 fpm) and absolute ceiling, left engine out?", "a": "5,200 ft service; 6,600 ft absolute", "d": ["3,650 ft service; 5,000 ft absolute", "6,600 ft service; 5,200 ft absolute", "18,800 ft service; 20,000 ft absolute"], "why": "Section 1 lists 5,200 ft (service, 50 fpm) and 6,600 ft (absolute) at 4,000 lb. 3,650 and 5,000 ft are the 4,200-lb figures, and 18,800 and 20,000 ft are the two-engine ceilings at 4,000 lb. Shedding 200 lb buys about 1,500 ft of single-engine ceiling.", "src": "OEG App 2 · POH 1-1"},
{"id": "p3-064", "t": "s-perf", "q": "What are the Seneca I's two-engine service ceilings?", "a": "17,900 ft at 4,200 lb; 18,800 ft at 4,000 lb", "d": ["19,400 ft at 4,200 lb; 20,000 ft at 4,000 lb", "3,650 ft at 4,200 lb; 5,200 ft at 4,000 lb", "15,000 ft at 4,200 lb; 16,000 ft at 4,000 lb"], "why": "Section 1 gives service ceilings of 17,900 ft (4,200 lb) and 18,800 ft (4,000 lb). The absolute ceilings are 19,400 ft and 20,000 ft.", "src": "OEG App 2 · POH 1-1"},
{"id": "p3-066", "t": "s-perf", "q": "Cruising at 6,000 ft density altitude at 4,200 lb, you lose an engine. What does the POH predict?", "a": "No climb; it's above the 5,000-ft SE absolute ceiling and will descend", "d": ["A 50-fpm climb, since 6,000 ft is the single-engine service ceiling", "About 190 fpm of climb, the same as the sea-level figure", "Level flight, if you hold a 5 degree bank into the dead engine"], "why": "At 4,200 lb the single-engine absolute ceiling is 5,000 ft, so at 6,000 ft the airplane cannot hold altitude even when perfectly configured. It will drift down toward that altitude.", "src": "POH 1-1 · POH 8-8"},
{"id": "p3-067", "t": "s-perf", "q": "Per the POH, at what weight does the Seneca I's single-engine service ceiling equal 5,000 ft?", "a": "4,030 lb", "d": ["4,200 lb", "3,700 lb", "3,400 lb"], "why": "A Section 1 footnote says the 5,000-ft single-engine service ceiling occurs at 4,030 lb gross weight. It is 3,650 ft at 4,200 lb and 5,200 ft at 4,000 lb.", "src": "POH 1-1"},
{"id": "p3-068", "t": "s-perf", "q": "Up to what weight can the Seneca I climb on one engine at sea level standard?", "a": "Up to its 4,200-lb maximum, at about 190 fpm", "d": ["Only up to 4,000 lb, the maximum landing weight", "Only up to 3,400 lb; above that it needs both engines", "Only up to 4,030 lb; above that it cannot climb"], "why": "Chart 8-8 and Section 1 show positive single-engine climb even at 4,200 lb: about 190 fpm at sea level, with an absolute ceiling of 5,000 ft. 4,030 lb is the weight for a 5,000-ft service ceiling, not a climb limit.", "src": "OEG App 2 · POH 1-1 · POH 8-8"},
{"id": "p3-069", "t": "s-perf", "q": "What conditions does the cruise true-airspeed chart (8-9) assume?", "a": "4,200 lb, gear and flaps up, cowl flaps closed, mixture leaned", "d": ["4,200 lb, gear up, cowl flaps open, mixture full rich", "3,600 lb, gear and flaps up, cowl flaps closed, mixture leaned", "4,000 lb, gear up, cowl flaps open, mixture leaned"], "why": "Chart 8-9 is drawn for 4,200 lb gross weight, flaps up, gear up, cowl flaps closed and mixture leaned per Lycoming instructions, with lines for 45 to 75% power and full throttle at 2,400 and 2,700 RPM.", "src": "POH 8-9"},
{"id": "p3-070", "t": "s-perf", "q": "On the cruise TAS chart, up to about what density altitude can you get 75% power at 2,400 RPM?", "a": "About 6,000 ft", "d": ["About 9,000 ft", "About 13,000 ft", "About 18,000 ft"], "why": "The 75% line ends at the full-throttle 2,400 RPM line near 6,000 ft (about 186 MPH TAS). 65% runs to about 9,000 ft, 55% to about 13,000 ft and 45% to about 18,500 ft.", "src": "POH 8-9 · POH 1-1"},
{"id": "p3-071", "t": "s-perf", "q": "What conditions does the cruise range chart (8-10) assume?", "a": "4,200 lb, 93 gal usable, 2,400 RPM, best economy, gear up", "d": ["4,200 lb, 98 gal usable, 2,700 RPM, best power, gear up", "3,600 lb, 93 gal usable, 2,400 RPM, best power, gear up", "4,200 lb, 93 gal usable, 2,100 RPM, full rich, gear up"], "why": "The range chart assumes 4,200 lb, 98 gallons total (93 usable), 2,400 RPM, best economy mixture leaned per Lycoming instructions, gear up. Range is in statute miles.", "src": "POH 8-10"},
{"id": "p3-072", "t": "s-perf", "q": "On the cruise range chart, what do the solid and dashed lines mean?", "a": "Solid: no reserve; dashed: 45-min reserve at 55% power", "d": ["Solid: 45-min reserve at 55% power; dashed: no reserve", "Solid: gear up; dashed: gear down", "Solid: 2,400 RPM; dashed: 2,100 RPM"], "why": "The legend shows solid lines for no reserve and dashed lines for a 45-minute reserve at 55% power. Higher altitude and lower power both increase range.", "src": "POH 8-10"},
{"id": "p3-073", "t": "s-perf", "q": "You plan a 3-hour leg at 65% power. Using the range chart's fuel table, about how much fuel will both engines burn?", "a": "About 55 gal", "d": ["About 62 gal", "About 27 gal", "About 48 gal"], "why": "The table gives total (both engines) fuel flow: 75% 20.6, 65% 18.3, 55% 16.0 and 45% 14.0 GPH. 18.3 x 3 = about 55 gal. 62 gal would be 75% power, 48 gal 55% power, and 27 gal is what one engine alone would burn.", "src": "POH 8-10 · POH 1-1"},
{"id": "p3-074", "t": "s-perf", "q": "The Section 8 stall-speed charts (8-11 and 8-12) are drawn for what power setting and gear position?", "a": "Power off, gear extended; lines for flaps up and flaps 40 degrees", "d": ["Power off, gear retracted; lines for flaps up and flaps 40 degrees", "Power on, gear extended; lines for flaps up and flaps 25 degrees", "Power on, gear retracted; lines for flaps up and flaps 40 degrees"], "why": "Both charts are labeled power off, with one line for gear extended and flaps retracted and one for gear extended and flaps 40 degrees; chart 8-12 is drawn at 4,200 lb. Stall speed falls with weight on 8-11 (at 3,800 lb about 72 MPH CAS flaps up and 65 MPH flaps 40) and rises with bank on 8-12.", "src": "POH 8-11 · POH 8-12"},
{"id": "p3-075", "t": "s-perf", "q": "At 4,200 lb, power off, gear down, flaps 40 degrees, about what is the stall speed in a 60 degree bank (chart 8-12)?", "a": "About 98 MPH CAS", "d": ["About 69 MPH CAS", "About 108 MPH CAS", "About 138 MPH CAS"], "why": "The flaps-40 line rises from 69 MPH CAS wings level to about 98 MPH at 60 degrees, matching the AFM stall table. 108 MPH is the flaps-up line. A 60 degree bank doubles the load factor, which raises stall speed by about 1.41 times, not 2.", "src": "POH 8-12 · POH 3-19"},
{"id": "p3-076", "t": "s-perf", "q": "What technique does the landing ground run chart (8-13) assume?", "a": "67 MPH touchdown, flaps 40, flaps retracted on rollout, full braking", "d": ["87 MPH touchdown, flaps 40, flaps left down, full braking", "67 MPH touchdown, flaps 25, flaps left down, moderate braking", "80 MPH touchdown, flaps 40, flaps retracted on rollout, full braking"], "why": "Chart 8-13 assumes a paved level dry runway, 67 MPH touchdown, 40-degree flaps retracted on the rollout, and full braking. 87 MPH is the approach speed on the 50-ft chart.", "src": "POH 8-13"},
{"id": "p3-077", "t": "s-perf", "q": "What approach and touchdown speeds does the landing distance over 50 ft chart (8-14) assume?", "a": "Power-off approach at 87 MPH; touchdown at 67 MPH", "d": ["Power-off approach at 105 MPH; touchdown at 87 MPH", "Partial-power approach at 87 MPH; touchdown at 80 MPH", "Power-off approach at 92 MPH; touchdown at 76 MPH"], "why": "Chart 8-14 assumes a power-off approach at 87 MPH, a 67-MPH touchdown, flaps 40 retracted on the rollout, full braking and short-field effort on a paved, level, dry runway. 87 MPH is also the POH short-field approach speed; partial power until shortly before touchdown is the normal-landing technique.", "src": "POH 8-14 · POH 6-11"},
{"id": "p3-078", "t": "s-perf", "q": "With the optional heavy-duty wheels, brakes and tires, how may the landing distances be reduced?", "a": "About 25% off ground roll; about 12% off distance over 50 ft", "d": ["About 12% off ground roll; about 25% off distance over 50 ft", "About 25% off both landing distances", "About 10% off both takeoff and landing distances"], "why": "Notes on charts 8-13 and 8-14 allow about 25% off the ground run and about 12% off the distance over 50 ft with the heavy-duty equipment. Check the equipment list to see whether it is installed.", "src": "POH 8-13 · POH 8-14"},
{"id": "p3-079", "t": "s-perf", "q": "Chart 8-14 example: 70 degrees F, 4,000 ft pressure altitude, 3,600 lb, 5 MPH headwind. Landing distance over 50 ft?", "a": "1,240 ft", "d": ["615 ft", "1,650 ft", "2,400 ft"], "why": "The printed example on the 50-ft landing chart gives 1,240 ft. 615 ft is the ground-roll chart's example, which is at 2,000 ft pressure altitude.", "src": "POH 8-14"},
{"id": "p3-081", "t": "s-perf", "q": "The landing charts assume flaps retracted on the rollout. When does the POH say to actually retract them during the rollout?", "a": "Only when extra braking, a strong crosswind or gusty air calls for it", "d": ["Always, immediately after every touchdown, to shorten the roll", "Never; always wait until the airplane has cleared the runway", "Only on grass or wet runways, where braking is poor"], "why": "Retracting flaps, holding back pressure and braking gives maximum braking. Otherwise the POH recommends waiting until turning off the runway, to avoid grabbing the gear handle by mistake.", "src": "POH 6-11 · POH 8-13"},
{"id": "p3-082", "t": "s-perf", "q": "You want 75% power at 4,000 ft pressure altitude, standard temperature. What does the power setting table (8-16) give?", "a": "2,400 RPM and 24.4 in. MP", "d": ["2,400 RPM and 25.5 in. MP", "2,100 RPM and 24.8 in. MP", "2,700 RPM and 24.4 in. MP"], "why": "The IO-360-C table lists 75% (150 HP) only at 2,400 RPM: 25.5 in. at sea level, 24.4 in. at 4,000 ft, and full throttle by 5,000 ft. 2,100 RPM and 24.8 in. is the 65% setting at 4,000 ft.", "src": "POH 8-16"},
{"id": "p3-084", "t": "s-wb", "q": "What does retracting the Seneca's landing gear do to weight and balance, per the envelope page?", "a": "Changes the moment by only -32 in-lb, a negligible CG shift", "d": ["Moves the CG about 3.2 in forward, so recheck the envelope", "Changes the moment by +32 in-lb, moving the CG noticeably aft", "Changes the moment by -32,000 in-lb, a large forward CG shift"], "why": "The weight vs. CG envelope notes a -32 in-lb moment change when the gear retracts. At 4,000 lb that moves the CG less than 0.01 in, and the POH says gear movement and fuel burn do not significantly affect the CG.", "src": "POH 4-9 · POH 4-10"},
{"id": "p3-085", "t": "s-wb", "q": "N56307 (2,831.0 lb at 85.00 in): 390 lb in the front seats, 93 gal of fuel, 100 lb in the nose baggage, nothing else. Within limits?", "a": "No: about 3,879 lb at 84.7 in, forward of the ~85.5-in limit", "d": ["Yes: about 3,879 lb at 84.7 in, aft of the 80.7-in forward limit", "Yes: about 3,879 lb at 88.7 in, well inside the envelope", "No: about 3,879 lb at 84.7 in, over the maximum landing weight"], "why": "Weight 2,831.0 + 390 + 558 + 100 = 3,879 lb; moment 240,641.4 + 33,345 + 52,228.8 + 2,250 = 328,465.2 in-lb, so CG = 84.7 in. At 3,879 lb the forward limit is 82.0 + (479/800 x 5.9) = about 85.5 in, so the CG is almost an inch too far forward. Moving the 100 lb to the aft compartment gives about 88.7 in, inside the envelope.", "src": "N56307 W&B report 11/18/2022 · POH 4-7 · POH 3-3"},
{"id": "p3-086", "t": "s-wb", "q": "A placard requires all weight above 4,000 lb to be fuel. What is the most N56307 (empty 2,831.0 lb) can carry in people, bags and cargo?", "a": "About 1,169 lb", "d": ["About 1,369 lb", "About 811 lb", "About 1,575 lb"], "why": "Weight without fuel may not exceed 4,000 lb, so 4,000 - 2,831 = about 1,169 lb, and then only 200 lb (about 33 gal) of fuel fits under 4,200 lb. 1,369 lb is the useful load, which must include the fuel; 811 lb is what remains with full fuel; 1,575 lb is the POH's standard-airplane useful load.", "src": "POH 3-4 · N56307 W&B report 11/18/2022"},
{"id": "p3-087", "t": "s-wb", "q": "N56307's 2022 weight and balance report weighed the airplane with usable fuel aboard. What adjustment produced its corrected empty weight?", "a": "Subtract 558 lb (93 gal) of usable fuel at 93.6 in", "d": ["Subtract 588 lb (98 gal) of fuel at 93.6 in", "Add 30 lb (5 gal) of unusable fuel at 103.0 in", "Subtract 558 lb (93 gal) of usable fuel at 103.0 in"], "why": "The report lists 3,389.0 lb as weighed, then a usable-fuel adjustment of -558 lb at 93.6 in (moment -52,228.8 in-lb), giving 2,831.0 lb at 85.00 in. The unusable fuel stays in the empty weight. The POH method instead weighs the airplane drained and adds 30 lb of unusable fuel at 103.0 in.", "src": "N56307 W&B report 11/18/2022 · POH 4-5 · POH 4-7"},
{"id": "p3-088", "t": "s-wb", "q": "Removing the usable fuel moved N56307's CG from 86.42 in (as weighed) to 85.00 in. Why did the CG move forward?", "a": "The fuel's 93.6-in arm is aft of the 86.42-in CG", "d": ["The fuel's 93.6-in arm is forward of the 86.42-in CG", "Removing any weight always moves the CG forward", "The fuel tanks are ahead of the datum, at a negative arm"], "why": "Removing weight that sits aft of the CG moves the CG forward; removing weight ahead of it moves the CG aft. The fuel at 93.6 in is aft of 86.42 in, so taking out 558 lb moved the CG forward to 85.00 in. In flight the POH says fuel burn does not significantly affect the CG.", "src": "N56307 W&B report 11/18/2022 · POH 4-7 · POH 4-10"}
];
